mpact, goal or constraint in either supplies the motivation.\n- NOT_MET: an explanation is supplied but misleads — it orients the reviewer toward a different change than the one described. Quote the misleading clause.\n- NOT_MET: the WHY is required and missing — the body is empty or template, or the only candidate clauses restate the action. Name which is missing: a title that names nothing, a body that names no reason, or both. An empty body and a restatement require different evidence and different feedback; say which you found.\n- UNDETERMINED: you genuinely cannot tell a restatement from a motivation AND that clause is the only candidate WHY. Quote it and do not resolve the doubt in either direction. Having read the whole title and body and found no reason is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a formative practice, not a merge gate. Calibrate by review cost, not by counters. MINOR, the usual band: a small, single-file change with a thin description; a generic-but-harmless title atop an otherwise informative body, the lightest case; a body that enumerates what changed but omits the why — it still orients. Toward MAJOR: a contentless body on a broad, multi-file change, the heaviest cost. Do not inflate a small change with a thin description.\n\n## Grounding\nTo claim the WHY is observed, quote the exact rationale clause verbatim from the title or body. To call it absent, cite the boundary: the title, and the body quoted or stated to be empty or template, with every bullet, nested list and checklist item read. Never fabricate a verdict on a field you cannot see. Never claim what the diff does; the description is the subject. Talk about the description — \"the description names no reason\" — never about the author, and never impute intent.\n\n## Defer\nCode quality belongs to the craft practices; how the commits are partitioned belongs to `commits-are-atomic-and-cohesive`; conduct in review threads belongs to `engaging-with-inline-review-comments`; whether a reviewer can verify the change belongs to `states-how-to-verify-the-change`. Here only: does the description orient a reviewer on what and why?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Your reviewer was not in your head when you wrote the code. A sentence on why you made the change spares them from reverse-engineering your intent from the diff, and missing that rationale is one of the most common things that leaves a reviewer confused.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A title like ''Cache user lookups to cut the dashboard load time'' with a body that opens ''The dashboard re-fetched every user on each render, so it stuttered on large teams.''","groupSlug":"review-ready-work","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":true,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 188\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">What only</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"126.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Cache user lookups</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"124\" width=\"232\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"136\" width=\"168\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"16.75\" y=\"80.75\" width=\"110.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"28\" y=\"101\" font-size=\"15\">No reason</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"184\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer guesses why</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">What and why</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"126.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Cache user lookups</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"124\" width=\"232\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"136\" width=\"168\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"101\" font-size=\"15\">Dashboard stalls on big teams</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"184\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer knows why</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A pull request titled “Cache user lookups” gives no reason, so the reviewer guesses why. The same pull request with “Dashboard stalls on big teams” in its description states the reason, so the reviewer knows why."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Write a title that names the concrete change, as an instruction. A prefix such as a story ID or a tag, like “[Feat] US5.2:”, is fine.\n- Say why the change exists, in the title or the description. Name the problem it solves, the effect for users, the goal, or a constraint. For example, instead of listing files, write “Let organizers export results as CSV, so they can share them”.\n- Give the reason for the whole change, not only for one choice inside it. “Moved the helpers to match the naming” explains a choice. It does not say why the change exists.\n- If the change answers an issue, also write its main point in the description. A link tells the reviewer where to look, but it does not say why.\n- Before you ask for a review, replace every template placeholder with your own words.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- There is no usual case where it does not apply. Every pull or merge request needs a title and a description that say what changed and why.\n\nIt still applies when the change is small.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking you need a “Why” heading or words such as “because” or “so that”. Instead, name the problem or the goal anywhere in the title or the description. A bullet or a checklist item works too.\n- Repeating the change as its reason. “I added the export screen as planned” says what, and “Sprint 3” says when. Instead, name the problem or the user goal that the change serves.\n- Listing only the parts you built. The reviewer then knows what changed, but not why. Instead, name the problem or the goal that those parts serve. It can be part of the same sentence.\n- Leaving an old reason in place after the change grew or changed direction. A reason for a different change sends the reviewer the wrong way. Instead, update the description when the work changes.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Writing good CL descriptions](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/developer/cl-descriptions.html), Google Engineering Practices. The first line says what the change does. The description also says why, because code can show what it does but not why it exists.\n- [Describe your changes](https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/process/submitting-patches.html#describe-your-changes), Linux kernel documentation. Describe the problem and its effect on users, and make the description clear without links.\n- [Confusion in code reviews: reasons, impacts, and coping strategies](https://doi.org/10.1109/SANER.2019.8668024), Ebert, Castor, Novielli and Serebrenik, 2019. Missing rationale was the reason for confusion that reviewers reported most often, and confusion delays the merge decision.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Every description says what changed and why"'::jsonb),'v5:d07333b94f4ab73608997253b222bae9410e98cd1c6eb86fc624bd9f0a1239d4','practice:v3:dd0e11a97278081a7d91fa7911177e48c08e7492600b177c87433d8c5a40de01');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','states-how-to-verify-the-change','scm.pull_request','{"name":"State how to verify the change","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.linked-work-items","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: for the material effect of this change, a reviewer who knows the repository''s documented setup can find where to start, what to do and what to expect — from what the author supplied or explicitly adopted.\n\n## The standard\nJudge ONE check, for the main material effect in the patch: the one the title, description or adopted issue says the change is for, else the bulk of it. It is communicated when three elements are available, each from any permitted source: an ENTRY (a command, a screen, a named test, an authored preview, a documented setup step), an ACTION (run, tap, enter, configure, restart, or — for an appearance-only change — render and inspect) and an EXPECTED RESULT (a stated requirement, an assertion, a visible state, a persisted value). A sentence that names where to look and what is now there — \"the settings screen now shows a dark-mode switch\", \"the chart now shows a weekly total\", \"verify the list updates\" — is an entry and an expected result; do not demand a literal value or a numbered step list on top of it. A list of the types added (\"a service, a DTO and a view model\") is neither, and so is \"fixed the login flow\" without the failure it removes or what now happens. The lapse is a material effect with nothing anywhere saying what success looks like. \"Testing: N/A\" or an empty heading is evidence of nothing; read every source below.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. When the change is one of these kinds and nothing else, record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the kind, and stop: these have no runtime result to check. The kinds: an empty diff; whitespace, formatting or unchanged file moves; planning prose; a conceptual diagram and its explanatory README; README prose that explains architecture or tool usage without an operational procedure; scaffolding — a template \"Hello World\" view nothing references, a control still holding Xcode''s placeholder tokens, the deletion of template files the project does not reference. Scaffolding beside a material effect is never the main effect. Judge the patch, not the title; a documentation change that adds setup or run instructions IS material. Material effects: behaviour, appearance, navigation, persistence, configuration or project settings (build manifests, dependencies, ignore patterns, editor settings that run commands), tests, build scripts, and operational documentation (setup, run, deploy).\n\n## Sources\nRead all of these before judging; an element found anywhere here counts as author-supplied.\n1. The title and the WHOLE description (`context/description.md`): every section, checklist and step list, not only a \"Testing\" heading. A captured image that can be inspected may communicate a visual expected result; its caption or surrounding text can independently state one. An image reference or heading alone does not establish what the image shows. If the image is the only candidate evidence and required acquisition failed, report a collection gap; if it was acquired but cannot be interpreted, state the unresolved question.\n2. A linked issue the author adopted (`context/linked_work_items/<n>.md`): named with a closing keyword (Closes, Resolves, Fixes #N) in the author''s own prose, the title or branch was created from it, or the description says the change implements it. Its acceptance criteria are expected results; its steps and title are the author''s instructions. A \"#N\" inside a template''s example or comment, a number without \"#\", or an issue only found by number search is NOT adopted.\n3. The patch (`work/change/diff.patch`): an authored preview, story, runnable sample or executable test is an ENTRY for the view or unit it exercises. A preview is live: rendering it and using the view''s own controls (a toggle, a field) is an ACTION; it does not establish navigation from other screens, callbacks or persistence.\n4. The repository''s documented setup (README, CONTRIBUTING, a documented generate/build/run route in `<repositoryRoot>`): prerequisites and generic run/build steps; do not demand them repeated. For a project-configuration change (a renamed target, a build manifest, package additions) that route IS the check, and a project that generates and builds with the new configuration is its expected result.\n5. The author''s own words in the discussion (`context/general_comments.json`, `context/review_threads.json`) where they state a check or a prerequisite. A reviewer''s report of testing is NOT the author''s instructions.\nThe implementation can confirm reachability or contradict an instruction; it never supplies instructions by itself: a view''s own literal text is not a stated expected result.\n\n## Judge\nAfter the Occasion gate, assess the standard against all relevant evidence.\n- MET: the main material effect has an entry, an action and an expected result across the sources, and following them would let a reviewer see the effect. This holds when the description says \"N/A\", when the elements are split between a title, a preview, an issue and a screenshot, and when secondary effects have no check. Looks like a lapse but is not: \"put your own OpenAI key in Secrets.xcconfig\" — a declared prerequisite is part of a good check.\n- NOT_MET: followed as written, the check for the main effect cannot be performed as communicated or checks the wrong thing: it describes a different change than the patch makes (a button said to do nothing navigates), names a control or screen the code lacks, needs a secret, input or device no source says how to obtain (a key the code reads from an untracked file), or declares \"no UI change\" when the UI changes. An adopted issue''s criterion the change does not implement, or a slip the title and code correct (\"player\" for \"team\"), is not misleading while the rest of the check reaches the effect. Quote the instruction and the contradicting code or fact.\n- NOT_MET: no source supplies a usable check for the main material effect AND it needs running, interacting, configuring or observing to confirm. Name the missing element(s). A preview alone for an interactive change, with no stated or shown result, is absent.\n- MET also applies when the author explicitly removed or refused a harmful or plainly unnecessary procedure and the remaining verification is adequate. Removal alone and an empty heading do not establish conformance.\n- UNDETERMINED when successfully captured sources leave a deciding fact genuinely unresolved; state the question that would settle it. Failed acquisition of an adopted issue needed to settle the check is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED. Having read every source and found no usable check for a material effect is NOT_MET.\nOne observation per review; keep the same check in the summary, the evidence and the rationale.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; almost always MINOR: a bounded addition or correction fixes it — one missing element, one misleading detail, one undocumented prerequisite. MAJOR: a significant effect is unverifiable as communicated, or the instruction sends the reviewer to a materially wrong check. CRITICAL only for a communicated procedure that demonstrably risks production data loss or live-credential disclosure; never hypothetical.\n\n## Grounding\nCite the exact lines that supply each element: description or issue lines, the diff lines of a preview or test, the screenshot reference, README lines. For a claim based on absence, evidence.search records the boundary you searched and the citations show the diff lines of the effect that needs the check. Never claim a build, test or app run was executed. Judge the instructions, not correctness: a clear check that would expose a defect is still a useful check.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the description explains what and why (`describe-what-and-why`), whether tests exist (`ships-tests-with-the-change`), and whether the code is correct belong to other practices. Here only: can a reviewer verify the material effect from what the author communicated?","precomputeScript":"import {\n\tbranchIssueReferences,\n\tclosingReferences,\n\tissueNumberReferences,\n\twithoutHtmlComments,\n} from \"../lib/references.ts\";\n// Precompute FACTS for states-how-to-verify-the-change: what kind of change this is, and where\n// guidance-shaped text sits. The occasion gate is where a small model slips most (a diagram or a\n// self-introduction judged as lacking instructions), so the facts it needs for that gate\n// are stated up front. Nothing here is a verdict — the model reads the sources and judges.\nimport { readLinkedWorkItemCapture } from \"../lib/review.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nconst IMAGE = /\\.(?:png|jpe?g|gif|svg|webp|pdf)$/iu;\nconst DIAGRAM_PATH = /(?:^|\\/)(?:diagrams?|uml|models?|architecture)\\//iu;\nconst DIAGRAM_JSON = /\\.(?:json|drawio|puml|plantuml|mmd)$/iu;\nconst PROSE = /\\.(?:md|markdown|txt|rst|adoc)$/iu;\nconst PROJECT_CONFIG =\n\t/(?:^|\\/)(?:project\\.yml|project\\.yaml|package\\.json|Package\\.swift|Package\\.resolved|Podfile|Cartfile|build\\.gradle(?:\\.kts)?|pom\\.xml|pyproject\\.toml|requirements\\.txt|\\.gitignore|\\.editorconfig|\\.swiftlint\\.yml|Info\\.plist)$/iu;\nconst TEST_PATH =\n\t/(?:^|\\/)(?:tests?|specs?|__tests__)(?:\\/)|[._-](?:test|tests|spec|specs)\\.[a-z]+$|Tests?\\.[a-z0-9]+$|Spec\\.[a-z0-9]+$/iu;\nconst CODE = /\\.(?:swift|ts|tsx|js|jsx|py|java|kt|go|rb|cs|cpp|cc|cxx|c|m|mm|h|hpp|vue|dart|rs)$/iu;\nconst PREVIEW = /^\\+.*(?:#Preview\\b|PreviewProvider\\b|\\.stories\\.[jt]sx?|storiesOf\\()/u;\n// A CommonMark heading may be indented by up to three spaces.\nconst TESTING_HEADING =\n\t/^ {0,3}#+\\s*(?:testing|test(?:ing)? instructions|how to test|verification|steps to (?:test|verify|reproduce))/iu;\nconst PLACEHOLDER_TESTING_TEXT = /^(?:n\\/?a|none|not applicable|-|—)\\.?$/iu;\nconst SCREENSHOT = /!\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\([^)]*\\)/gu;\n// Xcode''s library inserts these around a control''s defaults until the author replaces them.\nconst PLACEHOLDER_TOKEN = /\\/\\*@(?:START_MENU_TOKEN|PLACEHOLDER=)/u;\n// A key or secret the code reads at runtime: a secret-shaped name beside a runtime lookup.\nconst SECRET_NAME =\n\t/api[_-]?key|apikey|client[_-]?secret|\\bsecrets?\\b|secret[A-Z_]|access[_-]?token/iu;\nconst RUNTIME_LOOKUP =\n\t/Bundle\\.main|ProcessInfo|\\benvironment\\[|\\.plist|xcconfig|Keychain|SecItem|UserDefaults|infoDictionary|process\\.env|System\\.getenv|os\\.environ/u;\n\n/** The section under a testing heading, stripped of template comments; empty when it holds nothing. */\nfunction testingSection(body: string): { heading: string; content: string } | null {\n\tconst lines = body.split(/\\r?\\n/u);\n\tfor (let index = 0; index < lines.length; index += 1) {\n\t\tconst line = lines[index] ?? \"\";\n\t\tif (!TESTING_HEADING.test(line)) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst level = (/#+/u.exec(line)?.[0] ?? \"#\").length;\n\t\tconst content: string[] = [];\n\t\tfor (let next = index + 1; next < lines.length; next += 1) {\n\t\t\tconst candidate = lines[next] ?? \"\";\n\t\t\tconst nextLevel = /^ {0,3}(?<hashes>#+)\\s/u.exec(candidate)?.groups?.hashes?.length;\n\t\t\tif (nextLevel !== undefined && nextLevel <= level) {\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontent.push(candidate);\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst text = withoutHtmlComments(content.join(\"\\n\")).trim();\n\t\treturn { heading: line.trim(), content: text };\n\t}\n\treturn null;\n}\n\n/** `path:line, line` for each file with a matching added line. */\nfunction addedLinesMatching(\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tmatches: (path: string, line: string) => boolean,\n): string[] {\n\treturn [...diffFiles.values()].flatMap((file) => {\n\t\tconst lines = [...file.addedLines]\n\t\t\t.filter(([, line]) => matches(file.path, line))\n\t\t\t.map(([number]) => String(number));\n\t\treturn lines.length === 0 ? [] : [`${file.path}:${lines.join(\", \")}`];\n\t});\n}\n\nexport default async function statesHowToVerifyTheChange(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tmetadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir?: string,\n) {\n\tconst paths = [...diffFiles.keys()];\n\t// A move, a rename or a binary file changes no line; an image among them is still content.\n\tconst unchanged = [...diffFiles.values()]\n\t\t.filter(\n\t\t\t(file) => file.hunks.length === 0 && file.addedLines.size + file.removedLines.size === 0,\n\t\t)\n\t\t.map((file) => file.path)\n\t\t.filter((path) => !IMAGE.test(path));\n\tconst content = paths.filter((path) => !unchanged.includes(path));\n\tconst kinds = {\n\t\timages: content.filter((path) => IMAGE.test(path)).length,\n\t\tdiagramFiles: content.filter(\n\t\t\t(path) =>\n\t\t\t\tDIAGRAM_PATH.test(path) ||\n\t\t\t\t(DIAGRAM_JSON.test(path) && /diagram|aom|uml|model/iu.test(path)),\n\t\t).length,\n\t\tprose: content.filter((path) => PROSE.test(path)).length,\n\t\tprojectConfig: content.filter((path) => PROJECT_CONFIG.test(path)).length,\n\t\ttests: content.filter((path) => TEST_PATH.test(path)).length,\n\t\tcode: content.filter((path) => CODE.test(path) && !TEST_PATH.test(path)).length,\n\t};\n\tconst addedLines = [...diffFiles.values()].reduce((sum, file) => sum + file.addedLines.size, 0);\n\tconst removedLines = [...diffFiles.values()].reduce(\n\t\t(sum, file) => sum + file.removedLines.size,\n\t\t0,\n\t);\n\tconst previews = [...diffFiles.values()].filter((file) =>\n\t\t[...file.addedLines.values()].some((line) => PREVIEW.test(`+${line}`)),\n\t);\n\tconst placeholders = addedLinesMatching(diffFiles, (_path, line) => PLACEHOLDER_TOKEN.test(line));\n\tconst secretReads = addedLinesMatching(\n\t\tdiffFiles,\n\t\t(path, line) =>\n\t\t\t!path.endsWith(\".gitignore\") && SECRET_NAME.test(line) && RUNTIME_LOOKUP.test(line),\n\t);\n\tconst ignoredSecrets = [...diffFiles.values()]\n\t\t.filter((file) => file.path.endsWith(\".gitignore\"))\n\t\t.flatMap((file) => [...file.addedLines.values()])\n\t\t.map((line) => line.trim())\n\t\t.filter((line) => SECRET_NAME.test(line) || /\\.xcconfig$|\\.env\\b/iu.test(line));\n\n\tconst body = typeof metadata.body === \"string\" ? metadata.body : \"\";\n\tconst testing = testingSection(body);\n\tconst screenshots = body.match(SCREENSHOT)?.length ?? 0;\n\t// Only the author''s own words adopt an issue: a closing keyword in their prose, the title, the\n\t// branch, or the provider''s closing link. A template''s example in code or a comment names nothing.\n\tconst capture = await readLinkedWorkItemCapture(contextDir);\n\tconst captured = capture?.items.map((item) => item.number) ?? [];\n\tconst named = new Map<number, string[]>();\n\tconst name = (how: string, numbers: number[]) => {\n\t\tfor (const n of numbers) {\n\t\t\tnamed.set(n, [...(named.get(n) ?? []), how]);\n\t\t}\n\t};\n\tname(\n\t\t\"provider closing link\",\n\t\t(capture?.items ?? [])\n\t\t\t.filter((item) => item.how === \"closesOnMerge\")\n\t\t\t.map((item) => item.number),\n\t);\n\tname(\"closing keyword in the description\", closingReferences(body));\n\tname(\"title\", issueNumberReferences(metadata.title ?? \"\"));\n\tname(\"branch\", branchIssueReferences(metadata.source_branch));\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (paths.length === 0 || addedLines + removedLines === 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\"The pinned range changes no lines: an empty diff is one of the kinds the criteria''s Occasion section names; cite it through metadata.json changed_files or work/change/files.json.\",\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (kinds.code === 0 && kinds.tests === 0 && kinds.projectConfig === 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`No code, test or project-configuration file has line changes; the change is ${kinds.images} image(s), ${kinds.diagramFiles} diagram file(s) and ${kinds.prose} prose file(s). Read the prose hunks: if they add setup or run instructions the change is material; if they add an introduction, a glossary, user stories or a diagram''s embedding, it is one of the kinds the criteria''s Occasion section names.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Files with line changes: ${kinds.code} code, ${kinds.tests} test, ${kinds.projectConfig} project-configuration, ${kinds.prose} prose, ${kinds.images} image.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (unchanged.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`${unchanged.length} file(s) change no line — moves, renames or binary files: ${unchanged.slice(0, 5).join(\", \")}${unchanged.length > 5 ? \", …\" : \"\"}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\t// A bare testing section reads as \"nothing anywhere\" unless the direction says what it settles.\n\tconst elsewhere =\n\t\t\" That settles nothing either way: the entry, action and expected result can be in other sections, an adopted issue, a preview, the documented setup or the author''s discussion.\";\n\tif (testing === null) {\n\t\tdirections.push(`The description has no testing heading.${elsewhere}`);\n\t} else if (testing.content.length === 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The description''s testing heading (\"${testing.heading}\") has no content beneath it once template comments are removed.${elsewhere}`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (PLACEHOLDER_TESTING_TEXT.test(testing.content)) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The description''s testing section (\"${testing.heading}\") holds only \"${testing.content}\".${elsewhere}`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The description''s testing section (\"${testing.heading}\") holds ${testing.content.split(/\\r?\\n/u).filter((line) => line.trim()).length} line(s) of author text.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (named.size > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Issue(s) the author names: ${[...named].map(([n, how]) => `#${n} (${how.join(\", \")})`).join(\"; \")}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tconst unnamed = captured.filter((n) => !named.has(n));\n\tif (unnamed.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Captured as linked_work_items/<n>.md but not named by a closing keyword, the title, the branch or a provider closing link: ${unnamed.map((n) => `#${n}`).join(\", \")}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tconst uncaptured = [...named.keys()].filter((n) => !captured.includes(n));\n\tif (capture !== null && uncaptured.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Named by the author but not captured: ${uncaptured.map((n) => `#${n}`).join(\", \")}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (previews.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The patch adds an authored preview or story in: ${previews.map((file) => file.path).join(\", \")}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (placeholders.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(`Added lines keep Xcode placeholder tokens: ${placeholders.join(\"; \")}.`);\n\t}\n\tif (secretReads.length > 0 || ignoredSecrets.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Added lines read a key or secret at runtime: ${secretReads.join(\"; \") || \"none\"}.${ignoredSecrets.length > 0 ? ` The ignore file gains: ${ignoredSecrets.join(\", \")}.` : \"\"}`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (screenshots > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The description embeds ${screenshots} image(s); read their captions and the sentences around them — the image bytes are not available to you.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: [],\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tchangedFiles: paths.length,\n\t\t\tfilesWithoutLineChanges: unchanged.length,\n\t\t\taddedLines,\n\t\t\tremovedLines,\n\t\t\timageFiles: kinds.images,\n\t\t\tdiagramFiles: kinds.diagramFiles,\n\t\t\tproseFiles: kinds.prose,\n\t\t\tprojectConfigFiles: kinds.projectConfig,\n\t\t\ttestFiles: kinds.tests,\n\t\t\tcodeFiles: kinds.code,\n\t\t\tpreviewFiles: previews.length,\n\t\t\tscreenshotsInDescription: screenshots,\n\t\t\ttestingSectionLines: testing\n\t\t\t\t? testing.content.split(/\\r?\\n/u).filter((line) => line.trim()).length\n\t\t\t\t: 0,\n\t\t\tlinkedIssues: captured.length,\n\t\t\tnamedIssues: named.size,\n\t\t\tsecretReadLines: secretReads.length,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A reviewer who knows where to start, what to do and what to expect can check the change in minutes and trusts what they see. Without that, they either guess at a procedure, skip the check, or ask — and the change waits.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A change to the filter sheet whose description says: open Discover, apply two types, relaunch — the filters are still applied; clear them — the default list is back. The steps name the screen, the action and what success looks like, and the linked issue''s acceptance criteria say the same.","groupSlug":"review-ready-work","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 212\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Check left out</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"150.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"44\" y=\"51\" width=\"160\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"80\" width=\"232\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"92\" width=\"168\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"15\">Fixed the login flow</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Change waits</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Check spelled out</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"150.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"380\" y=\"51\" width=\"160\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"80\" width=\"232\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"92\" width=\"168\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"15\">Open login</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"143\" font-size=\"15\">Enter wrong password</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"15\">Expect error message</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Checked in minutes</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A change that says only “Fixed the login flow” leaves the check out, so the change waits. A change that lists “Open login”, “Enter wrong password” and “Expect error message” is checked in minutes."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Write the check for the main effect of your change, the one that your title or issue names.\n- Say where to start, such as a screen, a command, a named test or a preview that you wrote. You do not need to repeat the setup steps that your README gives.\n- Say what to do there, such as run, select, enter or restart.\n- Say what the reviewer will see when it works. For example, instead of “Fixed the login flow”, write “Sign in with a wrong password. The form says that the password is wrong”.\n- If the check needs a key, a device or test data, say how to get it.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The change is empty.\n- The change only fixes whitespace or formatting, or moves files without changing them.\n- The change only adds planning notes.\n- The change only adds a diagram and the README text that explains it.\n- The change only explains the design or a tool in the README, with no setup, run or deploy steps.\n- The change only adds or removes starter code, such as an unused “Hello World” view or Xcode’s placeholder text.\n\nIt still applies when the change adds setup, run or deploy steps, or changes settings, dependencies, tests or build scripts.\nIt also applies when starter code sits beside a real change.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that you need a long, numbered test plan. One sentence that names where to look and what is there now can be enough. An example is “The settings screen now shows a dark mode switch”.\n- Writing “Testing: N/A” or leaving the heading empty. That tells the reviewer nothing. Instead, write the check, or name the issue whose acceptance criteria give it, such as with “Closes #12”.\n- Listing the parts that you added, such as “a service, a model and a view”. Instead, say what the reviewer will now see.\n- Leaving a check that names a button or a screen that the change does not have. Instead, update the check to match the button or screen that the change has.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Merge request workflow](https://docs.gitlab.com/development/contributing/merge_request_workflow/), GitLab. Its definition of done asks for the steps or setup that reviewers need to see the change.\n- [Differential user guide: test plans](https://we.phorge.it/book/phorge/article/differential_test_plans/), Phorge, the maintained fork of the Phabricator code review tool. A test plan lists repeatable steps, with enough detail that someone new to the change can check how it behaves.\n- [Commit message style guide](https://fuchsia.dev/fuchsia-src/contribute/commit-message-style-guide), Fuchsia project at Google. A `Test:` line tells reviewers which tests ran, and complex test steps go in a linked issue.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"State how to verify the change","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.linked-work-items","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: for the material effect of this change, a reviewer who knows the repository''s documented setup can find where to start, what to do and what to expect — from what the author supplied or explicitly adopted.\n\n## The standard\nJudge ONE check, for the main material effect in the patch: the one the title, description or adopted issue says the change is for, else the bulk of it. It is communicated when three elements are available, each from any permitted source: an ENTRY (a command, a screen, a named test, an authored preview, a documented setup step), an ACTION (run, tap, enter, configure, restart, or — for an appearance-only change — render and inspect) and an EXPECTED RESULT (a stated requirement, an assertion, a visible state, a persisted value). A sentence that names where to look and what is now there — \"the settings screen now shows a dark-mode switch\", \"the chart now shows a weekly total\", \"verify the list updates\" — is an entry and an expected result; do not demand a literal value or a numbered step list on top of it. A list of the types added (\"a service, a DTO and a view model\") is neither, and so is \"fixed the login flow\" without the failure it removes or what now happens. The lapse is a material effect with nothing anywhere saying what success looks like. \"Testing: N/A\" or an empty heading is evidence of nothing; read every source below.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. When the change is one of these kinds and nothing else, record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the kind, and stop: these have no runtime result to check. The kinds: an empty diff; whitespace, formatting or unchanged file moves; planning prose; a conceptual diagram and its explanatory README; README prose that explains architecture or tool usage without an operational procedure; scaffolding — a template \"Hello World\" view nothing references, a control still holding Xcode''s placeholder tokens, the deletion of template files the project does not reference. Scaffolding beside a material effect is never the main effect. Judge the patch, not the title; a documentation change that adds setup or run instructions IS material. Material effects: behaviour, appearance, navigation, persistence, configuration or project settings (build manifests, dependencies, ignore patterns, editor settings that run commands), tests, build scripts, and operational documentation (setup, run, deploy).\n\n## Sources\nRead all of these before judging; an element found anywhere here counts as author-supplied.\n1. The title and the WHOLE description (`context/description.md`): every section, checklist and step list, not only a \"Testing\" heading. A captured image that can be inspected may communicate a visual expected result; its caption or surrounding text can independently state one. An image reference or heading alone does not establish what the image shows. If the image is the only candidate evidence and required acquisition failed, report a collection gap; if it was acquired but cannot be interpreted, state the unresolved question.\n2. A linked issue the author adopted (`context/linked_work_items/<n>.md`): named with a closing keyword (Closes, Resolves, Fixes #N) in the author''s own prose, the title or branch was created from it, or the description says the change implements it. Its acceptance criteria are expected results; its steps and title are the author''s instructions. A \"#N\" inside a template''s example or comment, a number without \"#\", or an issue only found by number search is NOT adopted.\n3. The patch (`work/change/diff.patch`): an authored preview, story, runnable sample or executable test is an ENTRY for the view or unit it exercises. A preview is live: rendering it and using the view''s own controls (a toggle, a field) is an ACTION; it does not establish navigation from other screens, callbacks or persistence.\n4. The repository''s documented setup (README, CONTRIBUTING, a documented generate/build/run route in `<repositoryRoot>`): prerequisites and generic run/build steps; do not demand them repeated. For a project-configuration change (a renamed target, a build manifest, package additions) that route IS the check, and a project that generates and builds with the new configuration is its expected result.\n5. The author''s own words in the discussion (`context/general_comments.json`, `context/review_threads.json`) where they state a check or a prerequisite. A reviewer''s report of testing is NOT the author''s instructions.\nThe implementation can confirm reachability or contradict an instruction; it never supplies instructions by itself: a view''s own literal text is not a stated expected result.\n\n## Judge\nAfter the Occasion gate, assess the standard against all relevant evidence.\n- MET: the main material effect has an entry, an action and an expected result across the sources, and following them would let a reviewer see the effect. This holds when the description says \"N/A\", when the elements are split between a title, a preview, an issue and a screenshot, and when secondary effects have no check. Looks like a lapse but is not: \"put your own OpenAI key in Secrets.xcconfig\" — a declared prerequisite is part of a good check.\n- NOT_MET: followed as written, the check for the main effect cannot be performed as communicated or checks the wrong thing: it describes a different change than the patch makes (a button said to do nothing navigates), names a control or screen the code lacks, needs a secret, input or device no source says how to obtain (a key the code reads from an untracked file), or declares \"no UI change\" when the UI changes. An adopted issue''s criterion the change does not implement, or a slip the title and code correct (\"player\" for \"team\"), is not misleading while the rest of the check reaches the effect. Quote the instruction and the contradicting code or fact.\n- NOT_MET: no source supplies a usable check for the main material effect AND it needs running, interacting, configuring or observing to confirm. Name the missing element(s). A preview alone for an interactive change, with no stated or shown result, is absent.\n- MET also applies when the author explicitly removed or refused a harmful or plainly unnecessary procedure and the remaining verification is adequate. Removal alone and an empty heading do not establish conformance.\n- UNDETERMINED when successfully captured sources leave a deciding fact genuinely unresolved; state the question that would settle it. Failed acquisition of an adopted issue needed to settle the check is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED. Having read every source and found no usable check for a material effect is NOT_MET.\nOne observation per review; keep the same check in the summary, the evidence and the rationale.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; almost always MINOR: a bounded addition or correction fixes it — one missing element, one misleading detail, one undocumented prerequisite. MAJOR: a significant effect is unverifiable as communicated, or the instruction sends the reviewer to a materially wrong check. CRITICAL only for a communicated procedure that demonstrably risks production data loss or live-credential disclosure; never hypothetical.\n\n## Grounding\nCite the exact lines that supply each element: description or issue lines, the diff lines of a preview or test, the screenshot reference, README lines. For a claim based on absence, evidence.search records the boundary you searched and the citations show the diff lines of the effect that needs the check. Never claim a build, test or app run was executed. Judge the instructions, not correctness: a clear check that would expose a defect is still a useful check.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the description explains what and why (`describe-what-and-why`), whether tests exist (`ships-tests-with-the-change`), and whether the code is correct belong to other practices. Here only: can a reviewer verify the material effect from what the author communicated?","precomputeScript":"import {\n\tbranchIssueReferences,\n\tclosingReferences,\n\tissueNumberReferences,\n\twithoutHtmlComments,\n} from \"../lib/references.ts\";\n// Precompute FACTS for states-how-to-verify-the-change: what kind of change this is, and where\n// guidance-shaped text sits. The occasion gate is where a small model slips most (a diagram or a\n// self-introduction judged as lacking instructions), so the facts it needs for that gate\n// are stated up front. Nothing here is a verdict — the model reads the sources and judges.\nimport { readLinkedWorkItemCapture } from \"../lib/review.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nconst IMAGE = /\\.(?:png|jpe?g|gif|svg|webp|pdf)$/iu;\nconst DIAGRAM_PATH = /(?:^|\\/)(?:diagrams?|uml|models?|architecture)\\//iu;\nconst DIAGRAM_JSON = /\\.(?:json|drawio|puml|plantuml|mmd)$/iu;\nconst PROSE = /\\.(?:md|markdown|txt|rst|adoc)$/iu;\nconst PROJECT_CONFIG =\n\t/(?:^|\\/)(?:project\\.yml|project\\.yaml|package\\.json|Package\\.swift|Package\\.resolved|Podfile|Cartfile|build\\.gradle(?:\\.kts)?|pom\\.xml|pyproject\\.toml|requirements\\.txt|\\.gitignore|\\.editorconfig|\\.swiftlint\\.yml|Info\\.plist)$/iu;\nconst TEST_PATH =\n\t/(?:^|\\/)(?:tests?|specs?|__tests__)(?:\\/)|[._-](?:test|tests|spec|specs)\\.[a-z]+$|Tests?\\.[a-z0-9]+$|Spec\\.[a-z0-9]+$/iu;\nconst CODE = /\\.(?:swift|ts|tsx|js|jsx|py|java|kt|go|rb|cs|cpp|cc|cxx|c|m|mm|h|hpp|vue|dart|rs)$/iu;\nconst PREVIEW = /^\\+.*(?:#Preview\\b|PreviewProvider\\b|\\.stories\\.[jt]sx?|storiesOf\\()/u;\n// A CommonMark heading may be indented by up to three spaces.\nconst TESTING_HEADING =\n\t/^ {0,3}#+\\s*(?:testing|test(?:ing)? instructions|how to test|verification|steps to (?:test|verify|reproduce))/iu;\nconst PLACEHOLDER_TESTING_TEXT = /^(?:n\\/?a|none|not applicable|-|—)\\.?$/iu;\nconst SCREENSHOT = /!\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\([^)]*\\)/gu;\n// Xcode''s library inserts these around a control''s defaults until the author replaces them.\nconst PLACEHOLDER_TOKEN = /\\/\\*@(?:START_MENU_TOKEN|PLACEHOLDER=)/u;\n// A key or secret the code reads at runtime: a secret-shaped name beside a runtime lookup.\nconst SECRET_NAME =\n\t/api[_-]?key|apikey|client[_-]?secret|\\bsecrets?\\b|secret[A-Z_]|access[_-]?token/iu;\nconst RUNTIME_LOOKUP =\n\t/Bundle\\.main|ProcessInfo|\\benvironment\\[|\\.plist|xcconfig|Keychain|SecItem|UserDefaults|infoDictionary|process\\.env|System\\.getenv|os\\.environ/u;\n\n/** The section under a testing heading, stripped of template comments; empty when it holds nothing. */\nfunction testingSection(body: string): { heading: string; content: string } | null {\n\tconst lines = body.split(/\\r?\\n/u);\n\tfor (let index = 0; index < lines.length; index += 1) {\n\t\tconst line = lines[index] ?? \"\";\n\t\tif (!TESTING_HEADING.test(line)) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst level = (/#+/u.exec(line)?.[0] ?? \"#\").length;\n\t\tconst content: string[] = [];\n\t\tfor (let next = index + 1; next < lines.length; next += 1) {\n\t\t\tconst candidate = lines[next] ?? \"\";\n\t\t\tconst nextLevel = /^ {0,3}(?<hashes>#+)\\s/u.exec(candidate)?.groups?.hashes?.length;\n\t\t\tif (nextLevel !== undefined && nextLevel <= level) {\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontent.push(candidate);\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst text = withoutHtmlComments(content.join(\"\\n\")).trim();\n\t\treturn { heading: line.trim(), content: text };\n\t}\n\treturn null;\n}\n\n/** `path:line, line` for each file with a matching added line. */\nfunction addedLinesMatching(\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tmatches: (path: string, line: string) => boolean,\n): string[] {\n\treturn [...diffFiles.values()].flatMap((file) => {\n\t\tconst lines = [...file.addedLines]\n\t\t\t.filter(([, line]) => matches(file.path, line))\n\t\t\t.map(([number]) => String(number));\n\t\treturn lines.length === 0 ? [] : [`${file.path}:${lines.join(\", \")}`];\n\t});\n}\n\nexport default async function statesHowToVerifyTheChange(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tmetadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir?: string,\n) {\n\tconst paths = [...diffFiles.keys()];\n\t// A move, a rename or a binary file changes no line; an image among them is still content.\n\tconst unchanged = [...diffFiles.values()]\n\t\t.filter(\n\t\t\t(file) => file.hunks.length === 0 && file.addedLines.size + file.removedLines.size === 0,\n\t\t)\n\t\t.map((file) => file.path)\n\t\t.filter((path) => !IMAGE.test(path));\n\tconst content = paths.filter((path) => !unchanged.includes(path));\n\tconst kinds = {\n\t\timages: content.filter((path) => IMAGE.test(path)).length,\n\t\tdiagramFiles: content.filter(\n\t\t\t(path) =>\n\t\t\t\tDIAGRAM_PATH.test(path) ||\n\t\t\t\t(DIAGRAM_JSON.test(path) && /diagram|aom|uml|model/iu.test(path)),\n\t\t).length,\n\t\tprose: content.filter((path) => PROSE.test(path)).length,\n\t\tprojectConfig: content.filter((path) => PROJECT_CONFIG.test(path)).length,\n\t\ttests: content.filter((path) => TEST_PATH.test(path)).length,\n\t\tcode: content.filter((path) => CODE.test(path) && !TEST_PATH.test(path)).length,\n\t};\n\tconst addedLines = [...diffFiles.values()].reduce((sum, file) => sum + file.addedLines.size, 0);\n\tconst removedLines = [...diffFiles.values()].reduce(\n\t\t(sum, file) => sum + file.removedLines.size,\n\t\t0,\n\t);\n\tconst previews = [...diffFiles.values()].filter((file) =>\n\t\t[...file.addedLines.values()].some((line) => PREVIEW.test(`+${line}`)),\n\t);\n\tconst placeholders = addedLinesMatching(diffFiles, (_path, line) => PLACEHOLDER_TOKEN.test(line));\n\tconst secretReads = addedLinesMatching(\n\t\tdiffFiles,\n\t\t(path, line) =>\n\t\t\t!path.endsWith(\".gitignore\") && SECRET_NAME.test(line) && RUNTIME_LOOKUP.test(line),\n\t);\n\tconst ignoredSecrets = [...diffFiles.values()]\n\t\t.filter((file) => file.path.endsWith(\".gitignore\"))\n\t\t.flatMap((file) => [...file.addedLines.values()])\n\t\t.map((line) => line.trim())\n\t\t.filter((line) => SECRET_NAME.test(line) || /\\.xcconfig$|\\.env\\b/iu.test(line));\n\n\tconst body = typeof metadata.body === \"string\" ? metadata.body : \"\";\n\tconst testing = testingSection(body);\n\tconst screenshots = body.match(SCREENSHOT)?.length ?? 0;\n\t// Only the author''s own words adopt an issue: a closing keyword in their prose, the title, the\n\t// branch, or the provider''s closing link. A template''s example in code or a comment names nothing.\n\tconst capture = await readLinkedWorkItemCapture(contextDir);\n\tconst captured = capture?.items.map((item) => item.number) ?? [];\n\tconst named = new Map<number, string[]>();\n\tconst name = (how: string, numbers: number[]) => {\n\t\tfor (const n of numbers) {\n\t\t\tnamed.set(n, [...(named.get(n) ?? []), how]);\n\t\t}\n\t};\n\tname(\n\t\t\"provider closing link\",\n\t\t(capture?.items ?? [])\n\t\t\t.filter((item) => item.how === \"closesOnMerge\")\n\t\t\t.map((item) => item.number),\n\t);\n\tname(\"closing keyword in the description\", closingReferences(body));\n\tname(\"title\", issueNumberReferences(metadata.title ?? \"\"));\n\tname(\"branch\", branchIssueReferences(metadata.source_branch));\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (paths.length === 0 || addedLines + removedLines === 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\"The pinned range changes no lines: an empty diff is one of the kinds the criteria''s Occasion section names; cite it through metadata.json changed_files or work/change/files.json.\",\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (kinds.code === 0 && kinds.tests === 0 && kinds.projectConfig === 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`No code, test or project-configuration file has line changes; the change is ${kinds.images} image(s), ${kinds.diagramFiles} diagram file(s) and ${kinds.prose} prose file(s). Read the prose hunks: if they add setup or run instructions the change is material; if they add an introduction, a glossary, user stories or a diagram''s embedding, it is one of the kinds the criteria''s Occasion section names.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Files with line changes: ${kinds.code} code, ${kinds.tests} test, ${kinds.projectConfig} project-configuration, ${kinds.prose} prose, ${kinds.images} image.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (unchanged.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`${unchanged.length} file(s) change no line — moves, renames or binary files: ${unchanged.slice(0, 5).join(\", \")}${unchanged.length > 5 ? \", …\" : \"\"}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\t// A bare testing section reads as \"nothing anywhere\" unless the direction says what it settles.\n\tconst elsewhere =\n\t\t\" That settles nothing either way: the entry, action and expected result can be in other sections, an adopted issue, a preview, the documented setup or the author''s discussion.\";\n\tif (testing === null) {\n\t\tdirections.push(`The description has no testing heading.${elsewhere}`);\n\t} else if (testing.content.length === 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The description''s testing heading (\"${testing.heading}\") has no content beneath it once template comments are removed.${elsewhere}`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (PLACEHOLDER_TESTING_TEXT.test(testing.content)) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The description''s testing section (\"${testing.heading}\") holds only \"${testing.content}\".${elsewhere}`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The description''s testing section (\"${testing.heading}\") holds ${testing.content.split(/\\r?\\n/u).filter((line) => line.trim()).length} line(s) of author text.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (named.size > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Issue(s) the author names: ${[...named].map(([n, how]) => `#${n} (${how.join(\", \")})`).join(\"; \")}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tconst unnamed = captured.filter((n) => !named.has(n));\n\tif (unnamed.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Captured as linked_work_items/<n>.md but not named by a closing keyword, the title, the branch or a provider closing link: ${unnamed.map((n) => `#${n}`).join(\", \")}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tconst uncaptured = [...named.keys()].filter((n) => !captured.includes(n));\n\tif (capture !== null && uncaptured.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Named by the author but not captured: ${uncaptured.map((n) => `#${n}`).join(\", \")}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (previews.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The patch adds an authored preview or story in: ${previews.map((file) => file.path).join(\", \")}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (placeholders.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(`Added lines keep Xcode placeholder tokens: ${placeholders.join(\"; \")}.`);\n\t}\n\tif (secretReads.length > 0 || ignoredSecrets.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Added lines read a key or secret at runtime: ${secretReads.join(\"; \") || \"none\"}.${ignoredSecrets.length > 0 ? ` The ignore file gains: ${ignoredSecrets.join(\", \")}.` : \"\"}`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (screenshots > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The description embeds ${screenshots} image(s); read their captions and the sentences around them — the image bytes are not available to you.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: [],\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tchangedFiles: paths.length,\n\t\t\tfilesWithoutLineChanges: unchanged.length,\n\t\t\taddedLines,\n\t\t\tremovedLines,\n\t\t\timageFiles: kinds.images,\n\t\t\tdiagramFiles: kinds.diagramFiles,\n\t\t\tproseFiles: kinds.prose,\n\t\t\tprojectConfigFiles: kinds.projectConfig,\n\t\t\ttestFiles: kinds.tests,\n\t\t\tcodeFiles: kinds.code,\n\t\t\tpreviewFiles: previews.length,\n\t\t\tscreenshotsInDescription: screenshots,\n\t\t\ttestingSectionLines: testing\n\t\t\t\t? testing.content.split(/\\r?\\n/u).filter((line) => line.trim()).length\n\t\t\t\t: 0,\n\t\t\tlinkedIssues: captured.length,\n\t\t\tnamedIssues: named.size,\n\t\t\tsecretReadLines: secretReads.length,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A reviewer who knows where to start, what to do and what to expect can check the change in minutes and trusts what they see. Without that, they either guess at a procedure, skip the check, or ask — and the change waits.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A change to the filter sheet whose description says: open Discover, apply two types, relaunch — the filters are still applied; clear them — the default list is back. The steps name the screen, the action and what success looks like, and the linked issue''s acceptance criteria say the same.","groupSlug":"review-ready-work","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 212\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Check left out</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"150.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"44\" y=\"51\" width=\"160\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"80\" width=\"232\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"92\" width=\"168\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"15\">Fixed the login flow</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Change waits</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Check spelled out</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"150.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"380\" y=\"51\" width=\"160\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"80\" width=\"232\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"92\" width=\"168\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"15\">Open login</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"143\" font-size=\"15\">Enter wrong password</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"15\">Expect error message</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Checked in minutes</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A change that says only “Fixed the login flow” leaves the check out, so the change waits. A change that lists “Open login”, “Enter wrong password” and “Expect error message” is checked in minutes."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Write the check for the main effect of your change, the one that your title or issue names.\n- Say where to start, such as a screen, a command, a named test or a preview that you wrote. You do not need to repeat the setup steps that your README gives.\n- Say what to do there, such as run, select, enter or restart.\n- Say what the reviewer will see when it works. For example, instead of “Fixed the login flow”, write “Sign in with a wrong password. The form says that the password is wrong”.\n- If the check needs a key, a device or test data, say how to get it.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The change is empty.\n- The change only fixes whitespace or formatting, or moves files without changing them.\n- The change only adds planning notes.\n- The change only adds a diagram and the README text that explains it.\n- The change only explains the design or a tool in the README, with no setup, run or deploy steps.\n- The change only adds or removes starter code, such as an unused “Hello World” view or Xcode’s placeholder text.\n\nIt still applies when the change adds setup, run or deploy steps, or changes settings, dependencies, tests or build scripts.\nIt also applies when starter code sits beside a real change.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that you need a long, numbered test plan. One sentence that names where to look and what is there now can be enough. An example is “The settings screen now shows a dark mode switch”.\n- Writing “Testing: N/A” or leaving the heading empty. That tells the reviewer nothing. Instead, write the check, or name the issue whose acceptance criteria give it, such as with “Closes #12”.\n- Listing the parts that you added, such as “a service, a model and a view”. Instead, say what the reviewer will now see.\n- Leaving a check that names a button or a screen that the change does not have. Instead, update the check to match the button or screen that the change has.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Merge request workflow](https://docs.gitlab.com/development/contributing/merge_request_workflow/), GitLab. Its definition of done asks for the steps or setup that reviewers need to see the change.\n- [Differential user guide: test plans](https://we.phorge.it/book/phorge/article/differential_test_plans/), Phorge, the maintained fork of the Phabricator code review tool. A test plan lists repeatable steps, with enough detail that someone new to the change can check how it behaves.\n- [Commit message style guide](https://fuchsia.dev/fuchsia-src/contribute/commit-message-style-guide), Fuchsia project at Google. A `Test:` line tells reviewers which tests ran, and complex test steps go in a linked issue.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Every change says how to check that it works"'::jsonb),'v5:81464c63094d661f1e4e086d8347fb34ef282ec25c24d70ac4947b9348dec7b6','practice:v3:21eb1dfb50b8e853922d387ab760673f66278ac5b24d7b402c1bc37b30afb4ba');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','ready-and-traceable-handoff','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Make the change’s status clear and link its issue","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"REQUIRED"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: how the change is handed off — presented as genuinely ready for others'' time, and traceable back to the issue it answers.\n\n## The standard\nJudge two things about the handoff, combined into one observation. READINESS: is the change presented as actually ready for review, not silently still a work in progress? TRACEABILITY: can a reader follow it back to the motivating issue? Good: an out-of-draft pull request with no authored unfinished-work or review-hold signal and a quotable closing reference — \"Closes #482\", or an issue id embedded in the branch slug. Bad: a mixed-signal handoff — a title that declares the work unfinished or asks reviewers to hold off while the pull request is openly non-draft — or a substantive feature or fix change with no traceable reference anywhere a reader would look. Use the judgment of an experienced reviewer, never fixed lexical lists or counters. Title text is a readiness signal only when it says the work is unfinished or that review should wait; a word that names something the product handles or the feature being built is not. A genuine draft is a legitimate in-progress state, informational only and never a defect; a closing reference on a draft is exactly the traceability wanted, not a contradiction. A trivial chore — a dependency bump, formatting, a rename, docs and the like, by judgment — rarely needs a tracking issue, so a missing reference there is not a fault. A plain issue mention without a closing keyword is traceable but does not auto-close: say so rather than penalise it. Readiness is read from what the author says, not from the form, against the slice the handoff says it delivers: ready for review means the author asks for review now, not that review, checks or approval already happened. An authored statement that implementation within that slice is unfinished — \"still need to add the error state\", \"not done yet\", an open to-do item the author wrote — or that reviewers should wait before reviewing, is a mixed signal on a non-draft pull request. A checklist the form supplies, left as supplied, is boilerplate: it states nothing about the work, ticked or not. When the author adopted a checklist — wrote it, edited its items or ticked some — read its open items, together with any prose that accounts for them, as the author''s account of who owes each item and when: an open item whose substance is the author''s unfinished work within the slice, and that nothing the author wrote settles, says the work is not finished. Verification the author asks reviewers to do as part of the review, a condition before merge such as passing checks or an approval, and follow-on work the author places outside this slice do not by themselves say the slice is unready; \"do not merge until approved\" states a merge condition, not a hold on review, unless the surrounding prose says review should wait. Never infer that something was or was not built, tested or run from a tick, a missing tick or a missing file.\n\n## Occasion\nNOT_APPLICABLE has no ordinary case: a pull request IS a handoff, so the occasion for readiness and traceability always arises. A trivial chore needing no tracking issue is an observed handoff judged on its readiness, not an inapplicable one.\n\n## Sources\nReason only from metadata that is actually present.\n1. The pull request record (`context/metadata.json`): the draft flag, the title, the branch name, and `head_checks` when the provider''s checks or pipeline reported on the reviewed head (SUCCESS, FAILURE, PENDING, CANCELLED, NONE; absent when never observed). It establishes the declared state, any title marker, an issue id in the branch slug, and the checks reported for the reviewed head. A failing check does not independently establish a readiness or traceability failure; do not add a separate CI criterion. Never assert a build, CI or merge state the record does not carry. The linked items (`context/linked_work_items.json`) say for each issue whether the provider records it as a closing candidate for this change (`how: closesOnMerge`) or the text merely mentions it (`how: mentions`); quote that field rather than inferring a link from a number.\n2. The description (`context/description.md`) and its authored view (`work/change/description.authored.md`, which separates the author''s lines from the form''s and lists the form''s items the author ticked): the closing reference or plain mention, any authored statement of what is finished or not, and any checklist with its ticks. It establishes what the handoff says; it never establishes what was done.\n3. The change (`work/change/files.json`, `work/change/diff.patch`): which files changed and what their changed lines do — enough to tell a trivial chore from a substantive feature or fix. It never establishes that the change builds, runs or was tested: manual verification, external test suites and existing unchanged tests need no test file in this diff. Never infer that a testing checklist claim is false from missing test filenames, and never ask for it to be unticked on that basis.\nWhen the deciding fact is not observable — the draft flag was never populated, or the context is un-enriched — the handoff still happened and you could not read it: report a collection gap, name the fact you could not read, and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: the pull request is out of draft with no title or authored statement that the work is unfinished or that review should wait, and a reference is quotable in the title, the description or the branch — or the change is a trivial chore that needs none. A genuine draft with a closing reference is also MET.\n- NOT_MET: a mixed signal on a non-draft pull request — a title that declares the work unfinished or holds review, or an authored statement that the work within its slice is unfinished or that review should wait, including an open item of an adopted checklist as described above. A form''s checklist left as supplied, reviewer verification, a merge condition or follow-on work outside the slice is never this cell on its own. Quote the offending token or the author''s lines.\n- NOT_MET: a substantive feature or fix change with no traceable reference on any of the three surfaces — title, description, branch — after scanning all of them.\n- UNDETERMINED only when the changed lines themselves leave open whether a change is a trivial chore or substantive; name what would settle it. A missing draft flag is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED. Having scanned the three surfaces and found no reference on a substantive change is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, combining the sub-checks by taking the most severe. A genuine draft never makes a NOT_MET on readiness; note it as context, and it never lifts the severity.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on handoff alone — non-blocking and formative, a workflow practice to grow. MINOR, the usual band: a mixed marker alone, a missing reference alone, or an authored statement of unfinished work. MAJOR: a substantive handoff that is both mis-signalled and untraceable.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the exact token relied on: the issue reference, the offending title marker, the draft flag value, the author''s lines that say the work is unfinished. For NOT_MET, name the surfaces scanned — title, description, branch — and the changed files or lines that make the change substantive. Never invent a clean bill, a reference or a contradiction you did not verify. Describe the observable signal; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nTest-suite adequacy and automated-test presence belong to `ships-tests-with-the-change`; scope cohesion belongs to `scope-one-reviewable-change`; commit-message quality belongs to `commit-subjects-explain-each-change`. Here only: is the handoff ready and traceable?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute traceability hints and checklist facts; the model judges readiness from the captured handoff.\nimport { readCommits } from \"../lib/change.ts\";\nimport { branchIssueReferences, issueNumberReferences } from \"../lib/references.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default async function readyAndTraceableHandoff(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\t_d: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tm: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir?: string,\n) {\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\n\t// Traceability: does the handoff reference a motivating issue at all?\n\tconst commits = await readCommits(contextDir);\n\tconst body = `${m.body ?? \"\"}\\n${commits.map((c) => c.message).join(\"\\n\")}`;\n\tconst branch = m.source_branch;\n\tconst bodyRefs = new Set(issueNumberReferences(body).map((n) => `#${n}`));\n\tconst branchRefs = new Set(branchIssueReferences(branch).map((n) => `#${n}`));\n\tconst allRefs = new Set<string>([...bodyRefs, ...branchRefs]);\n\tif (allRefs.size > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Issue-mention syntax candidates: ${[...allRefs].join(\", \")}${branchRefs.size > 0 ? ` (branch ''${branch}'' encodes ${[...branchRefs].join(\", \")})` : \"\"}. Inspect each mention in context before treating it as the author''s motivating issue: templates, examples and branch numbers may be unrelated. A genuine reference need not contain a closing keyword.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Traceability fact: no issue reference (#N, ''Refs #N'', closing keyword, or issue-number branch prefix) was found in the body, the ${commits.length} commit message(s), or branch ''${branch}'' — confirm in the body before concluding the handoff is untraceable.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\n\t// Readiness: the checklist as written, counted, so a tick is never guessed at. The counts cannot say whose\n\t// lines they are; the model reads that from the description.\n\tconst description = m.body ?? \"\";\n\tconst ticked = (description.match(/^\\s*[-*+]\\s*\\[[xX]\\]/gmu) ?? []).length;\n\tconst unticked = (description.match(/^\\s*[-*+]\\s*\\[ \\]/gmu) ?? []).length;\n\tif (ticked + unticked > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Checklist fact: the description carries ${ticked} ticked and ${unticked} unticked checkbox line(s). These counts say neither whose lines they are nor what was done. Where work/change/description.authored.md exists, it separates the form''s lines from the author''s; otherwise compare description.md with the form. Judge whether the author adopted the checklist or wrote equivalent prose; a form''s line left unticked as supplied is boilerplate, not unfinished work, and a tick is a claim, not a result.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: [],\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tissueMentionSyntaxCandidateCount: allRefs.size,\n\t\t\tcommitCount: commits.length,\n\t\t\tchecklistTicked: ticked,\n\t\t\tchecklistUnticked: unticked,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A clear handoff helps reviewers decide when to give work their attention and follow it back to the need it answers.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A feature change kept in Draft while unfinished, with an issue reference such as ''#482''. When the work is ready for review, its status and description agree. A small formatting chore can stand on its own without a tracking issue.","groupSlug":"review-ready-work","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 172\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Mixed handoff</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"110.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">WIP: timeout</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"16.75\" y=\"76.75\" width=\"150.5\" height=\"22.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"28\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Ready for review</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"15\">No issue linked</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewers guess</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Clear handoff</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"110.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Fix timeout</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"352.75\" y=\"76.75\" width=\"150.5\" height=\"22.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"364\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Ready for review</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"15\">Fixes issue #482</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewers know</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"Mixed handoff: a change titled “WIP: timeout” is marked “Ready for review” with “No issue linked”, so reviewers guess. Clear handoff: “Fix timeout” is marked “Ready for review” and says “Fixes issue #482”, so reviewers know."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- While the work is unfinished, open the pull or merge request as a draft.\n- When your work is finished, remove title or description words that say work is left or that review should wait.\n- Finish each open to-do item, or say that it moves to follow-on work outside this change.\n- Name the issue that the change answers in the title, the description or the branch name. For example, instead of the title “Fix login timeout” and nothing else, write “Fixes #482” in the description.\n- Use a closing keyword, such as “Closes” or “Fixes”, when the merge should close the issue. A plain mention links the change to the issue, but the issue stays open.\n\n![If the work is unfinished, three steps follow in order. First, open a draft. Next, finish the work and settle your own to-dos. Last, mark it ready to ask for review now.](figures/draft-to-ready.svg)\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- There is no usual case where it does not apply. Every pull or merge request hands work to a reviewer.\n\nIt still applies when the change is a small chore with no tracking issue. Then only its readiness for review counts.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Treating a draft as a fault. A draft is a normal state for unfinished work. Instead, keep unfinished work in draft with its issue reference.\n- Marking a change ready while your own words say work is left. An example is “still need to add the error state”. Instead, finish that work first, or keep the change in draft.\n- Holding back a small chore because it has no issue. A dependency bump, a formatting fix, a rename or a docs fix can stand on its own. Instead, mark it ready when the work is done.\n- Reading a merge condition as a hold. “Do not merge until approved” asks for a review. It does not ask reviewers to wait.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [About pull requests: draft pull requests](https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/collaborating-with-pull-requests/proposing-changes-to-your-work-with-pull-requests/about-pull-requests#draft-pull-requests), GitHub Docs. A draft shares work in progress without a formal review request. Marking it ready requests reviews from code owners.\n- [Linking a pull request to an issue](https://docs.github.com/en/issues/tracking-your-work-with-issues/using-issues/linking-a-pull-request-to-an-issue), GitHub Docs. A closing keyword such as “Fixes” closes the issue when the change merges into the default branch.\n- [Crosslinking issues](https://docs.gitlab.com/user/project/issues/crosslinking_issues/), GitLab Docs. A mention in a description, a commit or a branch name links the issue. Closing it on merge is a separate choice.\n- [Writing good CL descriptions](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/developer/cl-descriptions.html), Google Engineering Practices. Relevant bug numbers and other background help reviewers and later readers understand the change.\n","figures":{"draft-to-ready":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 76\">\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"12\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M32 12h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M216 12l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"236\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"236\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M256 12h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M440 12l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"460\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Open a draft</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">While work is unfinished</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"224\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Finish the work</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"224\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">Settle your own to-dos</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"448\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Mark it ready</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"448\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">Ask for review now</text>\n</svg>\n"}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Make the change’s status clear and link its issue","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"REQUIRED"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: how the change is handed off — presented as genuinely ready for others'' time, and traceable back to the issue it answers.\n\n## The standard\nJudge two things about the handoff, combined into one observation. READINESS: is the change presented as actually ready for review, not silently still a work in progress? TRACEABILITY: can a reader follow it back to the motivating issue? Good: an out-of-draft pull request with no authored unfinished-work or review-hold signal and a quotable closing reference — \"Closes #482\", or an issue id embedded in the branch slug. Bad: a mixed-signal handoff — a title that declares the work unfinished or asks reviewers to hold off while the pull request is openly non-draft — or a substantive feature or fix change with no traceable reference anywhere a reader would look. Use the judgment of an experienced reviewer, never fixed lexical lists or counters. Title text is a readiness signal only when it says the work is unfinished or that review should wait; a word that names something the product handles or the feature being built is not. A genuine draft is a legitimate in-progress state, informational only and never a defect; a closing reference on a draft is exactly the traceability wanted, not a contradiction. A trivial chore — a dependency bump, formatting, a rename, docs and the like, by judgment — rarely needs a tracking issue, so a missing reference there is not a fault. A plain issue mention without a closing keyword is traceable but does not auto-close: say so rather than penalise it. Readiness is read from what the author says, not from the form, against the slice the handoff says it delivers: ready for review means the author asks for review now, not that review, checks or approval already happened. An authored statement that implementation within that slice is unfinished — \"still need to add the error state\", \"not done yet\", an open to-do item the author wrote — or that reviewers should wait before reviewing, is a mixed signal on a non-draft pull request. A checklist the form supplies, left as supplied, is boilerplate: it states nothing about the work, ticked or not. When the author adopted a checklist — wrote it, edited its items or ticked some — read its open items, together with any prose that accounts for them, as the author''s account of who owes each item and when: an open item whose substance is the author''s unfinished work within the slice, and that nothing the author wrote settles, says the work is not finished. Verification the author asks reviewers to do as part of the review, a condition before merge such as passing checks or an approval, and follow-on work the author places outside this slice do not by themselves say the slice is unready; \"do not merge until approved\" states a merge condition, not a hold on review, unless the surrounding prose says review should wait. Never infer that something was or was not built, tested or run from a tick, a missing tick or a missing file.\n\n## Occasion\nNOT_APPLICABLE has no ordinary case: a pull request IS a handoff, so the occasion for readiness and traceability always arises. A trivial chore needing no tracking issue is an observed handoff judged on its readiness, not an inapplicable one.\n\n## Sources\nReason only from metadata that is actually present.\n1. The pull request record (`context/metadata.json`): the draft flag, the title, the branch name, and `head_checks` when the provider''s checks or pipeline reported on the reviewed head (SUCCESS, FAILURE, PENDING, CANCELLED, NONE; absent when never observed). It establishes the declared state, any title marker, an issue id in the branch slug, and the checks reported for the reviewed head. A failing check does not independently establish a readiness or traceability failure; do not add a separate CI criterion. Never assert a build, CI or merge state the record does not carry. The linked items (`context/linked_work_items.json`) say for each issue whether the provider records it as a closing candidate for this change (`how: closesOnMerge`) or the text merely mentions it (`how: mentions`); quote that field rather than inferring a link from a number.\n2. The description (`context/description.md`) and its authored view (`work/change/description.authored.md`, which separates the author''s lines from the form''s and lists the form''s items the author ticked): the closing reference or plain mention, any authored statement of what is finished or not, and any checklist with its ticks. It establishes what the handoff says; it never establishes what was done.\n3. The change (`work/change/files.json`, `work/change/diff.patch`): which files changed and what their changed lines do — enough to tell a trivial chore from a substantive feature or fix. It never establishes that the change builds, runs or was tested: manual verification, external test suites and existing unchanged tests need no test file in this diff. Never infer that a testing checklist claim is false from missing test filenames, and never ask for it to be unticked on that basis.\nWhen the deciding fact is not observable — the draft flag was never populated, or the context is un-enriched — the handoff still happened and you could not read it: report a collection gap, name the fact you could not read, and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: the pull request is out of draft with no title or authored statement that the work is unfinished or that review should wait, and a reference is quotable in the title, the description or the branch — or the change is a trivial chore that needs none. A genuine draft with a closing reference is also MET.\n- NOT_MET: a mixed signal on a non-draft pull request — a title that declares the work unfinished or holds review, or an authored statement that the work within its slice is unfinished or that review should wait, including an open item of an adopted checklist as described above. A form''s checklist left as supplied, reviewer verification, a merge condition or follow-on work outside the slice is never this cell on its own. Quote the offending token or the author''s lines.\n- NOT_MET: a substantive feature or fix change with no traceable reference on any of the three surfaces — title, description, branch — after scanning all of them.\n- UNDETERMINED only when the changed lines themselves leave open whether a change is a trivial chore or substantive; name what would settle it. A missing draft flag is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED. Having scanned the three surfaces and found no reference on a substantive change is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, combining the sub-checks by taking the most severe. A genuine draft never makes a NOT_MET on readiness; note it as context, and it never lifts the severity.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on handoff alone — non-blocking and formative, a workflow practice to grow. MINOR, the usual band: a mixed marker alone, a missing reference alone, or an authored statement of unfinished work. MAJOR: a substantive handoff that is both mis-signalled and untraceable.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the exact token relied on: the issue reference, the offending title marker, the draft flag value, the author''s lines that say the work is unfinished. For NOT_MET, name the surfaces scanned — title, description, branch — and the changed files or lines that make the change substantive. Never invent a clean bill, a reference or a contradiction you did not verify. Describe the observable signal; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nTest-suite adequacy and automated-test presence belong to `ships-tests-with-the-change`; scope cohesion belongs to `scope-one-reviewable-change`; commit-message quality belongs to `commit-subjects-explain-each-change`. Here only: is the handoff ready and traceable?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute traceability hints and checklist facts; the model judges readiness from the captured handoff.\nimport { readCommits } from \"../lib/change.ts\";\nimport { branchIssueReferences, issueNumberReferences } from \"../lib/references.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default async function readyAndTraceableHandoff(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\t_d: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tm: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir?: string,\n) {\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\n\t// Traceability: does the handoff reference a motivating issue at all?\n\tconst commits = await readCommits(contextDir);\n\tconst body = `${m.body ?? \"\"}\\n${commits.map((c) => c.message).join(\"\\n\")}`;\n\tconst branch = m.source_branch;\n\tconst bodyRefs = new Set(issueNumberReferences(body).map((n) => `#${n}`));\n\tconst branchRefs = new Set(branchIssueReferences(branch).map((n) => `#${n}`));\n\tconst allRefs = new Set<string>([...bodyRefs, ...branchRefs]);\n\tif (allRefs.size > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Issue-mention syntax candidates: ${[...allRefs].join(\", \")}${branchRefs.size > 0 ? ` (branch ''${branch}'' encodes ${[...branchRefs].join(\", \")})` : \"\"}. Inspect each mention in context before treating it as the author''s motivating issue: templates, examples and branch numbers may be unrelated. A genuine reference need not contain a closing keyword.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Traceability fact: no issue reference (#N, ''Refs #N'', closing keyword, or issue-number branch prefix) was found in the body, the ${commits.length} commit message(s), or branch ''${branch}'' — confirm in the body before concluding the handoff is untraceable.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\n\t// Readiness: the checklist as written, counted, so a tick is never guessed at. The counts cannot say whose\n\t// lines they are; the model reads that from the description.\n\tconst description = m.body ?? \"\";\n\tconst ticked = (description.match(/^\\s*[-*+]\\s*\\[[xX]\\]/gmu) ?? []).length;\n\tconst unticked = (description.match(/^\\s*[-*+]\\s*\\[ \\]/gmu) ?? []).length;\n\tif (ticked + unticked > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Checklist fact: the description carries ${ticked} ticked and ${unticked} unticked checkbox line(s). These counts say neither whose lines they are nor what was done. Where work/change/description.authored.md exists, it separates the form''s lines from the author''s; otherwise compare description.md with the form. Judge whether the author adopted the checklist or wrote equivalent prose; a form''s line left unticked as supplied is boilerplate, not unfinished work, and a tick is a claim, not a result.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: [],\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tissueMentionSyntaxCandidateCount: allRefs.size,\n\t\t\tcommitCount: commits.length,\n\t\t\tchecklistTicked: ticked,\n\t\t\tchecklistUnticked: unticked,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A clear handoff helps reviewers decide when to give work their attention and follow it back to the need it answers.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A feature change kept in Draft while unfinished, with an issue reference such as ''#482''. When the work is ready for review, its status and description agree. A small formatting chore can stand on its own without a tracking issue.","groupSlug":"review-ready-work","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 172\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Mixed handoff</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"110.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">WIP: timeout</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"16.75\" y=\"76.75\" width=\"150.5\" height=\"22.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"28\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Ready for review</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"15\">No issue linked</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewers guess</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Clear handoff</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"110.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Fix timeout</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"352.75\" y=\"76.75\" width=\"150.5\" height=\"22.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"364\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Ready for review</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"15\">Fixes issue #482</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewers know</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"Mixed handoff: a change titled “WIP: timeout” is marked “Ready for review” with “No issue linked”, so reviewers guess. Clear handoff: “Fix timeout” is marked “Ready for review” and says “Fixes issue #482”, so reviewers know."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- While the work is unfinished, open the pull or merge request as a draft.\n- When your work is finished, remove title or description words that say work is left or that review should wait.\n- Finish each open to-do item, or say that it moves to follow-on work outside this change.\n- Name the issue that the change answers in the title, the description or the branch name. For example, instead of the title “Fix login timeout” and nothing else, write “Fixes #482” in the description.\n- Use a closing keyword, such as “Closes” or “Fixes”, when the merge should close the issue. A plain mention links the change to the issue, but the issue stays open.\n\n![If the work is unfinished, three steps follow in order. First, open a draft. Next, finish the work and settle your own to-dos. Last, mark it ready to ask for review now.](figures/draft-to-ready.svg)\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- There is no usual case where it does not apply. Every pull or merge request hands work to a reviewer.\n\nIt still applies when the change is a small chore with no tracking issue. Then only its readiness for review counts.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Treating a draft as a fault. A draft is a normal state for unfinished work. Instead, keep unfinished work in draft with its issue reference.\n- Marking a change ready while your own words say work is left. An example is “still need to add the error state”. Instead, finish that work first, or keep the change in draft.\n- Holding back a small chore because it has no issue. A dependency bump, a formatting fix, a rename or a docs fix can stand on its own. Instead, mark it ready when the work is done.\n- Reading a merge condition as a hold. “Do not merge until approved” asks for a review. It does not ask reviewers to wait.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [About pull requests: draft pull requests](https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/collaborating-with-pull-requests/proposing-changes-to-your-work-with-pull-requests/about-pull-requests#draft-pull-requests), GitHub Docs. A draft shares work in progress without a formal review request. Marking it ready requests reviews from code owners.\n- [Linking a pull request to an issue](https://docs.github.com/en/issues/tracking-your-work-with-issues/using-issues/linking-a-pull-request-to-an-issue), GitHub Docs. A closing keyword such as “Fixes” closes the issue when the change merges into the default branch.\n- [Crosslinking issues](https://docs.gitlab.com/user/project/issues/crosslinking_issues/), GitLab Docs. A mention in a description, a commit or a branch name links the issue. Closing it on merge is a separate choice.\n- [Writing good CL descriptions](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/developer/cl-descriptions.html), Google Engineering Practices. Relevant bug numbers and other background help reviewers and later readers understand the change.\n","figures":{"draft-to-ready":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 76\">\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"12\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M32 12h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M216 12l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"236\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"236\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M256 12h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M440 12l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"460\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Open a draft</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">While work is unfinished</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"224\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Finish the work</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"224\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">Settle your own to-dos</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"448\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Mark it ready</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"448\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">Ask for review now</text>\n</svg>\n"}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"The change''s status is clear, and substantive work names the issue it answers."'::jsonb),'v5:9fba9baf22afc5bb894a1fe783dcbc59e9a76940bb6463c3bc1cb818c4ead34d','practice:v3:b13b6db25f48ba7a899461a8b58c91f8c506b31d754d0d53ccc3ef35fa6941c4');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','commit-subjects-explain-each-change','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Write commit subjects a reviewer can follow","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"REQUIRED"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: each authored commit subject names a specific action and object, so a reviewer can follow the history without opening the diff.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether the pull request''s commit SUBJECTS let a reviewer follow the change step by step. A legible subject names a specific action and object, so someone reading the history understands the described step without opening the diff. Judge clarity, not format: a `type:` prefix (feat, fix, refactor, chore) is acceptable but never required, and terse is fine when the object of the change is clear (\"wire up depth buffer\", \"cache detection results\", \"split parser into lexer + reader\" are all legible). Uninformative: a subject that carries no specific information about the change — a bare \"wip\", \"fix\", \"update\", \"lint\" or \"stuff\" with no object, a lone punctuation mark, placeholder junk, a bare issue reference or branch slug with no description, \"address comments\" with no specifics, a subject cut off mid-phrase, or the same subject repeated verbatim across commits. The test is the object: \"update changelog\", \"rename settings tab\" and \"ignore build output\" name what was touched and are legible however terse; \"small changes\", \"fix\" and \"cleanup\" name nothing. These are open examples, not a closed list; reason as an experienced reviewer would about whether each subject carries real information. A subject pairing a reference WITH a real summary (\"#39: switch logging to the shared Logger\") is legible, not noise. This is a workflow practice about how history is written, never a claim about the code itself.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Automatic merge commits (\"Merge branch ...\", \"Merge pull request ...\", or two or more `parents`) are git plumbing, not authored summaries; exclude them from the judgment. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, say so, and stop when only merge commits remain after that exclusion: no authored subject was written here.\n\n## Sources\n1. The commits (`context/commits.json`), staged from the pinned range of the change: each subject, its body and its `parents`. They establish what each authored subject says. Judge each subject on its own. The subject describes the step; the record does not establish what the code does or whether it matches that description.\nThe precomputed leads below these criteria, when present, show authored subjects and their shapes: bare, repeated or cut off. They are places to look, never verdicts. If `context/commits.json` is unavailable, report a collection gap and record no observation; uncollected subjects are never authored silence and never UNDETERMINED.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: every authored subject names a specific action and object; a reviewer can read top to bottom and follow the work.\n- NOT_MET: some authored subjects carry no specific information about the change. Quote the noisy subjects and explain what information their words leave unclear.\n- UNDETERMINED: no ordinary case. A subject you have read is either informative or not; never invent a subject or a clean bill you did not read.\nOne observation per review, over every authored subject. State how many subjects you saw.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on subject quality alone — a formative practice to grow, never a merge gate. Scale to how much the noise degrades a reviewer''s ability to follow the history, and weigh review burden proportionally: a single-commit change is inherently low-burden. INFO: a lone straggler in an otherwise clear history, the gentlest nudge. MINOR, the usual band: several uninformative subjects that make the history hard to follow. MAJOR: a history that is mostly noise, where a reviewer genuinely cannot follow the change commit by commit.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery claim rests on the subject text you quote: the deciding subjects — the noisy ones for NOT_MET, and the subjects you accepted for MET. Quote the deciding subjects verbatim with their short SHA and cite the corresponding lines in the captured `context/commits.json` artifact. Never cite a diff line or a repository file for this observation — its evidence is subject text — and never record UNDETERMINED because the subjects have no line of their own to cite. Describe observable behaviour only; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nLegibility of the pull request title and description belongs to `describe-what-and-why`; the scope and cohesion of what a commit touches belongs to `commits-are-atomic-and-cohesive`; code correctness belongs to other practices. Here only: does each authored subject describe a specific step to the reader?","precomputeScript":"// Subject clarity reads authored subject text and its shapes from the captured commit record.\nimport { readCapturedCommits } from \"../lib/change.ts\";\nimport { describeCommitCount, subjectFacts, subjectRows } from \"../lib/commit-subjects.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default async function commitSubjectsExplainEachChange(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\t_diffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_metadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir: string | undefined,\n\t_changeDir: string | undefined,\n\tcontextReference: string,\n) {\n\tconst captured = await readCapturedCommits(contextDir);\n\tconst facts = subjectFacts(captured ?? []);\n\tconst authored = facts.filter((f) => !f.merge);\n\tlet directions = [\n\t\tdescribeCommitCount(facts),\n\t\t\"Judge every subject on whether it names a specific action and object for a reader of the history. A flag names a shape to check, not a lapse.\",\n\t];\n\tif (captured === null) {\n\t\tdirections = [\n\t\t\t\"commits.json is missing or malformed: a collection gap, not a history without authored commits.\",\n\t\t];\n\t} else if (authored.length === 0) {\n\t\tdirections = [\"No authored commit in the reviewed range: only merge commits, or none.\"];\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: subjectRows(facts, contextReference),\n\t\tmetrics:\n\t\t\tcaptured === null\n\t\t\t\t? {}\n\t\t\t\t: {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tauthoredCommits: authored.length,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tmergeCommits: facts.length - authored.length,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tbareSubjects: authored.filter((f) => f.bare).length,\n\t\t\t\t\t\trepeatedSubjects: authored.filter((f) => f.repeat).length,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tcutOffSubjects: authored.filter((f) => f.cutOff).length,\n\t\t\t\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Six months from now, someone, maybe you, will run git blame on a confusing line and read your commit subject to understand it. ''wip'' and ''fix stuff'' tell them nothing, while a clear subject tells the whole story.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A history you can read top to bottom, where each subject names exactly what that step did, such as ''Extract the retry logic into a helper'' then ''Add a timeout to the upload call''.","groupSlug":"review-ready-work","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":true,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 220\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Vague subjects</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">wip</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"88.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(16 104)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"44\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">fix</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"144.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(16 160)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"44\" y=\"173\" font-size=\"15\">fix</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"15\">Tells nothing</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Clear subjects</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Extract retry helper</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"88.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(352 104)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">Add upload timeout</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"144.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(352 160)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"173\" font-size=\"15\">Log failed uploads</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"15\">Tells the story</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"Vague subjects such as “wip”, “fix” and “fix” tell nothing about each commit. Clear subjects such as “Extract retry helper”, “Add upload timeout” and “Log failed uploads” tell the story."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- In each commit subject, name what that commit changed, for a reader who will not open the diff.\n- Name the thing that you changed. For example, instead of “fix”, write “Stop the export from dropping the last row”.\n- Before you ask for a review, read your subjects from top to bottom.\n- Reword each subject that a teammate cannot follow without the diff.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Merge commits, which have two or more parents, such as “Merge branch …” or “Merge pull request …”. Git or your provider writes these subjects, so they do not count.\n- A pull or merge request that has only merge commits. There is no subject that you wrote.\n\nIt still applies when the pull or merge request has only one commit.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that a subject needs a prefix such as “feat:” or a set length. It needs neither. “Update the changelog” is short and still names what changed. Instead, check that each subject names the thing that you changed.\n- Writing a word with no object, such as “wip”, “cleanup” or “small changes”. Instead, add the thing you changed, such as “Remove unused imports from the parser”.\n- Giving several commits the same subject, such as “fix” three times. Instead, name what each step did, so a reviewer can follow each step.\n- Writing only an issue number, a branch name or “address comments”. Instead, add a short summary beside the reference, such as “#39: switch logging to the shared logger”.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [The canonical patch format](https://docs.kernel.org/process/submitting-patches.html#the-canonical-patch-format), Linux kernel documentation. The summary phrase should describe its patch, and a series should not use the same phrase for every patch. People who scan history with `git log --oneline` may see only that phrase.\n- [git-commit](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-commit#_discussion), Git documentation. Git recommends a first line that summarizes the change. Git uses the text up to the first blank line as the commit title.\n- [How to write a Git commit message](https://cbea.ms/git-commit/), Chris Beams, 2014. A clear log lets you review other people’s commits on your own. It also helps you learn later why a change happened.\n- [What makes a good commit message?](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2202.02974), Tian, Zhang, Stol, Jiang and Liu, 2022. A commit message is an audit trail of how code changed and why. Many messages in active open source projects lack information.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Write commit subjects a reviewer can follow","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"REQUIRED"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: each authored commit subject names a specific action and object, so a reviewer can follow the history without opening the diff.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether the pull request''s commit SUBJECTS let a reviewer follow the change step by step. A legible subject names a specific action and object, so someone reading the history understands the described step without opening the diff. Judge clarity, not format: a `type:` prefix (feat, fix, refactor, chore) is acceptable but never required, and terse is fine when the object of the change is clear (\"wire up depth buffer\", \"cache detection results\", \"split parser into lexer + reader\" are all legible). Uninformative: a subject that carries no specific information about the change — a bare \"wip\", \"fix\", \"update\", \"lint\" or \"stuff\" with no object, a lone punctuation mark, placeholder junk, a bare issue reference or branch slug with no description, \"address comments\" with no specifics, a subject cut off mid-phrase, or the same subject repeated verbatim across commits. The test is the object: \"update changelog\", \"rename settings tab\" and \"ignore build output\" name what was touched and are legible however terse; \"small changes\", \"fix\" and \"cleanup\" name nothing. These are open examples, not a closed list; reason as an experienced reviewer would about whether each subject carries real information. A subject pairing a reference WITH a real summary (\"#39: switch logging to the shared Logger\") is legible, not noise. This is a workflow practice about how history is written, never a claim about the code itself.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Automatic merge commits (\"Merge branch ...\", \"Merge pull request ...\", or two or more `parents`) are git plumbing, not authored summaries; exclude them from the judgment. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, say so, and stop when only merge commits remain after that exclusion: no authored subject was written here.\n\n## Sources\n1. The commits (`context/commits.json`), staged from the pinned range of the change: each subject, its body and its `parents`. They establish what each authored subject says. Judge each subject on its own. The subject describes the step; the record does not establish what the code does or whether it matches that description.\nThe precomputed leads below these criteria, when present, show authored subjects and their shapes: bare, repeated or cut off. They are places to look, never verdicts. If `context/commits.json` is unavailable, report a collection gap and record no observation; uncollected subjects are never authored silence and never UNDETERMINED.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: every authored subject names a specific action and object; a reviewer can read top to bottom and follow the work.\n- NOT_MET: some authored subjects carry no specific information about the change. Quote the noisy subjects and explain what information their words leave unclear.\n- UNDETERMINED: no ordinary case. A subject you have read is either informative or not; never invent a subject or a clean bill you did not read.\nOne observation per review, over every authored subject. State how many subjects you saw.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on subject quality alone — a formative practice to grow, never a merge gate. Scale to how much the noise degrades a reviewer''s ability to follow the history, and weigh review burden proportionally: a single-commit change is inherently low-burden. INFO: a lone straggler in an otherwise clear history, the gentlest nudge. MINOR, the usual band: several uninformative subjects that make the history hard to follow. MAJOR: a history that is mostly noise, where a reviewer genuinely cannot follow the change commit by commit.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery claim rests on the subject text you quote: the deciding subjects — the noisy ones for NOT_MET, and the subjects you accepted for MET. Quote the deciding subjects verbatim with their short SHA and cite the corresponding lines in the captured `context/commits.json` artifact. Never cite a diff line or a repository file for this observation — its evidence is subject text — and never record UNDETERMINED because the subjects have no line of their own to cite. Describe observable behaviour only; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nLegibility of the pull request title and description belongs to `describe-what-and-why`; the scope and cohesion of what a commit touches belongs to `commits-are-atomic-and-cohesive`; code correctness belongs to other practices. Here only: does each authored subject describe a specific step to the reader?","precomputeScript":"// Subject clarity reads authored subject text and its shapes from the captured commit record.\nimport { readCapturedCommits } from \"../lib/change.ts\";\nimport { describeCommitCount, subjectFacts, subjectRows } from \"../lib/commit-subjects.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default async function commitSubjectsExplainEachChange(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\t_diffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_metadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir: string | undefined,\n\t_changeDir: string | undefined,\n\tcontextReference: string,\n) {\n\tconst captured = await readCapturedCommits(contextDir);\n\tconst facts = subjectFacts(captured ?? []);\n\tconst authored = facts.filter((f) => !f.merge);\n\tlet directions = [\n\t\tdescribeCommitCount(facts),\n\t\t\"Judge every subject on whether it names a specific action and object for a reader of the history. A flag names a shape to check, not a lapse.\",\n\t];\n\tif (captured === null) {\n\t\tdirections = [\n\t\t\t\"commits.json is missing or malformed: a collection gap, not a history without authored commits.\",\n\t\t];\n\t} else if (authored.length === 0) {\n\t\tdirections = [\"No authored commit in the reviewed range: only merge commits, or none.\"];\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: subjectRows(facts, contextReference),\n\t\tmetrics:\n\t\t\tcaptured === null\n\t\t\t\t? {}\n\t\t\t\t: {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tauthoredCommits: authored.length,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tmergeCommits: facts.length - authored.length,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tbareSubjects: authored.filter((f) => f.bare).length,\n\t\t\t\t\t\trepeatedSubjects: authored.filter((f) => f.repeat).length,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tcutOffSubjects: authored.filter((f) => f.cutOff).length,\n\t\t\t\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Six months from now, someone, maybe you, will run git blame on a confusing line and read your commit subject to understand it. ''wip'' and ''fix stuff'' tell them nothing, while a clear subject tells the whole story.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A history you can read top to bottom, where each subject names exactly what that step did, such as ''Extract the retry logic into a helper'' then ''Add a timeout to the upload call''.","groupSlug":"review-ready-work","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":true,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 220\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Vague subjects</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">wip</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"88.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(16 104)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"44\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">fix</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"144.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(16 160)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"44\" y=\"173\" font-size=\"15\">fix</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"15\">Tells nothing</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Clear subjects</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Extract retry helper</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"88.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(352 104)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">Add upload timeout</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"144.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<g transform=\"translate(352 160)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M0 8h4.5M11.5 8H16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></g>,<text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"173\" font-size=\"15\">Log failed uploads</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"15\">Tells the story</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"Vague subjects such as “wip”, “fix” and “fix” tell nothing about each commit. Clear subjects such as “Extract retry helper”, “Add upload timeout” and “Log failed uploads” tell the story."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- In each commit subject, name what that commit changed, for a reader who will not open the diff.\n- Name the thing that you changed. For example, instead of “fix”, write “Stop the export from dropping the last row”.\n- Before you ask for a review, read your subjects from top to bottom.\n- Reword each subject that a teammate cannot follow without the diff.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Merge commits, which have two or more parents, such as “Merge branch …” or “Merge pull request …”. Git or your provider writes these subjects, so they do not count.\n- A pull or merge request that has only merge commits. There is no subject that you wrote.\n\nIt still applies when the pull or merge request has only one commit.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that a subject needs a prefix such as “feat:” or a set length. It needs neither. “Update the changelog” is short and still names what changed. Instead, check that each subject names the thing that you changed.\n- Writing a word with no object, such as “wip”, “cleanup” or “small changes”. Instead, add the thing you changed, such as “Remove unused imports from the parser”.\n- Giving several commits the same subject, such as “fix” three times. Instead, name what each step did, so a reviewer can follow each step.\n- Writing only an issue number, a branch name or “address comments”. Instead, add a short summary beside the reference, such as “#39: switch logging to the shared logger”.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [The canonical patch format](https://docs.kernel.org/process/submitting-patches.html#the-canonical-patch-format), Linux kernel documentation. The summary phrase should describe its patch, and a series should not use the same phrase for every patch. People who scan history with `git log --oneline` may see only that phrase.\n- [git-commit](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-commit#_discussion), Git documentation. Git recommends a first line that summarizes the change. Git uses the text up to the first blank line as the commit title.\n- [How to write a Git commit message](https://cbea.ms/git-commit/), Chris Beams, 2014. A clear log lets you review other people’s commits on your own. It also helps you learn later why a change happened.\n- [What makes a good commit message?](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2202.02974), Tian, Zhang, Stol, Jiang and Liu, 2022. A commit message is an audit trail of how code changed and why. Many messages in active open source projects lack information.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Commit subjects say what each step changed"'::jsonb),'v5:1c45fc8b3e72ca5ee2a9408248ff09fd5f78bfc9d5287b089ed15173e673183c','practice:v3:994b3527677e980df54c58c23c7af9387eb0feb530706c8337c07676d7964552');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','honours-linked-issue-acceptance-criteria','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Say which acceptance criteria are done","signals":["scm.pull_request.merged","scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.linked-work-items","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: a pull request that closes a tracked work item with checkable acceptance criteria says which of those criteria it satisfies and which it defers.\n\n## The standard\nJudge ONE thing: when a change CLOSES a tracked work item that carries checkable acceptance criteria — a Definition-of-Done or acceptance-criteria heading, a task list, a Given-When-Then block, an enumerated must/should list — can a reviewer tell from the pull request itself which criteria this change satisfies and which are deferred? A good handoff maps the change to those criteria: \"Closes #57: login and reset done here; rate-limit deferred to #61\", or a checked task list mirroring the item''s entries, so the change can be confirmed against its own done-list without reverse-engineering. This is a delivery and traceability practice about how the change is handed off — never a code-defect claim and never an assertion that a criterion is unmet.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. The occasion exists only when the authored handoff claims to close a tracked item that carries a quotable, checkable criterion. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name what ruled it out, and stop when the linked item you could read carries no closing entry with a checkable criterion: no closing reference at all, or an item that states no checkable criterion — a title alone, a user story (\"As a …, I want …\"), or prose with no list, checklist or acceptance section is not a checkable criterion. A candidate item alone does not open the occasion, and neither does a body that references an item without a checkable criterion. How an item is referenced — a closing keyword, a bare mention, a branch number — is a syntax fact, not adoption: inspect the exact mention in context, since examples, templates and code may name unrelated work.\n\n## Sources\n1. The description (`context/description.md`) and the title (`context/metadata.json`): the closing reference and any done/deferred mapping — a checked task list, a Given-When-Then or DoD block, a sentence naming what is done and what moves elsewhere. They establish what the handoff says. They never establish whether a criterion is met.\n2. The source branch (`context/metadata.json`) and the commits (`context/commits.json`): further places a work-item number may appear. They establish how the item is referenced, nothing more.\n3. Each linked item (`context/linked_work_items/<n>.md`: number, title, url, state, labels, body; `how` in `linked_work_items.json`): the criteria block to map against. It establishes whether a checkable criterion exists and its exact wording. It never establishes that the pull request adopted it; the closing reference in the text or the provider''s own link (`how: closesOnMerge`) does that.\n4. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the touched-file set, read only to ground a heavier severity. It never establishes that a criterion is met or unmet.\n5. The inventory (`context/project_inventory.json`): open it and state either the linked or closing issue you found, quoting its `#number \"title\" (state)`, or that you scanned the inventory and found none.\nIf the linked items were not captured, you cannot tell whether there was a done-list to map to: when the body carries a closing reference but no mapping and the item''s criteria are unreadable, report a collection gap and record no observation. The MET path does not depend on that file: a body that carries a closing reference together with an explicit checked task list, Given-When-Then or DoD block mapping the change to enumerated criteria is judged from the body alone.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: the closing pull request maps the change to the item''s criteria — stating what is done here and what is deferred. The mapping may be implicit: a body whose statements name the substance of each criterion as done (or deferred) honours it, and a checklist that copies the criteria is a mapping; a checklist format is never required. A template''s own Definition of Done — items about the merge request''s form (title format, lint, a local build, screenshots) rather than the item''s criteria — maps nothing, however many of its boxes are ticked. Quote the closing reference and the criteria span.\n- NOT_MET: a quotable closing reference to an item with a checkable criteria block, and the body neither names the substance of the criteria as done nor defers them — a restatement of the title, an empty template, or a body that speaks of something else. Frame it as a forward-looking handoff request — \"state which of #N''s criteria this satisfies and which are deferred\" — never as a failure.\n- UNDETERMINED: the captured text leaves genuinely open whether the mention closes the item; name the question that would settle it. Having read the body and the item and found no mapping is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review. Use your judgment as an experienced reviewer for cases not enumerated here.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: a single nudge to state coverage. MAJOR only when, having actually scanned the touched-file set in the diff, you can ground a plausible gap: a concrete quoted criterion has no corresponding surface in the touched files — the item requires an error state and the diff adds only the happy path — and the body does not mark it deferred. Phrase even this as \"state whether <criterion> is in scope here or deferred\". When you cannot ground a gap, stay MINOR. Identical inputs yield identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery observation quotes the work-item number, its closing reference, and the exact criterion span from the linked item''s body, citing the item''s url. For NOT_MET, the citations show the closing reference, the criteria block, and the body read through without a mapping. You have the diff and an excerpt, not the running system: never claim to have run, compiled or verified the change, and never assert a specific criterion is unmet, missing or broken — you point at a handoff gap, not at correctness. Never invent or demand a criterion the item never stated. Describe what the body says against what the item states; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the issue itself is well written belongs to the issue-authoring practices (`issue-has-checkable-outcome`); the pull request description in general belongs to `describe-what-and-why`; marking the change ready and linking its issue at all belongs to `ready-and-traceable-handoff`. Here only: does the closing change say which stated criteria it covers?","precomputeScript":"// Surface linked-issue references and acceptance-criteria blocks, not compliance verdicts.\nimport { readContextJson } from \"../lib/context.ts\";\nimport { isJsonObject } from \"../lib/practice-contract.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nconst CLOSE_KEYWORD = /\\b(?:close[sd]?|fix(?:e[sd])?|resolve[sd]?)\\b/giu;\n// \"closes #12\", \"fixes GH-12\", \"resolves group/proj#12\" — capture the trailing issue number.\nconst CLOSE_REF =\n\t/\\b(?:close[sd]?|fix(?:e[sd])?|resolve[sd]?)\\s*:?\\s+(?:[\\w./~-]*[#!]|GH-)(?<number>\\d+)/giu;\n// Full issue/MR URLs on either host: .../issues/12, .../-/issues/12.\nconst ISSUE_URL = /https?:\\/\\/[^\\s)]+?\\/(?:-\\/)?issues\\/(?<number>\\d+)/giu;\n// Issue number embedded in a branch name: feature/123-foo, issue-123, 123-fix-thing, bugfix/GH-123.\nconst BRANCH_REF = /(?:^|[/_-])(?:issue[-_]?|gh[-_]?|#)?(?<number>\\d{1,6})(?:[-_/]|$)/giu;\n\n// Every pattern below is global and captures the issue number in group 1; matchAll iterates a clone,\n// so the shared module-level regexes keep a lastIndex of 0 between calls.\nfunction collect(re: RegExp, text: string, into: Set<string>): void {\n\tif (!text) {\n\t\treturn;\n\t}\n\tre.lastIndex = 0;\n\tfor (const match of text.matchAll(re)) {\n\t\tinto.add(`#${match.groups?.number ?? \"\"}`);\n\t}\n}\n\ninterface LinkedWorkItem {\n\tnumber?: number | string;\n\tiid?: number | string;\n\ttitle?: string;\n\t// The SCM connector (LinkedWorkItemContentSource) emits the issue body under `body`; `description`\n\t// is a host-general fallback for other connectors.\n\tbody?: string;\n\tdescription?: string;\n}\n\nfunction optionalText(value: unknown): string | undefined {\n\treturn typeof value === \"string\" ? value : undefined;\n}\n\nfunction optionalRef(value: unknown): number | string | undefined {\n\treturn typeof value === \"number\" || typeof value === \"string\" ? value : undefined;\n}\n\n// An entry carrying none of the fields above still counts as a linked item: `linkedIssueCount` reports\n// how many items the connector linked, which is a fact about the tracker, not about how much of each\n// entry this side could read.\nfunction toLinkedWorkItem(entry: unknown): LinkedWorkItem {\n\tif (!isJsonObject(entry)) {\n\t\treturn {};\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\tnumber: optionalRef(entry.number),\n\t\tiid: optionalRef(entry.iid),\n\t\ttitle: optionalText(entry.title),\n\t\tbody: optionalText(entry.body),\n\t\tdescription: optionalText(entry.description),\n\t};\n}\n\n// Unwrap whatever shape linked_work_items.json carries into a flat item list (or null if not a usable object).\nfunction unwrapLinkedItems(data: unknown): LinkedWorkItem[] | null {\n\tif (Array.isArray(data)) {\n\t\treturn data.map(toLinkedWorkItem);\n\t}\n\tif (!isJsonObject(data)) {\n\t\treturn null;\n\t}\n\t// The SCM connector wraps items under `workItems`; check it FIRST, then host-general fallbacks.\n\tif (Array.isArray(data.workItems)) {\n\t\treturn data.workItems.map(toLinkedWorkItem);\n\t}\n\tif (Array.isArray(data.items)) {\n\t\treturn data.items.map(toLinkedWorkItem);\n\t}\n\treturn [toLinkedWorkItem(data)];\n}\n\n// A checkable acceptance-criteria artifact: an AC/DoD heading or a \"- [ ]\" checklist in the issue body.\nfunction acFacts(body: string): { heading: boolean; boxes: number } {\n\tconst heading =\n\t\t/(?:acceptance criteria|definition of done|\\bDoD\\b|done when|expected (?:outcome|result|behaviou?r))/iu.test(\n\t\t\tbody,\n\t\t);\n\tconst boxes = (body.match(/^[\\s>]*[-*]\\s+\\[[ xX]\\]/gmu) ?? []).length;\n\treturn { heading, boxes };\n}\n\nexport default async function honoursLinkedIssueAcceptanceCriteria(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\t_diff: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tmeta: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir?: string,\n) {\n\tconst title = meta.title ?? \"\";\n\tconst body = meta.body ?? \"\";\n\tconst branch = meta.source_branch;\n\n\t// Syntax matches are candidates, including examples and code spans. They establish neither a\n\t// provider-reported relationship nor adoption of another issue''s criteria by the author.\n\tconst refs = new Set<string>();\n\tcollect(CLOSE_REF, `${title}\\n${body}`, refs);\n\tcollect(ISSUE_URL, `${title}\\n${body}`, refs);\n\tconst closingRefs = [...refs];\n\tconst hasClosingRef = closingRefs.length > 0;\n\n\tconst branchRefSet = new Set<string>();\n\tcollect(BRANCH_REF, branch, branchRefSet);\n\tconst branchRefs = [...branchRefSet];\n\tconst hasBranchRef = branchRefs.length > 0;\n\n\tconst keywordHits = (`${title}\\n${body}`.match(CLOSE_KEYWORD) ?? []).length;\n\tCLOSE_KEYWORD.lastIndex = 0;\n\n\tconst linked = unwrapLinkedItems(await readContextJson(contextDir, \"linked_work_items.json\"));\n\tconst linkedIssueBodyPresent = linked?.some(\n\t\t(i) => (i.body ?? i.description ?? \"\").trim().length > 0,\n\t);\n\tlet acHeading = false;\n\tlet acBoxes = 0;\n\tif (linked) {\n\t\tfor (const i of linked) {\n\t\t\tconst f = acFacts((i.body ?? i.description ?? \"\").trim());\n\t\t\tacHeading ||= f.heading;\n\t\t\tacBoxes += f.boxes;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tconst hasCheckableAcBlock = acHeading || acBoxes > 0;\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (hasClosingRef) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Issue-reference syntax candidate(s) (${closingRefs.join(\", \")}) from title/body — inspect the exact mention in context, including templates/examples, before establishing that the author claims to close this issue. Only then map those criteria to the change.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (hasBranchRef) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Branch name encodes a possible issue number (${branchRefs.join(\", \")}) but no closing keyword/URL was found in title/body — treat this as a traceability candidate, not a closing claim: confirm it maps to a tracked issue before mapping any acceptance criteria.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (keywordHits > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Closing keyword(s) present but no issue number parsed — investigate whether a tracked issue is linked another way before treating this as having no acceptance criteria to honour.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`No closing reference, issue URL, or branch-encoded number found in title/body/branch — confirm there is genuinely no linked tracker issue before concluding there are no acceptance criteria to honour.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\n\tif (linked === null) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`No linked_work_items.json was projected into context — the linked issue''s body and its acceptance-criteria block are NOT visible here, so this practice cannot be assessed from the diff alone.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (linkedIssueBodyPresent === true) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Candidate issue facts: bodyPresent=true, acceptanceCriteriaBlockPresent=${hasCheckableAcBlock} (heading=${acHeading}, checkboxes=${acBoxes}) — these are text facts, not applicability. Confirm an authored closing claim in the original mention context before mapping criteria to done or deferred.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Linked-issue context exists but carries no issue body — there is no quotable acceptance-criteria text to map the change against.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: [],\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tissueReferenceSyntaxCandidateCount: closingRefs.length,\n\t\t\thasIssueReferenceSyntaxCandidate: hasClosingRef ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tbranchRefCount: branchRefs.length,\n\t\t\thasBranchRef: hasBranchRef ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tclosingKeywordHits: keywordHits,\n\t\t\tlinkedItemsFilePresent: linked === null ? 0 : 1,\n\t\t\tlinkedIssueCount: linked?.length ?? 0,\n\t\t\tlinkedIssueBodyPresent: linkedIssueBodyPresent === true ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tacceptanceCriteriaBlockPresent: hasCheckableAcBlock ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tacceptanceCriteriaCheckboxes: acBoxes,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"When you close an issue, everyone assumes its whole checklist is done. A quick note on what you finished and what you left for later spares your reviewer from re-checking each item, and keeps the unfinished work from quietly disappearing.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A line in the description such as ''Closes #57: login and reset are done here, the rate-limit item moves to #61'', so the change can be checked against its own definition of done.","groupSlug":"review-ready-work","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 212\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Closes only</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"150.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">PR</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Closes #57</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"16.75\" y=\"104.75\" width=\"182.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"28\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">Criteria unmentioned</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Assumed done</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Notes each item</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"150.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">PR</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Closes #57</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"352.75\" y=\"104.75\" width=\"14.5\" height=\"14.5\" rx=\"3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M356 112.5l3 3 5.5-6\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">Login</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"352.75\" y=\"128.75\" width=\"14.5\" height=\"14.5\" rx=\"3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M356 136.5l3 3 5.5-6\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"141\" font-size=\"15\">Reset</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"15\">Rate limit deferred to #61</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Nothing dropped</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A pull request says “Closes #57” with its criteria unmentioned, so all are assumed done. A pull request that also notes Login and Reset as done and “Rate limit deferred to #61” lets nothing get dropped."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, find the acceptance criteria in the issue that your change closes. They can be a checklist, a list of musts, or “Given …, when …, then …” steps.\n- In the description, after the closing reference, name each criterion that this change finishes. For example, write “Fixes #212. Done here: CSV export and the download button. Deferred to #215: PDF export.” instead of “Fixes #212”.\n- Say that each criterion you leave for later is deferred. Add its follow-up issue if it has one.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change does not say that it closes an issue.\n- The issue that it closes has no criteria that you can check. It has only a title or a user story such as “As a …, I want …”. Or its text is prose with no list, checklist or acceptance section.\n- An issue looks related, but your change does not name it.\n- The issue number shows up only in an example, a template or code, and it names other work.\n\nIt still applies when the provider links the issue to close on merge, even if the description does not say so.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that you must copy the issue’s checklist. A checklist is one way. Plain sentences that say which criteria are done and which are deferred work just as well.\n- Ticking the boxes of the description template, such as a passing lint or added screenshots, and stopping there. Those boxes are about the form of the change. Instead, match the change to the criteria in the issue.\n- Writing only the closing reference, or repeating the issue title. The issue then closes as if every item were done. Instead, state which criteria are done here and which are deferred.\n- Leaving an open item out of the description. It disappears when the issue closes. Instead, say that it is deferred. Add its follow-up issue if it has one.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Linking a pull request to an issue](https://docs.github.com/en/issues/tracking-your-work-with-issues/using-issues/linking-a-pull-request-to-an-issue), GitHub Docs. A closing keyword such as “Closes” closes the issue when the pull request merges into the default branch. A link from the side panel does too.\n- [Managing issues](https://docs.gitlab.com/user/project/issues/managing_issues/), GitLab Docs. A closing pattern in a merge request closes the issue on merge.\n- [The Scrum Guide](https://scrumguides.org/scrum-guide.html), Ken Schwaber and Jeff Sutherland, 2020. An item that does not meet the Definition of Done returns to the Product Backlog for future consideration.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Say which acceptance criteria are done","signals":["scm.pull_request.merged","scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.linked-work-items","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: a pull request that closes a tracked work item with checkable acceptance criteria says which of those criteria it satisfies and which it defers.\n\n## The standard\nJudge ONE thing: when a change CLOSES a tracked work item that carries checkable acceptance criteria — a Definition-of-Done or acceptance-criteria heading, a task list, a Given-When-Then block, an enumerated must/should list — can a reviewer tell from the pull request itself which criteria this change satisfies and which are deferred? A good handoff maps the change to those criteria: \"Closes #57: login and reset done here; rate-limit deferred to #61\", or a checked task list mirroring the item''s entries, so the change can be confirmed against its own done-list without reverse-engineering. This is a delivery and traceability practice about how the change is handed off — never a code-defect claim and never an assertion that a criterion is unmet.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. The occasion exists only when the authored handoff claims to close a tracked item that carries a quotable, checkable criterion. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name what ruled it out, and stop when the linked item you could read carries no closing entry with a checkable criterion: no closing reference at all, or an item that states no checkable criterion — a title alone, a user story (\"As a …, I want …\"), or prose with no list, checklist or acceptance section is not a checkable criterion. A candidate item alone does not open the occasion, and neither does a body that references an item without a checkable criterion. How an item is referenced — a closing keyword, a bare mention, a branch number — is a syntax fact, not adoption: inspect the exact mention in context, since examples, templates and code may name unrelated work.\n\n## Sources\n1. The description (`context/description.md`) and the title (`context/metadata.json`): the closing reference and any done/deferred mapping — a checked task list, a Given-When-Then or DoD block, a sentence naming what is done and what moves elsewhere. They establish what the handoff says. They never establish whether a criterion is met.\n2. The source branch (`context/metadata.json`) and the commits (`context/commits.json`): further places a work-item number may appear. They establish how the item is referenced, nothing more.\n3. Each linked item (`context/linked_work_items/<n>.md`: number, title, url, state, labels, body; `how` in `linked_work_items.json`): the criteria block to map against. It establishes whether a checkable criterion exists and its exact wording. It never establishes that the pull request adopted it; the closing reference in the text or the provider''s own link (`how: closesOnMerge`) does that.\n4. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the touched-file set, read only to ground a heavier severity. It never establishes that a criterion is met or unmet.\n5. The inventory (`context/project_inventory.json`): open it and state either the linked or closing issue you found, quoting its `#number \"title\" (state)`, or that you scanned the inventory and found none.\nIf the linked items were not captured, you cannot tell whether there was a done-list to map to: when the body carries a closing reference but no mapping and the item''s criteria are unreadable, report a collection gap and record no observation. The MET path does not depend on that file: a body that carries a closing reference together with an explicit checked task list, Given-When-Then or DoD block mapping the change to enumerated criteria is judged from the body alone.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: the closing pull request maps the change to the item''s criteria — stating what is done here and what is deferred. The mapping may be implicit: a body whose statements name the substance of each criterion as done (or deferred) honours it, and a checklist that copies the criteria is a mapping; a checklist format is never required. A template''s own Definition of Done — items about the merge request''s form (title format, lint, a local build, screenshots) rather than the item''s criteria — maps nothing, however many of its boxes are ticked. Quote the closing reference and the criteria span.\n- NOT_MET: a quotable closing reference to an item with a checkable criteria block, and the body neither names the substance of the criteria as done nor defers them — a restatement of the title, an empty template, or a body that speaks of something else. Frame it as a forward-looking handoff request — \"state which of #N''s criteria this satisfies and which are deferred\" — never as a failure.\n- UNDETERMINED: the captured text leaves genuinely open whether the mention closes the item; name the question that would settle it. Having read the body and the item and found no mapping is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review. Use your judgment as an experienced reviewer for cases not enumerated here.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: a single nudge to state coverage. MAJOR only when, having actually scanned the touched-file set in the diff, you can ground a plausible gap: a concrete quoted criterion has no corresponding surface in the touched files — the item requires an error state and the diff adds only the happy path — and the body does not mark it deferred. Phrase even this as \"state whether <criterion> is in scope here or deferred\". When you cannot ground a gap, stay MINOR. Identical inputs yield identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery observation quotes the work-item number, its closing reference, and the exact criterion span from the linked item''s body, citing the item''s url. For NOT_MET, the citations show the closing reference, the criteria block, and the body read through without a mapping. You have the diff and an excerpt, not the running system: never claim to have run, compiled or verified the change, and never assert a specific criterion is unmet, missing or broken — you point at a handoff gap, not at correctness. Never invent or demand a criterion the item never stated. Describe what the body says against what the item states; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the issue itself is well written belongs to the issue-authoring practices (`issue-has-checkable-outcome`); the pull request description in general belongs to `describe-what-and-why`; marking the change ready and linking its issue at all belongs to `ready-and-traceable-handoff`. Here only: does the closing change say which stated criteria it covers?","precomputeScript":"// Surface linked-issue references and acceptance-criteria blocks, not compliance verdicts.\nimport { readContextJson } from \"../lib/context.ts\";\nimport { isJsonObject } from \"../lib/practice-contract.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nconst CLOSE_KEYWORD = /\\b(?:close[sd]?|fix(?:e[sd])?|resolve[sd]?)\\b/giu;\n// \"closes #12\", \"fixes GH-12\", \"resolves group/proj#12\" — capture the trailing issue number.\nconst CLOSE_REF =\n\t/\\b(?:close[sd]?|fix(?:e[sd])?|resolve[sd]?)\\s*:?\\s+(?:[\\w./~-]*[#!]|GH-)(?<number>\\d+)/giu;\n// Full issue/MR URLs on either host: .../issues/12, .../-/issues/12.\nconst ISSUE_URL = /https?:\\/\\/[^\\s)]+?\\/(?:-\\/)?issues\\/(?<number>\\d+)/giu;\n// Issue number embedded in a branch name: feature/123-foo, issue-123, 123-fix-thing, bugfix/GH-123.\nconst BRANCH_REF = /(?:^|[/_-])(?:issue[-_]?|gh[-_]?|#)?(?<number>\\d{1,6})(?:[-_/]|$)/giu;\n\n// Every pattern below is global and captures the issue number in group 1; matchAll iterates a clone,\n// so the shared module-level regexes keep a lastIndex of 0 between calls.\nfunction collect(re: RegExp, text: string, into: Set<string>): void {\n\tif (!text) {\n\t\treturn;\n\t}\n\tre.lastIndex = 0;\n\tfor (const match of text.matchAll(re)) {\n\t\tinto.add(`#${match.groups?.number ?? \"\"}`);\n\t}\n}\n\ninterface LinkedWorkItem {\n\tnumber?: number | string;\n\tiid?: number | string;\n\ttitle?: string;\n\t// The SCM connector (LinkedWorkItemContentSource) emits the issue body under `body`; `description`\n\t// is a host-general fallback for other connectors.\n\tbody?: string;\n\tdescription?: string;\n}\n\nfunction optionalText(value: unknown): string | undefined {\n\treturn typeof value === \"string\" ? value : undefined;\n}\n\nfunction optionalRef(value: unknown): number | string | undefined {\n\treturn typeof value === \"number\" || typeof value === \"string\" ? value : undefined;\n}\n\n// An entry carrying none of the fields above still counts as a linked item: `linkedIssueCount` reports\n// how many items the connector linked, which is a fact about the tracker, not about how much of each\n// entry this side could read.\nfunction toLinkedWorkItem(entry: unknown): LinkedWorkItem {\n\tif (!isJsonObject(entry)) {\n\t\treturn {};\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\tnumber: optionalRef(entry.number),\n\t\tiid: optionalRef(entry.iid),\n\t\ttitle: optionalText(entry.title),\n\t\tbody: optionalText(entry.body),\n\t\tdescription: optionalText(entry.description),\n\t};\n}\n\n// Unwrap whatever shape linked_work_items.json carries into a flat item list (or null if not a usable object).\nfunction unwrapLinkedItems(data: unknown): LinkedWorkItem[] | null {\n\tif (Array.isArray(data)) {\n\t\treturn data.map(toLinkedWorkItem);\n\t}\n\tif (!isJsonObject(data)) {\n\t\treturn null;\n\t}\n\t// The SCM connector wraps items under `workItems`; check it FIRST, then host-general fallbacks.\n\tif (Array.isArray(data.workItems)) {\n\t\treturn data.workItems.map(toLinkedWorkItem);\n\t}\n\tif (Array.isArray(data.items)) {\n\t\treturn data.items.map(toLinkedWorkItem);\n\t}\n\treturn [toLinkedWorkItem(data)];\n}\n\n// A checkable acceptance-criteria artifact: an AC/DoD heading or a \"- [ ]\" checklist in the issue body.\nfunction acFacts(body: string): { heading: boolean; boxes: number } {\n\tconst heading =\n\t\t/(?:acceptance criteria|definition of done|\\bDoD\\b|done when|expected (?:outcome|result|behaviou?r))/iu.test(\n\t\t\tbody,\n\t\t);\n\tconst boxes = (body.match(/^[\\s>]*[-*]\\s+\\[[ xX]\\]/gmu) ?? []).length;\n\treturn { heading, boxes };\n}\n\nexport default async function honoursLinkedIssueAcceptanceCriteria(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\t_diff: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tmeta: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir?: string,\n) {\n\tconst title = meta.title ?? \"\";\n\tconst body = meta.body ?? \"\";\n\tconst branch = meta.source_branch;\n\n\t// Syntax matches are candidates, including examples and code spans. They establish neither a\n\t// provider-reported relationship nor adoption of another issue''s criteria by the author.\n\tconst refs = new Set<string>();\n\tcollect(CLOSE_REF, `${title}\\n${body}`, refs);\n\tcollect(ISSUE_URL, `${title}\\n${body}`, refs);\n\tconst closingRefs = [...refs];\n\tconst hasClosingRef = closingRefs.length > 0;\n\n\tconst branchRefSet = new Set<string>();\n\tcollect(BRANCH_REF, branch, branchRefSet);\n\tconst branchRefs = [...branchRefSet];\n\tconst hasBranchRef = branchRefs.length > 0;\n\n\tconst keywordHits = (`${title}\\n${body}`.match(CLOSE_KEYWORD) ?? []).length;\n\tCLOSE_KEYWORD.lastIndex = 0;\n\n\tconst linked = unwrapLinkedItems(await readContextJson(contextDir, \"linked_work_items.json\"));\n\tconst linkedIssueBodyPresent = linked?.some(\n\t\t(i) => (i.body ?? i.description ?? \"\").trim().length > 0,\n\t);\n\tlet acHeading = false;\n\tlet acBoxes = 0;\n\tif (linked) {\n\t\tfor (const i of linked) {\n\t\t\tconst f = acFacts((i.body ?? i.description ?? \"\").trim());\n\t\t\tacHeading ||= f.heading;\n\t\t\tacBoxes += f.boxes;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tconst hasCheckableAcBlock = acHeading || acBoxes > 0;\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (hasClosingRef) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Issue-reference syntax candidate(s) (${closingRefs.join(\", \")}) from title/body — inspect the exact mention in context, including templates/examples, before establishing that the author claims to close this issue. Only then map those criteria to the change.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (hasBranchRef) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Branch name encodes a possible issue number (${branchRefs.join(\", \")}) but no closing keyword/URL was found in title/body — treat this as a traceability candidate, not a closing claim: confirm it maps to a tracked issue before mapping any acceptance criteria.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (keywordHits > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Closing keyword(s) present but no issue number parsed — investigate whether a tracked issue is linked another way before treating this as having no acceptance criteria to honour.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`No closing reference, issue URL, or branch-encoded number found in title/body/branch — confirm there is genuinely no linked tracker issue before concluding there are no acceptance criteria to honour.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\n\tif (linked === null) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`No linked_work_items.json was projected into context — the linked issue''s body and its acceptance-criteria block are NOT visible here, so this practice cannot be assessed from the diff alone.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (linkedIssueBodyPresent === true) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Candidate issue facts: bodyPresent=true, acceptanceCriteriaBlockPresent=${hasCheckableAcBlock} (heading=${acHeading}, checkboxes=${acBoxes}) — these are text facts, not applicability. Confirm an authored closing claim in the original mention context before mapping criteria to done or deferred.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Linked-issue context exists but carries no issue body — there is no quotable acceptance-criteria text to map the change against.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: [],\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tissueReferenceSyntaxCandidateCount: closingRefs.length,\n\t\t\thasIssueReferenceSyntaxCandidate: hasClosingRef ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tbranchRefCount: branchRefs.length,\n\t\t\thasBranchRef: hasBranchRef ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tclosingKeywordHits: keywordHits,\n\t\t\tlinkedItemsFilePresent: linked === null ? 0 : 1,\n\t\t\tlinkedIssueCount: linked?.length ?? 0,\n\t\t\tlinkedIssueBodyPresent: linkedIssueBodyPresent === true ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tacceptanceCriteriaBlockPresent: hasCheckableAcBlock ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tacceptanceCriteriaCheckboxes: acBoxes,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"When you close an issue, everyone assumes its whole checklist is done. A quick note on what you finished and what you left for later spares your reviewer from re-checking each item, and keeps the unfinished work from quietly disappearing.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A line in the description such as ''Closes #57: login and reset are done here, the rate-limit item moves to #61'', so the change can be checked against its own definition of done.","groupSlug":"review-ready-work","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 212\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Closes only</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"150.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">PR</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Closes #57</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"16.75\" y=\"104.75\" width=\"182.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"28\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">Criteria unmentioned</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Assumed done</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Notes each item</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"150.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">PR</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Closes #57</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"352.75\" y=\"104.75\" width=\"14.5\" height=\"14.5\" rx=\"3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M356 112.5l3 3 5.5-6\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">Login</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"352.75\" y=\"128.75\" width=\"14.5\" height=\"14.5\" rx=\"3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M356 136.5l3 3 5.5-6\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"141\" font-size=\"15\">Reset</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"15\">Rate limit deferred to #61</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Nothing dropped</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A pull request says “Closes #57” with its criteria unmentioned, so all are assumed done. A pull request that also notes Login and Reset as done and “Rate limit deferred to #61” lets nothing get dropped."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, find the acceptance criteria in the issue that your change closes. They can be a checklist, a list of musts, or “Given …, when …, then …” steps.\n- In the description, after the closing reference, name each criterion that this change finishes. For example, write “Fixes #212. Done here: CSV export and the download button. Deferred to #215: PDF export.” instead of “Fixes #212”.\n- Say that each criterion you leave for later is deferred. Add its follow-up issue if it has one.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change does not say that it closes an issue.\n- The issue that it closes has no criteria that you can check. It has only a title or a user story such as “As a …, I want …”. Or its text is prose with no list, checklist or acceptance section.\n- An issue looks related, but your change does not name it.\n- The issue number shows up only in an example, a template or code, and it names other work.\n\nIt still applies when the provider links the issue to close on merge, even if the description does not say so.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that you must copy the issue’s checklist. A checklist is one way. Plain sentences that say which criteria are done and which are deferred work just as well.\n- Ticking the boxes of the description template, such as a passing lint or added screenshots, and stopping there. Those boxes are about the form of the change. Instead, match the change to the criteria in the issue.\n- Writing only the closing reference, or repeating the issue title. The issue then closes as if every item were done. Instead, state which criteria are done here and which are deferred.\n- Leaving an open item out of the description. It disappears when the issue closes. Instead, say that it is deferred. Add its follow-up issue if it has one.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Linking a pull request to an issue](https://docs.github.com/en/issues/tracking-your-work-with-issues/using-issues/linking-a-pull-request-to-an-issue), GitHub Docs. A closing keyword such as “Closes” closes the issue when the pull request merges into the default branch. A link from the side panel does too.\n- [Managing issues](https://docs.gitlab.com/user/project/issues/managing_issues/), GitLab Docs. A closing pattern in a merge request closes the issue on merge.\n- [The Scrum Guide](https://scrumguides.org/scrum-guide.html), Ken Schwaber and Jeff Sutherland, 2020. An item that does not meet the Definition of Done returns to the Product Backlog for future consideration.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Each change says which acceptance criteria it met"'::jsonb),'v5:f75d1ea3224ed7feadbd13d85d7c858348af2ff000db9de9f9132f559ce3a09e','practice:v3:2d15158d7ac0814e121884e6d88a2053defa43926ab4cebc1f26beab069f7784');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('GROUP','acting-on-review-feedback',NULL,'{"name":"Acting on review feedback","description":"Close the review loop. When a reviewer leaves a substantive comment on a line, answer it with a reply that names what changed or a follow-up commit that addresses it, and resolve the thread before merging. Feedback only improves the work once someone acts on it.","icon":"MessageSquareReply","color":"cyan"}'::jsonb,'{"name":"Acting on review feedback","description":"Close the review loop. When a reviewer leaves a substantive comment on a line, answer it with a reply that names what changed or a follow-up commit that addresses it, and resolve the thread before merging. Feedback only improves the work once someone acts on it.","icon":"MessageSquareReply","color":"cyan"}'::jsonb,'a30403316ef5e7e1f9c54c76a01eae7d0705f8a14404d8756b82b4347dea8d88','group:v1:a30403316ef5e7e1f9c54c76a01eae7d0705f8a14404d8756b82b4347dea8d88');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','engaging-with-inline-review-comments','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Respond to each review comment","signals":["scm.pull_request.merged","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":{"skipReason":"nobody left a review comment on this pull request","anyOf":[{"evidenceHasItems":"scm.review-threads"},{"evidenceHasItems":"scm.inline-review-comments"},{"evidenceHasItems":"scm.general-review-comments"}]},"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: the author visibly engages with each substantive reviewer request within the captured review record.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one practice: when a reviewer left a substantive inline or conversation comment — a question or a change request on this work — did the author visibly engage with it rather than leave it sitting silent? The standard is ENGAGEMENT, not agreement and not completeness. A loop is closed by any visible response: a reply that fixes it; a reply that declines with a stated reason; a clarifying question back; a pointer to a commit or SHA that addresses it; a thread the reviewer or the author marked resolved; a global \"Done\" or \"Addressed\" posted after the review batch, even unanchored and unresolved; a request about the pull request itself that the record shows carried out — the description, title or link edited as asked, or a draft marked ready, which a merge also shows since a draft cannot be merged; or the reviewer''s own later note saying the point was taken up (\"Thanks, all addressed\"). A bare later approval or \"Looks good\" says the reviewer accepted the work, not that the author engaged. Looks like a lapse but is not: resolved nitpicks followed by the reviewer''s \"Thanks, all addressed\", despite a leftover template header. Bad: a question or change request on this work with no reply, no resolution and no commit that plausibly addresses it — \"What does this attribute mean?\" still unanswered when the work merged.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Reviewer input is a note whose author login differs from the pull request author character-for-character; a note by the pull request author is never reviewer input, and the author gets no credit for replying to themselves. Treat a human''s login as a real reviewer whatever its shape (a numeric or token-like login is still a person on a provider that anonymises accounts). A comment the record flags `bot` (an access token, an app, a review service; this tool''s own notes are never in the record) is reviewer input when it asks something of the change: silence toward an automated finding is the same open loop. A note is substantive only when it asks something of this change. It asks nothing when it is praise, thanks, an acknowledgement (\"LGTM\", \"+1\", \"Looks good\"), an explanation, or help addressed to someone else; nor when it is advice the reviewer attached to their own approval — the same reviewer approved after it with no commit or author note between them — that defers itself to later work (\"for next iteration\", \"next time\", \"in general\", \"be mindful\") or explicitly leaves the choice to the author (\"optional\", \"non-blocking nitpick\"). Polite wording such as \"consider\" or \"if possible\" alone does not make a substantive request optional. Such a note asks something again when it makes the approval conditional (\"approving once X is fixed\") or asks a question. An author who wrote no note at all is not an identity problem: the author''s side is then the commits, the resolutions and the description. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when no note asks something of this change: a freshly opened pull request, or a reviewer''s \"Approved. Two ideas for next time: …\" posted with their approval. One exception: when the same login is recorded on a comment and on the reply that answers it (a known mirror artifact that collapses identities), you cannot tell whether a reviewer spoke at all — that is UNDETERMINED, never NOT_APPLICABLE. A reviewer login that differs from the author''s is never that case.\n\n## Sources\n1. The inline comments (`context/comments.json`: `path`, `line`, `body`, `author`, `created_at`, and `thread` and `in_reply_to` where the provider records them) and the threads (`context/review_threads.json`: each thread''s resolution state and who resolved it, and `reviewDecisions`: who approved and when). Scan the ENTIRE record for a later author note that answers a comment. The precomputed leads below these criteria list every note by someone other than the author, inline and conversation, one row each, with the author''s replies, the resolution, the reviewer''s later note, the reviewer''s approval after it and what came between, whether it came after the hand-off, and the commits after it: decide every row.\n2. The general comments (`context/general_comments.json`): conversation-level questions and change requests, and author replies posted outside a thread. Same partition, same scan.\n3. The pull request author and state (`context/metadata.json`): the login to partition by, the description as captured, and whether and when the work merged or closed.\n4. The commits (`context/commits.json`: each with its message, `committedAt` and the `files` it touched): a follow-up commit counts as uptake when it comes after the comment and touches the commented file, or when its message observably ties to the comment — a shared path or a shared concrete term. Uptake is judged on the request''s substance, not its letter. When a commit is the only candidate response, read its changed lines before crediting it: a later commit on the file says the file moved, not that the ask was taken up.\nA visible reply, resolution or fulfilled workflow request can settle engagement without commit history. When no such response is captured, unavailable commits cannot establish that no change addressed the request: report that collection gap and record no observation.\nAn image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing. When a substantive reviewer comment is visible but the facts that would settle its uptake are not, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: every substantive reviewer comment has a visible response of one of the kinds above; a resolution or the reviewer''s settling note counts whatever the head still shows. Quote the reply, the resolution marker, or the matching commit-message phrase.\n- NOT_MET: at least one substantive reviewer comment drew no response of any kind in the complete captured record while the work remains open, or was posted before the work merged or closed and remained unanswered. Name the single most consequential open thread, quote it, and point forward to replying in-thread or pushing a fix; a missing same-line reply, RESOLVED mark or commit reference alone is never the deciding clause.\n- UNDETERMINED: the collapsed-identity case above, or when the only substantive notes were posted after the work merged or closed and drew no reply here — their uptake would sit in later work this record does not carry. Name the question. A note posted before the hand-off with no response found anywhere is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, over all reviewer comments; an unanswered note posted after the hand-off is set aside when earlier notes decide.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this alone — a formative practice, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: an open question on still-open work. MAJOR: an ignored change request, or an open loop on work already handed off — merged or closed.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the reviewer comment in every cell. When you assert uptake, quote the reply, the resolution marker, the reviewer''s settling note or the commit-message phrase that ties to it; never infer uptake from a commit''s mere presence or timing. Never invent a reply, a resolution, or a clean bill you did not verify. Describe the state of the work — \"this thread is still open\" — never the person, and never impute intent or a causal outcome.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the resulting code is correct or the fix complete belongs to other practices. Merge timing and merging past unresolved threads belong to `merged-past-unresolved-review-threads`. Here only: was each substantive reviewer comment engaged with?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for engaging-with-inline-review-comments: every note by someone other than the\n// author, inline and in the conversation, one row each, with what the record shows beside it — the\n// author''s later reply, the thread''s resolution and who resolved it, the reviewer''s own later note,\n// whether and how soon the same reviewer approved afterwards, whether the note came after the work\n// merged or closed, and the authored commits that came after it. The review decides for each row\n// whether the note asked something of this change and whether the author engaged; the script never does.\nimport { type ChangeCommit, readCapturedCommits } from \"../lib/change.ts\";\nimport { contextFile } from \"../lib/context.ts\";\nimport {\n\ttype GeneralComment,\n\ttype ReviewDecision,\n\tchangeNear,\n\tcommitsAfter,\n\texcerpt,\n\tlater,\n\tmillisBetween,\n\treadGeneralComments,\n\treadReviewComments,\n\treadReviewThreads,\n\treviewerCommentRows,\n} from \"../lib/review.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n/** An instant strictly between two others; false when any is missing. */\nfunction between(\n\tat: string | undefined,\n\tfrom: string | undefined,\n\tto: string | undefined,\n): boolean {\n\treturn later(at, from) && !later(at, to);\n}\n\n/**\n * The same reviewer''s first approval at or after the note, with what came between them: advice posted\n * with an approval reads differently from an approval given after the author answered. Empty when that\n * reviewer did not approve afterwards.\n */\nfunction approvalAfter(\n\tdecisions: readonly ReviewDecision[],\n\treviewer: string | undefined,\n\tat: string | undefined,\n\tcommits: readonly ChangeCommit[] | null,\n\tauthorNotes: readonly { createdAt?: string }[],\n): string {\n\tconst approval = decisions\n\t\t.filter(\n\t\t\t(d) =>\n\t\t\t\td.state === \"APPROVED\" &&\n\t\t\t\td.dismissed !== true &&\n\t\t\t\td.author === reviewer &&\n\t\t\t\t(millisBetween(at, d.submittedAt) ?? -1) >= 0,\n\t\t)\n\t\t.toSorted((a, b) => Date.parse(a.submittedAt ?? \"\") - Date.parse(b.submittedAt ?? \"\"))[0];\n\tif (approval === undefined) {\n\t\treturn \"\";\n\t}\n\tconst seconds = Math.round((millisBetween(at, approval.submittedAt) ?? 0) / 1000);\n\tconst commitsBetween = (commits ?? []).filter(\n\t\t(c) => c.parents.length < 2 && between(c.committedAt, at, approval.submittedAt),\n\t).length;\n\tconst notesBetween = authorNotes.filter((n) =>\n\t\tbetween(n.createdAt, at, approval.submittedAt),\n\t).length;\n\treturn `${String(seconds)}s later; ${commits === null ? \"unknown commit count\" : `${String(commitsBetween)} commit(s)`} and ${String(notesBetween)} author note(s) between`;\n}\n\nexport default async function engagingWithInlineReviewComments(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tmetadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir: string | undefined,\n\t_changeDir: string | undefined,\n\tcontextReference: string,\n) {\n\tconst author = metadata.author ?? \"\";\n\tconst inline = await readReviewComments(contextDir);\n\tconst general = await readGeneralComments(contextDir);\n\tconst record = await readReviewThreads(contextDir);\n\tconst capturedCommits = await readCapturedCommits(contextDir);\n\tconst commits = capturedCommits ?? [];\n\tconst decisions = record?.decisions ?? [];\n\tconst handOff = metadata.merged_at ?? metadata.closed_at;\n\tconst byOthers = (note: { author?: string }) =>\n\t\tnote.author !== undefined && note.author !== author;\n\tconst authorNotes = [\n\t\t...(inline ?? []).filter((c) => c.author === author),\n\t\t...(general ?? []).filter((c) => c.author === author),\n\t];\n\t/** The reviewer''s next conversation note after `at`, where a settling \"thank you\" usually sits. */\n\tconst reviewerNextNote = (reviewer: string | undefined, at: string | undefined) =>\n\t\t(general ?? []).find((c) => c.author === reviewer && later(c.createdAt, at))?.body ?? \"\";\n\n\tconst inlineHints: Hint[] = reviewerCommentRows(inline ?? [], record?.threads ?? [], author).map(\n\t\t({ comment, replies, followUps, thread }) => ({\n\t\t\tfile: comment.path,\n\t\t\tline: comment.line ?? 0,\n\t\t\tpattern: \"reviewer comment\",\n\t\t\tcontext: excerpt(comment.body),\n\t\t\tinDiff: false,\n\t\t\tflags: {\n\t\t\t\tby: comment.author ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\tbot: comment.bot,\n\t\t\t\tat: comment.createdAt ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\tside: comment.side ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\toutdated: comment.outdated,\n\t\t\t\tauthorReplied: replies.length > 0,\n\t\t\t\treplyExcerpt: excerpt(replies.map((r) => r.body).join(\" \"), 80),\n\t\t\t\tthreadResolved: thread?.state === \"RESOLVED\",\n\t\t\t\tresolvedBy: thread?.resolvedBy ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\treviewerLaterNote: excerpt(\n\t\t\t\t\tfollowUps.find((f) => f.author === comment.author)?.body ??\n\t\t\t\t\t\treviewerNextNote(comment.author, comment.createdAt),\n\t\t\t\t\t80,\n\t\t\t\t),\n\t\t\t\treviewerApprovedAfter: approvalAfter(\n\t\t\t\t\tdecisions,\n\t\t\t\t\tcomment.author,\n\t\t\t\t\tcomment.createdAt,\n\t\t\t\t\tcapturedCommits,\n\t\t\t\t\tauthorNotes,\n\t\t\t\t),\n\t\t\t\tafterHandOff: later(comment.createdAt, handOff),\n\t\t\t\tfileInChange: diffFiles.has(comment.path),\n\t\t\t\tchangeNearLine: changeNear(diffFiles.get(comment.path), comment.line),\n\t\t\t\tcommitsAfter:\n\t\t\t\t\tcapturedCommits === null ? \"unknown\" : commitsAfter(commits, comment.createdAt),\n\t\t\t\tcommitsAfterTouchingFile:\n\t\t\t\t\tcapturedCommits === null\n\t\t\t\t\t\t? \"unknown\"\n\t\t\t\t\t\t: commitsAfter(commits, comment.createdAt, comment.path),\n\t\t\t},\n\t\t}),\n\t);\n\tconst conversationHints: Hint[] = (general ?? []).filter(byOthers).map((note: GeneralComment) => {\n\t\tconst authorAfter = (general ?? []).filter(\n\t\t\t(c) => c.author === author && later(c.createdAt, note.createdAt),\n\t\t);\n\t\treturn {\n\t\t\tfile: contextFile(contextReference, \"general_comments.json\"),\n\t\t\tline: 0,\n\t\t\tpattern: \"conversation comment\",\n\t\t\tcontext: excerpt(note.body),\n\t\t\tinDiff: false,\n\t\t\tflags: {\n\t\t\t\tby: note.author ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\tbot: note.bot,\n\t\t\t\tat: note.createdAt ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\tauthorNotesAfter: authorAfter.length,\n\t\t\t\tauthorNoteExcerpt: excerpt(authorAfter[0]?.body ?? \"\", 80),\n\t\t\t\treviewerLaterNote: excerpt(reviewerNextNote(note.author, note.createdAt), 80),\n\t\t\t\treviewerApprovedAfter: approvalAfter(\n\t\t\t\t\tdecisions,\n\t\t\t\t\tnote.author,\n\t\t\t\t\tnote.createdAt,\n\t\t\t\t\tcapturedCommits,\n\t\t\t\t\tauthorNotes,\n\t\t\t\t),\n\t\t\t\tafterHandOff: later(note.createdAt, handOff),\n\t\t\t\tcommitsAfter: capturedCommits === null ? \"unknown\" : commitsAfter(commits, note.createdAt),\n\t\t\t},\n\t\t};\n\t});\n\tconst hints = [...inlineHints, ...conversationHints];\n\tconst byPeople = hints.filter((h) => h.flags.bot !== true);\n\tconst replied = byPeople.filter(\n\t\t(h) => h.flags.authorReplied === true || Number(h.flags.authorNotesAfter) > 0,\n\t).length;\n\t// Without the commit record each note''s count is unknown, and so is their sum: never a measured 0.\n\tconst committedAfter =\n\t\tcapturedCommits === null\n\t\t\t? null\n\t\t\t: byPeople.filter((h) => Number(h.flags.commitsAfterTouchingFile) > 0).length;\n\tconst afterHandOff = byPeople.filter((h) => h.flags.afterHandOff === true).length;\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (inline === null && general === null) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\"No comments file was captured (neither comments.json nor general_comments.json): the record of reviewer notes is not available here.\",\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (hints.length === 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`No note by anyone other than the author in the captured record (${inline === null ? \"comments.json not captured\" : `${String(inline.length)} inline comment(s)`}, ${general === null ? \"general_comments.json not captured\" : `${String(general.length)} conversation comment(s)`}).`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tconst bots = hints.length - byPeople.length;\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`${String(byPeople.length)} reviewer note(s) (${String(inlineHints.filter((h) => h.flags.bot !== true).length)} inline, ${String(conversationHints.filter((h) => h.flags.bot !== true).length)} conversation)${bots > 0 ? `, ${String(bots)} more by bots` : \"\"}; ${String(replied)} have a later author note; ${committedAfter === null ? \"how many have a later authored commit touching the commented file is unknown\" : `${String(committedAfter)} have a later authored commit touching the commented file`}; ${String(afterHandOff)} were posted after the work merged or closed. Commit capture: ${capturedCommits === null ? \"unavailable\" : `${String(commits.length)} captured commit(s)`} in ${contextFile(contextReference, \"commits.json\")}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tcommentsFileAbsent: inline === null ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tgeneralCommentsFileAbsent: general === null ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\treviewerComments: inlineHints.filter((h) => h.flags.bot !== true).length,\n\t\t\tconversationComments: conversationHints.filter((h) => h.flags.bot !== true).length,\n\t\t\tbotComments: hints.length - byPeople.length,\n\t\t\twithAuthorReply: replied,\n\t\t\t...(committedAfter === null ? {} : { withLaterCommitOnFile: committedAfter }),\n\t\t\tafterHandOff,\n\t\t\tcommitsFileAbsent: capturedCommits === null ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A reviewer spent real attention pointing at a specific line. When the thread just sits there, they cannot tell whether you agreed, fixed it, or missed it, and they will hesitate before putting in that effort again.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Every reviewer comment gets a visible response, either a follow-up commit that addresses it or a short reply like ''Good catch, switched to the shared client in a2b1c3'', so no thread sits unanswered.","groupSlug":"acting-on-review-feedback","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 104\">\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer asks</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M136 52h20v-32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M192 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"0.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"216\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">No response</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M424 20h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M448 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer guesses</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M156 52v32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M192 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-accent-soft pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"64.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"216\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">Fixed in <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">a2b1c3</tspan></text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M424 84h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M448 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer knows</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A reviewer asks something. With no response, the reviewer guesses what happened. With a visible response such as “Fixed in a2b1c3”, the reviewer knows."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Address each question or change request from a reviewer in a way that they can see. For example, instead of leaving “What does this attribute mean?” unanswered, reply with what it means.\n- Choose a response that fits: a fix, a link to an issue for later work, or a question back.\n- If you reply after a fix, name the commit, such as “Done, renamed it to `retryLimit` in 4f2e9a1”.\n- After many small fixes in one push, post one note such as “Addressed all comments in 4f2e9a1”.\n- When you do not agree, reply with your reason.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Nobody else has left a comment yet, such as on a pull or merge request that you just opened.\n- The comment is your own. Only comments from other people ask something of you.\n- The comment asks nothing of the change. Examples are praise, thanks, “LGTM”, “+1”, an explanation, or help for someone else.\n- The reviewer approved with advice for later work, such as “next time”. The same goes for a choice they left to you, such as “optional”.\n\nIt still applies when a request uses polite words, such as “consider” or “if possible”, or when an approval waits on a fix. It also applies when advice asks a question, or when a review bot asks for a change.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that you must agree with every comment. You only need to respond, and a reason to decline is a response.\n- Taking the reviewer’s approval as your answer. An approval shows that they accept the work, not that you answered them. Answer each open question.\n- Counting any later commit on the file as your answer. A commit can touch the file and still miss the point. Instead, make the change that was asked, or reply with what changed.\n- Leaving a comment from a review bot without a response. Fix the point, or say why it does not apply.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Handling reviewer comments](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/developer/handling-comments.html), Google Engineering Practices. Ask the reviewer when you do not understand a comment, and explain your reasons when you disagree.\n- [Respectful changes](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/HEAD/docs/cl_respect.md), Chromium project. Answer each question, and adopt, defer, or push back on each suggestion with more information.\n- [Jetpack code review etiquette](https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/support/+/refs/heads/androidx-main/code-review.md), AndroidX project. Follow up on every comment, with the fix or with your reason for not making it.\n- [An empirical study of overlooked code review comments in OSS projects](https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2026.3681776), Li and others, 2026. In 80 Java projects, about 7.5% of pull requests had a comment with no reply and no fix. They took longer to review and were accepted less often.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Respond to each review comment","signals":["scm.pull_request.merged","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":{"skipReason":"nobody left a review comment on this pull request","anyOf":[{"evidenceHasItems":"scm.review-threads"},{"evidenceHasItems":"scm.inline-review-comments"},{"evidenceHasItems":"scm.general-review-comments"}]},"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: the author visibly engages with each substantive reviewer request within the captured review record.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one practice: when a reviewer left a substantive inline or conversation comment — a question or a change request on this work — did the author visibly engage with it rather than leave it sitting silent? The standard is ENGAGEMENT, not agreement and not completeness. A loop is closed by any visible response: a reply that fixes it; a reply that declines with a stated reason; a clarifying question back; a pointer to a commit or SHA that addresses it; a thread the reviewer or the author marked resolved; a global \"Done\" or \"Addressed\" posted after the review batch, even unanchored and unresolved; a request about the pull request itself that the record shows carried out — the description, title or link edited as asked, or a draft marked ready, which a merge also shows since a draft cannot be merged; or the reviewer''s own later note saying the point was taken up (\"Thanks, all addressed\"). A bare later approval or \"Looks good\" says the reviewer accepted the work, not that the author engaged. Looks like a lapse but is not: resolved nitpicks followed by the reviewer''s \"Thanks, all addressed\", despite a leftover template header. Bad: a question or change request on this work with no reply, no resolution and no commit that plausibly addresses it — \"What does this attribute mean?\" still unanswered when the work merged.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Reviewer input is a note whose author login differs from the pull request author character-for-character; a note by the pull request author is never reviewer input, and the author gets no credit for replying to themselves. Treat a human''s login as a real reviewer whatever its shape (a numeric or token-like login is still a person on a provider that anonymises accounts). A comment the record flags `bot` (an access token, an app, a review service; this tool''s own notes are never in the record) is reviewer input when it asks something of the change: silence toward an automated finding is the same open loop. A note is substantive only when it asks something of this change. It asks nothing when it is praise, thanks, an acknowledgement (\"LGTM\", \"+1\", \"Looks good\"), an explanation, or help addressed to someone else; nor when it is advice the reviewer attached to their own approval — the same reviewer approved after it with no commit or author note between them — that defers itself to later work (\"for next iteration\", \"next time\", \"in general\", \"be mindful\") or explicitly leaves the choice to the author (\"optional\", \"non-blocking nitpick\"). Polite wording such as \"consider\" or \"if possible\" alone does not make a substantive request optional. Such a note asks something again when it makes the approval conditional (\"approving once X is fixed\") or asks a question. An author who wrote no note at all is not an identity problem: the author''s side is then the commits, the resolutions and the description. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when no note asks something of this change: a freshly opened pull request, or a reviewer''s \"Approved. Two ideas for next time: …\" posted with their approval. One exception: when the same login is recorded on a comment and on the reply that answers it (a known mirror artifact that collapses identities), you cannot tell whether a reviewer spoke at all — that is UNDETERMINED, never NOT_APPLICABLE. A reviewer login that differs from the author''s is never that case.\n\n## Sources\n1. The inline comments (`context/comments.json`: `path`, `line`, `body`, `author`, `created_at`, and `thread` and `in_reply_to` where the provider records them) and the threads (`context/review_threads.json`: each thread''s resolution state and who resolved it, and `reviewDecisions`: who approved and when). Scan the ENTIRE record for a later author note that answers a comment. The precomputed leads below these criteria list every note by someone other than the author, inline and conversation, one row each, with the author''s replies, the resolution, the reviewer''s later note, the reviewer''s approval after it and what came between, whether it came after the hand-off, and the commits after it: decide every row.\n2. The general comments (`context/general_comments.json`): conversation-level questions and change requests, and author replies posted outside a thread. Same partition, same scan.\n3. The pull request author and state (`context/metadata.json`): the login to partition by, the description as captured, and whether and when the work merged or closed.\n4. The commits (`context/commits.json`: each with its message, `committedAt` and the `files` it touched): a follow-up commit counts as uptake when it comes after the comment and touches the commented file, or when its message observably ties to the comment — a shared path or a shared concrete term. Uptake is judged on the request''s substance, not its letter. When a commit is the only candidate response, read its changed lines before crediting it: a later commit on the file says the file moved, not that the ask was taken up.\nA visible reply, resolution or fulfilled workflow request can settle engagement without commit history. When no such response is captured, unavailable commits cannot establish that no change addressed the request: report that collection gap and record no observation.\nAn image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing. When a substantive reviewer comment is visible but the facts that would settle its uptake are not, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: every substantive reviewer comment has a visible response of one of the kinds above; a resolution or the reviewer''s settling note counts whatever the head still shows. Quote the reply, the resolution marker, or the matching commit-message phrase.\n- NOT_MET: at least one substantive reviewer comment drew no response of any kind in the complete captured record while the work remains open, or was posted before the work merged or closed and remained unanswered. Name the single most consequential open thread, quote it, and point forward to replying in-thread or pushing a fix; a missing same-line reply, RESOLVED mark or commit reference alone is never the deciding clause.\n- UNDETERMINED: the collapsed-identity case above, or when the only substantive notes were posted after the work merged or closed and drew no reply here — their uptake would sit in later work this record does not carry. Name the question. A note posted before the hand-off with no response found anywhere is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, over all reviewer comments; an unanswered note posted after the hand-off is set aside when earlier notes decide.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this alone — a formative practice, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: an open question on still-open work. MAJOR: an ignored change request, or an open loop on work already handed off — merged or closed.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the reviewer comment in every cell. When you assert uptake, quote the reply, the resolution marker, the reviewer''s settling note or the commit-message phrase that ties to it; never infer uptake from a commit''s mere presence or timing. Never invent a reply, a resolution, or a clean bill you did not verify. Describe the state of the work — \"this thread is still open\" — never the person, and never impute intent or a causal outcome.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the resulting code is correct or the fix complete belongs to other practices. Merge timing and merging past unresolved threads belong to `merged-past-unresolved-review-threads`. Here only: was each substantive reviewer comment engaged with?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for engaging-with-inline-review-comments: every note by someone other than the\n// author, inline and in the conversation, one row each, with what the record shows beside it — the\n// author''s later reply, the thread''s resolution and who resolved it, the reviewer''s own later note,\n// whether and how soon the same reviewer approved afterwards, whether the note came after the work\n// merged or closed, and the authored commits that came after it. The review decides for each row\n// whether the note asked something of this change and whether the author engaged; the script never does.\nimport { type ChangeCommit, readCapturedCommits } from \"../lib/change.ts\";\nimport { contextFile } from \"../lib/context.ts\";\nimport {\n\ttype GeneralComment,\n\ttype ReviewDecision,\n\tchangeNear,\n\tcommitsAfter,\n\texcerpt,\n\tlater,\n\tmillisBetween,\n\treadGeneralComments,\n\treadReviewComments,\n\treadReviewThreads,\n\treviewerCommentRows,\n} from \"../lib/review.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n/** An instant strictly between two others; false when any is missing. */\nfunction between(\n\tat: string | undefined,\n\tfrom: string | undefined,\n\tto: string | undefined,\n): boolean {\n\treturn later(at, from) && !later(at, to);\n}\n\n/**\n * The same reviewer''s first approval at or after the note, with what came between them: advice posted\n * with an approval reads differently from an approval given after the author answered. Empty when that\n * reviewer did not approve afterwards.\n */\nfunction approvalAfter(\n\tdecisions: readonly ReviewDecision[],\n\treviewer: string | undefined,\n\tat: string | undefined,\n\tcommits: readonly ChangeCommit[] | null,\n\tauthorNotes: readonly { createdAt?: string }[],\n): string {\n\tconst approval = decisions\n\t\t.filter(\n\t\t\t(d) =>\n\t\t\t\td.state === \"APPROVED\" &&\n\t\t\t\td.dismissed !== true &&\n\t\t\t\td.author === reviewer &&\n\t\t\t\t(millisBetween(at, d.submittedAt) ?? -1) >= 0,\n\t\t)\n\t\t.toSorted((a, b) => Date.parse(a.submittedAt ?? \"\") - Date.parse(b.submittedAt ?? \"\"))[0];\n\tif (approval === undefined) {\n\t\treturn \"\";\n\t}\n\tconst seconds = Math.round((millisBetween(at, approval.submittedAt) ?? 0) / 1000);\n\tconst commitsBetween = (commits ?? []).filter(\n\t\t(c) => c.parents.length < 2 && between(c.committedAt, at, approval.submittedAt),\n\t).length;\n\tconst notesBetween = authorNotes.filter((n) =>\n\t\tbetween(n.createdAt, at, approval.submittedAt),\n\t).length;\n\treturn `${String(seconds)}s later; ${commits === null ? \"unknown commit count\" : `${String(commitsBetween)} commit(s)`} and ${String(notesBetween)} author note(s) between`;\n}\n\nexport default async function engagingWithInlineReviewComments(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tmetadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir: string | undefined,\n\t_changeDir: string | undefined,\n\tcontextReference: string,\n) {\n\tconst author = metadata.author ?? \"\";\n\tconst inline = await readReviewComments(contextDir);\n\tconst general = await readGeneralComments(contextDir);\n\tconst record = await readReviewThreads(contextDir);\n\tconst capturedCommits = await readCapturedCommits(contextDir);\n\tconst commits = capturedCommits ?? [];\n\tconst decisions = record?.decisions ?? [];\n\tconst handOff = metadata.merged_at ?? metadata.closed_at;\n\tconst byOthers = (note: { author?: string }) =>\n\t\tnote.author !== undefined && note.author !== author;\n\tconst authorNotes = [\n\t\t...(inline ?? []).filter((c) => c.author === author),\n\t\t...(general ?? []).filter((c) => c.author === author),\n\t];\n\t/** The reviewer''s next conversation note after `at`, where a settling \"thank you\" usually sits. */\n\tconst reviewerNextNote = (reviewer: string | undefined, at: string | undefined) =>\n\t\t(general ?? []).find((c) => c.author === reviewer && later(c.createdAt, at))?.body ?? \"\";\n\n\tconst inlineHints: Hint[] = reviewerCommentRows(inline ?? [], record?.threads ?? [], author).map(\n\t\t({ comment, replies, followUps, thread }) => ({\n\t\t\tfile: comment.path,\n\t\t\tline: comment.line ?? 0,\n\t\t\tpattern: \"reviewer comment\",\n\t\t\tcontext: excerpt(comment.body),\n\t\t\tinDiff: false,\n\t\t\tflags: {\n\t\t\t\tby: comment.author ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\tbot: comment.bot,\n\t\t\t\tat: comment.createdAt ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\tside: comment.side ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\toutdated: comment.outdated,\n\t\t\t\tauthorReplied: replies.length > 0,\n\t\t\t\treplyExcerpt: excerpt(replies.map((r) => r.body).join(\" \"), 80),\n\t\t\t\tthreadResolved: thread?.state === \"RESOLVED\",\n\t\t\t\tresolvedBy: thread?.resolvedBy ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\treviewerLaterNote: excerpt(\n\t\t\t\t\tfollowUps.find((f) => f.author === comment.author)?.body ??\n\t\t\t\t\t\treviewerNextNote(comment.author, comment.createdAt),\n\t\t\t\t\t80,\n\t\t\t\t),\n\t\t\t\treviewerApprovedAfter: approvalAfter(\n\t\t\t\t\tdecisions,\n\t\t\t\t\tcomment.author,\n\t\t\t\t\tcomment.createdAt,\n\t\t\t\t\tcapturedCommits,\n\t\t\t\t\tauthorNotes,\n\t\t\t\t),\n\t\t\t\tafterHandOff: later(comment.createdAt, handOff),\n\t\t\t\tfileInChange: diffFiles.has(comment.path),\n\t\t\t\tchangeNearLine: changeNear(diffFiles.get(comment.path), comment.line),\n\t\t\t\tcommitsAfter:\n\t\t\t\t\tcapturedCommits === null ? \"unknown\" : commitsAfter(commits, comment.createdAt),\n\t\t\t\tcommitsAfterTouchingFile:\n\t\t\t\t\tcapturedCommits === null\n\t\t\t\t\t\t? \"unknown\"\n\t\t\t\t\t\t: commitsAfter(commits, comment.createdAt, comment.path),\n\t\t\t},\n\t\t}),\n\t);\n\tconst conversationHints: Hint[] = (general ?? []).filter(byOthers).map((note: GeneralComment) => {\n\t\tconst authorAfter = (general ?? []).filter(\n\t\t\t(c) => c.author === author && later(c.createdAt, note.createdAt),\n\t\t);\n\t\treturn {\n\t\t\tfile: contextFile(contextReference, \"general_comments.json\"),\n\t\t\tline: 0,\n\t\t\tpattern: \"conversation comment\",\n\t\t\tcontext: excerpt(note.body),\n\t\t\tinDiff: false,\n\t\t\tflags: {\n\t\t\t\tby: note.author ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\tbot: note.bot,\n\t\t\t\tat: note.createdAt ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\tauthorNotesAfter: authorAfter.length,\n\t\t\t\tauthorNoteExcerpt: excerpt(authorAfter[0]?.body ?? \"\", 80),\n\t\t\t\treviewerLaterNote: excerpt(reviewerNextNote(note.author, note.createdAt), 80),\n\t\t\t\treviewerApprovedAfter: approvalAfter(\n\t\t\t\t\tdecisions,\n\t\t\t\t\tnote.author,\n\t\t\t\t\tnote.createdAt,\n\t\t\t\t\tcapturedCommits,\n\t\t\t\t\tauthorNotes,\n\t\t\t\t),\n\t\t\t\tafterHandOff: later(note.createdAt, handOff),\n\t\t\t\tcommitsAfter: capturedCommits === null ? \"unknown\" : commitsAfter(commits, note.createdAt),\n\t\t\t},\n\t\t};\n\t});\n\tconst hints = [...inlineHints, ...conversationHints];\n\tconst byPeople = hints.filter((h) => h.flags.bot !== true);\n\tconst replied = byPeople.filter(\n\t\t(h) => h.flags.authorReplied === true || Number(h.flags.authorNotesAfter) > 0,\n\t).length;\n\t// Without the commit record each note''s count is unknown, and so is their sum: never a measured 0.\n\tconst committedAfter =\n\t\tcapturedCommits === null\n\t\t\t? null\n\t\t\t: byPeople.filter((h) => Number(h.flags.commitsAfterTouchingFile) > 0).length;\n\tconst afterHandOff = byPeople.filter((h) => h.flags.afterHandOff === true).length;\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (inline === null && general === null) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\"No comments file was captured (neither comments.json nor general_comments.json): the record of reviewer notes is not available here.\",\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (hints.length === 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`No note by anyone other than the author in the captured record (${inline === null ? \"comments.json not captured\" : `${String(inline.length)} inline comment(s)`}, ${general === null ? \"general_comments.json not captured\" : `${String(general.length)} conversation comment(s)`}).`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tconst bots = hints.length - byPeople.length;\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`${String(byPeople.length)} reviewer note(s) (${String(inlineHints.filter((h) => h.flags.bot !== true).length)} inline, ${String(conversationHints.filter((h) => h.flags.bot !== true).length)} conversation)${bots > 0 ? `, ${String(bots)} more by bots` : \"\"}; ${String(replied)} have a later author note; ${committedAfter === null ? \"how many have a later authored commit touching the commented file is unknown\" : `${String(committedAfter)} have a later authored commit touching the commented file`}; ${String(afterHandOff)} were posted after the work merged or closed. Commit capture: ${capturedCommits === null ? \"unavailable\" : `${String(commits.length)} captured commit(s)`} in ${contextFile(contextReference, \"commits.json\")}.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tcommentsFileAbsent: inline === null ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tgeneralCommentsFileAbsent: general === null ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\treviewerComments: inlineHints.filter((h) => h.flags.bot !== true).length,\n\t\t\tconversationComments: conversationHints.filter((h) => h.flags.bot !== true).length,\n\t\t\tbotComments: hints.length - byPeople.length,\n\t\t\twithAuthorReply: replied,\n\t\t\t...(committedAfter === null ? {} : { withLaterCommitOnFile: committedAfter }),\n\t\t\tafterHandOff,\n\t\t\tcommitsFileAbsent: capturedCommits === null ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A reviewer spent real attention pointing at a specific line. When the thread just sits there, they cannot tell whether you agreed, fixed it, or missed it, and they will hesitate before putting in that effort again.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Every reviewer comment gets a visible response, either a follow-up commit that addresses it or a short reply like ''Good catch, switched to the shared client in a2b1c3'', so no thread sits unanswered.","groupSlug":"acting-on-review-feedback","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 104\">\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer asks</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M136 52h20v-32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M192 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"0.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"216\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">No response</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M424 20h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M448 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer guesses</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M156 52v32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M192 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-accent-soft pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"64.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"216\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">Fixed in <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">a2b1c3</tspan></text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M424 84h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M448 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer knows</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A reviewer asks something. With no response, the reviewer guesses what happened. With a visible response such as “Fixed in a2b1c3”, the reviewer knows."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Address each question or change request from a reviewer in a way that they can see. For example, instead of leaving “What does this attribute mean?” unanswered, reply with what it means.\n- Choose a response that fits: a fix, a link to an issue for later work, or a question back.\n- If you reply after a fix, name the commit, such as “Done, renamed it to `retryLimit` in 4f2e9a1”.\n- After many small fixes in one push, post one note such as “Addressed all comments in 4f2e9a1”.\n- When you do not agree, reply with your reason.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Nobody else has left a comment yet, such as on a pull or merge request that you just opened.\n- The comment is your own. Only comments from other people ask something of you.\n- The comment asks nothing of the change. Examples are praise, thanks, “LGTM”, “+1”, an explanation, or help for someone else.\n- The reviewer approved with advice for later work, such as “next time”. The same goes for a choice they left to you, such as “optional”.\n\nIt still applies when a request uses polite words, such as “consider” or “if possible”, or when an approval waits on a fix. It also applies when advice asks a question, or when a review bot asks for a change.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that you must agree with every comment. You only need to respond, and a reason to decline is a response.\n- Taking the reviewer’s approval as your answer. An approval shows that they accept the work, not that you answered them. Answer each open question.\n- Counting any later commit on the file as your answer. A commit can touch the file and still miss the point. Instead, make the change that was asked, or reply with what changed.\n- Leaving a comment from a review bot without a response. Fix the point, or say why it does not apply.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Handling reviewer comments](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/developer/handling-comments.html), Google Engineering Practices. Ask the reviewer when you do not understand a comment, and explain your reasons when you disagree.\n- [Respectful changes](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/HEAD/docs/cl_respect.md), Chromium project. Answer each question, and adopt, defer, or push back on each suggestion with more information.\n- [Jetpack code review etiquette](https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/support/+/refs/heads/androidx-main/code-review.md), AndroidX project. Follow up on every comment, with the fix or with your reason for not making it.\n- [An empirical study of overlooked code review comments in OSS projects](https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2026.3681776), Li and others, 2026. In 80 Java projects, about 7.5% of pull requests had a comment with no reply and no fix. They took longer to review and were accepted less often.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Every reviewer comment gets a visible answer"'::jsonb),'v5:02de851e19e5c01cf0c78e8fe828779d029a62a7f59a3cefda8f47b33eb6f50f','practice:v3:f85d22618065eb8a5fbba77c80bb4e0ef204c93f25827dacfee6d515f80d313f');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','merged-past-unresolved-review-threads','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Resolve open threads before merging","signals":["scm.pull_request.merged"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: dated evidence establishes that standing reviewer requests and open threads were resolved or superseded before merge.\n\n## The standard\nAt the moment the change MERGED, was the review loop closed first — every reviewer''s gate either resolved or superseded — or did the change land while a reviewer''s request or an open thread still stood? This is a process practice about closing the loop before merge, never a claim that the merged code is wrong: the code may be perfectly correct and still have skipped the re-review a reviewer asked for. A later approval supersedes a change-request only if it occurred before the merge; a resolution after merge does not establish that the loop was closed beforehand. A gate is a formal request for changes (`CHANGES_REQUESTED`), a review thread not resolved, or a reviewer''s explicit request that the work not merge until something is done (\"do not merge until the crash is fixed\"), wherever it is written, until the same reviewer lifts it. Read what the reviewer asked of this merge, not the politeness of its wording. Advice for later work and a suggestion the reviewer plainly leaves to the author''s choice are no gate: whether such a comment was answered or taken up belongs to `engaging-with-inline-review-comments`. A thread resolved before the merge is closed, whatever it asked for.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. If the change did not merge, record NOT_APPLICABLE, name that fact, and stop: there is no merged loop to assess. NOT_APPLICABLE has no other ordinary case.\n\n## Sources\n1. The pull request record (`context/metadata.json`): `state`, and `merged_at` when the provider recorded it. It establishes that the change merged and when. It never establishes the review state.\n2. The review-decision and thread-resolution records (`context/review_threads.json`): each reviewer''s decision, each thread''s path, line, resolution and `resolvedAt` when the provider dated it, and their dates. They establish the review state at the merge timestamp when read as dated events: a thread whose `resolvedAt` is after `merged_at` was open at the merge, and the precomputed leads list it beside the never-resolved ones. A current decision or resolution field is a current-state fact, not automatically a historical one; a latest-state snapshot, a filename or a record count never by itself proves the historical record is complete.\n3. The discussion (`context/general_comments.json`, and the inline comments in `context/comments.json`, each carrying the `thread` it belongs to): dated comments that ask for the merge to wait, or explain a supersession or a resolution. They establish a blocking request a reviewer wrote, and why a gate was lifted, when dated. They never turn advice into a gate. The precomputed leads below these criteria list the merge — who, when — and one row per thread the record does not mark RESOLVED, with its `id`, anchor, opening time and whether it is outdated; it says whether the threads file was captured at all.\nThe diff (`work/change/diff.patch`) establishes nothing here: never use the code to infer a reviewer''s decision. If the change merged but `context/review_threads.json` is absent or truncated, the loop had some state at merge and you could not read it: report a collection gap and record no observation. Never treat a missing file as an empty loop.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context. Judge the captured gates reviewer by reviewer: each reviewer''s own requests and threads, settled or standing at `merged_at`. A reviewer''s standing approval dated before the merge supersedes that same reviewer''s earlier formal change-request decisions (`CHANGES_REQUESTED`), never another reviewer''s. Approval does not lift an explicit hold the reviewer retains or qualifies in the approval or accompanying discussion. A thread resolution dated before the merge closes that thread. `decisionHistoryComplete: false` warns that withdrawn or replaced decisions may be missing from the capture; it is not itself a gate, and it does not undo a dated closure.\n- NOT_MET: dated evidence shows a gate still standing at merge — a thread whose `resolvedAt` follows `merged_at`, a thread that nobody resolved (quote its path:line), or a reviewer''s change-request or explicit request to hold the merge that the record shows still stood then (name the reviewer, quote the decision or the request). Another reviewer''s approval never lifts it.\n- MET: every captured gate was settled before the merge, and the required sources were captured without truncation. State the evidence relied on: \"merged; reviewer X''s change-requests superseded by their approval dated before the merge; every thread resolved before the merge\". Record MET without claiming that the review history is exhaustive.\n- UNDETERMINED when a captured fact that decides the result is unknown — a change-request with no date that could precede or follow the merge; a latest approval or resolution with no date, so it cannot be placed before the merge; or one reviewer''s request beside another reviewer''s approval when the captured record cannot settle whether the requester''s gate was lifted or replaced before the merge. Name the missing fact. Never record UNDETERMINED for a gate the capture does not show. An approval''s time decides only whether it lifted a standing gate: with none, no approval time is needed. A thread whose `resolvedAt` precedes `merged_at` needs no further date, such as when a requested edit took effect.\nA missing or truncated required source is a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review, on the single at-merge question.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on loop closure alone — non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: a merely open thread with no standing request, the lighter lapse. MAJOR: landing over a reviewer''s still-standing request-changes gate, the heavier miss. Equivalent observed states get equivalent treatment.\n\n## Grounding\nFor NOT_MET, quote the deciding review evidence and the dated facts that show it still stood at the merge timestamp. For MET, cite the captured bounded review record — the merge timestamp, each captured decision and each thread''s resolution with dates — that shows none remained. Describe only the observable state: \"merged with N request-changes still standing; thread at <path:line> unresolved at merge\". Never \"you ignored the reviewer\" or \"you merged broken code\"; never a claim about the code''s correctness; never a current open or resolved state offered as proof of the state at merge.\n\n## Defer\nWhether threads on an OPEN pull request are being taken up belongs to `engaging-with-inline-review-comments`. Here only: was the loop closed at the moment of merge? Do not double-flag.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for merged-past-unresolved-review-threads: the merge as the record holds it —\n// who merged, when — and one row per review thread the record does not show resolved by the merge,\n// with where it is anchored, when it was opened and whether the provider calls it outdated. The review\n// reads each thread against the merge; the rows say what the record holds, not whether the merge was owed.\nimport {\n\tdated,\n\tmergeFacts,\n\tmergeRow,\n\treadReviewThreads,\n\tresolvedByMerge,\n\tunresolvedThreadRows,\n} from \"../lib/review.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default async function mergedPastUnresolvedReviewThreads(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\t_diffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tmetadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir: string | undefined,\n\t_changeDir: string | undefined,\n\tcontextReference: string,\n) {\n\tconst merge = mergeFacts(metadata);\n\tconst mergeUndated = merge.merged && !dated(merge.mergedAt);\n\tconst record = await readReviewThreads(contextDir);\n\tconst threads = record?.threads ?? [];\n\tconst unresolved = unresolvedThreadRows(threads, contextReference, merge);\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (!merge.merged) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\"The pull request is not merged: the occasion did not arise.\");\n\t} else if (record === null) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\"No review threads file was captured (review_threads.json): the thread record is not available here.\",\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Merged${merge.mergedBy === undefined ? \"\" : ` by ${merge.mergedBy}`}${merge.mergedByIsAuthor ? \" (the author)\" : \"\"}; ${threads.length} thread(s) captured, ${unresolved.length} candidate thread(s) — currently not marked RESOLVED, resolved after the merge, or resolved at a time the record does not establish — one row each.${mergeUndated ? \" The record gives no usable merge time, so no resolution is placed before or after the merge.\" : \"\"} These are not proven open at merge: compare opening and resolution times, and treat an unknown historical order as UNDETERMINED. Read each thread''s comments in comments.json by its id (the comments'' \\`thread\\`) before deciding what it asked.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: [mergeRow(metadata, merge, contextReference), ...unresolved],\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tmerged: merge.merged ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tmergedByIsAuthor: merge.mergedByIsAuthor ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tthreads: threads.length,\n\t\t\tcandidateThreads: unresolved.length,\n\t\t\tresolutionTimeUnknown: unresolved.filter(\n\t\t\t\t(h) =>\n\t\t\t\t\th.flags.state === \"RESOLVED\" &&\n\t\t\t\t\ttypeof h.flags.resolvedAt === \"string\" &&\n\t\t\t\t\t!dated(h.flags.resolvedAt),\n\t\t\t).length,\n\t\t\t// Undated, the merge places no resolution before it, so the count is left out rather than zero.\n\t\t\t...(mergeUndated\n\t\t\t\t? {}\n\t\t\t\t: {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tresolvedBeforeMerge: threads.filter(\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(t) => t.state === \"RESOLVED\" && resolvedByMerge(t, merge),\n\t\t\t\t\t\t).length,\n\t\t\t\t\t}),\n\t\t\tthreadsFileAbsent: record === null ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Merging while a reviewer still has changes requested skips the very check they asked for, and tells them their review did not count. It is also a quality signal, not just a courtesy: changes that land with little reviewer discussion tend to carry more defects that surface after release. Resolving the threads first keeps review worth doing.","whatGoodLooksLike":"By the time you merge, every open thread is resolved and anyone who requested changes has come back with an approval, so nothing lands over an objection.","groupSlug":"acting-on-review-feedback","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 200\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Merge first</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"44\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"12\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>,<path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M32 44h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>,<path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M216 44l-8-5v10z\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Review</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" cx=\"236\" cy=\"44\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"236\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>,<path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M256 44h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>,<path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M440 44l-8-5v10z\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"224\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Merge</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" cx=\"460\" cy=\"44\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>,,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"448\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Resolve threads</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Resolve first</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"160\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"12\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>,<path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M32 160h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>,<path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M216 160l-8-5v10z\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Review</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-accent\" cx=\"236\" cy=\"160\" r=\"12\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-surface\" x=\"236\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>,<path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M256 160h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>,<path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M440 160l-8-5v10z\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"224\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Resolve threads</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" cx=\"460\" cy=\"160\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>,,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"448\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Merge</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"Merge first: after the review, the work merges and its threads are resolved later. Resolve first: after the review, its threads are resolved, and then the work merges."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you merge, look at the pull or merge request for open threads and for reviewers who requested changes.\n- When a comment says “do not merge until the crash is fixed”, wait until its reviewer lifts it.\n- Resolve each thread before you merge.\n- When a reviewer requests changes, ask that reviewer to look again after your fix. For example, instead of merging right after you push the fix, wait for that reviewer to approve it.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The pull or merge request did not merge.\n\nIt still applies when the merged code is correct.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Merging because the code works. This practice is about the review, not about the code. Correct code can still skip the second look that a reviewer asked for. Instead, wait for their approval.\n- Resolving threads after you merge. A thread you resolve after the merge was still open when the work landed. Instead, resolve it first, then merge.\n- Treating every comment as a request to wait. A suggestion the reviewer leaves to you, or advice for later work, is not a request to hold the merge. Instead, look for what the reviewer asked of this merge, not how politely they wrote it. Still resolve its thread before you merge.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [About protected branches](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches), GitHub Docs. A repository can block a merge until every conversation is resolved. With required reviews, an admin who requests changes must approve before the merge.\n- [Comments and threads](https://docs.gitlab.com/user/discussions/), GitLab Docs. A project can block a merge until every thread is resolved.\n- [How to write code review comments](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/comments.html), Google Engineering Practices. Reviewers can label a comment as a nit, optional, or for later. Then the author can tell required changes from suggestions.\n- [The impact of code review coverage and code review participation on software quality](https://rebels.cs.uwaterloo.ca/confpaper/2014/05/30/the-impact-of-code-review-coverage-and-code-review-participation-on-software-quality.html), McIntosh and others, 2014. In Qt, VTK and ITK, components with low reviewer participation had more defects after release.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Resolve open threads before merging","signals":["scm.pull_request.merged"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: dated evidence establishes that standing reviewer requests and open threads were resolved or superseded before merge.\n\n## The standard\nAt the moment the change MERGED, was the review loop closed first — every reviewer''s gate either resolved or superseded — or did the change land while a reviewer''s request or an open thread still stood? This is a process practice about closing the loop before merge, never a claim that the merged code is wrong: the code may be perfectly correct and still have skipped the re-review a reviewer asked for. A later approval supersedes a change-request only if it occurred before the merge; a resolution after merge does not establish that the loop was closed beforehand. A gate is a formal request for changes (`CHANGES_REQUESTED`), a review thread not resolved, or a reviewer''s explicit request that the work not merge until something is done (\"do not merge until the crash is fixed\"), wherever it is written, until the same reviewer lifts it. Read what the reviewer asked of this merge, not the politeness of its wording. Advice for later work and a suggestion the reviewer plainly leaves to the author''s choice are no gate: whether such a comment was answered or taken up belongs to `engaging-with-inline-review-comments`. A thread resolved before the merge is closed, whatever it asked for.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. If the change did not merge, record NOT_APPLICABLE, name that fact, and stop: there is no merged loop to assess. NOT_APPLICABLE has no other ordinary case.\n\n## Sources\n1. The pull request record (`context/metadata.json`): `state`, and `merged_at` when the provider recorded it. It establishes that the change merged and when. It never establishes the review state.\n2. The review-decision and thread-resolution records (`context/review_threads.json`): each reviewer''s decision, each thread''s path, line, resolution and `resolvedAt` when the provider dated it, and their dates. They establish the review state at the merge timestamp when read as dated events: a thread whose `resolvedAt` is after `merged_at` was open at the merge, and the precomputed leads list it beside the never-resolved ones. A current decision or resolution field is a current-state fact, not automatically a historical one; a latest-state snapshot, a filename or a record count never by itself proves the historical record is complete.\n3. The discussion (`context/general_comments.json`, and the inline comments in `context/comments.json`, each carrying the `thread` it belongs to): dated comments that ask for the merge to wait, or explain a supersession or a resolution. They establish a blocking request a reviewer wrote, and why a gate was lifted, when dated. They never turn advice into a gate. The precomputed leads below these criteria list the merge — who, when — and one row per thread the record does not mark RESOLVED, with its `id`, anchor, opening time and whether it is outdated; it says whether the threads file was captured at all.\nThe diff (`work/change/diff.patch`) establishes nothing here: never use the code to infer a reviewer''s decision. If the change merged but `context/review_threads.json` is absent or truncated, the loop had some state at merge and you could not read it: report a collection gap and record no observation. Never treat a missing file as an empty loop.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context. Judge the captured gates reviewer by reviewer: each reviewer''s own requests and threads, settled or standing at `merged_at`. A reviewer''s standing approval dated before the merge supersedes that same reviewer''s earlier formal change-request decisions (`CHANGES_REQUESTED`), never another reviewer''s. Approval does not lift an explicit hold the reviewer retains or qualifies in the approval or accompanying discussion. A thread resolution dated before the merge closes that thread. `decisionHistoryComplete: false` warns that withdrawn or replaced decisions may be missing from the capture; it is not itself a gate, and it does not undo a dated closure.\n- NOT_MET: dated evidence shows a gate still standing at merge — a thread whose `resolvedAt` follows `merged_at`, a thread that nobody resolved (quote its path:line), or a reviewer''s change-request or explicit request to hold the merge that the record shows still stood then (name the reviewer, quote the decision or the request). Another reviewer''s approval never lifts it.\n- MET: every captured gate was settled before the merge, and the required sources were captured without truncation. State the evidence relied on: \"merged; reviewer X''s change-requests superseded by their approval dated before the merge; every thread resolved before the merge\". Record MET without claiming that the review history is exhaustive.\n- UNDETERMINED when a captured fact that decides the result is unknown — a change-request with no date that could precede or follow the merge; a latest approval or resolution with no date, so it cannot be placed before the merge; or one reviewer''s request beside another reviewer''s approval when the captured record cannot settle whether the requester''s gate was lifted or replaced before the merge. Name the missing fact. Never record UNDETERMINED for a gate the capture does not show. An approval''s time decides only whether it lifted a standing gate: with none, no approval time is needed. A thread whose `resolvedAt` precedes `merged_at` needs no further date, such as when a requested edit took effect.\nA missing or truncated required source is a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review, on the single at-merge question.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on loop closure alone — non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: a merely open thread with no standing request, the lighter lapse. MAJOR: landing over a reviewer''s still-standing request-changes gate, the heavier miss. Equivalent observed states get equivalent treatment.\n\n## Grounding\nFor NOT_MET, quote the deciding review evidence and the dated facts that show it still stood at the merge timestamp. For MET, cite the captured bounded review record — the merge timestamp, each captured decision and each thread''s resolution with dates — that shows none remained. Describe only the observable state: \"merged with N request-changes still standing; thread at <path:line> unresolved at merge\". Never \"you ignored the reviewer\" or \"you merged broken code\"; never a claim about the code''s correctness; never a current open or resolved state offered as proof of the state at merge.\n\n## Defer\nWhether threads on an OPEN pull request are being taken up belongs to `engaging-with-inline-review-comments`. Here only: was the loop closed at the moment of merge? Do not double-flag.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for merged-past-unresolved-review-threads: the merge as the record holds it —\n// who merged, when — and one row per review thread the record does not show resolved by the merge,\n// with where it is anchored, when it was opened and whether the provider calls it outdated. The review\n// reads each thread against the merge; the rows say what the record holds, not whether the merge was owed.\nimport {\n\tdated,\n\tmergeFacts,\n\tmergeRow,\n\treadReviewThreads,\n\tresolvedByMerge,\n\tunresolvedThreadRows,\n} from \"../lib/review.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default async function mergedPastUnresolvedReviewThreads(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\t_diffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\tmetadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n\tcontextDir: string | undefined,\n\t_changeDir: string | undefined,\n\tcontextReference: string,\n) {\n\tconst merge = mergeFacts(metadata);\n\tconst mergeUndated = merge.merged && !dated(merge.mergedAt);\n\tconst record = await readReviewThreads(contextDir);\n\tconst threads = record?.threads ?? [];\n\tconst unresolved = unresolvedThreadRows(threads, contextReference, merge);\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (!merge.merged) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\"The pull request is not merged: the occasion did not arise.\");\n\t} else if (record === null) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\"No review threads file was captured (review_threads.json): the thread record is not available here.\",\n\t\t);\n\t} else {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Merged${merge.mergedBy === undefined ? \"\" : ` by ${merge.mergedBy}`}${merge.mergedByIsAuthor ? \" (the author)\" : \"\"}; ${threads.length} thread(s) captured, ${unresolved.length} candidate thread(s) — currently not marked RESOLVED, resolved after the merge, or resolved at a time the record does not establish — one row each.${mergeUndated ? \" The record gives no usable merge time, so no resolution is placed before or after the merge.\" : \"\"} These are not proven open at merge: compare opening and resolution times, and treat an unknown historical order as UNDETERMINED. Read each thread''s comments in comments.json by its id (the comments'' \\`thread\\`) before deciding what it asked.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: [mergeRow(metadata, merge, contextReference), ...unresolved],\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tmerged: merge.merged ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tmergedByIsAuthor: merge.mergedByIsAuthor ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tthreads: threads.length,\n\t\t\tcandidateThreads: unresolved.length,\n\t\t\tresolutionTimeUnknown: unresolved.filter(\n\t\t\t\t(h) =>\n\t\t\t\t\th.flags.state === \"RESOLVED\" &&\n\t\t\t\t\ttypeof h.flags.resolvedAt === \"string\" &&\n\t\t\t\t\t!dated(h.flags.resolvedAt),\n\t\t\t).length,\n\t\t\t// Undated, the merge places no resolution before it, so the count is left out rather than zero.\n\t\t\t...(mergeUndated\n\t\t\t\t? {}\n\t\t\t\t: {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tresolvedBeforeMerge: threads.filter(\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t(t) => t.state === \"RESOLVED\" && resolvedByMerge(t, merge),\n\t\t\t\t\t\t).length,\n\t\t\t\t\t}),\n\t\t\tthreadsFileAbsent: record === null ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Merging while a reviewer still has changes requested skips the very check they asked for, and tells them their review did not count. It is also a quality signal, not just a courtesy: changes that land with little reviewer discussion tend to carry more defects that surface after release. Resolving the threads first keeps review worth doing.","whatGoodLooksLike":"By the time you merge, every open thread is resolved and anyone who requested changes has come back with an approval, so nothing lands over an objection.","groupSlug":"acting-on-review-feedback","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 200\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Merge first</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"44\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"12\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>,<path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M32 44h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>,<path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M216 44l-8-5v10z\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Review</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" cx=\"236\" cy=\"44\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"236\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>,<path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M256 44h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>,<path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M440 44l-8-5v10z\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"224\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Merge</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" cx=\"460\" cy=\"44\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>,,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"448\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Resolve threads</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Resolve first</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"160\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"12\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>,<path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M32 160h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>,<path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M216 160l-8-5v10z\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Review</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-accent\" cx=\"236\" cy=\"160\" r=\"12\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-surface\" x=\"236\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>,<path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M256 160h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>,<path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M440 160l-8-5v10z\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"224\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Resolve threads</text>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" cx=\"460\" cy=\"160\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>,<text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>,,<text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"448\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Merge</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"Merge first: after the review, the work merges and its threads are resolved later. Resolve first: after the review, its threads are resolved, and then the work merges."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you merge, look at the pull or merge request for open threads and for reviewers who requested changes.\n- When a comment says “do not merge until the crash is fixed”, wait until its reviewer lifts it.\n- Resolve each thread before you merge.\n- When a reviewer requests changes, ask that reviewer to look again after your fix. For example, instead of merging right after you push the fix, wait for that reviewer to approve it.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The pull or merge request did not merge.\n\nIt still applies when the merged code is correct.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Merging because the code works. This practice is about the review, not about the code. Correct code can still skip the second look that a reviewer asked for. Instead, wait for their approval.\n- Resolving threads after you merge. A thread you resolve after the merge was still open when the work landed. Instead, resolve it first, then merge.\n- Treating every comment as a request to wait. A suggestion the reviewer leaves to you, or advice for later work, is not a request to hold the merge. Instead, look for what the reviewer asked of this merge, not how politely they wrote it. Still resolve its thread before you merge.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [About protected branches](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches), GitHub Docs. A repository can block a merge until every conversation is resolved. With required reviews, an admin who requests changes must approve before the merge.\n- [Comments and threads](https://docs.gitlab.com/user/discussions/), GitLab Docs. A project can block a merge until every thread is resolved.\n- [How to write code review comments](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/comments.html), Google Engineering Practices. Reviewers can label a comment as a nit, optional, or for later. Then the author can tell required changes from suggestions.\n- [The impact of code review coverage and code review participation on software quality](https://rebels.cs.uwaterloo.ca/confpaper/2014/05/30/the-impact-of-code-review-coverage-and-code-review-participation-on-software-quality.html), McIntosh and others, 2014. In Qt, VTK and ITK, components with low reviewer participation had more defects after release.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Nothing merges over an open thread or objection"'::jsonb),'v5:586787fdf2fbf6d55b7c119c8ce8c2f9e6bdb33883fcce6cbd09fbb5b173eba8','practice:v3:87b7ddf699fa912f73856658ab30b514413c71d4aa615ada1d6a11d6ed04a02e');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('GROUP','actionable-issue-authoring',NULL,'{"name":"Writing issues a maintainer can act on","description":"Write issues someone can pick up without coming back to ask what you meant. State the problem clearly, what you saw versus expected plus steps to reproduce for a bug, or the who and why for a request, keep each issue to one concern, say how to tell when it is done, and link the design or decision the work depends on.","icon":"FileText","color":"violet"}'::jsonb,'{"name":"Writing issues a maintainer can act on","description":"Write issues someone can pick up without coming back to ask what you meant. State the problem clearly, what you saw versus expected plus steps to reproduce for a bug, or the who and why for a request, keep each issue to one concern, say how to tell when it is done, and link the design or decision the work depends on.","icon":"FileText","color":"violet"}'::jsonb,'30450d0e6e173b6db1aa0cc446b80bb96363ef2591e338fe6b565e9bffb5452c','group:v1:30450d0e6e173b6db1aa0cc446b80bb96363ef2591e338fe6b565e9bffb5452c');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','issue-states-an-actionable-problem','scm.issue','{"name":"State a problem a maintainer can act on","signals":["scm.issue.opened","scm.issue.updated"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured issue: its title, description, labels and the discussion the practice names. Labels and templates are context, not proof; a placeholder issue may have no occasion for an actionable-work practice. Do not infer that a bug reproduces or that an outcome was implemented.\n\nFor a practice about requested work, establish the current request from the complete issue body and the discussion the practice names. Brief, vague or title-only requests still pass this premise.\n\nWhen genuinely conflicting current statements leave that request unresolved, record UNDETERMINED for the request-dependent judgment, cite the conflict, and judge neither statement under the standard. Position or date alone does not resolve it. If an explicit authored withdrawal leaves no work requested, record NOT_APPLICABLE for that judgment and treat earlier requirements as history. Judge a remaining request under the practice''s standard. Historical and closure practices retain their own subjects, including accounting for dropped or deferred work.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: the issue, read on its own, lets a maintainer picture the work at the scope it states and begin.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether the issue states enough for a maintainer to begin the kind of work it requests. A specific title must locate the work. Its body, comments or a materialized document it delegates to must state the following core:\n\n- Defect, including an untyped issue asserting a malfunction: actual behaviour, expected behaviour and a concrete reproduction path. Steps, a stack trace, an exact error or a failing test can provide that path.\n- Capability request: a concrete requested behaviour a maintainer can begin building. Who benefits, why, an acceptance block, a design and a specification are not additional prerequisites.\n- Epic, umbrella or requirement card: substantive scope at that level; implementation detail can live in child work.\n\nA concrete, quotable acceptance-criteria, Definition-of-Done or Given-When-Then block can supply the core on its own. Assess other forms by the kind of work they actually request.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there is no work-kind to judge: a pure question or open discussion that asserts no defect and requests no capability. Do not demand reproduction steps of a discussion. A title that names a task or a capability (\"Add a dark-mode toggle\", \"Update the setup guide\") is engineering work even when the body is empty: the gate passes and the empty body is the lapse, never NOT_APPLICABLE.\n\n## Sources\n1. The title and the WHOLE body (`context/description.md`): every section, template field, step list and criteria block. It establishes the kind of work and the substance stated inline. An image: read its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n2. The title and labels (`context/metadata.json`): a deliverable-type label names the kind of work. It never establishes substance by itself; an empty template echoing the title under such a label is still empty.\n3. The comments (`context/comments.json`): part of the observable artifact; substance stated there counts. They never establish substance you cannot quote from them.\n4. Linked design, decision or spec documents (`context/docs/`): when the issue delegates its substance to a document materialized here, read it. A concrete capability or interface the document actually specifies is substance an implementer can start from; count it as observed rather than demanding the issue restate it inline. A generic document or a placeholder marker that does not concretely pin THIS work supplies nothing.\nWhen the issue delegates its substance to a specific named document that was NOT materialized, you cannot tell whether that substance exists: report a collection gap and record no observation, never a claim against prose you were never shown. If the issue text was not captured completely, the same.\n\n## Judge\nThese conformance cases apply after the shared issue premise establishes a current request.\n- MET: a specific title locates the work and its kind''s core is genuinely present and quotable — inline, in a comment, or in a materialized document the issue delegates to.\n- NOT_MET: the title does not locate the work, or a required element of its kind''s core is missing because the issue delegates to nothing and states none itself, or the materialized document is generic or a placeholder. Name the missing element.\n- UNDETERMINED: the standard itself has no ordinary undecidable case. A complete but empty or vague body is an assessed gap — NOT_MET — not UNDETERMINED; an incomplete capture is a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review, over the whole issue.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR — a non-blocking authoring-practice nudge, not a merge gate. Identify the missing required part of this standard before assigning severity.\n\nSeverity depends on the kind of work.\n\n- Defect: MINOR when the issue states a concrete observable malfunction or a reproduction path. A stated concrete malfunction is part of a defect''s core even without reproduction steps. MAJOR when the issue states neither a concrete observable malfunction nor a reproduction path.\n- Capability request, epic or card: MINOR when the requested work is concrete and startable but its title does not locate it. MAJOR when the work is unstartable because no concrete capability or scope is stated.\n\nAn empty body, a placeholder or repetition of a vague title supplies no core.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery element you call present must be quotable from the artifact — the title, the body, a comment, or the materialized document; an element you cannot quote is absent. For NOT_MET, evidence.search records the boundary you searched, and the citations quote from it: the whole body, the labels, the comments, and the `context/docs/` documents or their absence. Never invent content and never grant a clean bill you did not verify. Describe the observable gap — \"the body names a symptom but no reproduction path\" — never the reporter''s intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the issue has a checkable or measurable outcome belongs to `issue-has-checkable-outcome`. Here only: can a maintainer picture the work and start?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for issue-states-an-actionable-problem: what the captured issue carries — whether\n// its body is empty or only repeats the title, its labels and its native type. Whether a maintainer\n// can picture the work and start is the criteria''s to decide from the whole text.\nimport { bodyFact, classifyIssue, type IssueMetadata } from \"../lib/issue-classification.ts\";\nimport type { Hint } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default function issueStatesAnActionableProblem(\n\t_repo: string,\n\t_diff: Map<string, unknown>,\n\tm: IssueMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst shape = classifyIssue(m);\n\tconst { body, issueType, labels, emptyOrTitleEcho, hasDeliverableType, looksUmbrella } = shape;\n\n\tconst directions = [\n\t\t...bodyFact(shape),\n\t\t`Captured: ${String(body.length)} body character(s); labels ${labels.join(\", \") || \"none\"}; native type ${issueType || \"none\"}. Judge the standard once over the title, the whole body, the comments and any materialised document the issue delegates to.`,\n\t];\n\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tbodyLength: body.length,\n\t\t\tlabelCount: labels.length,\n\t\t\temptyOrTitleEcho: emptyOrTitleEcho ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\thasDeliverableType: hasDeliverableType ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tlooksUmbrella: looksUmbrella ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"IMPLEMENTATION_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Issue evidence does not establish whether the described work was implemented correctly."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Clear descriptions help the next maintainer understand the requested change or problem. They reduce guesswork and give planning and discussion a useful starting point.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A bug report that reads ''uploading a 0-byte file returns 500, I expected a clear validation error'' and lists the exact steps to reproduce, so a maintainer can start straight away. Or a request that names one concrete capability with who it helps and why, such as ''reviewers lose track of which files changed in large diffs, so add a per-file changed-lines summary to the review view''.","groupSlug":"actionable-issue-authoring","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 156\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Vague issue</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"94.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">It doesn’t work</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"15\">Maintainer guesses</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Actionable issue</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"94.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">Empty file upload returns 500</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"15\">Expected: validation error</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"15\">Maintainer can start</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A vague issue says only “It doesn’t work”, so the maintainer guesses. An actionable issue says “Empty file upload returns 500” and “Expected: validation error”, so the maintainer can start."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Write a title that says which work the issue is about. For example, instead of “Search broken”, write “Search with a quote mark shows an error page”.\n- For a bug, say what happened and what you expected to happen.\n- For a bug, give a way to see it fail. Steps, a stack trace, the exact error or a failing test all work.\n- For a request, describe the behavior you want, so that someone can start to build it. You can leave out who it helps, why, and a design.\n- For an epic, an issue that groups related work, state the scope of the whole. The detail can go in the smaller issues under it.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The issue only asks a question or opens a discussion. It reports no bug and asks for no new behavior.\n\nIt still applies when the title names a task, such as “Add a dark-mode toggle”, and the body is empty.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Filling in a template that only repeats the title, even under a bug or feature label. A label names the kind of work, not the work. Instead, write the problem or the behavior in your own words.\n- Writing “It does not work”. Instead, say what you saw and what you expected, so a maintainer knows what is wrong.\n- Writing only your guess at the cause. Instead, report what you saw. Add your guess only as an extra.\n- Pointing to a general document or a placeholder. Instead, link a document that describes this work, or write the work in the issue.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Bug writing guidelines](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/page.cgi?id=bug-writing.html), Mozilla. A good bug report has a summary that names the problem. It also gives steps to reproduce, the actual result and the expected result.\n- [What makes a good bug report?](https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2010.63), Zimmermann and others, 2010. Developers at Apache, Eclipse and Mozilla found steps to reproduce, stack traces and test cases most helpful. Reporters found them hardest to give.\n- [How to report bugs effectively](https://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/bugs.html), Simon Tatham, 1999. Let the programmer see the failure, and say exactly what you expected. Report the symptoms along with any guess.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"State a problem a maintainer can act on","signals":["scm.issue.opened","scm.issue.updated"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured issue: its title, description, labels and the discussion the practice names. Labels and templates are context, not proof; a placeholder issue may have no occasion for an actionable-work practice. Do not infer that a bug reproduces or that an outcome was implemented.\n\nFor a practice about requested work, establish the current request from the complete issue body and the discussion the practice names. Brief, vague or title-only requests still pass this premise.\n\nWhen genuinely conflicting current statements leave that request unresolved, record UNDETERMINED for the request-dependent judgment, cite the conflict, and judge neither statement under the standard. Position or date alone does not resolve it. If an explicit authored withdrawal leaves no work requested, record NOT_APPLICABLE for that judgment and treat earlier requirements as history. Judge a remaining request under the practice''s standard. Historical and closure practices retain their own subjects, including accounting for dropped or deferred work.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: the issue, read on its own, lets a maintainer picture the work at the scope it states and begin.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether the issue states enough for a maintainer to begin the kind of work it requests. A specific title must locate the work. Its body, comments or a materialized document it delegates to must state the following core:\n\n- Defect, including an untyped issue asserting a malfunction: actual behaviour, expected behaviour and a concrete reproduction path. Steps, a stack trace, an exact error or a failing test can provide that path.\n- Capability request: a concrete requested behaviour a maintainer can begin building. Who benefits, why, an acceptance block, a design and a specification are not additional prerequisites.\n- Epic, umbrella or requirement card: substantive scope at that level; implementation detail can live in child work.\n\nA concrete, quotable acceptance-criteria, Definition-of-Done or Given-When-Then block can supply the core on its own. Assess other forms by the kind of work they actually request.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there is no work-kind to judge: a pure question or open discussion that asserts no defect and requests no capability. Do not demand reproduction steps of a discussion. A title that names a task or a capability (\"Add a dark-mode toggle\", \"Update the setup guide\") is engineering work even when the body is empty: the gate passes and the empty body is the lapse, never NOT_APPLICABLE.\n\n## Sources\n1. The title and the WHOLE body (`context/description.md`): every section, template field, step list and criteria block. It establishes the kind of work and the substance stated inline. An image: read its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n2. The title and labels (`context/metadata.json`): a deliverable-type label names the kind of work. It never establishes substance by itself; an empty template echoing the title under such a label is still empty.\n3. The comments (`context/comments.json`): part of the observable artifact; substance stated there counts. They never establish substance you cannot quote from them.\n4. Linked design, decision or spec documents (`context/docs/`): when the issue delegates its substance to a document materialized here, read it. A concrete capability or interface the document actually specifies is substance an implementer can start from; count it as observed rather than demanding the issue restate it inline. A generic document or a placeholder marker that does not concretely pin THIS work supplies nothing.\nWhen the issue delegates its substance to a specific named document that was NOT materialized, you cannot tell whether that substance exists: report a collection gap and record no observation, never a claim against prose you were never shown. If the issue text was not captured completely, the same.\n\n## Judge\nThese conformance cases apply after the shared issue premise establishes a current request.\n- MET: a specific title locates the work and its kind''s core is genuinely present and quotable — inline, in a comment, or in a materialized document the issue delegates to.\n- NOT_MET: the title does not locate the work, or a required element of its kind''s core is missing because the issue delegates to nothing and states none itself, or the materialized document is generic or a placeholder. Name the missing element.\n- UNDETERMINED: the standard itself has no ordinary undecidable case. A complete but empty or vague body is an assessed gap — NOT_MET — not UNDETERMINED; an incomplete capture is a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review, over the whole issue.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR — a non-blocking authoring-practice nudge, not a merge gate. Identify the missing required part of this standard before assigning severity.\n\nSeverity depends on the kind of work.\n\n- Defect: MINOR when the issue states a concrete observable malfunction or a reproduction path. A stated concrete malfunction is part of a defect''s core even without reproduction steps. MAJOR when the issue states neither a concrete observable malfunction nor a reproduction path.\n- Capability request, epic or card: MINOR when the requested work is concrete and startable but its title does not locate it. MAJOR when the work is unstartable because no concrete capability or scope is stated.\n\nAn empty body, a placeholder or repetition of a vague title supplies no core.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery element you call present must be quotable from the artifact — the title, the body, a comment, or the materialized document; an element you cannot quote is absent. For NOT_MET, evidence.search records the boundary you searched, and the citations quote from it: the whole body, the labels, the comments, and the `context/docs/` documents or their absence. Never invent content and never grant a clean bill you did not verify. Describe the observable gap — \"the body names a symptom but no reproduction path\" — never the reporter''s intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the issue has a checkable or measurable outcome belongs to `issue-has-checkable-outcome`. Here only: can a maintainer picture the work and start?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for issue-states-an-actionable-problem: what the captured issue carries — whether\n// its body is empty or only repeats the title, its labels and its native type. Whether a maintainer\n// can picture the work and start is the criteria''s to decide from the whole text.\nimport { bodyFact, classifyIssue, type IssueMetadata } from \"../lib/issue-classification.ts\";\nimport type { Hint } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default function issueStatesAnActionableProblem(\n\t_repo: string,\n\t_diff: Map<string, unknown>,\n\tm: IssueMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst shape = classifyIssue(m);\n\tconst { body, issueType, labels, emptyOrTitleEcho, hasDeliverableType, looksUmbrella } = shape;\n\n\tconst directions = [\n\t\t...bodyFact(shape),\n\t\t`Captured: ${String(body.length)} body character(s); labels ${labels.join(\", \") || \"none\"}; native type ${issueType || \"none\"}. Judge the standard once over the title, the whole body, the comments and any materialised document the issue delegates to.`,\n\t];\n\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tbodyLength: body.length,\n\t\t\tlabelCount: labels.length,\n\t\t\temptyOrTitleEcho: emptyOrTitleEcho ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\thasDeliverableType: hasDeliverableType ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tlooksUmbrella: looksUmbrella ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"IMPLEMENTATION_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Issue evidence does not establish whether the described work was implemented correctly."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Clear descriptions help the next maintainer understand the requested change or problem. They reduce guesswork and give planning and discussion a useful starting point.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A bug report that reads ''uploading a 0-byte file returns 500, I expected a clear validation error'' and lists the exact steps to reproduce, so a maintainer can start straight away. Or a request that names one concrete capability with who it helps and why, such as ''reviewers lose track of which files changed in large diffs, so add a per-file changed-lines summary to the review view''.","groupSlug":"actionable-issue-authoring","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 156\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Vague issue</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"94.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">It doesn’t work</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"15\">Maintainer guesses</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Actionable issue</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"94.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">Empty file upload returns 500</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"15\">Expected: validation error</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"15\">Maintainer can start</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A vague issue says only “It doesn’t work”, so the maintainer guesses. An actionable issue says “Empty file upload returns 500” and “Expected: validation error”, so the maintainer can start."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Write a title that says which work the issue is about. For example, instead of “Search broken”, write “Search with a quote mark shows an error page”.\n- For a bug, say what happened and what you expected to happen.\n- For a bug, give a way to see it fail. Steps, a stack trace, the exact error or a failing test all work.\n- For a request, describe the behavior you want, so that someone can start to build it. You can leave out who it helps, why, and a design.\n- For an epic, an issue that groups related work, state the scope of the whole. The detail can go in the smaller issues under it.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The issue only asks a question or opens a discussion. It reports no bug and asks for no new behavior.\n\nIt still applies when the title names a task, such as “Add a dark-mode toggle”, and the body is empty.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Filling in a template that only repeats the title, even under a bug or feature label. A label names the kind of work, not the work. Instead, write the problem or the behavior in your own words.\n- Writing “It does not work”. Instead, say what you saw and what you expected, so a maintainer knows what is wrong.\n- Writing only your guess at the cause. Instead, report what you saw. Add your guess only as an extra.\n- Pointing to a general document or a placeholder. Instead, link a document that describes this work, or write the work in the issue.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Bug writing guidelines](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/page.cgi?id=bug-writing.html), Mozilla. A good bug report has a summary that names the problem. It also gives steps to reproduce, the actual result and the expected result.\n- [What makes a good bug report?](https://doi.org/10.1109/TSE.2010.63), Zimmermann and others, 2010. Developers at Apache, Eclipse and Mozilla found steps to reproduce, stack traces and test cases most helpful. Reporters found them hardest to give.\n- [How to report bugs effectively](https://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/bugs.html), Simon Tatham, 1999. Let the programmer see the failure, and say exactly what you expected. Report the symptoms along with any guess.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Issues state a problem a maintainer can act on"'::jsonb),'v5:81ff78f18f7bed0844ffade8f5fe8a23150a329db7cd986f36d96a33bf6ab890','practice:v3:ae5a8bb082094a57e184aec9f3069ed1733e63508b5b25e6643ab31e1d073ced');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','issue-scoped-to-single-concern','scm.issue','{"name":"Keep the issue to one concern","signals":["scm.issue.opened","scm.issue.updated"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured issue: its title, description, labels and the discussion the practice names. Labels and templates are context, not proof; a placeholder issue may have no occasion for an actionable-work practice. Do not infer that a bug reproduces or that an outcome was implemented.\n\nFor a practice about requested work, establish the current request from the complete issue body and the discussion the practice names. Brief, vague or title-only requests still pass this premise.\n\nWhen genuinely conflicting current statements leave that request unresolved, record UNDETERMINED for the request-dependent judgment, cite the conflict, and judge neither statement under the standard. Position or date alone does not resolve it. If an explicit authored withdrawal leaves no work requested, record NOT_APPLICABLE for that judgment and treat earlier requirements as history. Judge a remaining request under the practice''s standard. Historical and closure practices retain their own subjects, including accounting for dropped or deferred work.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: how the issue is scoped — one cohesive deliverable at the issue''s intended level, rather than unrelated asks stapled into one ticket.\n\n## The standard\nA good issue tracks ONE cohesive deliverable: something that can be picked up, worked and closed as a single unit. Judge cohesion as an experienced reviewer would: is there a single thread of intent, or have unrelated asks been stapled together so that part can land while the rest lingers? The test is whether a maintainer would naturally assign, estimate and close it as one thing, or would be fighting to. One feature, one bug, one question is one concern. Internal decomposition is still ONE concern: implementation sub-steps, a checklist that builds up one capability, a sub-issue rollup that breaks one feature into trackable children. An epic that bundles several RELATED facets of the same feature area (create, rename, delete plus search for one entity) is ONE concern at its altitude; that breadth is what makes it an epic. The tell of bundling is separable threads of intent targeting different concerns, never the mere presence of \"and\" or a long checklist.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there is no concrete deliverable to scope at all: a pure question, a support request, an open discussion, an empty or title-only body, or an untouched template. Do not manufacture a MET from that absence.\n\n## Sources\nJudge only from the issue body, its discussion, its labels and its sub-issue rollup as captured.\n1. The title and the WHOLE body (`context/description.md`): every section, checklist and step list, and the sub-issue rollup when the capture carries it. It establishes the deliverable(s) named and how they relate. It never establishes what the author meant beyond the written spans.\n2. The labels and title (`context/metadata.json`): the issue''s intended level — epic, feature, bug, task. They establish the altitude at which to judge cohesion. They never establish cohesion by themselves.\n3. The WHOLE discussion (`context/comments.json`): an authored decision or an accepted clarification can establish the current request. A proposed change is not an adopted request merely because it is later. Read it together with the body under the shared issue premise.\nAn image in the body: read its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing. If the body or discussion was not captured completely, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nThese conformance cases apply after the shared issue premise establishes a current request.\n- MET: one cohesive deliverable, including any internal decomposition or related-facet epic described above.\n- NOT_MET: genuinely unrelated deliverables fused into one ticket — \"add CSV export, fix the date picker, and bump the chart library\". Feed forward the single most consequential split: name the concerns to break into linked issues. Never enumerate every clause.\n- UNDETERMINED when the complete text genuinely leaves one journey versus separate deliverables unresolved; quote the ambiguity and say what would settle it. A partly captured issue is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, over the whole issue.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on scoping alone — a formative practice, never a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: separable asks that a maintainer could still split without stranding work. MAJOR: clearly unrelated bundling that will genuinely strand work, with part landing and the rest lingering open.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the actual span(s) that name the deliverable(s) you are judging; for NOT_MET, the spans that name each separable concern. Describe the observable bundling — \"the body names three deliverables with no shared thread\" — never carelessness or intent. Never invent a deliverable, a clean bill, or a bundling you did not read.\n\n## Defer\nWhether an epic should itself be split into tracked child stories belongs to `breaks-large-work-into-trackable-subtasks`; do not double-count it. Here only: is this issue one concern at its level?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for issue-scoped-to-single-concern: the task-list items and issue-number mentions the\n// body carries and the captured sub-issue rollup with its source. Whether the issue is one concern at its level\n// is the criteria''s to decide.\nimport {\n\tbodyFact,\n\tclassifyIssue,\n\tsubIssueRollup,\n\ttype IssueMetadata,\n} from \"../lib/issue-classification.ts\";\nimport { checkableItems } from \"../lib/review.ts\";\nimport type { Hint } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default function issueScopedToSingleConcern(\n\t_repo: string,\n\t_diff: Map<string, unknown>,\n\tm: IssueMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst shape = classifyIssue(m);\n\tconst { body, labels, emptyOrTitleEcho } = shape;\n\tconst checkboxes = checkableItems(body).length;\n\tconst issueMentions = new Set((body.match(/(?:^|\\s)#\\d+\\b/gu) ?? []).map((s) => s.trim())).size;\n\tconst rollup = subIssueRollup(m);\n\tconst directions = [\n\t\t...bodyFact(shape),\n\t\t`Captured: ${String(checkboxes)} task-list item(s), ${String(issueMentions)} issue-number mention(s), ${rollup.text}; labels ${labels.join(\", \") || \"none\"}. Judge the standard once over the title, whole body and captured discussion; these counts locate text and decide nothing.`,\n\t];\n\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tbodyLength: body.length,\n\t\t\temptyOrTitleEcho: emptyOrTitleEcho ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tcheckboxes,\n\t\t\tchildIssueRefs: issueMentions,\n\t\t\t...rollup.metrics,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"IMPLEMENTATION_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Issue evidence does not establish whether the described work was implemented correctly."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"An issue that quietly bundles three unrelated asks cannot be assigned, estimated, or closed cleanly. One part lands, the rest linger, and the whole thing stays open forever, tracking nothing.","whatGoodLooksLike":"One issue, one deliverable, such as ''add CSV export to the reports page'', rather than ''add CSV export, fix the date picker, and bump the chart library'' crammed into one ticket.","groupSlug":"actionable-issue-authoring","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":"issue-structure","redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 212\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Bundled issue</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"150.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\">Add CSV export</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"72\" width=\"232\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"84\" width=\"176\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"139\" font-size=\"15\">Fix date picker</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"152\" width=\"208\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"164\" width=\"144\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Rest lingers open</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">One issue per ask</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"70.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\">Add CSV export</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"72\" width=\"232\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"84\" width=\"176\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"112.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"70.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"139\" font-size=\"15\">Fix date picker</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"152\" width=\"208\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"164\" width=\"144\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Each closes cleanly</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A bundled issue holds two separate asks, “Add CSV export” and “Fix date picker”, so one lands and the rest lingers open. With one issue per ask, each closes cleanly."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you file the issue, name the one result that closes it. If closing it also needs a result with a different goal, that result is a second concern.\n- Open one issue for each concern. For example, instead of one issue for dark mode and the login timeout, open two issues.\n- Before you open a new issue, search the open issues for each ask. If an open issue already covers an ask, link to that issue in place of the ask.\n- Keep all the steps of one result in its issue. A checklist that builds one feature belongs together, so a maintainer can assign, estimate and close it as one thing.\n- For an epic, a large issue for one feature area, keep the related parts of that area together. Create, rename and delete for one item are one concern at that level.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The issue asks a question and asks for no work.\n- The issue is a support request.\n- The issue opens a discussion and names nothing to build or fix yet.\n- The body is empty, or the issue has only a title.\n- The body is the issue template, with nothing changed.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Splitting an issue because its title has “and” or its checklist is long. Neither one makes it a bundle. Instead, look for asks with different goals that could each land and close on their own.\n- Adding an unrelated ask to an open issue to save time. Different people often handle different asks, and an ask that hides in another issue can stay undone. Instead, open a new issue for it.\n- Splitting the asks into issues that do not point to each other. Instead, link each new issue to the others, so the context stays easy to find.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Bug Writing Guidelines](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/page.cgi?id=bug-writing.html), Mozilla. Open a new report for each issue, so the fix workflow can finish for each one.\n- [How to pre-screen Browser-General bugs](https://www-archive.mozilla.org/quality/browser/prescreening), Mozilla, 2004, archived page. A report that lists several unrelated problems goes to different engineers, and some of its problems may never get fixed.\n- [INVEST in good stories, and SMART tasks](https://xp123.com/invest-in-good-stories-and-smart-tasks/), Bill Wake, 2003. Stories are easiest to work with when they do not overlap and can be built in any order. A good story can be estimated.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Keep the issue to one concern","signals":["scm.issue.opened","scm.issue.updated"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured issue: its title, description, labels and the discussion the practice names. Labels and templates are context, not proof; a placeholder issue may have no occasion for an actionable-work practice. Do not infer that a bug reproduces or that an outcome was implemented.\n\nFor a practice about requested work, establish the current request from the complete issue body and the discussion the practice names. Brief, vague or title-only requests still pass this premise.\n\nWhen genuinely conflicting current statements leave that request unresolved, record UNDETERMINED for the request-dependent judgment, cite the conflict, and judge neither statement under the standard. Position or date alone does not resolve it. If an explicit authored withdrawal leaves no work requested, record NOT_APPLICABLE for that judgment and treat earlier requirements as history. Judge a remaining request under the practice''s standard. Historical and closure practices retain their own subjects, including accounting for dropped or deferred work.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: how the issue is scoped — one cohesive deliverable at the issue''s intended level, rather than unrelated asks stapled into one ticket.\n\n## The standard\nA good issue tracks ONE cohesive deliverable: something that can be picked up, worked and closed as a single unit. Judge cohesion as an experienced reviewer would: is there a single thread of intent, or have unrelated asks been stapled together so that part can land while the rest lingers? The test is whether a maintainer would naturally assign, estimate and close it as one thing, or would be fighting to. One feature, one bug, one question is one concern. Internal decomposition is still ONE concern: implementation sub-steps, a checklist that builds up one capability, a sub-issue rollup that breaks one feature into trackable children. An epic that bundles several RELATED facets of the same feature area (create, rename, delete plus search for one entity) is ONE concern at its altitude; that breadth is what makes it an epic. The tell of bundling is separable threads of intent targeting different concerns, never the mere presence of \"and\" or a long checklist.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there is no concrete deliverable to scope at all: a pure question, a support request, an open discussion, an empty or title-only body, or an untouched template. Do not manufacture a MET from that absence.\n\n## Sources\nJudge only from the issue body, its discussion, its labels and its sub-issue rollup as captured.\n1. The title and the WHOLE body (`context/description.md`): every section, checklist and step list, and the sub-issue rollup when the capture carries it. It establishes the deliverable(s) named and how they relate. It never establishes what the author meant beyond the written spans.\n2. The labels and title (`context/metadata.json`): the issue''s intended level — epic, feature, bug, task. They establish the altitude at which to judge cohesion. They never establish cohesion by themselves.\n3. The WHOLE discussion (`context/comments.json`): an authored decision or an accepted clarification can establish the current request. A proposed change is not an adopted request merely because it is later. Read it together with the body under the shared issue premise.\nAn image in the body: read its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing. If the body or discussion was not captured completely, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nThese conformance cases apply after the shared issue premise establishes a current request.\n- MET: one cohesive deliverable, including any internal decomposition or related-facet epic described above.\n- NOT_MET: genuinely unrelated deliverables fused into one ticket — \"add CSV export, fix the date picker, and bump the chart library\". Feed forward the single most consequential split: name the concerns to break into linked issues. Never enumerate every clause.\n- UNDETERMINED when the complete text genuinely leaves one journey versus separate deliverables unresolved; quote the ambiguity and say what would settle it. A partly captured issue is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, over the whole issue.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on scoping alone — a formative practice, never a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: separable asks that a maintainer could still split without stranding work. MAJOR: clearly unrelated bundling that will genuinely strand work, with part landing and the rest lingering open.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the actual span(s) that name the deliverable(s) you are judging; for NOT_MET, the spans that name each separable concern. Describe the observable bundling — \"the body names three deliverables with no shared thread\" — never carelessness or intent. Never invent a deliverable, a clean bill, or a bundling you did not read.\n\n## Defer\nWhether an epic should itself be split into tracked child stories belongs to `breaks-large-work-into-trackable-subtasks`; do not double-count it. Here only: is this issue one concern at its level?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for issue-scoped-to-single-concern: the task-list items and issue-number mentions the\n// body carries and the captured sub-issue rollup with its source. Whether the issue is one concern at its level\n// is the criteria''s to decide.\nimport {\n\tbodyFact,\n\tclassifyIssue,\n\tsubIssueRollup,\n\ttype IssueMetadata,\n} from \"../lib/issue-classification.ts\";\nimport { checkableItems } from \"../lib/review.ts\";\nimport type { Hint } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default function issueScopedToSingleConcern(\n\t_repo: string,\n\t_diff: Map<string, unknown>,\n\tm: IssueMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst shape = classifyIssue(m);\n\tconst { body, labels, emptyOrTitleEcho } = shape;\n\tconst checkboxes = checkableItems(body).length;\n\tconst issueMentions = new Set((body.match(/(?:^|\\s)#\\d+\\b/gu) ?? []).map((s) => s.trim())).size;\n\tconst rollup = subIssueRollup(m);\n\tconst directions = [\n\t\t...bodyFact(shape),\n\t\t`Captured: ${String(checkboxes)} task-list item(s), ${String(issueMentions)} issue-number mention(s), ${rollup.text}; labels ${labels.join(\", \") || \"none\"}. Judge the standard once over the title, whole body and captured discussion; these counts locate text and decide nothing.`,\n\t];\n\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tbodyLength: body.length,\n\t\t\temptyOrTitleEcho: emptyOrTitleEcho ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tcheckboxes,\n\t\t\tchildIssueRefs: issueMentions,\n\t\t\t...rollup.metrics,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"IMPLEMENTATION_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Issue evidence does not establish whether the described work was implemented correctly."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"An issue that quietly bundles three unrelated asks cannot be assigned, estimated, or closed cleanly. One part lands, the rest linger, and the whole thing stays open forever, tracking nothing.","whatGoodLooksLike":"One issue, one deliverable, such as ''add CSV export to the reports page'', rather than ''add CSV export, fix the date picker, and bump the chart library'' crammed into one ticket.","groupSlug":"actionable-issue-authoring","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":"issue-structure","redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 212\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Bundled issue</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"150.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\">Add CSV export</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"72\" width=\"232\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"84\" width=\"176\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"139\" font-size=\"15\">Fix date picker</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"152\" width=\"208\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"164\" width=\"144\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Rest lingers open</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">One issue per ask</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"70.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\">Add CSV export</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"72\" width=\"232\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"84\" width=\"176\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"112.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"70.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"139\" font-size=\"15\">Fix date picker</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"152\" width=\"208\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"164\" width=\"144\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"15\">Each closes cleanly</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A bundled issue holds two separate asks, “Add CSV export” and “Fix date picker”, so one lands and the rest lingers open. With one issue per ask, each closes cleanly."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you file the issue, name the one result that closes it. If closing it also needs a result with a different goal, that result is a second concern.\n- Open one issue for each concern. For example, instead of one issue for dark mode and the login timeout, open two issues.\n- Before you open a new issue, search the open issues for each ask. If an open issue already covers an ask, link to that issue in place of the ask.\n- Keep all the steps of one result in its issue. A checklist that builds one feature belongs together, so a maintainer can assign, estimate and close it as one thing.\n- For an epic, a large issue for one feature area, keep the related parts of that area together. Create, rename and delete for one item are one concern at that level.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The issue asks a question and asks for no work.\n- The issue is a support request.\n- The issue opens a discussion and names nothing to build or fix yet.\n- The body is empty, or the issue has only a title.\n- The body is the issue template, with nothing changed.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Splitting an issue because its title has “and” or its checklist is long. Neither one makes it a bundle. Instead, look for asks with different goals that could each land and close on their own.\n- Adding an unrelated ask to an open issue to save time. Different people often handle different asks, and an ask that hides in another issue can stay undone. Instead, open a new issue for it.\n- Splitting the asks into issues that do not point to each other. Instead, link each new issue to the others, so the context stays easy to find.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Bug Writing Guidelines](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/page.cgi?id=bug-writing.html), Mozilla. Open a new report for each issue, so the fix workflow can finish for each one.\n- [How to pre-screen Browser-General bugs](https://www-archive.mozilla.org/quality/browser/prescreening), Mozilla, 2004, archived page. A report that lists several unrelated problems goes to different engineers, and some of its problems may never get fixed.\n- [INVEST in good stories, and SMART tasks](https://xp123.com/invest-in-good-stories-and-smart-tasks/), Bill Wake, 2003. Stories are easiest to work with when they do not overlap and can be built in any order. A good story can be estimated.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Each issue asks for one deliverable, not a bundle"'::jsonb),'v5:deb7f6d3dc48f4cbedd0a3ae335f66b17bf7e3d55c5b14318d641b27464627ad','practice:v3:985cd381f61206db6a1a2b060a117bf5cb67d512e81846390b1599293ed1ebb4');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','issue-has-checkable-outcome','scm.issue','{"name":"Define a checkable outcome","signals":["scm.issue.closed","scm.issue.opened","scm.issue.updated"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured issue: its title, description, labels and the discussion the practice names. Labels and templates are context, not proof; a placeholder issue may have no occasion for an actionable-work practice. Do not infer that a bug reproduces or that an outcome was implemented.\n\nFor a practice about requested work, establish the current request from the complete issue body and the discussion the practice names. Brief, vague or title-only requests still pass this premise.\n\nWhen genuinely conflicting current statements leave that request unresolved, record UNDETERMINED for the request-dependent judgment, cite the conflict, and judge neither statement under the standard. Position or date alone does not resolve it. If an explicit authored withdrawal leaves no work requested, record NOT_APPLICABLE for that judgment and treat earlier requirements as history. Judge a remaining request under the practice''s standard. Historical and closure practices retain their own subjects, including accounting for dropped or deferred work.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: a quotable finish line — an outcome a maintainer could later verify as DONE from the issue alone.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one thing: does this issue define an outcome a maintainer could later verify as done? A good issue draws a clear finish line: a reader can tell, from the issue alone, what state of the world means the work is complete. The line can take many shapes — an acceptance checklist, a Given-When-Then block, a story''s who/what/why phrased so the result is testable, or, for a bug, a stated expected behaviour a fix must restore. The form does not matter; the testability does. Use judgment as an experienced reviewer would; do not hunt for a keyword or label. Good: \"export downloads a valid CSV; an empty result shows a friendly message\". Bad: \"improve performance\" with nothing that could ever read as done, or a body that only echoes its title. For an umbrella, epic or requirement card, the right checkable outcome is a definition of done that decomposes into child work, not an inline checklist: bundling many capabilities is expected of it.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there is nothing whose \"done\" this practice could define: a question, a support request, an open discussion, or a bare board or ceremony placeholder with no asserted problem and no requested capability. A missing checklist on something that was never engineering work is not a defect. A title that names a task or a capability (\"Add a dark-mode toggle\", \"Add package\") with an empty body is engineering work whose done was never defined: the gate passes and NOT_MET is the answer, never NOT_APPLICABLE.\n\n## Sources\n1. The title and the WHOLE body (`context/description.md`): every section, checklist, criteria block and expected-behaviour statement. It establishes a finish line you can quote. An image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n2. The labels and title (`context/metadata.json`): the kind of issue, including whether it is an umbrella, epic or requirement card. They never establish a finish line by themselves.\n3. The comments (`context/comments.json`): a finish line stated there counts. They never establish one you cannot quote.\n4. A linked design or spec document the issue delegates to, materialised as a `.md` file under `context/docs/`: when it states a concrete, testable contract THIS work must satisfy — specific behaviours, status codes, API or state conditions — that contract IS a quotable finish line. Read and quote it before concluding none exists. A generic guideline, or a placeholder document that does not pin this deliverable''s done-state, supplies nothing. A document never materialised is not evidence in either direction: when the issue delegates its finish line to a specific document you were not shown, report a collection gap and record no observation, never a claim against prose you were not shown.\n5. Child work: sub-issues or a task list referenced in the body or comments, or child entries in `context/project_inventory.json` when captured. For an umbrella, they establish the decomposition that makes its outcome checkable.\n\n## Judge\nThese conformance cases apply after the shared issue premise establishes a current request.\n- MET: the finish line is quotable from the body, a comment, or a materialised document; or, for an umbrella, children or tracked sub-issues already carry the decomposition — MET with no severity.\n- NOT_MET: a real deliverable to build, and no quotable outcome in the issue or any materialised document it delegates to. For an umbrella with substantive prose but no child-mapped done, this is a gentle decomposition nudge, not a hard failure; do not double-alarm.\n- UNDETERMINED: the standard itself has no ordinary undecidable case. A body read in full that states no finish line is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED; a document you were not shown is a collection gap.\nOne observation per review, over the whole issue.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR — a formative authoring practice, never a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: everything softer is a single nudge, including the umbrella decomposition nudge. MAJOR: a maintainer genuinely cannot tell when ANY of the work is done — a real deliverable with neither a verifiable outcome nor a decomposition.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the span you relied on: the checklist, criteria block, expected behaviour, the document sentence with its `context/docs/...` file path, or the child references. For NOT_MET, evidence.search records the boundary searched, and the citations quote from it: the whole body, the labels, the comments, and the `context/docs/` documents or their absence. Never invent a checklist, a clean bill or an exoneration you did not read. Never assert technical feasibility, design correctness or the author''s competence. Describe the observable gap in the issue, never the author''s character or motive.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the problem itself is well stated — observed versus expected, who/what/why as a narrative — belongs to `issue-states-an-actionable-problem`; do not write that relationship into the feedback. Here only: is there a verifiable finish line?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for issue-has-checkable-outcome: the task-list items the body carries and how the\n// issue is labelled and typed. Whether any of the text is a finish line is the criteria''s to decide.\nimport { bodyFact, classifyIssue, type IssueMetadata } from \"../lib/issue-classification.ts\";\nimport { checkableItems } from \"../lib/review.ts\";\nimport type { Hint } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default function issueHasCheckableOutcome(\n\t_repo: string,\n\t_diff: Map<string, unknown>,\n\tm: IssueMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst shape = classifyIssue(m);\n\tconst { body, issueType, labels, emptyOrTitleEcho, hasDeliverableType, looksUmbrella } = shape;\n\tconst items = checkableItems(body);\n\tconst checkedBoxes = items.filter((item) => item.checked).length;\n\n\tconst directions = [\n\t\t...bodyFact(shape),\n\t\t`Captured: ${String(items.length)} task-list item(s) in the body (${String(checkedBoxes)} ticked); labels ${labels.join(\", \") || \"none\"}; native type ${issueType || \"none\"}. Judge the standard once over the title, the whole body, the comments and any materialised document; these counts locate text and decide nothing.`,\n\t];\n\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tbodyLength: body.length,\n\t\t\ttaskCheckboxes: items.length,\n\t\t\tuncheckedBoxes: items.length - checkedBoxes,\n\t\t\tcheckedBoxes,\n\t\t\temptyOrTitleEcho: emptyOrTitleEcho ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\thasDeliverableType: hasDeliverableType ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tlooksUmbrella: looksUmbrella ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"IMPLEMENTATION_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Issue evidence does not establish whether the described work was implemented correctly."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Without a clear line for ''done'', nobody can tell when the work is finished, including the person doing it. A checkable outcome turns ''improve performance'' from an open-ended wish into a task someone can actually complete.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A short acceptance checklist a maintainer can tick off when the work is truly finished, such as ''export downloads a valid CSV'' and ''an empty result shows a friendly message'', rather than a vague hope of improvement.","groupSlug":"actionable-issue-authoring","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":"issue-structure","redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 182\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Open-ended wish</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"120.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"80\" width=\"144\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">Make export better</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"15\">Done is unclear</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Checkable outcome</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"120.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"80\" width=\"144\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"352.75\" y=\"98.75\" width=\"14.5\" height=\"14.5\" rx=\"3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">Downloads a valid CSV</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"352.75\" y=\"122.75\" width=\"14.5\" height=\"14.5\" rx=\"3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"15\">Empty results show a message</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"15\">Done is clear</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"An open-ended wish such as “Make export better” leaves done unclear. A checkable outcome lists “Downloads a valid CSV” and “Empty results show a message” as boxes to tick, so done is clear."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Write each point of done so that someone who reads the issue can answer yes or no. For example, instead of “make search better”, write “a search for an exact title shows that issue first”.\n- Pick the form that suits the work, such as a short checklist or a Given, When, Then block. The block gives the start state, the action, and the result you expect.\n- For a bug, state the behavior that the fix must bring back.\n- If a linked design document holds the finish line, check that it states what this work must do. A general guideline does not tell anyone when this issue is done.\n- For an epic or another umbrella issue, define done as its child issues. Each child issue then carries its own finish line.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The issue asks a question.\n- The issue is a support request.\n- The issue opens a discussion.\n- The issue is a placeholder on a board or for a team meeting, with no problem and no requested change.\n\nIt still applies when the title names a task, such as “Add a dark-mode toggle”, and the body is empty.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Adding a heading such as “Acceptance criteria” with vague points under it. The form does not matter. Instead, write each point so that someone can test it.\n- Repeating the title in the body. Instead, add what will be true when the work is finished.\n- Writing a goal that never ends, such as “improve performance”. Instead, name a result that someone can test.\n- Agreeing on the finish line only in a call or a chat. Instead, write it in the issue or in a comment, so that someone can quote it later.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [INVEST in good stories, and SMART tasks](https://xp123.com/invest-in-good-stories-and-smart-tasks/), Bill Wake, 2003. A good story is testable. If nobody knows how to test it, the story may not be clear enough.\n- [Given When Then](https://martinfowler.com/bliki/GivenWhenThen.html), Martin Fowler, 2013. A scenario states the starting state, the action, and the change that you expect.\n- [Acceptance testing](https://agilealliance.org/glossary/acceptance/), Agile Alliance glossary. Acceptance tests describe behavior as examples or scenarios, and each one passes or fails.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Define a checkable outcome","signals":["scm.issue.closed","scm.issue.opened","scm.issue.updated"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured issue: its title, description, labels and the discussion the practice names. Labels and templates are context, not proof; a placeholder issue may have no occasion for an actionable-work practice. Do not infer that a bug reproduces or that an outcome was implemented.\n\nFor a practice about requested work, establish the current request from the complete issue body and the discussion the practice names. Brief, vague or title-only requests still pass this premise.\n\nWhen genuinely conflicting current statements leave that request unresolved, record UNDETERMINED for the request-dependent judgment, cite the conflict, and judge neither statement under the standard. Position or date alone does not resolve it. If an explicit authored withdrawal leaves no work requested, record NOT_APPLICABLE for that judgment and treat earlier requirements as history. Judge a remaining request under the practice''s standard. Historical and closure practices retain their own subjects, including accounting for dropped or deferred work.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: a quotable finish line — an outcome a maintainer could later verify as DONE from the issue alone.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one thing: does this issue define an outcome a maintainer could later verify as done? A good issue draws a clear finish line: a reader can tell, from the issue alone, what state of the world means the work is complete. The line can take many shapes — an acceptance checklist, a Given-When-Then block, a story''s who/what/why phrased so the result is testable, or, for a bug, a stated expected behaviour a fix must restore. The form does not matter; the testability does. Use judgment as an experienced reviewer would; do not hunt for a keyword or label. Good: \"export downloads a valid CSV; an empty result shows a friendly message\". Bad: \"improve performance\" with nothing that could ever read as done, or a body that only echoes its title. For an umbrella, epic or requirement card, the right checkable outcome is a definition of done that decomposes into child work, not an inline checklist: bundling many capabilities is expected of it.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there is nothing whose \"done\" this practice could define: a question, a support request, an open discussion, or a bare board or ceremony placeholder with no asserted problem and no requested capability. A missing checklist on something that was never engineering work is not a defect. A title that names a task or a capability (\"Add a dark-mode toggle\", \"Add package\") with an empty body is engineering work whose done was never defined: the gate passes and NOT_MET is the answer, never NOT_APPLICABLE.\n\n## Sources\n1. The title and the WHOLE body (`context/description.md`): every section, checklist, criteria block and expected-behaviour statement. It establishes a finish line you can quote. An image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n2. The labels and title (`context/metadata.json`): the kind of issue, including whether it is an umbrella, epic or requirement card. They never establish a finish line by themselves.\n3. The comments (`context/comments.json`): a finish line stated there counts. They never establish one you cannot quote.\n4. A linked design or spec document the issue delegates to, materialised as a `.md` file under `context/docs/`: when it states a concrete, testable contract THIS work must satisfy — specific behaviours, status codes, API or state conditions — that contract IS a quotable finish line. Read and quote it before concluding none exists. A generic guideline, or a placeholder document that does not pin this deliverable''s done-state, supplies nothing. A document never materialised is not evidence in either direction: when the issue delegates its finish line to a specific document you were not shown, report a collection gap and record no observation, never a claim against prose you were not shown.\n5. Child work: sub-issues or a task list referenced in the body or comments, or child entries in `context/project_inventory.json` when captured. For an umbrella, they establish the decomposition that makes its outcome checkable.\n\n## Judge\nThese conformance cases apply after the shared issue premise establishes a current request.\n- MET: the finish line is quotable from the body, a comment, or a materialised document; or, for an umbrella, children or tracked sub-issues already carry the decomposition — MET with no severity.\n- NOT_MET: a real deliverable to build, and no quotable outcome in the issue or any materialised document it delegates to. For an umbrella with substantive prose but no child-mapped done, this is a gentle decomposition nudge, not a hard failure; do not double-alarm.\n- UNDETERMINED: the standard itself has no ordinary undecidable case. A body read in full that states no finish line is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED; a document you were not shown is a collection gap.\nOne observation per review, over the whole issue.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR — a formative authoring practice, never a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: everything softer is a single nudge, including the umbrella decomposition nudge. MAJOR: a maintainer genuinely cannot tell when ANY of the work is done — a real deliverable with neither a verifiable outcome nor a decomposition.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the span you relied on: the checklist, criteria block, expected behaviour, the document sentence with its `context/docs/...` file path, or the child references. For NOT_MET, evidence.search records the boundary searched, and the citations quote from it: the whole body, the labels, the comments, and the `context/docs/` documents or their absence. Never invent a checklist, a clean bill or an exoneration you did not read. Never assert technical feasibility, design correctness or the author''s competence. Describe the observable gap in the issue, never the author''s character or motive.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the problem itself is well stated — observed versus expected, who/what/why as a narrative — belongs to `issue-states-an-actionable-problem`; do not write that relationship into the feedback. Here only: is there a verifiable finish line?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for issue-has-checkable-outcome: the task-list items the body carries and how the\n// issue is labelled and typed. Whether any of the text is a finish line is the criteria''s to decide.\nimport { bodyFact, classifyIssue, type IssueMetadata } from \"../lib/issue-classification.ts\";\nimport { checkableItems } from \"../lib/review.ts\";\nimport type { Hint } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default function issueHasCheckableOutcome(\n\t_repo: string,\n\t_diff: Map<string, unknown>,\n\tm: IssueMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst shape = classifyIssue(m);\n\tconst { body, issueType, labels, emptyOrTitleEcho, hasDeliverableType, looksUmbrella } = shape;\n\tconst items = checkableItems(body);\n\tconst checkedBoxes = items.filter((item) => item.checked).length;\n\n\tconst directions = [\n\t\t...bodyFact(shape),\n\t\t`Captured: ${String(items.length)} task-list item(s) in the body (${String(checkedBoxes)} ticked); labels ${labels.join(\", \") || \"none\"}; native type ${issueType || \"none\"}. Judge the standard once over the title, the whole body, the comments and any materialised document; these counts locate text and decide nothing.`,\n\t];\n\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tbodyLength: body.length,\n\t\t\ttaskCheckboxes: items.length,\n\t\t\tuncheckedBoxes: items.length - checkedBoxes,\n\t\t\tcheckedBoxes,\n\t\t\temptyOrTitleEcho: emptyOrTitleEcho ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\thasDeliverableType: hasDeliverableType ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tlooksUmbrella: looksUmbrella ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"IMPLEMENTATION_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Issue evidence does not establish whether the described work was implemented correctly."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Without a clear line for ''done'', nobody can tell when the work is finished, including the person doing it. A checkable outcome turns ''improve performance'' from an open-ended wish into a task someone can actually complete.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A short acceptance checklist a maintainer can tick off when the work is truly finished, such as ''export downloads a valid CSV'' and ''an empty result shows a friendly message'', rather than a vague hope of improvement.","groupSlug":"actionable-issue-authoring","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":"issue-structure","redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 182\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Open-ended wish</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"120.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"80\" width=\"144\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">Make export better</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"15\">Done is unclear</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Checkable outcome</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"120.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"80\" width=\"144\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"352.75\" y=\"98.75\" width=\"14.5\" height=\"14.5\" rx=\"3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">Downloads a valid CSV</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"352.75\" y=\"122.75\" width=\"14.5\" height=\"14.5\" rx=\"3\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"15\">Empty results show a message</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"15\">Done is clear</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"An open-ended wish such as “Make export better” leaves done unclear. A checkable outcome lists “Downloads a valid CSV” and “Empty results show a message” as boxes to tick, so done is clear."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Write each point of done so that someone who reads the issue can answer yes or no. For example, instead of “make search better”, write “a search for an exact title shows that issue first”.\n- Pick the form that suits the work, such as a short checklist or a Given, When, Then block. The block gives the start state, the action, and the result you expect.\n- For a bug, state the behavior that the fix must bring back.\n- If a linked design document holds the finish line, check that it states what this work must do. A general guideline does not tell anyone when this issue is done.\n- For an epic or another umbrella issue, define done as its child issues. Each child issue then carries its own finish line.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The issue asks a question.\n- The issue is a support request.\n- The issue opens a discussion.\n- The issue is a placeholder on a board or for a team meeting, with no problem and no requested change.\n\nIt still applies when the title names a task, such as “Add a dark-mode toggle”, and the body is empty.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Adding a heading such as “Acceptance criteria” with vague points under it. The form does not matter. Instead, write each point so that someone can test it.\n- Repeating the title in the body. Instead, add what will be true when the work is finished.\n- Writing a goal that never ends, such as “improve performance”. Instead, name a result that someone can test.\n- Agreeing on the finish line only in a call or a chat. Instead, write it in the issue or in a comment, so that someone can quote it later.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [INVEST in good stories, and SMART tasks](https://xp123.com/invest-in-good-stories-and-smart-tasks/), Bill Wake, 2003. A good story is testable. If nobody knows how to test it, the story may not be clear enough.\n- [Given When Then](https://martinfowler.com/bliki/GivenWhenThen.html), Martin Fowler, 2013. A scenario states the starting state, the action, and the change that you expect.\n- [Acceptance testing](https://agilealliance.org/glossary/acceptance/), Agile Alliance glossary. Acceptance tests describe behavior as examples or scenarios, and each one passes or fails.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Every issue says what done looks like"'::jsonb),'v5:e8963f55575654401600ae1d3edc7b2ef48980e700cba35a9842f621f3997811','practice:v3:f531e5dc4e005cb0db012abbb9a4e3354a37eaf0f6861e60cc557c965fe6daab');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','issue-points-to-relevant-context','scm.issue','{"name":"Point the issue to the context it depends on","signals":["scm.issue.opened","scm.issue.updated"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured issue: its title, description, labels and the discussion the practice names. Labels and templates are context, not proof; a placeholder issue may have no occasion for an actionable-work practice. Do not infer that a bug reproduces or that an outcome was implemented.\n\nFor a practice about requested work, establish the current request from the complete issue body and the discussion the practice names. Brief, vague or title-only requests still pass this premise.\n\nWhen genuinely conflicting current statements leave that request unresolved, record UNDETERMINED for the request-dependent judgment, cite the conflict, and judge neither statement under the standard. Position or date alone does not resolve it. If an explicit authored withdrawal leaves no work requested, record NOT_APPLICABLE for that judgment and treat earlier requirements as history. Judge a remaining request under the practice''s standard. Historical and closure practices retain their own subjects, including accounting for dropped or deferred work.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: an issue that depends on a design, decision, spec, runbook or data model recorded elsewhere points the implementer to the document that holds it.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one thing: when an issue clearly depends on prior context an implementer would need to act on it correctly — an existing design, a decision record, a spec, a runbook, a data model — does the issue point them to that context with a link to the relevant document, instead of assuming the reader already carries it? The signal is judged from the issue''s own text: it leans on decisions or designs made elsewhere — \"per our architecture\", \"following the agreed rollout plan\", \"as decided for the billing redesign\", \"must match the data model\" — such that someone picking it up cold could not proceed correctly without finding that source. A link count is never the signal, and a link to an unrelated document is not credit. Do not infer a hidden dependency the text does not express.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the issue is self-contained: a clear bug report, a small well-described change, a question. Most issues are; prefer NOT_APPLICABLE whenever the issue reads as self-contained, and never penalize a self-contained issue for lacking links.\n\n## Sources\n1. The title and the whole body (`context/description.md`, `context/metadata.json`): every phrase that appeals to context made elsewhere, and every link. It establishes whether the issue leans on external context and whether it points anywhere. It never establishes that a linked document is relevant; a bare link is not evidence on its own. An image: read its caption and the sentence around it. A link outside the wiki counts on the same terms as a wiki link. The source location does not decide relevance.\n2. The captured linked documents, including wiki documents under `context/docs/`: read each to judge whether it is actually the context the issue leans on. A document establishes relevance when a passage in it carries the design, decision or spec the issue depends on. It never establishes relevance by its title alone.\n3. The whole discussion (`context/comments.json`): establish the current request under the shared issue premise before judging the dependency and its pointer.\nIf the body or discussion was not captured completely, report a collection gap and record no observation.\nWhen the issue links a specific document that was NOT materialized for you, you cannot tell whether it carries the context: report a collection gap and record no observation — neither credit nor criticism.\n\n## Judge\nThese conformance cases apply after the shared issue premise establishes a current request.\n- MET: the issue genuinely rests on prior design, decision or spec context and links the specific materialized document that carries it, so an implementer can proceed without hunting or guessing.\n- NOT_MET: the issue plainly leans on context made elsewhere but leaves the reader no pointer to it — nothing, or only a materialized document that does not carry that context — so they must reconstruct or reverse-engineer the intent.\n- UNDETERMINED: the standard itself has no ordinary undecidable case. Having read the whole issue and the materialized documents and found no pointer to the context it leans on is NOT_MET; a linked document that was not materialized is a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review, over the whole issue.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR — a non-blocking authoring nudge, not a gate. MINOR, the usual band: a mild lean on unstated context — a nudge to link the source. MAJOR only when the missing context is load-bearing enough that an implementer would likely build the wrong thing without it. When unsure, prefer the lower severity.\n\n## Grounding\nFor NOT_MET quote the exact phrase in the issue that appeals to external context (\"as decided…\", \"per the design\", \"must match…\") and state what context an implementer would need but is not pointed to. For MET cite the captured document''s file path and quote or closely paraphrase the passage that shows it is the relevant context. Never invent a document, a decision or a dependency; talk about the issue''s text, never the reporter''s intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the issue is actionable on its own terms belongs to `issue-states-an-actionable-problem`; the two are disjoint. Here only: does an issue that depends on external design or decision context actually point to it?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"IMPLEMENTATION_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Issue evidence does not establish whether the described work was implemented correctly."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"An issue that silently depends on decisions made elsewhere forces every implementer to re-discover the context — or, worse, to guess it and build the wrong thing.","whatGoodLooksLike":"When work depends on an existing design or decision, the issue links the document that holds it, so whoever picks it up starts with the rationale already in hand.","groupSlug":"actionable-issue-authoring","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 166\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Context assumed</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"190.5\" height=\"104.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">Per the agreed plan</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"104\" width=\"152\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"116\" width=\"112\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"224.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"78.5\" height=\"104.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(240 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M2.75 0.75h7l3.5 3.5v11h-10.5z\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M9.75 0.75v3.5h3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"240\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">Plan</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"240\" y=\"104\" width=\"48\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"240\" y=\"116\" width=\"32\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"162\" font-size=\"15\">Context guessed</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Context linked</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"190.5\" height=\"104.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">Per the rollout plan</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"104\" width=\"152\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"116\" width=\"112\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M528 56h32\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-accent\" cx=\"528\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-accent\" cx=\"560\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"560.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"78.5\" height=\"104.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(576 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M2.75 0.75h7l3.5 3.5v11h-10.5z\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M9.75 0.75v3.5h3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"576\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">Plan</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"576\" y=\"104\" width=\"48\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"576\" y=\"116\" width=\"32\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"162\" font-size=\"15\">Context known</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"An issue that says “Per the agreed plan” with no link to the plan leaves the context guessed. An issue that says “Per the rollout plan” and links the plan document gives the context known."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- When the issue says “as decided” or “per the design”, find the document that holds that design or decision.\n- Link that document. For example, instead of “follow the agreed rollout plan”, write “follow the billing rollout plan” and link it.\n- Say in your own words what the linked document decides. Some readers cannot open the link.\n- If nobody wrote the decision down, record it before you link to it.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The issue is a clear bug report that stands on its own.\n- The issue asks for a small change and describes it fully.\n- The issue asks a question.\n\nIt still applies when an issue says “as decided” or “per the design” and does not say where to find it.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Adding links to look thorough. More links do not help. A link to a document without the decision does not help either. Instead, link the document that holds the decision the issue leans on.\n- Linking a whole wiki space or a folder. The reader must still search for the decision. Instead, link the document that holds it.\n- Writing “as we discussed” and trusting that the reader was there. The person who picks up the issue may not have been. Instead, link the record of what was decided.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Writing good CL descriptions](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/developer/cl-descriptions.html), Google Engineering Practices. Google advises linking design documents in a change description. It also advises enough context in the text, because some readers cannot open a link.\n- [Documenting architecture decisions](https://www.cognitect.com/blog/2011/11/15/documenting-architecture-decisions), Michael Nygard, 2011. Without the recorded reason for a decision, a new teammate can only accept it blindly or change it blindly.\n- [Information needs in collocated software development teams](https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSE.2007.45), Ko, DeLine and Venolia, 2007. Developers often put off design questions, such as why code was written a certain way. The only source was a coworker who was away. Two of the authors work at Microsoft, which sells developer tools.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Point the issue to the context it depends on","signals":["scm.issue.opened","scm.issue.updated"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.issue.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured issue: its title, description, labels and the discussion the practice names. Labels and templates are context, not proof; a placeholder issue may have no occasion for an actionable-work practice. Do not infer that a bug reproduces or that an outcome was implemented.\n\nFor a practice about requested work, establish the current request from the complete issue body and the discussion the practice names. Brief, vague or title-only requests still pass this premise.\n\nWhen genuinely conflicting current statements leave that request unresolved, record UNDETERMINED for the request-dependent judgment, cite the conflict, and judge neither statement under the standard. Position or date alone does not resolve it. If an explicit authored withdrawal leaves no work requested, record NOT_APPLICABLE for that judgment and treat earlier requirements as history. Judge a remaining request under the practice''s standard. Historical and closure practices retain their own subjects, including accounting for dropped or deferred work.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: an issue that depends on a design, decision, spec, runbook or data model recorded elsewhere points the implementer to the document that holds it.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one thing: when an issue clearly depends on prior context an implementer would need to act on it correctly — an existing design, a decision record, a spec, a runbook, a data model — does the issue point them to that context with a link to the relevant document, instead of assuming the reader already carries it? The signal is judged from the issue''s own text: it leans on decisions or designs made elsewhere — \"per our architecture\", \"following the agreed rollout plan\", \"as decided for the billing redesign\", \"must match the data model\" — such that someone picking it up cold could not proceed correctly without finding that source. A link count is never the signal, and a link to an unrelated document is not credit. Do not infer a hidden dependency the text does not express.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the issue is self-contained: a clear bug report, a small well-described change, a question. Most issues are; prefer NOT_APPLICABLE whenever the issue reads as self-contained, and never penalize a self-contained issue for lacking links.\n\n## Sources\n1. The title and the whole body (`context/description.md`, `context/metadata.json`): every phrase that appeals to context made elsewhere, and every link. It establishes whether the issue leans on external context and whether it points anywhere. It never establishes that a linked document is relevant; a bare link is not evidence on its own. An image: read its caption and the sentence around it. A link outside the wiki counts on the same terms as a wiki link. The source location does not decide relevance.\n2. The captured linked documents, including wiki documents under `context/docs/`: read each to judge whether it is actually the context the issue leans on. A document establishes relevance when a passage in it carries the design, decision or spec the issue depends on. It never establishes relevance by its title alone.\n3. The whole discussion (`context/comments.json`): establish the current request under the shared issue premise before judging the dependency and its pointer.\nIf the body or discussion was not captured completely, report a collection gap and record no observation.\nWhen the issue links a specific document that was NOT materialized for you, you cannot tell whether it carries the context: report a collection gap and record no observation — neither credit nor criticism.\n\n## Judge\nThese conformance cases apply after the shared issue premise establishes a current request.\n- MET: the issue genuinely rests on prior design, decision or spec context and links the specific materialized document that carries it, so an implementer can proceed without hunting or guessing.\n- NOT_MET: the issue plainly leans on context made elsewhere but leaves the reader no pointer to it — nothing, or only a materialized document that does not carry that context — so they must reconstruct or reverse-engineer the intent.\n- UNDETERMINED: the standard itself has no ordinary undecidable case. Having read the whole issue and the materialized documents and found no pointer to the context it leans on is NOT_MET; a linked document that was not materialized is a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review, over the whole issue.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR — a non-blocking authoring nudge, not a gate. MINOR, the usual band: a mild lean on unstated context — a nudge to link the source. MAJOR only when the missing context is load-bearing enough that an implementer would likely build the wrong thing without it. When unsure, prefer the lower severity.\n\n## Grounding\nFor NOT_MET quote the exact phrase in the issue that appeals to external context (\"as decided…\", \"per the design\", \"must match…\") and state what context an implementer would need but is not pointed to. For MET cite the captured document''s file path and quote or closely paraphrase the passage that shows it is the relevant context. Never invent a document, a decision or a dependency; talk about the issue''s text, never the reporter''s intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the issue is actionable on its own terms belongs to `issue-states-an-actionable-problem`; the two are disjoint. Here only: does an issue that depends on external design or decision context actually point to it?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"IMPLEMENTATION_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Issue evidence does not establish whether the described work was implemented correctly."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"An issue that silently depends on decisions made elsewhere forces every implementer to re-discover the context — or, worse, to guess it and build the wrong thing.","whatGoodLooksLike":"When work depends on an existing design or decision, the issue links the document that holds it, so whoever picks it up starts with the rationale already in hand.","groupSlug":"actionable-issue-authoring","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 166\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Context assumed</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"190.5\" height=\"104.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">Per the agreed plan</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"104\" width=\"152\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"116\" width=\"112\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"224.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"78.5\" height=\"104.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(240 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M2.75 0.75h7l3.5 3.5v11h-10.5z\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M9.75 0.75v3.5h3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"240\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">Plan</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"240\" y=\"104\" width=\"48\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"240\" y=\"116\" width=\"32\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"162\" font-size=\"15\">Context guessed</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Context linked</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"190.5\" height=\"104.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"6.75\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-fill-ink\" cx=\"8\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.75\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Issue</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">Per the rollout plan</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"104\" width=\"152\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"116\" width=\"112\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M528 56h32\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-accent\" cx=\"528\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-accent\" cx=\"560\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"560.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"78.5\" height=\"104.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(576 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M2.75 0.75h7l3.5 3.5v11h-10.5z\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M9.75 0.75v3.5h3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"576\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">Plan</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"576\" y=\"104\" width=\"48\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"576\" y=\"116\" width=\"32\" height=\"6\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"162\" font-size=\"15\">Context known</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"An issue that says “Per the agreed plan” with no link to the plan leaves the context guessed. An issue that says “Per the rollout plan” and links the plan document gives the context known."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- When the issue says “as decided” or “per the design”, find the document that holds that design or decision.\n- Link that document. For example, instead of “follow the agreed rollout plan”, write “follow the billing rollout plan” and link it.\n- Say in your own words what the linked document decides. Some readers cannot open the link.\n- If nobody wrote the decision down, record it before you link to it.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The issue is a clear bug report that stands on its own.\n- The issue asks for a small change and describes it fully.\n- The issue asks a question.\n\nIt still applies when an issue says “as decided” or “per the design” and does not say where to find it.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Adding links to look thorough. More links do not help. A link to a document without the decision does not help either. Instead, link the document that holds the decision the issue leans on.\n- Linking a whole wiki space or a folder. The reader must still search for the decision. Instead, link the document that holds it.\n- Writing “as we discussed” and trusting that the reader was there. The person who picks up the issue may not have been. Instead, link the record of what was decided.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Writing good CL descriptions](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/developer/cl-descriptions.html), Google Engineering Practices. Google advises linking design documents in a change description. It also advises enough context in the text, because some readers cannot open a link.\n- [Documenting architecture decisions](https://www.cognitect.com/blog/2011/11/15/documenting-architecture-decisions), Michael Nygard, 2011. Without the recorded reason for a decision, a new teammate can only accept it blindly or change it blindly.\n- [Information needs in collocated software development teams](https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSE.2007.45), Ko, DeLine and Venolia, 2007. Developers often put off design questions, such as why code was written a certain way. The only source was a coworker who was away. Two of the authors work at Microsoft, which sells developer tools.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Issues link the design or decision they rest on"'::jsonb),'v5:5cef4d90a71d37cd2796286cb559baefc33d1f7b12f1f02fd309ff7c3c7f1a6e','practice:v3:1dcf2d695657887c43c4ba491e119ad1e3354fb96ca8e47736364217bc2acde3');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('GROUP','constructive-code-review',NULL,'{"name":"Reviewing a teammate''s work constructively","description":"Review a teammate''s change well. Leave specific, actionable comments, raise concerns as questions rather than commands, and read the change before approving it rather than waving it through.","icon":"Eye","color":"teal"}'::jsonb,'{"name":"Reviewing a teammate''s work constructively","description":"Review a teammate''s change well. Leave specific, actionable comments, raise concerns as questions rather than commands, and read the change before approving it rather than waving it through.","icon":"Eye","color":"teal"}'::jsonb,'b6bd4a5e8a8c5cfcfc65fcab47551c3e8fac00dec19051bb1ab261e2557dfbf7','group:v1:b6bd4a5e8a8c5cfcfc65fcab47551c3e8fac00dec19051bb1ab261e2557dfbf7');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','leaves-useful-specific-review-comments','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Leave specific, actionable review comments","signals":["scm.pull_request.reviewed"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"REVIEWER","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: the substantive comments a reviewer wrote on this pull request each point at something concrete and tell the author what to do about it.\n\n## The standard\nJudge the craft of the comments a reviewer wrote — never whether the reviewer''s claim about the code is correct, and never the author''s code. A useful comment names a specific construct or anchors to a real line AND states an actionable substance: what is wrong, what to change, a concrete alternative, or a pointed question a maintainer can actually answer. A vague comment is a content-free gesture that leaves the author guessing: \"this is messy\", \"fix this\", \"cleanup\", a bare \"?\". Inline (diff-anchored) notes and conversation-tab notes meet the same standard. A blunt but specific comment is useful here; a polite but vague one is not. A reviewer comment is one whose author differs from the pull request author in the metadata.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. The practice judges reviewer activity, not the changed code: \"a big pull request got little review\" is not an observation, and an unreviewed or draft pull request is never a substandard review. Build one list of every review note in your context, wherever it is carried — inline arrays, general or conversation notes, any discussion thread rendered in the artifact text. Set aside the notes that carry no reviewer-craft signal: the author''s own self-notes; automation output (a comment the record flags `bot`: an access token, an app, a review service — the reviewer craft judged here is a person''s); bare approvals or acknowledgements (\"LGTM\", \"+1\"); author-uptake or status replies that report what was done rather than ask for a change; off-topic chatter unconnected to anything this diff touches. When no substantive reviewer comment survives, the occasion never arose, whether or not `context/review_threads.json` records a bare approval — the common case for a freshly opened pull request: record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop — never absent. Do not abstain merely because a field name or flag is missing while a real reviewer comment is plainly visible: read it and judge it.\n\n## Sources\n1. The inline review comments (`context/comments.json`): each comment''s author, body and path:line anchor. The review decisions (`context/review_threads.json`) add the body a reviewer wrote with an approval or a request for changes. They establish the diff-anchored comments and their positions.\n2. The conversation notes (`context/general_comments.json`): each note''s author and body. A conversation note has no position; it establishes only author and body.\n3. The pull request metadata (`context/metadata.json`): the pull request author, which separates reviewer comments from author notes.\n4. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the hunk a comment anchors to. Read it to confirm the comment points at something real. It never establishes whether the comment''s claim is correct; assess the comment, not the code.\nIf the artifact shows a review happened but the comment bodies were not materialized for you, reviewer craft exists that you could not read: report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nassess the standard against all relevant evidence over the substantive reviewer comments that remain.\n- MET: every substantive comment is specific and actionable — the author can tell what to change.\n- NOT_MET: vague comments leave the author guessing — from a stray straggler among useful notes to a review that is predominantly empty gestures. Quote each one.\n- UNDETERMINED: no ordinary case. A comment you can read is judged; one you cannot read is a collection gap.\nOne observation per review, over all the substantive reviewer comments together.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on comment quality alone — a workflow practice, not a merge gate. Scale to how much of the review is content-free. MINOR, the usual band: a stray vague straggler; a lone unhelpful comment is a light cost, never a serious one. MAJOR: a review that is predominantly empty gestures, leaving the author unable to act. Equivalent situations yield equivalent severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote verbatim every comment you call vague — with its path:line for an inline comment; by author and body for a conversation note, which has no position, so never invent a file:line for it. Quote at least one useful comment when one exists. Never call a comment vague without quoting it; never fabricate a comment to avoid looking idle. Talk about the comments; never grade the reviewer as a person or impute intent.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the author took the comments up or left threads open belongs to `engaging-with-inline-review-comments`; tone and politeness belong to `reviews-respectfully-asks-rather-than-demands`. Here only: could the author act on what the reviewer wrote?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"''This looks off'' leaves the author guessing what to change and why. A comment that points at the exact line and names the concern gets fixed, while a vague one just slows the change down.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A note anchored to the line that says ''this throws when items is empty, so guard the loop or return early on a null list'', so the author knows exactly what to do next.","groupSlug":"constructive-code-review","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 196\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Vague comment</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">return items.get(0);</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M80 70h108M134 70v34\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M36.75 104.75H291.25A12 12 0 0 1 303.25 116.75V155.25A12 12 0 0 1 291.25 167.25H24.75V116.75A12 12 0 0 1 36.75 104.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"134\" cy=\"104\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"40\" y=\"133\" font-size=\"15\">Fix this</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"15\">Author left guessing</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Specific comment</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">return items.get(0);</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M416 70h108M470 70v34\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M372.75 104.75H627.25A12 12 0 0 1 639.25 116.75V155.25A12 12 0 0 1 627.25 167.25H360.75V116.75A12 12 0 0 1 372.75 104.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"470\" cy=\"104\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"376\" y=\"133\" font-size=\"15\">Throws on empty list,</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"376\" y=\"153\" font-size=\"15\">return early</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"15\">Author can act</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"On items.get(0) in return items.get(0);, a vague comment says only “Fix this”, and the author is left guessing. A specific comment says “Throws on empty list, return early”, so the author can act."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Point at the exact place. Comment on the line, or name the file, function or value.\n- Say what is wrong and what to change. Instead of “Fix this”, write “`retries` never resets, so reset it after a successful call”.\n- If you are not sure of the fix, name the problem or ask a question that the author can answer.\n- Give a note on the conversation the same detail as a note on a line. Name the file or function in it.\n- Mark an optional suggestion with “Optional:”. Then the author knows which changes you need.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The practice looks at your comments, not at the code. A large pull or merge request with little review is not a lapse.\n- The pull or merge request has no substantive review comment yet.\n- The note is the author’s own note.\n- The note comes from a bot, an app or an automated review service.\n- The note is only an approval, such as “LGTM” or “+1”.\n- The note reports what someone did and asks for no change.\n- The note is off-topic and is not about the change.\n\nIt still applies when you write the note on the conversation and not on a line.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Softening a point until it says nothing, such as “Maybe look at this again”. Instead, name the problem and the change.\n- Leaving a bare “?” on a line. Instead, write the question you want answered, such as which input reaches this branch.\n- Writing “Needs cleanup” on the conversation. Instead, name the file or function and say what to clean up.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [How to write code review comments](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/comments.html), Google Engineering Practices. Google advises a balance between pointing out problems and giving direct guidance. It also suggests labels that tell required changes from optional suggestions.\n- [Characteristics of useful code reviews: an empirical study at Microsoft](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/characteristics-of-useful-code-reviews-an-empirical-study-at-microsoft/), Bosu, Greiler and Bird, 2015. Comments that authors found useful often led to a change near the lines that they pointed at. Microsoft sells code review tools.\n- [Predicting usefulness of code review comments using textual features and developer experience](https://doi.org/10.1109/MSR.2017.17), Rahman, Roy and Kula, 2017. Useful comments named relevant code elements and shared more words with the changed code.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Leave specific, actionable review comments","signals":["scm.pull_request.reviewed"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"REVIEWER","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: the substantive comments a reviewer wrote on this pull request each point at something concrete and tell the author what to do about it.\n\n## The standard\nJudge the craft of the comments a reviewer wrote — never whether the reviewer''s claim about the code is correct, and never the author''s code. A useful comment names a specific construct or anchors to a real line AND states an actionable substance: what is wrong, what to change, a concrete alternative, or a pointed question a maintainer can actually answer. A vague comment is a content-free gesture that leaves the author guessing: \"this is messy\", \"fix this\", \"cleanup\", a bare \"?\". Inline (diff-anchored) notes and conversation-tab notes meet the same standard. A blunt but specific comment is useful here; a polite but vague one is not. A reviewer comment is one whose author differs from the pull request author in the metadata.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. The practice judges reviewer activity, not the changed code: \"a big pull request got little review\" is not an observation, and an unreviewed or draft pull request is never a substandard review. Build one list of every review note in your context, wherever it is carried — inline arrays, general or conversation notes, any discussion thread rendered in the artifact text. Set aside the notes that carry no reviewer-craft signal: the author''s own self-notes; automation output (a comment the record flags `bot`: an access token, an app, a review service — the reviewer craft judged here is a person''s); bare approvals or acknowledgements (\"LGTM\", \"+1\"); author-uptake or status replies that report what was done rather than ask for a change; off-topic chatter unconnected to anything this diff touches. When no substantive reviewer comment survives, the occasion never arose, whether or not `context/review_threads.json` records a bare approval — the common case for a freshly opened pull request: record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop — never absent. Do not abstain merely because a field name or flag is missing while a real reviewer comment is plainly visible: read it and judge it.\n\n## Sources\n1. The inline review comments (`context/comments.json`): each comment''s author, body and path:line anchor. The review decisions (`context/review_threads.json`) add the body a reviewer wrote with an approval or a request for changes. They establish the diff-anchored comments and their positions.\n2. The conversation notes (`context/general_comments.json`): each note''s author and body. A conversation note has no position; it establishes only author and body.\n3. The pull request metadata (`context/metadata.json`): the pull request author, which separates reviewer comments from author notes.\n4. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the hunk a comment anchors to. Read it to confirm the comment points at something real. It never establishes whether the comment''s claim is correct; assess the comment, not the code.\nIf the artifact shows a review happened but the comment bodies were not materialized for you, reviewer craft exists that you could not read: report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nassess the standard against all relevant evidence over the substantive reviewer comments that remain.\n- MET: every substantive comment is specific and actionable — the author can tell what to change.\n- NOT_MET: vague comments leave the author guessing — from a stray straggler among useful notes to a review that is predominantly empty gestures. Quote each one.\n- UNDETERMINED: no ordinary case. A comment you can read is judged; one you cannot read is a collection gap.\nOne observation per review, over all the substantive reviewer comments together.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on comment quality alone — a workflow practice, not a merge gate. Scale to how much of the review is content-free. MINOR, the usual band: a stray vague straggler; a lone unhelpful comment is a light cost, never a serious one. MAJOR: a review that is predominantly empty gestures, leaving the author unable to act. Equivalent situations yield equivalent severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote verbatim every comment you call vague — with its path:line for an inline comment; by author and body for a conversation note, which has no position, so never invent a file:line for it. Quote at least one useful comment when one exists. Never call a comment vague without quoting it; never fabricate a comment to avoid looking idle. Talk about the comments; never grade the reviewer as a person or impute intent.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the author took the comments up or left threads open belongs to `engaging-with-inline-review-comments`; tone and politeness belong to `reviews-respectfully-asks-rather-than-demands`. Here only: could the author act on what the reviewer wrote?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"''This looks off'' leaves the author guessing what to change and why. A comment that points at the exact line and names the concern gets fixed, while a vague one just slows the change down.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A note anchored to the line that says ''this throws when items is empty, so guard the loop or return early on a null list'', so the author knows exactly what to do next.","groupSlug":"constructive-code-review","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 196\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Vague comment</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">return items.get(0);</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M80 70h108M134 70v34\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M36.75 104.75H291.25A12 12 0 0 1 303.25 116.75V155.25A12 12 0 0 1 291.25 167.25H24.75V116.75A12 12 0 0 1 36.75 104.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"134\" cy=\"104\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"40\" y=\"133\" font-size=\"15\">Fix this</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"15\">Author left guessing</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Specific comment</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">return items.get(0);</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M416 70h108M470 70v34\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M372.75 104.75H627.25A12 12 0 0 1 639.25 116.75V155.25A12 12 0 0 1 627.25 167.25H360.75V116.75A12 12 0 0 1 372.75 104.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"470\" cy=\"104\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"376\" y=\"133\" font-size=\"15\">Throws on empty list,</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"376\" y=\"153\" font-size=\"15\">return early</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"15\">Author can act</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"On items.get(0) in return items.get(0);, a vague comment says only “Fix this”, and the author is left guessing. A specific comment says “Throws on empty list, return early”, so the author can act."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Point at the exact place. Comment on the line, or name the file, function or value.\n- Say what is wrong and what to change. Instead of “Fix this”, write “`retries` never resets, so reset it after a successful call”.\n- If you are not sure of the fix, name the problem or ask a question that the author can answer.\n- Give a note on the conversation the same detail as a note on a line. Name the file or function in it.\n- Mark an optional suggestion with “Optional:”. Then the author knows which changes you need.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The practice looks at your comments, not at the code. A large pull or merge request with little review is not a lapse.\n- The pull or merge request has no substantive review comment yet.\n- The note is the author’s own note.\n- The note comes from a bot, an app or an automated review service.\n- The note is only an approval, such as “LGTM” or “+1”.\n- The note reports what someone did and asks for no change.\n- The note is off-topic and is not about the change.\n\nIt still applies when you write the note on the conversation and not on a line.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Softening a point until it says nothing, such as “Maybe look at this again”. Instead, name the problem and the change.\n- Leaving a bare “?” on a line. Instead, write the question you want answered, such as which input reaches this branch.\n- Writing “Needs cleanup” on the conversation. Instead, name the file or function and say what to clean up.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [How to write code review comments](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/comments.html), Google Engineering Practices. Google advises a balance between pointing out problems and giving direct guidance. It also suggests labels that tell required changes from optional suggestions.\n- [Characteristics of useful code reviews: an empirical study at Microsoft](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/characteristics-of-useful-code-reviews-an-empirical-study-at-microsoft/), Bosu, Greiler and Bird, 2015. Comments that authors found useful often led to a change near the lines that they pointed at. Microsoft sells code review tools.\n- [Predicting usefulness of code review comments using textual features and developer experience](https://doi.org/10.1109/MSR.2017.17), Rahman, Roy and Kula, 2017. Useful comments named relevant code elements and shared more words with the changed code.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Review comments name the line and the fix"'::jsonb),'v5:7e3837c8bee1d842bacdb6020f3ab657880177b8eb4c31055f724b783731b19d','practice:v3:97ab51f62b22447151d801a62dc41174d67d57d7cd76efdc65c911401cc1f40d');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','reviews-respectfully-asks-rather-than-demands','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Comment on the code, not the person","signals":["scm.pull_request.reviewed"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"REVIEWER","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: how the REVIEWER''s substantive comments are voiced — as questions, suggestions and reasoned disagreement aimed at the code, or as orders, sarcasm or contempt aimed at the person.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether the reviewer''s comments stay civil and collaborative: concerns raised as questions or suggestions, disagreement carrying a reason (\"I''d prefer X because Y\"), rather than bare orders, belittling, sarcasm, contempt, name-calling or hostility aimed at the author. This is about HOW feedback is voiced, never whether the technical point is correct. Directness, brevity and strong technical disagreement are not incivility: \"This will NPE when items is empty\" is blunt and civil. A command paired with a reason is fine. Do not flag a word like \"just\" or \"obviously\" when it reads neutrally; judge the whole phrasing in context as an experienced reviewer would. The subject is the reviewer, never the changed code: \"a big pull request got little review\" is not an observation.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. A reviewer is anyone whose author differs from the pull request author; never grade the author''s own words. Skip comments the record flags `bot` and bare approvals (\"LGTM\", \"+1\") — they carry no human reviewer tone. The flag is the provider''s classification; never guess from the shape of a login: a numeric or token-like login without the flag is a real human reviewer, not automation. When no substantive reviewer comment survives this filter, whether or not a bare approval was recorded — the common case for a freshly opened, unreviewed pull request — the occasion never arose: record NOT_APPLICABLE, say so, and stop. Never absent; an unreviewed pull request is never a substandard review. This is the only NOT_APPLICABLE case.\n\n## Sources\n1. The pull request record (`context/metadata.json`): the author, to apply the filter, and the state. It establishes who is a reviewer. It never establishes tone.\n2. Inline review comments (`context/comments.json`): each carries path, line, body and author. The review decisions (`context/review_threads.json`) add the body a reviewer wrote with an approval or a request for changes. They establish what a reviewer said at a position in the code.\n3. Conversation-tab comments (`context/general_comments.json`): author and body, no path or line. They establish what a reviewer said about the change as a whole.\nJudge tone across both comment sources. An image in a comment: read its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing. If the record shows a review happened but the comment bodies are not in your context, there was reviewer speech you could not read: report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: at least one substantive reviewer comment survives the filter and every one is civil and collaborative — a concern posed so the author can respond, disagreement kept on the code.\n- NOT_MET: one or more comments carry a quotable incivility marker: a bare order with no reason and no softening (\"Change this.\"), belittling that implies the author should not have needed telling, sarcasm or contempt, name-calling, hostility aimed at the person.\n- UNDETERMINED when the complete captured context leaves the tone of a quoted comment genuinely ambiguous; name what would settle it. Comment bodies you could not acquire are a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review, over every surviving reviewer comment.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on tone alone — a formative communication practice, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a single sharp-but-impersonal edge. MAJOR: a pattern across several comments, or contempt, name-calling or hostility aimed at the person. Identical comments yield identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote every marker you count verbatim, with its author; never fabricate a comment, a clean bill or an exoneration. Cite an inline comment only by the path and line it actually carries; cite a conversation-tab comment by author and quoted text and leave the location empty. Frame everything about the COMMENT — \"this phrasing reads as a demand\" — never about the reviewer as a person; no hunches, no psychoanalysing, no intent inferred beyond the words. For MET, cite the surviving comments you read.\n\n## Defer\nWhether a comment is specific, actionable or technically right belongs to `leaves-useful-specific-review-comments` and `reviews-substantively-with-understanding`; a vague-but-civil comment is still MET here. Here only: does the phrasing land on the code and not on the author?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"The same point can be made as a question or as a put-down, and people remember which one you chose. A curious question gets acted on, while sarcasm and bare orders breed defensiveness and make the next review harder.","whatGoodLooksLike":"''What happens if this runs twice, could we hit a race here?'' rather than ''this is obviously broken, did you even test it?'', so disagreement lands on the code and never on the author.","groupSlug":"constructive-code-review","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 188\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">About the person</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12.75 32.75H291.25A12 12 0 0 1 303.25 44.75V67.25A12 12 0 0 1 291.25 79.25H0.75V44.75A12 12 0 0 1 12.75 32.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Did you even test this?</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M16 80v32\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"16\" cy=\"80\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"16\" cy=\"112\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"112.75\" width=\"106.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"24\" cy=\"136\" r=\"8\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"141\" font-size=\"15\">Author</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"184\" font-size=\"15\">Breeds defensiveness</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">About the code</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M348.75 32.75H627.25A12 12 0 0 1 639.25 44.75V67.25A12 12 0 0 1 627.25 79.25H336.75V44.75A12 12 0 0 1 348.75 32.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">What if this runs twice?</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M352 80v32\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-accent\" cx=\"352\" cy=\"80\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-accent\" cx=\"352\" cy=\"112\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"112.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"141\" font-size=\"15\">saveOrder()</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"184\" font-size=\"15\">Gets acted on</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A comment such as “Did you even test this?” points at the Author, and it breeds defensiveness. A question such as “What if this runs twice?” points at the code, saveOrder(), and it gets acted on."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Write about the code, never about the person who wrote it. Say what the code does, not what the author failed to do.\n- When you have a concern, ask a real question or make a suggestion that the author can answer.\n- When you ask for a change, give your reason. For example, instead of “Change this”, write “Could we use a map here? The list lookup scans the whole list for each item”.\n- When you disagree, say what you prefer and why: “I’d prefer X because Y”.\n- Before you post, remove sarcasm and any words that say the author should have known better.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- You wrote the pull or merge request. Your own comments on it do not count.\n- A bot wrote the comment.\n- The only comment is a bare approval, such as “LGTM” or “+1”.\n- No reviewer has left a real comment yet, such as on a new pull or merge request.\n\nIt still applies when an account name looks automated but the account is not marked as a bot.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that being direct is rude. A short, blunt comment about the code is fine, such as “This fails when the list is empty”. Strong technical disagreement is fine too. Keep the point clear, and keep it on the code.\n- Checking only for single words such as “just” or “obviously”. Such a word is fine when it reads neutrally. Read the whole comment, and check where it points: at the code or at the author.\n- Asking a question only to belittle, such as “Did you even run this?”. The author cannot answer it. Instead, say what you think is wrong and why.\n- Letting frustration show when a change comes back again. Frustration, name-calling and impatience are common in uncivil reviews. Instead, restate the open concern and give your reason.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [How to write code review comments](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/comments.html), Google Engineering Practices. Comment on the code and never on the developer, and often explain why you ask for a change.\n- [Destructive criticism in software code review impacts inclusion](https://research.google/pubs/destructive-criticism-in-software-code-review-impacts-inclusion/), Gunawardena and others, 2022. The study surveyed 93 software practitioners. Women saw destructive criticism as less appropriate. They were also less motivated to keep working with the developer who gave it.\n- [Characterizing incivility in open source code review discussions](https://doi.org/10.1145/3479497), Ferreira, Cheng and Adams, 2021. In Linux kernel reviews, the most frequent features of uncivil emails were frustration, name calling and impatience. The study also found civil ways to disagree.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Comment on the code, not the person","signals":["scm.pull_request.reviewed"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"REVIEWER","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: how the REVIEWER''s substantive comments are voiced — as questions, suggestions and reasoned disagreement aimed at the code, or as orders, sarcasm or contempt aimed at the person.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether the reviewer''s comments stay civil and collaborative: concerns raised as questions or suggestions, disagreement carrying a reason (\"I''d prefer X because Y\"), rather than bare orders, belittling, sarcasm, contempt, name-calling or hostility aimed at the author. This is about HOW feedback is voiced, never whether the technical point is correct. Directness, brevity and strong technical disagreement are not incivility: \"This will NPE when items is empty\" is blunt and civil. A command paired with a reason is fine. Do not flag a word like \"just\" or \"obviously\" when it reads neutrally; judge the whole phrasing in context as an experienced reviewer would. The subject is the reviewer, never the changed code: \"a big pull request got little review\" is not an observation.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. A reviewer is anyone whose author differs from the pull request author; never grade the author''s own words. Skip comments the record flags `bot` and bare approvals (\"LGTM\", \"+1\") — they carry no human reviewer tone. The flag is the provider''s classification; never guess from the shape of a login: a numeric or token-like login without the flag is a real human reviewer, not automation. When no substantive reviewer comment survives this filter, whether or not a bare approval was recorded — the common case for a freshly opened, unreviewed pull request — the occasion never arose: record NOT_APPLICABLE, say so, and stop. Never absent; an unreviewed pull request is never a substandard review. This is the only NOT_APPLICABLE case.\n\n## Sources\n1. The pull request record (`context/metadata.json`): the author, to apply the filter, and the state. It establishes who is a reviewer. It never establishes tone.\n2. Inline review comments (`context/comments.json`): each carries path, line, body and author. The review decisions (`context/review_threads.json`) add the body a reviewer wrote with an approval or a request for changes. They establish what a reviewer said at a position in the code.\n3. Conversation-tab comments (`context/general_comments.json`): author and body, no path or line. They establish what a reviewer said about the change as a whole.\nJudge tone across both comment sources. An image in a comment: read its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing. If the record shows a review happened but the comment bodies are not in your context, there was reviewer speech you could not read: report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: at least one substantive reviewer comment survives the filter and every one is civil and collaborative — a concern posed so the author can respond, disagreement kept on the code.\n- NOT_MET: one or more comments carry a quotable incivility marker: a bare order with no reason and no softening (\"Change this.\"), belittling that implies the author should not have needed telling, sarcasm or contempt, name-calling, hostility aimed at the person.\n- UNDETERMINED when the complete captured context leaves the tone of a quoted comment genuinely ambiguous; name what would settle it. Comment bodies you could not acquire are a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review, over every surviving reviewer comment.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on tone alone — a formative communication practice, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a single sharp-but-impersonal edge. MAJOR: a pattern across several comments, or contempt, name-calling or hostility aimed at the person. Identical comments yield identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote every marker you count verbatim, with its author; never fabricate a comment, a clean bill or an exoneration. Cite an inline comment only by the path and line it actually carries; cite a conversation-tab comment by author and quoted text and leave the location empty. Frame everything about the COMMENT — \"this phrasing reads as a demand\" — never about the reviewer as a person; no hunches, no psychoanalysing, no intent inferred beyond the words. For MET, cite the surviving comments you read.\n\n## Defer\nWhether a comment is specific, actionable or technically right belongs to `leaves-useful-specific-review-comments` and `reviews-substantively-with-understanding`; a vague-but-civil comment is still MET here. Here only: does the phrasing land on the code and not on the author?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"The same point can be made as a question or as a put-down, and people remember which one you chose. A curious question gets acted on, while sarcasm and bare orders breed defensiveness and make the next review harder.","whatGoodLooksLike":"''What happens if this runs twice, could we hit a race here?'' rather than ''this is obviously broken, did you even test it?'', so disagreement lands on the code and never on the author.","groupSlug":"constructive-code-review","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 188\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">About the person</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12.75 32.75H291.25A12 12 0 0 1 303.25 44.75V67.25A12 12 0 0 1 291.25 79.25H0.75V44.75A12 12 0 0 1 12.75 32.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Did you even test this?</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M16 80v32\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"16\" cy=\"80\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"16\" cy=\"112\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"112.75\" width=\"106.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"24\" cy=\"136\" r=\"8\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"141\" font-size=\"15\">Author</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"184\" font-size=\"15\">Breeds defensiveness</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">About the code</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M348.75 32.75H627.25A12 12 0 0 1 639.25 44.75V67.25A12 12 0 0 1 627.25 79.25H336.75V44.75A12 12 0 0 1 348.75 32.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">What if this runs twice?</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M352 80v32\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-accent\" cx=\"352\" cy=\"80\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-accent\" cx=\"352\" cy=\"112\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"112.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"141\" font-size=\"15\">saveOrder()</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"184\" font-size=\"15\">Gets acted on</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A comment such as “Did you even test this?” points at the Author, and it breeds defensiveness. A question such as “What if this runs twice?” points at the code, saveOrder(), and it gets acted on."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Write about the code, never about the person who wrote it. Say what the code does, not what the author failed to do.\n- When you have a concern, ask a real question or make a suggestion that the author can answer.\n- When you ask for a change, give your reason. For example, instead of “Change this”, write “Could we use a map here? The list lookup scans the whole list for each item”.\n- When you disagree, say what you prefer and why: “I’d prefer X because Y”.\n- Before you post, remove sarcasm and any words that say the author should have known better.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- You wrote the pull or merge request. Your own comments on it do not count.\n- A bot wrote the comment.\n- The only comment is a bare approval, such as “LGTM” or “+1”.\n- No reviewer has left a real comment yet, such as on a new pull or merge request.\n\nIt still applies when an account name looks automated but the account is not marked as a bot.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that being direct is rude. A short, blunt comment about the code is fine, such as “This fails when the list is empty”. Strong technical disagreement is fine too. Keep the point clear, and keep it on the code.\n- Checking only for single words such as “just” or “obviously”. Such a word is fine when it reads neutrally. Read the whole comment, and check where it points: at the code or at the author.\n- Asking a question only to belittle, such as “Did you even run this?”. The author cannot answer it. Instead, say what you think is wrong and why.\n- Letting frustration show when a change comes back again. Frustration, name-calling and impatience are common in uncivil reviews. Instead, restate the open concern and give your reason.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [How to write code review comments](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/comments.html), Google Engineering Practices. Comment on the code and never on the developer, and often explain why you ask for a change.\n- [Destructive criticism in software code review impacts inclusion](https://research.google/pubs/destructive-criticism-in-software-code-review-impacts-inclusion/), Gunawardena and others, 2022. The study surveyed 93 software practitioners. Women saw destructive criticism as less appropriate. They were also less motivated to keep working with the developer who gave it.\n- [Characterizing incivility in open source code review discussions](https://doi.org/10.1145/3479497), Ferreira, Cheng and Adams, 2021. In Linux kernel reviews, the most frequent features of uncivil emails were frustration, name calling and impatience. The study also found civil ways to disagree.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Comments land on the code, never on the author"'::jsonb),'v5:654c6ed84c3452636a82f1a9692bb1a8c96a529eefae27aa7946434397c738fa','practice:v3:659e08554b6cc286b2115067fda78a743fcb8b36ec97830dfdef3c7582d46b0e');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','reviews-substantively-with-understanding','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Explain consequential review judgments","signals":["scm.pull_request.reviewed"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"REVIEWER","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: a review whose captured context called for written reasoning carries a substantive comment grounded in the change.\n\n## The standard\nJudge the written explanation accompanying a review, not whether the reviewer read or understood the change. A substantive comment makes the reviewer''s reasoning inspectable: it names or quotes a concrete diff element — a path, a symbol or a term that actually appears in the diff or summary you can see — or asks a real clarifying question about the change. One such comment is enough; polish, tone and specificity are not graded here. A bare approval or acknowledgement (\"LGTM\", \"+1\", a lone thumbs-up) supplies no written reasoning, but it does not establish that the review was careless or superficial. Written reasoning is owed only when the captured context establishes an occasion for it: a request to explain a consequential trade-off, or a documented review requirement. Change size or risk alone does not make commentary mandatory. A reviewer is anyone whose login differs from the pull request author. Discount machine noise and empty ceremony: a comment or decision the record flags `bot` is not human review, and a content-free approval is not engagement. The flag is the provider''s classification of the account; a login''s shape decides nothing.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. This practice judges REVIEWER activity, never the changed code; \"a big PR got little review\" is not an observation. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when no review has happened yet (no reviewer comment and no approval), when `context/review_threads.json` has no review decision and no substantive reviewer comment survives the author-exclusion filter, or when the context demonstrates no need for an explanation and none was supplied. An unreviewed or draft pull request is never a substandard review; do not manufacture praise or criticism from a bare approval.\n\n## Sources\n1. The review decisions (`context/review_threads.json`) and the inline diff comments (`context/comments.json`), with their authors. They establish who reviewed, what was approved, and any inline reasoning. An empty inline thread never establishes missing reasoning across the other sources, nor an unperformed review.\n2. The general comments (`context/general_comments.json`): the conversation and discussion notes. The substance of a review may live here rather than inline; read whatever review notes are materialized, under whatever heading, before concluding nothing substantive exists. They also establish a request for an explanation, when one was made.\n3. The description and metadata (`context/description.md`, `context/metadata.json`): the author''s login for the reviewer filter, and any documented review requirement or trade-off the change raises. They never establish that reasoning was written.\n4. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`) and the checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): whether a comment''s named path, symbol or term actually appears in the change, and the real size of the change once obviously generated or vendored bulk (lockfiles, generated clients, migrations, snapshots) is discounted — a \"big\" diff that is mostly generated is smaller than its line count. They never make commentary mandatory on their own.\nJudge from the review notes and the change-size signals present in your context; do not claim per-comment or per-line facts you cannot see. Missing or incomplete required sources are a collection gap with no observation; never read a missing count as zero.\n\n## Judge\nassess the standard against all relevant evidence, after inspecting every declared source of review comments.\n- MET: an applicable review includes a substantive comment grounded in the change, inline or in the discussion.\n- NOT_MET: the captured context establishes that written reasoning was demonstrably required or requested, the review was approved without it, and every declared review-comment source holds nothing but bare approvals or acknowledgements.\n- UNDETERMINED only when the captured requirement or explanation remains genuinely ambiguous after every available source is inspected; name the unresolved question. Having read every source and found no reasoning where none was demonstrably required is NOT_APPLICABLE, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review. Equivalent inputs yield equivalent calls.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR — a non-blocking practice. Severity reflects the concrete coordination or decision-recording consequence of the missing explanation, never an assumption about how carefully the reviewer read. MINOR, the usual band: the missing reasoning costs the team little to recover. MAJOR: the missing reasoning leaves a consequential decision with no inspectable basis for the author or other reviewers.\n\n## Grounding\nCite the written comment for MET, and the diff element it names when it names one. For NOT_MET, cite the context that establishes why an explanation was needed, the approval that supplied none, and the boundary: every declared review-comment source inspected. Never invent a comment, a file, a size or a clean bill you did not verify. Neither a not-yet-reviewed change nor an approval without commentary establishes a superficial review; describe what was written, never how the reviewer read.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the comments were specific and well-worded belongs to `leaves-useful-specific-review-comments`; whether the author acted on them belongs to `engaging-with-inline-review-comments`. Do not double-flag them here. Here only: does the written explanation meet its demonstrated purpose?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A short explanation makes the basis of an important approval or concern available to the author and other reviewers. A bare approval alone does not show what was checked; it also does not prove that the reviewer failed to read the change.","whatGoodLooksLike":"For a consequential authorization change, a reviewer names the permission check they considered and explains why it addresses the concern, or asks a specific unresolved question. A routine change with no material concern may need only a brief approval.","groupSlug":"constructive-code-review","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 164\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Bare approval</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M12.75 32.75H247.25A12 12 0 0 1 259.25 44.75V67.25A12 12 0 0 1 247.25 79.25H0.75V44.75A12 12 0 0 1 12.75 32.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Can non-admins delete users?</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M36.75 88.75H291.25A12 12 0 0 1 303.25 100.75V123.25A12 12 0 0 1 291.25 135.25H24.75V100.75A12 12 0 0 1 36.75 88.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M41 112.5l3.5 3.5 6.5-7\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"64\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">LGTM</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\">Basis hidden</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Approval with a reason</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M348.75 32.75H583.25A12 12 0 0 1 595.25 44.75V67.25A12 12 0 0 1 583.25 79.25H336.75V44.75A12 12 0 0 1 348.75 32.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Can non-admins delete users?</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M372.75 88.75H627.25A12 12 0 0 1 639.25 100.75V123.25A12 12 0 0 1 627.25 135.25H360.75V100.75A12 12 0 0 1 372.75 88.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M377 112.5l3.5 3.5 6.5-7\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"400\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">Blocked by <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">requireAdmin</tspan></text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\">Basis visible</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A teammate asks “Can non-admins delete users?”. A bare approval says only “LGTM”, so its basis is hidden. An approval with a reason says “Blocked by requireAdmin”, so its basis is visible."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- When the author or another reviewer asks you to explain a choice, write your answer with your approval.\n- If your project’s review rules ask reviewers to record what they checked, write it down with your approval.\n- Name something that is in the change: a file, a function or a term that you checked. For example, a change sets who can edit a project. Instead of “LGTM” (looks good to me), write “`canEdit()` now checks the project role, so viewers cannot save”.\n- If you’re still unsure, ask one real question about the change, such as which code calls the new check. A real question also shows your reasoning.\n- Write it where your team reads it. An inline comment and a note in the discussion both work. One short comment is enough.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Nobody has reviewed the change yet. It has no approval and no reviewer comment. This includes a draft.\n- There is no review decision, and no reviewer has written a comment with substance.\n- Nobody asked for a reason, no review rule needs one, and you approved without one.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Treating size or risk alone as a reason you must explain. Instead, write your reason when someone asks for it or when your review rules need it.\n- Answering a request for your reasoning with only “LGTM” or a thumbs-up. Instead, write one sentence that says what you checked and why it answers the concern.\n- Giving a reason that fits any change, such as “Looks safe to me”. Instead, name the file, function or term that you checked, so others can check it too.\n- Reading a teammate’s bare approval as proof that they skipped the change. Instead, ask for their reason if you need it.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [How to write code review comments](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/comments.html), Google Engineering Practices. Saying why helps the author understand a comment. Google also says that you do not always need to.\n- [Information needs in contemporary code review](https://research.tudelft.nl/en/publications/information-needs-in-contemporary-code-review), Pascarella and others, 2018. In open-source code review, reviewers most often asked about alternatives, their own understanding of the change, and its reason.\n- [Expectations, outcomes, and challenges of modern code review](https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSE.2013.6606617), Bacchelli and Bird, 2013. At Microsoft, understanding the change was the key part of code review, and reviews spread knowledge and team awareness. Microsoft sells code review tools.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Explain consequential review judgments","signals":["scm.pull_request.reviewed"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.general-review-comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.comments","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"REQUIRED"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.review-threads","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"REVIEWER","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured pull or merge request at its pinned revision: the change itself, the description as written, the commits, the review discussion and the linked context the practice names. Distinguish what the author wrote from what a reviewer, a template or the provider''s current state says.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: a review whose captured context called for written reasoning carries a substantive comment grounded in the change.\n\n## The standard\nJudge the written explanation accompanying a review, not whether the reviewer read or understood the change. A substantive comment makes the reviewer''s reasoning inspectable: it names or quotes a concrete diff element — a path, a symbol or a term that actually appears in the diff or summary you can see — or asks a real clarifying question about the change. One such comment is enough; polish, tone and specificity are not graded here. A bare approval or acknowledgement (\"LGTM\", \"+1\", a lone thumbs-up) supplies no written reasoning, but it does not establish that the review was careless or superficial. Written reasoning is owed only when the captured context establishes an occasion for it: a request to explain a consequential trade-off, or a documented review requirement. Change size or risk alone does not make commentary mandatory. A reviewer is anyone whose login differs from the pull request author. Discount machine noise and empty ceremony: a comment or decision the record flags `bot` is not human review, and a content-free approval is not engagement. The flag is the provider''s classification of the account; a login''s shape decides nothing.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. This practice judges REVIEWER activity, never the changed code; \"a big PR got little review\" is not an observation. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when no review has happened yet (no reviewer comment and no approval), when `context/review_threads.json` has no review decision and no substantive reviewer comment survives the author-exclusion filter, or when the context demonstrates no need for an explanation and none was supplied. An unreviewed or draft pull request is never a substandard review; do not manufacture praise or criticism from a bare approval.\n\n## Sources\n1. The review decisions (`context/review_threads.json`) and the inline diff comments (`context/comments.json`), with their authors. They establish who reviewed, what was approved, and any inline reasoning. An empty inline thread never establishes missing reasoning across the other sources, nor an unperformed review.\n2. The general comments (`context/general_comments.json`): the conversation and discussion notes. The substance of a review may live here rather than inline; read whatever review notes are materialized, under whatever heading, before concluding nothing substantive exists. They also establish a request for an explanation, when one was made.\n3. The description and metadata (`context/description.md`, `context/metadata.json`): the author''s login for the reviewer filter, and any documented review requirement or trade-off the change raises. They never establish that reasoning was written.\n4. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`) and the checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): whether a comment''s named path, symbol or term actually appears in the change, and the real size of the change once obviously generated or vendored bulk (lockfiles, generated clients, migrations, snapshots) is discounted — a \"big\" diff that is mostly generated is smaller than its line count. They never make commentary mandatory on their own.\nJudge from the review notes and the change-size signals present in your context; do not claim per-comment or per-line facts you cannot see. Missing or incomplete required sources are a collection gap with no observation; never read a missing count as zero.\n\n## Judge\nassess the standard against all relevant evidence, after inspecting every declared source of review comments.\n- MET: an applicable review includes a substantive comment grounded in the change, inline or in the discussion.\n- NOT_MET: the captured context establishes that written reasoning was demonstrably required or requested, the review was approved without it, and every declared review-comment source holds nothing but bare approvals or acknowledgements.\n- UNDETERMINED only when the captured requirement or explanation remains genuinely ambiguous after every available source is inspected; name the unresolved question. Having read every source and found no reasoning where none was demonstrably required is NOT_APPLICABLE, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review. Equivalent inputs yield equivalent calls.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR — a non-blocking practice. Severity reflects the concrete coordination or decision-recording consequence of the missing explanation, never an assumption about how carefully the reviewer read. MINOR, the usual band: the missing reasoning costs the team little to recover. MAJOR: the missing reasoning leaves a consequential decision with no inspectable basis for the author or other reviewers.\n\n## Grounding\nCite the written comment for MET, and the diff element it names when it names one. For NOT_MET, cite the context that establishes why an explanation was needed, the approval that supplied none, and the boundary: every declared review-comment source inspected. Never invent a comment, a file, a size or a clean bill you did not verify. Neither a not-yet-reviewed change nor an approval without commentary establishes a superficial review; describe what was written, never how the reviewer read.\n\n## Defer\nWhether the comments were specific and well-worded belongs to `leaves-useful-specific-review-comments`; whether the author acted on them belongs to `engaging-with-inline-review-comments`. Do not double-flag them here. Here only: does the written explanation meet its demonstrated purpose?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A short explanation makes the basis of an important approval or concern available to the author and other reviewers. A bare approval alone does not show what was checked; it also does not prove that the reviewer failed to read the change.","whatGoodLooksLike":"For a consequential authorization change, a reviewer names the permission check they considered and explains why it addresses the concern, or asks a specific unresolved question. A routine change with no material concern may need only a brief approval.","groupSlug":"constructive-code-review","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 164\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Bare approval</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M12.75 32.75H247.25A12 12 0 0 1 259.25 44.75V67.25A12 12 0 0 1 247.25 79.25H0.75V44.75A12 12 0 0 1 12.75 32.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Can non-admins delete users?</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M36.75 88.75H291.25A12 12 0 0 1 303.25 100.75V123.25A12 12 0 0 1 291.25 135.25H24.75V100.75A12 12 0 0 1 36.75 88.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M41 112.5l3.5 3.5 6.5-7\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"64\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">LGTM</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\">Basis hidden</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Approval with a reason</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M348.75 32.75H583.25A12 12 0 0 1 595.25 44.75V67.25A12 12 0 0 1 583.25 79.25H336.75V44.75A12 12 0 0 1 348.75 32.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Can non-admins delete users?</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M372.75 88.75H627.25A12 12 0 0 1 639.25 100.75V123.25A12 12 0 0 1 627.25 135.25H360.75V100.75A12 12 0 0 1 372.75 88.75Z\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M377 112.5l3.5 3.5 6.5-7\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"400\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">Blocked by <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">requireAdmin</tspan></text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\">Basis visible</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A teammate asks “Can non-admins delete users?”. A bare approval says only “LGTM”, so its basis is hidden. An approval with a reason says “Blocked by requireAdmin”, so its basis is visible."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- When the author or another reviewer asks you to explain a choice, write your answer with your approval.\n- If your project’s review rules ask reviewers to record what they checked, write it down with your approval.\n- Name something that is in the change: a file, a function or a term that you checked. For example, a change sets who can edit a project. Instead of “LGTM” (looks good to me), write “`canEdit()` now checks the project role, so viewers cannot save”.\n- If you’re still unsure, ask one real question about the change, such as which code calls the new check. A real question also shows your reasoning.\n- Write it where your team reads it. An inline comment and a note in the discussion both work. One short comment is enough.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Nobody has reviewed the change yet. It has no approval and no reviewer comment. This includes a draft.\n- There is no review decision, and no reviewer has written a comment with substance.\n- Nobody asked for a reason, no review rule needs one, and you approved without one.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Treating size or risk alone as a reason you must explain. Instead, write your reason when someone asks for it or when your review rules need it.\n- Answering a request for your reasoning with only “LGTM” or a thumbs-up. Instead, write one sentence that says what you checked and why it answers the concern.\n- Giving a reason that fits any change, such as “Looks safe to me”. Instead, name the file, function or term that you checked, so others can check it too.\n- Reading a teammate’s bare approval as proof that they skipped the change. Instead, ask for their reason if you need it.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [How to write code review comments](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/comments.html), Google Engineering Practices. Saying why helps the author understand a comment. Google also says that you do not always need to.\n- [Information needs in contemporary code review](https://research.tudelft.nl/en/publications/information-needs-in-contemporary-code-review), Pascarella and others, 2018. In open-source code review, reviewers most often asked about alternatives, their own understanding of the change, and its reason.\n- [Expectations, outcomes, and challenges of modern code review](https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSE.2013.6606617), Bacchelli and Bird, 2013. At Microsoft, understanding the change was the key part of code review, and reviews spread knowledge and team awareness. Microsoft sells code review tools.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Reviews that call for reasoning get it in writing"'::jsonb),'v5:f945bf735a107c2dc1d08acdd45efdbdbb6f0d041c6444fc8a34385500d84f1c','practice:v3:c17c654c63666b57d6806a3fbaa5aae254bd1aaa36382080b2d518ea204bbe5b');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('GROUP','testing-discipline',NULL,'{"name":"Testing your changes","description":"When a change adds or fixes behaviour, it comes with the tests that exercise it. And it never quietly deletes, skips, or weakens a test just to make the build pass.","icon":"TestTube","color":"amber"}'::jsonb,'{"name":"Testing your changes","description":"When a change adds or fixes behaviour, it comes with the tests that exercise it. And it never quietly deletes, skips, or weakens a test just to make the build pass.","icon":"TestTube","color":"amber"}'::jsonb,'16d1a2828f9c6d2d2825c938b07819f8a50a3fa73d9bb8e215c953338f30a81f','group:v1:16d1a2828f9c6d2d2825c938b07819f8a50a3fa73d9bb8e215c953338f30a81f');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','ships-tests-with-the-change','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Include tests with the change","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: a test for the changed production behaviour arriving in the same change.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one workflow practice: when the change introduces non-trivial production behaviour, does a test for THAT behaviour arrive in the same diff? You are not hunting for missing edge cases, doing a lint sweep or re-judging the change''s size; you are asking whether the new logic ships locked in by a test that could observe it. Production behaviour: a new function or endpoint, a new branch or computation, a changed validation or state transition. A covering test plausibly exercises that behaviour: it touches the same symbol, endpoint or path, or asserts the corrected result. A bug fix is strongest when it ships a test that would fail on the old code and pass on the new. Judge the PRESENCE and plausible linkage of a covering test, never its naming, readability or assertion style.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there is no production surface to judge: the diff is empty, or — after setting aside generated, vendored, lock and migration files, which are neither production logic nor a test of it — it is test-only, docs-only, config-only, or a pure rename, format or type-only change with no quotable added behaviour. Layout is not behaviour: a view, control or style change with no branch, computation or state transition — a button whose action is empty, a title, a colour, a spacing — leaves nothing a test could observe, and is NOT_APPLICABLE. A test-only change does not establish the occasion.\n\n## Sources\nThis is decided from the diff alone.\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`) with the file list (`work/change/files.json`): classify each changed file as production or test by its path and name, using judgment about test conventions across languages. The production hunks establish the behaviour added or changed; the test hunks establish whether a test names the same symbol, endpoint or path, or asserts the corrected result. Neither establishes that any test ran.\n2. The commits (`context/commits.json`): subjects that say what the change does, such as naming a fix. They establish the shape of the change; they never establish a per-commit mapping, since per-commit patches are not in your context.\n3. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): existing tests near the changed code, for context on where a covering test would live. The absence of a repository-wide test inventory is never a reason to abstain: load-bearing, testable logic shipped into a repository with no test target at all is the lesson, and is judged, not abstained from.\nIf a required part of the production or test diff is truncated or unavailable, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: the diff adds or changes real production behaviour and the same diff adds or updates a test that plausibly exercises it. Quote the production line and the test span that names the shared symbol or asserts the corrected result.\n- NOT_MET: the diff adds testable production behaviour, and no test in the same diff exercises it — after scanning every test file in the diff. Never manufacture untested logic to force this cell.\n- UNDETERMINED only when the complete captured evidence leaves the relationship between a test and the changed behaviour genuinely ambiguous; name what would settle it. Having scanned every test file in the diff and found none that covers a quotable behaviour is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED. A truncated diff is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, on the single most consequential untested behaviour when there are several.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this alone — non-blocking. Reason about consequence, never a line count. MINOR, the usual band: a single small, focused piece of untested logic that one test would pin. MAJOR: a defect fix shipping with nothing to lock the corrected behaviour in, or substantial or branching untested logic. Identical evidence yields identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the exact added span relied on: the `[L<n>]` production line embodying the behaviour, and for MET the `[L<n>]` test span naming the shared symbol. NOT_MET requires the quoted untested production line AND a stated scan of the diff''s test files confirming that none covers it — that scan is the boundary. Never invent a test, a file or a clean bill you did not read. Never claim a test ran or passed, and never claim a per-commit mapping you did not observe. Describe the observable delivery; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nChange size belongs to `scope-one-reviewable-change`; weakening an EXISTING suite belongs to `keeps-the-test-suite-honest`. Here only: did a test for the new behaviour ship with it?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for ships-tests-with-the-change: which changed source files are tests and which are\n// production code, by path convention. Whether a test covers the changed behaviour is the review''s to\n// read from the diff.\nimport { isTestPath, languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default function shipsTestsWithTheChange(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst source = [...diffFiles.keys()].filter((path) => languageOf(path) !== null);\n\tconst tests = source.filter((path) => isTestPath(path));\n\tconst production = source.filter((path) => !isTestPath(path));\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (source.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The diff changes ${String(production.length)} production source file(s) and ${String(tests.length)} test file(s), by path${tests.length > 0 ? `: ${tests.slice(0, 10).join(\", \")}` : \"\"}. Whether the repository already has a test target does not decide this practice: judge the changed behaviour against the tests in this diff.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: [],\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tdiffProductionFiles: production.length,\n\t\t\tdiffTestFiles: tests.length,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Automated tests preserve checked behavior as the code changes and catch regressions without repeating every manual check. Shipping a focused test with a behavior change or defect fix gives later contributors a repeatable check of the behavior the change is meant to keep.","whatGoodLooksLike":"New logic arrives with a test beside it, and a bug fix comes with a test that fails on the old code and passes on the new, proving the behaviour is what you say it is.","groupSlug":"testing-discipline","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 220\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Untested new logic</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"158.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Change</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">signIn.ts</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"32\" y=\"104\" width=\"176\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"32\" y=\"120\" width=\"128\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"16.75\" y=\"146.75\" width=\"110.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"28\" y=\"167\" font-size=\"15\">No test</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"15\">Breaks unnoticed</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Tested new logic</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"158.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Change</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">signIn.ts</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"368\" y=\"104\" width=\"176\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"368\" y=\"120\" width=\"128\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 154)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M5.75 0.75h4.5M6.75 0.75v5L1.6 13.5a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1 1.75h10.8a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1-1.75L9.25 5.75v-5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"167\" font-size=\"15\">Test: rejects wrong password</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"15\">Stays checked</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A change to signIn.ts with no test breaks unnoticed. The same change with a test, rejects wrong password, stays checked."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, list the behavior that your change adds or changes. Look for a new function or endpoint, a new branch or calculation, and a changed validation or state change.\n- For each item, add or update a test in the same pull request or merge request. For example, you add a check for expired accounts to sign-in. Instead of leaving it untested, add a test that signs in with an expired account and expects a refusal.\n- Make the test call the function, endpoint or path that you changed, or check the corrected result.\n- Put the new test where the repository keeps the tests for the code near it.\n- When you fix a bug, write the test first. Run it on the old code to see it fail. Fix the code in the same change. Run the test again to see it pass.\n\n![Three steps for a bug fix follow in order. First you write the test, and it shows the bug. Then you fix the code in the same change. Last, you run the test again, and it confirms the fix.](figures/fix-order.svg)\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The change is empty.\n- The change holds only tests, only docs, or only configuration. Generated files, copied third-party code, lockfiles and database migrations do not count.\n- The change only renames, formats, or changes type declarations, and adds no behavior. Those files do not count.\n- The change only touches layout, such as a title, a color, the spacing, or a button that does nothing yet.\n\nIt still applies when your repository has no tests yet.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Chasing every edge case or polishing test names before the new behavior has any test. Instead, first add one test that exercises the new behavior.\n- Adding any test to the change and calling it done. A test counts only when it exercises the new behavior. Instead, make it call the code you changed, or check the corrected result.\n- Fixing a bug with nothing that holds the fix in place. Instead, add a test that shows the bug, and fix the bug in the same change.\n- Promising the tests in a later change. Instead, ship them with the code, so later changes cannot break the behavior unnoticed.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [What to look for in a code review](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/looking-for.html), Google Engineering Practices. Google advises adding tests in the same change as the production code.\n- [Testing overview](https://abseil.io/resources/swe-book/html/ch11.html), Adam Bender, in Software Engineering at Google, 2020. Automated tests catch mistakes early, so a team can change code with confidence and keep found bugs from coming back.\n- [Self-testing code](https://martinfowler.com/bliki/SelfTestingCode.html), Martin Fowler, 2014. Work is complete only with the tests that keep it working. A bug fix starts with a test that exposes the bug.\n","figures":{"fix-order":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 76\">\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"12\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M32 12h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M216 12l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"236\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"236\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M256 12h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M440 12l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"460\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Write the test</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">Shows the bug</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"224\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Fix the code</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"224\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">In the same change</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"448\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Run the test again</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"448\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">Confirms the fix</text>\n</svg>\n"}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Include tests with the change","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: a test for the changed production behaviour arriving in the same change.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one workflow practice: when the change introduces non-trivial production behaviour, does a test for THAT behaviour arrive in the same diff? You are not hunting for missing edge cases, doing a lint sweep or re-judging the change''s size; you are asking whether the new logic ships locked in by a test that could observe it. Production behaviour: a new function or endpoint, a new branch or computation, a changed validation or state transition. A covering test plausibly exercises that behaviour: it touches the same symbol, endpoint or path, or asserts the corrected result. A bug fix is strongest when it ships a test that would fail on the old code and pass on the new. Judge the PRESENCE and plausible linkage of a covering test, never its naming, readability or assertion style.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there is no production surface to judge: the diff is empty, or — after setting aside generated, vendored, lock and migration files, which are neither production logic nor a test of it — it is test-only, docs-only, config-only, or a pure rename, format or type-only change with no quotable added behaviour. Layout is not behaviour: a view, control or style change with no branch, computation or state transition — a button whose action is empty, a title, a colour, a spacing — leaves nothing a test could observe, and is NOT_APPLICABLE. A test-only change does not establish the occasion.\n\n## Sources\nThis is decided from the diff alone.\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`) with the file list (`work/change/files.json`): classify each changed file as production or test by its path and name, using judgment about test conventions across languages. The production hunks establish the behaviour added or changed; the test hunks establish whether a test names the same symbol, endpoint or path, or asserts the corrected result. Neither establishes that any test ran.\n2. The commits (`context/commits.json`): subjects that say what the change does, such as naming a fix. They establish the shape of the change; they never establish a per-commit mapping, since per-commit patches are not in your context.\n3. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): existing tests near the changed code, for context on where a covering test would live. The absence of a repository-wide test inventory is never a reason to abstain: load-bearing, testable logic shipped into a repository with no test target at all is the lesson, and is judged, not abstained from.\nIf a required part of the production or test diff is truncated or unavailable, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: the diff adds or changes real production behaviour and the same diff adds or updates a test that plausibly exercises it. Quote the production line and the test span that names the shared symbol or asserts the corrected result.\n- NOT_MET: the diff adds testable production behaviour, and no test in the same diff exercises it — after scanning every test file in the diff. Never manufacture untested logic to force this cell.\n- UNDETERMINED only when the complete captured evidence leaves the relationship between a test and the changed behaviour genuinely ambiguous; name what would settle it. Having scanned every test file in the diff and found none that covers a quotable behaviour is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED. A truncated diff is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, on the single most consequential untested behaviour when there are several.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this alone — non-blocking. Reason about consequence, never a line count. MINOR, the usual band: a single small, focused piece of untested logic that one test would pin. MAJOR: a defect fix shipping with nothing to lock the corrected behaviour in, or substantial or branching untested logic. Identical evidence yields identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the exact added span relied on: the `[L<n>]` production line embodying the behaviour, and for MET the `[L<n>]` test span naming the shared symbol. NOT_MET requires the quoted untested production line AND a stated scan of the diff''s test files confirming that none covers it — that scan is the boundary. Never invent a test, a file or a clean bill you did not read. Never claim a test ran or passed, and never claim a per-commit mapping you did not observe. Describe the observable delivery; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nChange size belongs to `scope-one-reviewable-change`; weakening an EXISTING suite belongs to `keeps-the-test-suite-honest`. Here only: did a test for the new behaviour ship with it?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for ships-tests-with-the-change: which changed source files are tests and which are\n// production code, by path convention. Whether a test covers the changed behaviour is the review''s to\n// read from the diff.\nimport { isTestPath, languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nexport default function shipsTestsWithTheChange(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst source = [...diffFiles.keys()].filter((path) => languageOf(path) !== null);\n\tconst tests = source.filter((path) => isTestPath(path));\n\tconst production = source.filter((path) => !isTestPath(path));\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (source.length > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The diff changes ${String(production.length)} production source file(s) and ${String(tests.length)} test file(s), by path${tests.length > 0 ? `: ${tests.slice(0, 10).join(\", \")}` : \"\"}. Whether the repository already has a test target does not decide this practice: judge the changed behaviour against the tests in this diff.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: [],\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tdiffProductionFiles: production.length,\n\t\t\tdiffTestFiles: tests.length,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Automated tests preserve checked behavior as the code changes and catch regressions without repeating every manual check. Shipping a focused test with a behavior change or defect fix gives later contributors a repeatable check of the behavior the change is meant to keep.","whatGoodLooksLike":"New logic arrives with a test beside it, and a bug fix comes with a test that fails on the old code and passes on the new, proving the behaviour is what you say it is.","groupSlug":"testing-discipline","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 220\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Untested new logic</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"158.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Change</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">signIn.ts</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"32\" y=\"104\" width=\"176\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"32\" y=\"120\" width=\"128\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"16.75\" y=\"146.75\" width=\"110.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"28\" y=\"167\" font-size=\"15\">No test</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"15\">Breaks unnoticed</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Tested new logic</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"158.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Change</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\">signIn.ts</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"368\" y=\"104\" width=\"176\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"368\" y=\"120\" width=\"128\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 154)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M5.75 0.75h4.5M6.75 0.75v5L1.6 13.5a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1 1.75h10.8a1.2 1.2 0 0 0 1-1.75L9.25 5.75v-5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"380\" y=\"167\" font-size=\"15\">Test: rejects wrong password</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"15\">Stays checked</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A change to signIn.ts with no test breaks unnoticed. The same change with a test, rejects wrong password, stays checked."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, list the behavior that your change adds or changes. Look for a new function or endpoint, a new branch or calculation, and a changed validation or state change.\n- For each item, add or update a test in the same pull request or merge request. For example, you add a check for expired accounts to sign-in. Instead of leaving it untested, add a test that signs in with an expired account and expects a refusal.\n- Make the test call the function, endpoint or path that you changed, or check the corrected result.\n- Put the new test where the repository keeps the tests for the code near it.\n- When you fix a bug, write the test first. Run it on the old code to see it fail. Fix the code in the same change. Run the test again to see it pass.\n\n![Three steps for a bug fix follow in order. First you write the test, and it shows the bug. Then you fix the code in the same change. Last, you run the test again, and it confirms the fix.](figures/fix-order.svg)\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The change is empty.\n- The change holds only tests, only docs, or only configuration. Generated files, copied third-party code, lockfiles and database migrations do not count.\n- The change only renames, formats, or changes type declarations, and adds no behavior. Those files do not count.\n- The change only touches layout, such as a title, a color, the spacing, or a button that does nothing yet.\n\nIt still applies when your repository has no tests yet.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Chasing every edge case or polishing test names before the new behavior has any test. Instead, first add one test that exercises the new behavior.\n- Adding any test to the change and calling it done. A test counts only when it exercises the new behavior. Instead, make it call the code you changed, or check the corrected result.\n- Fixing a bug with nothing that holds the fix in place. Instead, add a test that shows the bug, and fix the bug in the same change.\n- Promising the tests in a later change. Instead, ship them with the code, so later changes cannot break the behavior unnoticed.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [What to look for in a code review](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/looking-for.html), Google Engineering Practices. Google advises adding tests in the same change as the production code.\n- [Testing overview](https://abseil.io/resources/swe-book/html/ch11.html), Adam Bender, in Software Engineering at Google, 2020. Automated tests catch mistakes early, so a team can change code with confidence and keep found bugs from coming back.\n- [Self-testing code](https://martinfowler.com/bliki/SelfTestingCode.html), Martin Fowler, 2014. Work is complete only with the tests that keep it working. A bug fix starts with a test that exposes the bug.\n","figures":{"fix-order":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 76\">\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"12\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M32 12h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M216 12l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"236\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"236\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M256 12h176\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M440 12l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"460\" cy=\"12\" r=\"11.25\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"17\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Write the test</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">Shows the bug</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"224\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Fix the code</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"224\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">In the same change</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"448\" y=\"48\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Run the test again</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"448\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\">Confirms the fix</text>\n</svg>\n"}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"New logic and fixes arrive with a test beside them"'::jsonb),'v5:98a424377c0cb17a93fe9887f4d71d0029dc9577f5a933799e937a87cb36d99a','practice:v3:d93bb80ea2c0e49e0d58c0bc48b81b154b9d38ecb0a941929e58755dfdf52fe6');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','keeps-the-test-suite-honest','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Keep the test suite honest","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: changed existing checks retain their regression coverage, or their removal is justified by the captured change.\n\n## The standard\nJudge ONE thing: does the change preserve the existing checks, or does it weaken them without a justified reason? An honest suite keeps telling the truth: when a test starts failing, the fix addresses the underlying cause, and when a test genuinely must go, it goes with a stated, quotable reason. Unjustified weakening deletes, disables, skips, comments out or hollows out a test or its assertions without explaining why the check is gone. Do not infer the author''s motive or whether the build passed. The test is one of judgment, not keywords: can the check still fail on the regression it guarded? A faithful migration that preserves assertion strength — a rename, a move, an API upgrade, splitting one test into several, or deleting a test whose subject under test is itself removed in this same diff — is not a suppression. This is a craft practice about how the change treats its safety net, not a line-by-line lint sweep and never a merge gate.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there is no existing test the change could be lowering: the diff changes no existing hand-written test or assertion, or the patch is empty. Exclude generated or vendored test code, then inspect every remaining changed check regardless of how much generated output surrounds it. A feature with no test changes is NOT_APPLICABLE here, not an assessed observation. New tests alone create no existing check to compare. A changed existing check creates the occasion whether it is preserved or weakened.\n\n## Sources\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`, read exhaustively, with `work/change/files.json` to find the test files): every test hunk — an added skip or disable, a removed assertion or test block, commented-out executable test code, an assertion made trivially true, and any adjacent comment naming a tracker or a `reason=` / `@Disabled(\"…\")` argument. It establishes what happened to each check and any reason written beside it. It never establishes that the suite ran.\n2. The commits (`context/commits.json`) and the description (`context/description.md`): a line explaining a removal or skip. They establish a quotable reason tied to the change; they never establish a reason they do not state.\n3. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the unchanged surroundings of a touched test, to tell a hollowed assertion from a faithful move or split, and whether a deleted test''s subject is gone. It never supplies a justification nobody wrote.\nWhen the change touches a test surface but the patch is truncated so those hunks cannot be read, report a collection gap and record no observation; unread required evidence is not a judgment.\n\n## Judge\nThe undesirable behaviour is unjustified weakening. Assess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- NOT_MET: a real assertion becomes trivially true, an entire test block is dropped, executable test code is commented out, or a test is skipped or disabled — and no quotable reason is tied to it anywhere in the sources above. Quote the deciding line(s).\n- MET: the change touches real test code and, after a complete bounded search of every test hunk, every check is at least as strong as before, or each removal or skip has a quotable reason supported by the captured change. An arbitrary explanation does not justify discarding a still-relevant check. A faithful migration is MET, not a suppression. Name the test file(s) you inspected.\n- UNDETERMINED only when a read hunk genuinely cannot settle whether a check can still fail on what it guarded; name what would settle it. A missing or truncated patch is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review. Name the single most consequential suppressed test and show the shape of the fix: restore the check, or record a reason that explains why it is no longer appropriate.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on test suppression alone — formative, non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: a single unexplained skip. MAJOR: a deleted test, a hollowed assertion, or several unexplained suppressions, since more of the suite''s honesty is lost.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery observation quotes the deciding test line(s) from the diff: the added skip or weakened assertion, or the removed line for a deletion. For MET, cite the test hunks inspected and any quoted reason; never record it for a diff that touches no test surface. Never claim to have run, compiled or executed the suite; you see only the patch. Never assert a suppression, or a justification, you cannot point to in the captured files — if you cannot quote it, it does not exist. Describe the observable behaviour of the diff; never impute intent or character, and never grade the author.\n\n## Defer\nWhether NEW tests were added for new logic belongs to `ships-tests-with-the-change`. Here only: does the change WEAKEN the existing safety net without saying why?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Deleting or skipping a failing test to get a passing build hides the breakage instead of fixing it, and removes the alarm that would have caught it next time. The suite only protects you while it tells the truth.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Existing checks retain their regression coverage. A check that no longer applies is removed with a reason tied to the changed behavior; a still-relevant failing check is fixed at the cause rather than disabled.","groupSlug":"testing-discipline","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 104\">\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">Test fails</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M136 52h20v-32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M192 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"0.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">@Disabled</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M424 20h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M448 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">Test off, bug stays</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M156 52v32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M192 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-accent-soft pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"64.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"216\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">Cause fixed</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M424 84h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M448 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\">Bug fixed, test stays</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"When a test fails and someone adds @Disabled, the test is off and the bug stays. When the cause is fixed, the bug is fixed and the test stays."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- When an existing test fails after your change, fix the cause. Do not weaken the test to make it pass.\n- When you update a test for new behavior, keep its check as strong as before.\n- When you move, rename or split a test, carry every assertion with it.\n- If a test must go, say in the description or a commit message how your change made it obsolete. For example, instead of deleting a rounding test with no reason, write that your change removes rounding.\n- If your change means a test should no longer run, write why in the skip itself. Tie the reason to your change, such as the text of `@Disabled(\"…\")` in JUnit or `reason=` in pytest.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change edits no existing test or assertion. For example, it adds a feature and changes no tests.\n- Your change is empty.\n- The only tests that you changed are generated by a tool or copied in from another project.\n- Your change only adds new tests.\n\nIt still applies when you change an existing test and keep it just as strong. It also applies when a few hand-written test lines sit among many generated files.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Treating every edit to a test as weakening the suite. A move, a rename or a split is fine. So is deleting a test together with the code it tests. Instead, ask one question: can the test still fail on the bug it guarded?\n- Hollowing out a test instead of removing it. An always-true assertion or commented-out test code leaves a test that passes but checks nothing. Instead, restore the check, or remove the test and say why.\n- Giving a reason that explains nothing. “Fix later” does not say why a test that still matters can go. Instead, fix the cause, or explain how your change made the test obsolete.\n- Skipping a test and moving on. Many skipped tests stay skipped for years. Instead, fix the cause in this change when you can.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Unit testing](https://abseil.io/resources/swe-book/html/ch12.html), Erik Kuefler, Software Engineering at Google, 2020. A refactoring, a new feature or a bug fix should not need changes to existing tests. Only a change in behavior should.\n- [How disabled tests manifest in test maintainability challenges?](https://doi.org/10.1145/3468264.3468609), Kim, Yang, Yang and Chen, 2021. In 15 open-source Java projects, about 41% of disabled tests never came back, and most stayed disabled for years.\n- [Disabling tests](https://docs.junit.org/current/writing-tests/disabling-tests.html), JUnit User Guide, the JUnit team. A reason is optional, but the JUnit team recommends a short reason for each disabled test.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Keep the test suite honest","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: changed existing checks retain their regression coverage, or their removal is justified by the captured change.\n\n## The standard\nJudge ONE thing: does the change preserve the existing checks, or does it weaken them without a justified reason? An honest suite keeps telling the truth: when a test starts failing, the fix addresses the underlying cause, and when a test genuinely must go, it goes with a stated, quotable reason. Unjustified weakening deletes, disables, skips, comments out or hollows out a test or its assertions without explaining why the check is gone. Do not infer the author''s motive or whether the build passed. The test is one of judgment, not keywords: can the check still fail on the regression it guarded? A faithful migration that preserves assertion strength — a rename, a move, an API upgrade, splitting one test into several, or deleting a test whose subject under test is itself removed in this same diff — is not a suppression. This is a craft practice about how the change treats its safety net, not a line-by-line lint sweep and never a merge gate.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there is no existing test the change could be lowering: the diff changes no existing hand-written test or assertion, or the patch is empty. Exclude generated or vendored test code, then inspect every remaining changed check regardless of how much generated output surrounds it. A feature with no test changes is NOT_APPLICABLE here, not an assessed observation. New tests alone create no existing check to compare. A changed existing check creates the occasion whether it is preserved or weakened.\n\n## Sources\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`, read exhaustively, with `work/change/files.json` to find the test files): every test hunk — an added skip or disable, a removed assertion or test block, commented-out executable test code, an assertion made trivially true, and any adjacent comment naming a tracker or a `reason=` / `@Disabled(\"…\")` argument. It establishes what happened to each check and any reason written beside it. It never establishes that the suite ran.\n2. The commits (`context/commits.json`) and the description (`context/description.md`): a line explaining a removal or skip. They establish a quotable reason tied to the change; they never establish a reason they do not state.\n3. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the unchanged surroundings of a touched test, to tell a hollowed assertion from a faithful move or split, and whether a deleted test''s subject is gone. It never supplies a justification nobody wrote.\nWhen the change touches a test surface but the patch is truncated so those hunks cannot be read, report a collection gap and record no observation; unread required evidence is not a judgment.\n\n## Judge\nThe undesirable behaviour is unjustified weakening. Assess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- NOT_MET: a real assertion becomes trivially true, an entire test block is dropped, executable test code is commented out, or a test is skipped or disabled — and no quotable reason is tied to it anywhere in the sources above. Quote the deciding line(s).\n- MET: the change touches real test code and, after a complete bounded search of every test hunk, every check is at least as strong as before, or each removal or skip has a quotable reason supported by the captured change. An arbitrary explanation does not justify discarding a still-relevant check. A faithful migration is MET, not a suppression. Name the test file(s) you inspected.\n- UNDETERMINED only when a read hunk genuinely cannot settle whether a check can still fail on what it guarded; name what would settle it. A missing or truncated patch is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review. Name the single most consequential suppressed test and show the shape of the fix: restore the check, or record a reason that explains why it is no longer appropriate.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on test suppression alone — formative, non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: a single unexplained skip. MAJOR: a deleted test, a hollowed assertion, or several unexplained suppressions, since more of the suite''s honesty is lost.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery observation quotes the deciding test line(s) from the diff: the added skip or weakened assertion, or the removed line for a deletion. For MET, cite the test hunks inspected and any quoted reason; never record it for a diff that touches no test surface. Never claim to have run, compiled or executed the suite; you see only the patch. Never assert a suppression, or a justification, you cannot point to in the captured files — if you cannot quote it, it does not exist. Describe the observable behaviour of the diff; never impute intent or character, and never grade the author.\n\n## Defer\nWhether NEW tests were added for new logic belongs to `ships-tests-with-the-change`. Here only: does the change WEAKEN the existing safety net without saying why?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Deleting or skipping a failing test to get a passing build hides the breakage instead of fixing it, and removes the alarm that would have caught it next time. The suite only protects you while it tells the truth.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Existing checks retain their regression coverage. A check that no longer applies is removed with a reason tied to the changed behavior; a still-relevant failing check is fixed at the cause rather than disabled.","groupSlug":"testing-discipline","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 104\">\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">Test fails</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M136 52h20v-32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M192 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"0.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">@Disabled</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M424 20h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M448 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">Test off, bug stays</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M156 52v32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M192 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-accent-soft pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"64.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"216\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">Cause fixed</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M424 84h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M448 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\">Bug fixed, test stays</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"When a test fails and someone adds @Disabled, the test is off and the bug stays. When the cause is fixed, the bug is fixed and the test stays."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- When an existing test fails after your change, fix the cause. Do not weaken the test to make it pass.\n- When you update a test for new behavior, keep its check as strong as before.\n- When you move, rename or split a test, carry every assertion with it.\n- If a test must go, say in the description or a commit message how your change made it obsolete. For example, instead of deleting a rounding test with no reason, write that your change removes rounding.\n- If your change means a test should no longer run, write why in the skip itself. Tie the reason to your change, such as the text of `@Disabled(\"…\")` in JUnit or `reason=` in pytest.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change edits no existing test or assertion. For example, it adds a feature and changes no tests.\n- Your change is empty.\n- The only tests that you changed are generated by a tool or copied in from another project.\n- Your change only adds new tests.\n\nIt still applies when you change an existing test and keep it just as strong. It also applies when a few hand-written test lines sit among many generated files.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Treating every edit to a test as weakening the suite. A move, a rename or a split is fine. So is deleting a test together with the code it tests. Instead, ask one question: can the test still fail on the bug it guarded?\n- Hollowing out a test instead of removing it. An always-true assertion or commented-out test code leaves a test that passes but checks nothing. Instead, restore the check, or remove the test and say why.\n- Giving a reason that explains nothing. “Fix later” does not say why a test that still matters can go. Instead, fix the cause, or explain how your change made the test obsolete.\n- Skipping a test and moving on. Many skipped tests stay skipped for years. Instead, fix the cause in this change when you can.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Unit testing](https://abseil.io/resources/swe-book/html/ch12.html), Erik Kuefler, Software Engineering at Google, 2020. A refactoring, a new feature or a bug fix should not need changes to existing tests. Only a change in behavior should.\n- [How disabled tests manifest in test maintainability challenges?](https://doi.org/10.1145/3468264.3468609), Kim, Yang, Yang and Chen, 2021. In 15 open-source Java projects, about 41% of disabled tests never came back, and most stayed disabled for years.\n- [Disabling tests](https://docs.junit.org/current/writing-tests/disabling-tests.html), JUnit User Guide, the JUnit team. A reason is optional, but the JUnit team recommends a short reason for each disabled test.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Existing checks stay effective or their removal is justified"'::jsonb),'v5:430eb196e200f3010506b47ca99346a9203a66258f8d992e65d27463d663e84e','practice:v3:18104683c47c06734dffb7dfd251e94b12b8b928a3154852c3f1ca434e345a0b');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('GROUP','code-craftsmanship',NULL,'{"name":"Writing maintainable code","description":"Keep the changed code easy for the next person to read and change. Factor out duplication, keep units small and single-purpose, and leave no clutter behind, such as dead code, debug leftovers, or comments that just restate the line.","icon":"Wrench","color":"emerald"}'::jsonb,'{"name":"Writing maintainable code","description":"Keep the changed code easy for the next person to read and change. Factor out duplication, keep units small and single-purpose, and leave no clutter behind, such as dead code, debug leftovers, or comments that just restate the line.","icon":"Wrench","color":"emerald"}'::jsonb,'e63968d681abfa00c180199c3708a5d921ba71fb460f9976314357659dec2360','group:v1:e63968d681abfa00c180199c3708a5d921ba71fb460f9976314357659dec2360');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','removes-duplication-instead-of-copy-pasting','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Factor out duplication instead of copy-pasting","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added hand-written code expresses each maintained rule in one appropriate place, without unjustified copy-paste.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one closed corpus: the lines this pull request adds. Flag a maintained rule or computation expressed independently in two or more added blocks, where a change to that rule would need the same correction in each copy (Fowler; Hunt & Thomas, DRY). Similar syntax is a lead to inspect, not the standard. Separate concepts may use the same framework idiom without sharing a rule; combining them merely to shorten the code creates an unnecessary coupling. A coherent multi-line computation, validation, mapping or transform, or constant or config block can be a flaggable copy. A one-liner, a guard clause, a field assignment or a forced-similar idiom (equals/hashCode, builder chains, switch arms, DI or route registrations) is not — an idiom is a few lines the platform dictates; a repeated multi-line block can carry duplicated knowledge, including a view body or a fetch-decode-assign sequence. Length alone never establishes that two blocks express the same rule. Legitimately parallel repetition is not a defect: test cases, fixtures — including preview, story and demo setups that show the same sample in another state, appearance or environment — and table-driven or parametrized bodies are meant to look alike. The exception covers the sample setup, not a production rule copied into it. Duplication can live within one function: sibling `if`, `case` or branch bodies that each repeat the same multi-line computation differing only in a constant are as flaggable as cross-function copies. Use judgment on whether two blocks are a genuine copy-paste or a coincidental shared idiom.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop only when the change offers no surface to judge: a documentation, diagram, image diff, or one whose added lines are entirely generated, vendored or test fixtures. Hand-written configuration that repeats a maintained rule is a surface to judge. A diff you read and found clean is not one of these; NOT_APPLICABLE says the work had no subject here, which is false of it.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the `[L<n>]` new-side spans of the added hunks. It establishes every candidate pair — a flaggable pair must sit inside these lines. It never establishes duplication against unchanged repository code, which is out of scope either way; pre-existing duplication you cannot see being introduced here is not judged.\n2. The file spread (`work/change/files.json`): which paths are generated or vendored (`*.gen.*`, lockfiles, OpenAPI specs, route trees, migrations and the like) or test fixtures. It establishes the hand-authored remainder you assess. It never establishes a pair.\n3. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the surrounding lines of a hunk cut off mid-block. It establishes the full shape of an added block. It never establishes a pair against code the change did not add.\nThe precomputed leads below these criteria list every pair of added blocks whose lines share a shape once names and literals are set aside, with both spans, the run length and the names that differ: decide every pair, and quote both spans. If the diff was not captured completely, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nBefore choosing, scan every added hand-written block for structural sameness, including branch bodies, not just whole functions. Then assess the standard against all relevant evidence.\n- NOT_MET: two or more added blocks express the same maintained rule or computation. Quote both occurrences, identify that shared knowledge and explain which change to it would need to be repeated. Names or types that differ are not enough to establish shared knowledge.\n- MET: you read every added hand-written hunk and no pair repeats maintained knowledge under the standard, including when similar blocks serve independent concepts, follow the same framework idiom or are legitimately parallel repetition. This is a proof over a closed corpus, not a failure to find: the copy-paste that could have arrived with this change did not.\n- UNDETERMINED for a candidate pair you read and genuinely cannot call; quote it and name what would settle it. Do not resolve the doubt into MET, NOT_MET or NOT_APPLICABLE. Having read every added hunk and found no pair is MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, covering every flagged pair.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this signal alone — a formative practice, not a merge gate. Calibrate to consequence and spread. MINOR, the usual band: a duplicated presentation rule whose copies would need the same correction. Similar markup without a shared maintained rule is not a lapse. Duplicated logic where a divergent copy is a real bug risk weighs more. MAJOR: a copy already spreading to several places, or several distinct duplicated blocks. Identical added code yields identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nFor NOT_MET quote both occurrences by path and `[L<n>]` span, name the shared rule and explain its repeated maintenance; a duplication claim you cannot ground in two quoted added spans is not made. For MET record `evidence.search` over the diff, cite the repeated-looking blocks you actually checked, and state in the boundary that duplication against code outside the diff is out of scope either way. Describe only the observable copy-paste practice: never assert the code is buggy, will diverge or will break, and never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nWhether one block is too long or does too many things belongs to `keeps-functions-small-and-single-purpose`; never flag a single block here. Here only: repetition across added blocks.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for removes-duplication-instead-of-copy-pasting: every pair of added blocks\n// whose lines have the same shape once names and literals are set aside — the two spans, the run\n// length, how many lines are identical to the character, and the names that differ. The review\n// judges each pair: a unit of knowledge written twice, or an idiom the platform makes look alike.\nimport { duplicatePairs, MIN_RUN_LINES } from \"../lib/duplication.ts\";\nimport { isTestPath, languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nconst MAX_PAIRS = 20;\n\nexport default function removesDuplicationInsteadOfCopyPasting(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_metadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst sourceFiles = [...diffFiles.values()].filter(\n\t\t(file) => languageOf(file.path) !== null && !isTestPath(file.path),\n\t);\n\tconst linesAdded = sourceFiles.reduce((sum, file) => sum + file.addedLines.size, 0);\n\tconst pairs = duplicatePairs(diffFiles);\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = pairs.slice(0, MAX_PAIRS).map((pair) => ({\n\t\tfile: pair.a.path,\n\t\tline: pair.a.startLine,\n\t\tpattern: \"same-shaped added blocks\",\n\t\tcontext: `[L${String(pair.a.startLine)}]-[L${String(pair.a.endLine)}] and ${pair.b.path} [L${String(pair.b.startLine)}]-[L${String(pair.b.endLine)}]: ${String(pair.lines)} lines of one shape, ${String(pair.identicalLines)} identical`,\n\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\tflags: {\n\t\t\totherPath: pair.b.path,\n\t\t\totherStart: pair.b.startLine,\n\t\t\totherEnd: pair.b.endLine,\n\t\t\tlines: pair.lines,\n\t\t\tidenticalLines: pair.identicalLines,\n\t\t\tdiffering: pair.differing.join(\", \"),\n\t\t},\n\t}));\n\tconst directions =\n\t\tpairs.length === 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`No two runs of ${String(MIN_RUN_LINES)} or more added lines share a shape once names and literals are set aside. A copy this pairing cannot see — the same computation spelled with different statements, or shorter than ${String(MIN_RUN_LINES)} lines — is still yours to read for.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [\n\t\t\t\t\t`${String(pairs.length)} pair(s) of added blocks with the same line shapes, one row each with both spans and the names that differ. These are structural candidates, not established copies of maintained knowledge. Assess them under the practice standard.`,\n\t\t\t\t];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: { linesAdded, filesScanned: sourceFiles.length, pairs: pairs.length },\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Copy-pasted logic means every future fix has to land in every copy, and the copy you forget keeps the old behaviour, which is exactly the bug. Pull the shared part into one place and you fix it once.","whatGoodLooksLike":"By the time the same validation logic shows up a third time, you stop copying it and lift the shared concept into one named helper every caller uses, leaving a single source of truth instead of copies that drift apart. You hold off when blocks only look alike by coincidence, since forcing a shared abstraction on them is worse than the duplication.","groupSlug":"code-craftsmanship","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 204\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Copied logic</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"142.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"53\" font-size=\"15\">Checkout</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"32\" y=\"73\" font-size=\"15\">sum*rate</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"97\" font-size=\"15\">Invoice</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"32\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">sum*rate</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"15\">Copies drift apart</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Shared helper</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"142.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"53\" font-size=\"15\">Checkout</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"368\" y=\"73\" font-size=\"15\">taxOf(sum)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"97\" font-size=\"15\">Invoice</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"368\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">taxOf(sum)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"15\">taxOf(sum) = sum*rate</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"15\">One fix reaches both</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"Checkout and Invoice each copy sum*rate, so the copies drift apart. Both call taxOf(sum), which is defined once as sum*rate, so one fix reaches both."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Move logic that you would paste again into one named function, constant, loop or lookup table.\n- Pass in what differs between the copies, such as a value, a name or a type.\n- For example, instead of copying a tax calculation into two places, write one `addTax(sum)` and call it twice.\n- When `if` or `case` branches repeat the same steps, set the one constant that differs in each branch. Then run the steps once after them.\n- Name the shared part after the rule that it holds.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The change only updates documentation, diagrams or images.\n- Every added line is generated code, copied-in third-party (vendored) code or a test fixture.\n\nIt still applies when hand-written configuration repeats a rule that your team maintains.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Judging by length or by looks. Length alone does not make two blocks copies, and neither does a similar shape. Instead, ask if both blocks express the same rule. If they would change for different reasons, keep them apart.\n- Merging short idioms that only look alike. Some idioms repeat a few lines that the language or framework asks for. Examples are guard clauses (early `return` checks), field assignments, equality and hash methods, one-line `switch` arms and route registrations. Test cases and fixtures look alike on purpose too. Leave them as they are.\n- Growing a shared helper with flags. If each new caller adds a parameter and an `if` to the helper, the callers no longer share one rule. Instead, put the code back into each caller, and then extract only the part that they truly share.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Avoiding repetition](https://martinfowler.com/ieeeSoftware/repetition.pdf), Martin Fowler, IEEE Software, 2001 (DOI 10.1109/52.903160). Find what is the same and what varies, move the same part into one place, and pass in the difference. Some duplication is only a coincidence.\n- [Do code clones matter?](https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.05472), Juergens, Deissenboeck, Hummel and Wagner, ICSE 2009 (DOI 10.1109/ICSE.2009.5070547). In five industrial and open-source systems, copies of code were often changed in only some places. Nearly every second unplanned change of this kind was a fault.\n- [The wrong abstraction](https://sandimetz.com/blog/2016/1/20/the-wrong-abstraction), Sandi Metz, 2016. A shared helper that gains a parameter and a condition for each caller is the wrong abstraction. Put the code back into the callers and extract it again.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Factor out duplication instead of copy-pasting","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added hand-written code expresses each maintained rule in one appropriate place, without unjustified copy-paste.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one closed corpus: the lines this pull request adds. Flag a maintained rule or computation expressed independently in two or more added blocks, where a change to that rule would need the same correction in each copy (Fowler; Hunt & Thomas, DRY). Similar syntax is a lead to inspect, not the standard. Separate concepts may use the same framework idiom without sharing a rule; combining them merely to shorten the code creates an unnecessary coupling. A coherent multi-line computation, validation, mapping or transform, or constant or config block can be a flaggable copy. A one-liner, a guard clause, a field assignment or a forced-similar idiom (equals/hashCode, builder chains, switch arms, DI or route registrations) is not — an idiom is a few lines the platform dictates; a repeated multi-line block can carry duplicated knowledge, including a view body or a fetch-decode-assign sequence. Length alone never establishes that two blocks express the same rule. Legitimately parallel repetition is not a defect: test cases, fixtures — including preview, story and demo setups that show the same sample in another state, appearance or environment — and table-driven or parametrized bodies are meant to look alike. The exception covers the sample setup, not a production rule copied into it. Duplication can live within one function: sibling `if`, `case` or branch bodies that each repeat the same multi-line computation differing only in a constant are as flaggable as cross-function copies. Use judgment on whether two blocks are a genuine copy-paste or a coincidental shared idiom.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop only when the change offers no surface to judge: a documentation, diagram, image diff, or one whose added lines are entirely generated, vendored or test fixtures. Hand-written configuration that repeats a maintained rule is a surface to judge. A diff you read and found clean is not one of these; NOT_APPLICABLE says the work had no subject here, which is false of it.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the `[L<n>]` new-side spans of the added hunks. It establishes every candidate pair — a flaggable pair must sit inside these lines. It never establishes duplication against unchanged repository code, which is out of scope either way; pre-existing duplication you cannot see being introduced here is not judged.\n2. The file spread (`work/change/files.json`): which paths are generated or vendored (`*.gen.*`, lockfiles, OpenAPI specs, route trees, migrations and the like) or test fixtures. It establishes the hand-authored remainder you assess. It never establishes a pair.\n3. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the surrounding lines of a hunk cut off mid-block. It establishes the full shape of an added block. It never establishes a pair against code the change did not add.\nThe precomputed leads below these criteria list every pair of added blocks whose lines share a shape once names and literals are set aside, with both spans, the run length and the names that differ: decide every pair, and quote both spans. If the diff was not captured completely, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nBefore choosing, scan every added hand-written block for structural sameness, including branch bodies, not just whole functions. Then assess the standard against all relevant evidence.\n- NOT_MET: two or more added blocks express the same maintained rule or computation. Quote both occurrences, identify that shared knowledge and explain which change to it would need to be repeated. Names or types that differ are not enough to establish shared knowledge.\n- MET: you read every added hand-written hunk and no pair repeats maintained knowledge under the standard, including when similar blocks serve independent concepts, follow the same framework idiom or are legitimately parallel repetition. This is a proof over a closed corpus, not a failure to find: the copy-paste that could have arrived with this change did not.\n- UNDETERMINED for a candidate pair you read and genuinely cannot call; quote it and name what would settle it. Do not resolve the doubt into MET, NOT_MET or NOT_APPLICABLE. Having read every added hunk and found no pair is MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, covering every flagged pair.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this signal alone — a formative practice, not a merge gate. Calibrate to consequence and spread. MINOR, the usual band: a duplicated presentation rule whose copies would need the same correction. Similar markup without a shared maintained rule is not a lapse. Duplicated logic where a divergent copy is a real bug risk weighs more. MAJOR: a copy already spreading to several places, or several distinct duplicated blocks. Identical added code yields identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nFor NOT_MET quote both occurrences by path and `[L<n>]` span, name the shared rule and explain its repeated maintenance; a duplication claim you cannot ground in two quoted added spans is not made. For MET record `evidence.search` over the diff, cite the repeated-looking blocks you actually checked, and state in the boundary that duplication against code outside the diff is out of scope either way. Describe only the observable copy-paste practice: never assert the code is buggy, will diverge or will break, and never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nWhether one block is too long or does too many things belongs to `keeps-functions-small-and-single-purpose`; never flag a single block here. Here only: repetition across added blocks.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for removes-duplication-instead-of-copy-pasting: every pair of added blocks\n// whose lines have the same shape once names and literals are set aside — the two spans, the run\n// length, how many lines are identical to the character, and the names that differ. The review\n// judges each pair: a unit of knowledge written twice, or an idiom the platform makes look alike.\nimport { duplicatePairs, MIN_RUN_LINES } from \"../lib/duplication.ts\";\nimport { isTestPath, languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nconst MAX_PAIRS = 20;\n\nexport default function removesDuplicationInsteadOfCopyPasting(\n\t_repoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_metadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst sourceFiles = [...diffFiles.values()].filter(\n\t\t(file) => languageOf(file.path) !== null && !isTestPath(file.path),\n\t);\n\tconst linesAdded = sourceFiles.reduce((sum, file) => sum + file.addedLines.size, 0);\n\tconst pairs = duplicatePairs(diffFiles);\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = pairs.slice(0, MAX_PAIRS).map((pair) => ({\n\t\tfile: pair.a.path,\n\t\tline: pair.a.startLine,\n\t\tpattern: \"same-shaped added blocks\",\n\t\tcontext: `[L${String(pair.a.startLine)}]-[L${String(pair.a.endLine)}] and ${pair.b.path} [L${String(pair.b.startLine)}]-[L${String(pair.b.endLine)}]: ${String(pair.lines)} lines of one shape, ${String(pair.identicalLines)} identical`,\n\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\tflags: {\n\t\t\totherPath: pair.b.path,\n\t\t\totherStart: pair.b.startLine,\n\t\t\totherEnd: pair.b.endLine,\n\t\t\tlines: pair.lines,\n\t\t\tidenticalLines: pair.identicalLines,\n\t\t\tdiffering: pair.differing.join(\", \"),\n\t\t},\n\t}));\n\tconst directions =\n\t\tpairs.length === 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`No two runs of ${String(MIN_RUN_LINES)} or more added lines share a shape once names and literals are set aside. A copy this pairing cannot see — the same computation spelled with different statements, or shorter than ${String(MIN_RUN_LINES)} lines — is still yours to read for.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [\n\t\t\t\t\t`${String(pairs.length)} pair(s) of added blocks with the same line shapes, one row each with both spans and the names that differ. These are structural candidates, not established copies of maintained knowledge. Assess them under the practice standard.`,\n\t\t\t\t];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: { linesAdded, filesScanned: sourceFiles.length, pairs: pairs.length },\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Copy-pasted logic means every future fix has to land in every copy, and the copy you forget keeps the old behaviour, which is exactly the bug. Pull the shared part into one place and you fix it once.","whatGoodLooksLike":"By the time the same validation logic shows up a third time, you stop copying it and lift the shared concept into one named helper every caller uses, leaving a single source of truth instead of copies that drift apart. You hold off when blocks only look alike by coincidence, since forcing a shared abstraction on them is worse than the duplication.","groupSlug":"code-craftsmanship","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 204\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Copied logic</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"142.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"53\" font-size=\"15\">Checkout</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"32\" y=\"73\" font-size=\"15\">sum*rate</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"97\" font-size=\"15\">Invoice</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"32\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">sum*rate</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"15\">Copies drift apart</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Shared helper</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"142.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"53\" font-size=\"15\">Checkout</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"368\" y=\"73\" font-size=\"15\">taxOf(sum)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"97\" font-size=\"15\">Invoice</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"368\" y=\"117\" font-size=\"15\">taxOf(sum)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"15\">taxOf(sum) = sum*rate</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"15\">One fix reaches both</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"Checkout and Invoice each copy sum*rate, so the copies drift apart. Both call taxOf(sum), which is defined once as sum*rate, so one fix reaches both."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Move logic that you would paste again into one named function, constant, loop or lookup table.\n- Pass in what differs between the copies, such as a value, a name or a type.\n- For example, instead of copying a tax calculation into two places, write one `addTax(sum)` and call it twice.\n- When `if` or `case` branches repeat the same steps, set the one constant that differs in each branch. Then run the steps once after them.\n- Name the shared part after the rule that it holds.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The change only updates documentation, diagrams or images.\n- Every added line is generated code, copied-in third-party (vendored) code or a test fixture.\n\nIt still applies when hand-written configuration repeats a rule that your team maintains.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Judging by length or by looks. Length alone does not make two blocks copies, and neither does a similar shape. Instead, ask if both blocks express the same rule. If they would change for different reasons, keep them apart.\n- Merging short idioms that only look alike. Some idioms repeat a few lines that the language or framework asks for. Examples are guard clauses (early `return` checks), field assignments, equality and hash methods, one-line `switch` arms and route registrations. Test cases and fixtures look alike on purpose too. Leave them as they are.\n- Growing a shared helper with flags. If each new caller adds a parameter and an `if` to the helper, the callers no longer share one rule. Instead, put the code back into each caller, and then extract only the part that they truly share.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Avoiding repetition](https://martinfowler.com/ieeeSoftware/repetition.pdf), Martin Fowler, IEEE Software, 2001 (DOI 10.1109/52.903160). Find what is the same and what varies, move the same part into one place, and pass in the difference. Some duplication is only a coincidence.\n- [Do code clones matter?](https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.05472), Juergens, Deissenboeck, Hummel and Wagner, ICSE 2009 (DOI 10.1109/ICSE.2009.5070547). In five industrial and open-source systems, copies of code were often changed in only some places. Nearly every second unplanned change of this kind was a fault.\n- [The wrong abstraction](https://sandimetz.com/blog/2016/1/20/the-wrong-abstraction), Sandi Metz, 2016. A shared helper that gains a parameter and a condition for each caller is the wrong abstraction. Put the code back into the callers and extract it again.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Repeated logic gets lifted into one named helper"'::jsonb),'v5:7423cb9baf97e61bc71df3c8488369837ef3ba63a1482f0b34c64e6de11f5e5f','practice:v3:f51a6c15e6922122a1d4f6d1345365fceed7c33d30e943a68e823b216430e22f');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','keeps-functions-small-and-single-purpose','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Keep functions small and single-purpose","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added or substantially rewritten functions keep a coherent, scannable purpose without tangled concerns or obscuring nesting.\n\n## The standard\nAsk of the largest function the change introduces or substantially rewrites what a senior reviewer asks: can I hold its purpose in my head, name it in a phrase, and follow it at a glance? A focused function does one nameable job. The defect is a single body that visibly interleaves several SUBSTANTIAL, separable concerns — each a real multi-step block, genuinely interwoven, not a short serialize/encode/respond tail on a coherent pipeline — OR nests control flow so deep the core action is lost in indentation, OR runs so long its purpose stops being scannable. Not a defect: a linear pipeline of single-purpose stages (guard → build → encode → return; validate → operate → respond) is ONE purpose even when it ends by serializing a result; a thin body that delegates each step to a named helper in one call each (parse → fetch → render) is ONE orchestration, because the responsibilities live in the helpers — counting one-line delegated calls as separate concerns is the false positive to avoid. A defect: one body that parses input AND performs I/O AND applies business rules AND formats output, each written out and tangled together; a handler whose real action sits four conditionals deep. Reason like an expert about THIS code''s shape. Apply no line count and no fixed concern count, and never quote a threshold to the developer. Use your judgment for cases these examples do not cover.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the concrete fact, and stop only when the change adds no function body that could carry the defect: a documentation, diagram, image or configuration-only diff, a pure deletion or rename, or a diff dominated by generated or vendored files with essentially no hand-written body. That value says the work had no subject here, which is untrue of a change whose bodies you read and found focused.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added and rewritten bodies with their `[L<n>]` new-side markers — a closed set you can enumerate. It establishes each body''s length, nesting and the concerns written out in it. It never establishes that a function is correct, or how it is factored against collaborators you cannot see.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the pinned source of a body the diff truncates. Read it to finish reading that body. It never adds an unchanged caller, or a function merely renamed or called but not shown, to what you judge.\nIf a required body cannot be captured from either, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nRead every added or substantially rewritten body through. Then assess the standard against all relevant evidence.\n- NOT_MET: a quotable added body that carries the defect. Grade the single most consequential function; never sum across files, and never escalate because many separate small functions exist — many small focused units IS the baseline.\n- MET: the change adds bodies, you read every one, and none sprawls, nests deeply or interleaves separable concerns. The sprawl that could have arrived with this change did not. Record it with `evidence.search` over the diff, cite the largest body you read, and say in the boundary that you judged shape, not whether the functions are correct or well factored against collaborators you cannot see.\n- UNDETERMINED: no ordinary case. A body you can see is judged on its visible shape; a body you cannot see is a collection gap.\nOne observation per review.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on function shape alone — a craft practice about how work is shaped, never a correctness claim, non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: a reader can still follow the body and a single extraction would help. MAJOR: a body that clearly does it all, or whose core action is genuinely obscured by nesting.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery NOT_MET quotes the specific `[L<n>]` added span and names the responsibilities to split out or the nesting to flatten; a sprawl you cannot anchor to a quoted hunk is not emittable. Judge only function bodies visible in the diff or its pinned source; never reason about an unchanged caller, a function merely renamed or called but not shown, or code you did not read. Never claim to compile or run anything. Describe the observable shape; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nRepeated or near-identical blocks belong to `removes-duplication-instead-of-copy-pasting`; dead, commented-out or leftover scaffolding belongs to `leaves-the-code-clean-with-intent-revealing-comments`. Do not repeat another practice''s complaint. Duplication or dead code alone does not establish tangled responsibilities, but neither removes the function-body occasion: assess the shape on its own evidence, with MET only after the full read. Here only: does each introduced body hold one nameable job a reader can follow?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A function that does five things is hard to name, hard to test, and hard to change without breaking a part you forgot it touched. Small, focused functions are the ones you can actually hold in your head.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Instead of one long method that parses, validates, saves, and notifies, you have a few short functions, each doing one clearly named step you can read and test on its own.","groupSlug":"code-craftsmanship","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 172\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Tangled function</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"110.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">report() {</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"32\" y=\"81\" font-size=\"15\">for r in read(f) {</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"48\" y=\"101\" font-size=\"15\">sum += r</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"48\" y=\"121\" font-size=\"15\">print(r)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\">Hard to follow</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Focused functions</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"53\" font-size=\"15\">readRows()</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"72.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">totalRows()</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"112.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"133\" font-size=\"15\">renderReport()</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\">One job each</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A tangled function reads, sums and prints inside one loop, which is hard to follow. Focused functions readRows, totalRows and renderReport each do one job."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, name the job of each new or rewritten function in a short phrase. If its job is unclear, look for mixed jobs, deep nesting, or a body that is hard to scan.\n- When one body writes out several jobs and mixes them, move each job into its own function. For example, instead of one function that reads, totals and renders a report, write `readRows`, `totalRows` and `renderReport`.\n- When the main action sits deep inside nested conditions, handle each special case first and return early.\n\n![With nested checks, the main action sits deep inside the conditions and is buried. With early returns, each special case returns first, so the main action is no longer nested and is easy to find.](figures/early-returns.svg)\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change adds or rewrites no function body.\n- Your change only touches docs, diagrams, images or configuration.\n- Your change only deletes or renames code.\n- Your change is mostly generated files or copied third-party code, with almost no function body that you wrote.\n\nIt still applies when every function that you added or rewrote is already small and focused. It also applies when your change copies code or leaves dead code.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Counting lines or jobs. There is no line limit and no fixed number of jobs. Instead, ask if a reader can name the function’s job in a phrase and follow it at a glance.\n- Splitting a function that already does one job. A function that checks its input, does the work and returns the result does one job. So does a function that only calls named helpers one after another. Instead, split only where several full jobs are mixed in one body.\n- Splitting a long function into parts with vague names, such as `step2` or `handleData`. A vague name hides the purpose of the function. Instead, name each part after its job.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Function length](https://martinfowler.com/bliki/FunctionLength.html), Martin Fowler, 2016. When you must study code to see what it does, move it into a function named after what it does. Then the purpose of the function is clear, and a reader rarely needs the body.\n- [Replace nested conditional with guard clauses](https://refactoring.com/catalog/replaceNestedConditionalWithGuardClauses.html), Martin Fowler, Refactoring catalog. Check each special case first and return early, so the normal path is no longer nested.\n- [Write short functions](https://google.github.io/styleguide/cppguide.html#Write_Short_Functions), Google C++ Style Guide. Google prefers small, focused functions because they are easier to read, change and test. It sets no hard length limit.\n- [An empirical validation of cognitive complexity as a measure of source code understandability](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2007.12520), Muñoz Barón, Wyrich and Wagner, ESEM 2020. The study pooled about 24,000 measurements of how well people understood 427 code snippets. Code took longer to understand when it scored high on a measure that adds weight for deeper nesting. Results for correct answers were mixed.\n","figures":{"early-returns":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 152\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Nested checks</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"86.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"48\" width=\"112\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"32\" y=\"64\" width=\"128\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"48\" y=\"80\" width=\"104\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M65 96v16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"78\" y=\"109\" font-size=\"15\">save(order)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"148\" font-size=\"15\">Main action buried</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Early returns</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"86.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"48\" width=\"160\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"64\" width=\"176\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"80\" width=\"152\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M353 96v16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"366\" y=\"109\" font-size=\"15\">save(order)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"148\" font-size=\"15\">Main action easy to find</text>\n</svg>\n"}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Keep functions small and single-purpose","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added or substantially rewritten functions keep a coherent, scannable purpose without tangled concerns or obscuring nesting.\n\n## The standard\nAsk of the largest function the change introduces or substantially rewrites what a senior reviewer asks: can I hold its purpose in my head, name it in a phrase, and follow it at a glance? A focused function does one nameable job. The defect is a single body that visibly interleaves several SUBSTANTIAL, separable concerns — each a real multi-step block, genuinely interwoven, not a short serialize/encode/respond tail on a coherent pipeline — OR nests control flow so deep the core action is lost in indentation, OR runs so long its purpose stops being scannable. Not a defect: a linear pipeline of single-purpose stages (guard → build → encode → return; validate → operate → respond) is ONE purpose even when it ends by serializing a result; a thin body that delegates each step to a named helper in one call each (parse → fetch → render) is ONE orchestration, because the responsibilities live in the helpers — counting one-line delegated calls as separate concerns is the false positive to avoid. A defect: one body that parses input AND performs I/O AND applies business rules AND formats output, each written out and tangled together; a handler whose real action sits four conditionals deep. Reason like an expert about THIS code''s shape. Apply no line count and no fixed concern count, and never quote a threshold to the developer. Use your judgment for cases these examples do not cover.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the concrete fact, and stop only when the change adds no function body that could carry the defect: a documentation, diagram, image or configuration-only diff, a pure deletion or rename, or a diff dominated by generated or vendored files with essentially no hand-written body. That value says the work had no subject here, which is untrue of a change whose bodies you read and found focused.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added and rewritten bodies with their `[L<n>]` new-side markers — a closed set you can enumerate. It establishes each body''s length, nesting and the concerns written out in it. It never establishes that a function is correct, or how it is factored against collaborators you cannot see.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the pinned source of a body the diff truncates. Read it to finish reading that body. It never adds an unchanged caller, or a function merely renamed or called but not shown, to what you judge.\nIf a required body cannot be captured from either, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nRead every added or substantially rewritten body through. Then assess the standard against all relevant evidence.\n- NOT_MET: a quotable added body that carries the defect. Grade the single most consequential function; never sum across files, and never escalate because many separate small functions exist — many small focused units IS the baseline.\n- MET: the change adds bodies, you read every one, and none sprawls, nests deeply or interleaves separable concerns. The sprawl that could have arrived with this change did not. Record it with `evidence.search` over the diff, cite the largest body you read, and say in the boundary that you judged shape, not whether the functions are correct or well factored against collaborators you cannot see.\n- UNDETERMINED: no ordinary case. A body you can see is judged on its visible shape; a body you cannot see is a collection gap.\nOne observation per review.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on function shape alone — a craft practice about how work is shaped, never a correctness claim, non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: a reader can still follow the body and a single extraction would help. MAJOR: a body that clearly does it all, or whose core action is genuinely obscured by nesting.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery NOT_MET quotes the specific `[L<n>]` added span and names the responsibilities to split out or the nesting to flatten; a sprawl you cannot anchor to a quoted hunk is not emittable. Judge only function bodies visible in the diff or its pinned source; never reason about an unchanged caller, a function merely renamed or called but not shown, or code you did not read. Never claim to compile or run anything. Describe the observable shape; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nRepeated or near-identical blocks belong to `removes-duplication-instead-of-copy-pasting`; dead, commented-out or leftover scaffolding belongs to `leaves-the-code-clean-with-intent-revealing-comments`. Do not repeat another practice''s complaint. Duplication or dead code alone does not establish tangled responsibilities, but neither removes the function-body occasion: assess the shape on its own evidence, with MET only after the full read. Here only: does each introduced body hold one nameable job a reader can follow?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A function that does five things is hard to name, hard to test, and hard to change without breaking a part you forgot it touched. Small, focused functions are the ones you can actually hold in your head.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Instead of one long method that parses, validates, saves, and notifies, you have a few short functions, each doing one clearly named step you can read and test on its own.","groupSlug":"code-craftsmanship","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 172\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Tangled function</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"110.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">report() {</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"32\" y=\"81\" font-size=\"15\">for r in read(f) {</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"48\" y=\"101\" font-size=\"15\">sum += r</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"48\" y=\"121\" font-size=\"15\">print(r)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\">Hard to follow</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Focused functions</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"53\" font-size=\"15\">readRows()</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"72.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">totalRows()</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"112.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"133\" font-size=\"15\">renderReport()</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"15\">One job each</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A tangled function reads, sums and prints inside one loop, which is hard to follow. Focused functions readRows, totalRows and renderReport each do one job."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, name the job of each new or rewritten function in a short phrase. If its job is unclear, look for mixed jobs, deep nesting, or a body that is hard to scan.\n- When one body writes out several jobs and mixes them, move each job into its own function. For example, instead of one function that reads, totals and renders a report, write `readRows`, `totalRows` and `renderReport`.\n- When the main action sits deep inside nested conditions, handle each special case first and return early.\n\n![With nested checks, the main action sits deep inside the conditions and is buried. With early returns, each special case returns first, so the main action is no longer nested and is easy to find.](figures/early-returns.svg)\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change adds or rewrites no function body.\n- Your change only touches docs, diagrams, images or configuration.\n- Your change only deletes or renames code.\n- Your change is mostly generated files or copied third-party code, with almost no function body that you wrote.\n\nIt still applies when every function that you added or rewrote is already small and focused. It also applies when your change copies code or leaves dead code.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Counting lines or jobs. There is no line limit and no fixed number of jobs. Instead, ask if a reader can name the function’s job in a phrase and follow it at a glance.\n- Splitting a function that already does one job. A function that checks its input, does the work and returns the result does one job. So does a function that only calls named helpers one after another. Instead, split only where several full jobs are mixed in one body.\n- Splitting a long function into parts with vague names, such as `step2` or `handleData`. A vague name hides the purpose of the function. Instead, name each part after its job.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Function length](https://martinfowler.com/bliki/FunctionLength.html), Martin Fowler, 2016. When you must study code to see what it does, move it into a function named after what it does. Then the purpose of the function is clear, and a reader rarely needs the body.\n- [Replace nested conditional with guard clauses](https://refactoring.com/catalog/replaceNestedConditionalWithGuardClauses.html), Martin Fowler, Refactoring catalog. Check each special case first and return early, so the normal path is no longer nested.\n- [Write short functions](https://google.github.io/styleguide/cppguide.html#Write_Short_Functions), Google C++ Style Guide. Google prefers small, focused functions because they are easier to read, change and test. It sets no hard length limit.\n- [An empirical validation of cognitive complexity as a measure of source code understandability](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2007.12520), Muñoz Barón, Wyrich and Wagner, ESEM 2020. The study pooled about 24,000 measurements of how well people understood 427 code snippets. Code took longer to understand when it scored high on a measure that adds weight for deeper nesting. Results for correct answers were mixed.\n","figures":{"early-returns":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 152\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Nested checks</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"86.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"48\" width=\"112\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"32\" y=\"64\" width=\"128\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"48\" y=\"80\" width=\"104\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M65 96v16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"78\" y=\"109\" font-size=\"15\">save(order)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"148\" font-size=\"15\">Main action buried</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Early returns</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"86.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"48\" width=\"160\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"64\" width=\"176\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"352\" y=\"80\" width=\"152\" height=\"10\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M353 96v16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"366\" y=\"109\" font-size=\"15\">save(order)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"148\" font-size=\"15\">Main action easy to find</text>\n</svg>\n"}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Functions stay short and do one named step each"'::jsonb),'v5:31607ec39fc138c42028892d23f86c9a47e19185112222dfc052e9a057c99539','practice:v3:cab2c04eb3617978148320711a11951f6fd19a1f348d19f032cf1d8a49a080f3');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','leaves-the-code-clean-with-intent-revealing-comments','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Leave the code clean and comment the why","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added hand-written code has no established writing residue, and comments explain intent or constraints rather than restating code.\n\n## The standard\nJudge the added implementation lines, every one, for code that ships clean and comments that earn their place. Implementation is hand-written source, and markup or templates that implement application behavior. Decide each added surface by its purpose, never by its file extension or by what else the change contains: drawing data, documentation prose and configuration text are not implementation, even as editable text with comments and even beside added code, so a comment that labels the parts of a vector drawing is not residue. A senior reviewer scanning the implementation lines should find no residue: no commented-out code, no leftover debug or trace output, no throwaway scaffolding shipped as if final. Any comment that IS present should explain the WHY — intent, a non-obvious constraint, a unit, a caveat, a reference — rather than restate the WHAT the next line already says. An interface comment may instead tell callers the contract they rely on — behavior, inputs, results, failures — even though the body below implements it; whether it adds that context, rather than repeating the declaration, decides it. Blemishes, open and illustrative: an added line that is itself plausible source behind a comment marker (a commented-out assignment, call or control statement); a leftover breakpoint; a \"TEMP\" or \"remove before merge\" stub; \"// increment i\" above `i++`; a comment that narrates the editing session rather than the code (\"// FIX 2: added the date field\", \"// changed this to use X\", \"// moved from Foo\"); a comment with no code under it, left where code was meant to follow. Print-style diagnostics belong to `logs-through-the-platform-logger`, even when they are temporary traces. Do not report the same print here as residue. Clean, open and illustrative: a comment that gives a reason (\"retry once: upstream drops the first call after idle\"); output that is the file''s deliberate purpose (a CLI''s own stdout, a test''s expected output); a logger call through a real logging facility; a sanctioned TODO referencing a tracked issue. Code-shaped text inside a comment is not sufficient evidence of debris: documentation examples, API usage, commands and explanations may legitimately quote executable syntax. Assess the surrounding purpose, and flag an inactive implementation only when the captured context establishes it is leftover rather than an intentional example.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there were no hand-written added lines to read: an empty diff, or a diff that is all generated or vendored surface (lockfiles, `*.gen.*`, specs, build output, migrations). Exclude those files first; if no hand-written added surface remains, the work had no subject here. Implementation is the subject: a change that adds no implementation line — a documentation, diagram, image or configuration-only diff — has no subject here and is NOT_APPLICABLE. When the diff is truncated where the added lines live, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Sources\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): only the lines marked ADDED (`[L<n>] +` in the annotated diff). Residue is visible in the line itself. Removed and unchanged lines are not graded; code the author commented out and DELETED is a cleanup, never a blemish.\n2. The file list (`work/change/files.json`): which files are generated or vendored and so excluded.\n3. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the surrounding purpose of a file — whether its output is deliberate, whether a quoted snippet is an example. It never turns an unquotable line into a blemish.\nAn image or an external link: nothing.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- NOT_MET: a quotable blemish on an added hand-written implementation line. Do not count a print-style diagnostic as a blemish here; `logs-through-the-platform-logger` owns it. One blemish is enough: a single restating comment, marker or leftover you can quote is this cell at its lightest severity, never rounded down to MET because it is alone.\n- MET: you read all the added hand-written implementation lines through and none carries a blemish. This is a covered search, not a lucky miss: the residue that could have shipped did not. Record it.\n- UNDETERMINED only for a single candidate line you read and genuinely cannot call — a prose comment that may be restatement or explanation, or an inactive implementation that may be leftover or an example. Quote it and do not resolve the doubt in either direction.\nOne observation per review, over all added lines; a scatter of leftovers is one observation.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a proportionate, non-blocking nudge about clarity and maintenance burden. MINOR, the usual band: a lone stray comment or marker is the lightest touch; a scatter of leftovers is a modest cleanup. MAJOR only for a demonstrated, consequential source of confusion about the maintained implementation. Line count never raises severity: a long commented-out block is still one cleanliness nudge. Do not borrow error-handling or security consequences from other practices. Identical added lines yield identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the exact `[L<n>]` span and file path for every blemish; a blemish you cannot quote is not counted. For an inactive implementation, also quote the context that shows it is leftover. Never infer that a line is dead or debug-only unless its quoted text shows it. For MET, cite the search over the diff and state the boundary you did not cover: unchanged lines, files this diff does not touch, generated and vendored surface, added surface that is not implementation. Never claim a build or test ran. Describe what was left in the code; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nNaming, function and file size belong to `keeps-functions-small-and-single-purpose`; scope to `scope-one-reviewable-change`; duplication to `removes-duplication-instead-of-copy-pasting`; swallowed errors to `handles-errors-instead-of-swallowing-them`. Dead branches in live code and runtime defects belong to other practices. Here only: residue on added lines, and WHAT-versus-WHY comments.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for leaves-the-code-clean-with-intent-revealing-comments. Surfaces facts only — the LLM\n// judges whether each is real residue. On a large diff the model under-scans length and misses obvious\n// debug traces / leftover markers sitting right there; this points it at the exact added lines. General by\n// design: a per-language debug-output token table keyed off file extension. Adding a language = a row.\n// CANDIDATES, never an observation.\nimport { languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// language key -> [human label, regex] of debug-output / residue constructs that, when ADDED, are worth a look.\nconst LANG_PATTERNS: Record<string, [string, RegExp][]> = {\n\tswift: [\n\t\t[\"print(\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])print\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"debugPrint(\", /\\bdebugPrint\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"NSLog(\", /\\bNSLog\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"dump(\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])dump\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\ttypescript: [\n\t\t[\"console.*\", /\\bconsole\\.(?:log|debug|info|warn|error|trace)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"debugger\", /\\bdebugger\\b/u],\n\t],\n\tjavascript: [\n\t\t[\"console.*\", /\\bconsole\\.(?:log|debug|info|warn|error|trace)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"debugger\", /\\bdebugger\\b/u],\n\t],\n\tpython: [\n\t\t[\"print(\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])print\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"breakpoint(\", /\\bbreakpoint\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"pprint(\", /\\bpprint\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tjava: [\n\t\t[\"System.out/err.print\", /\\bSystem\\.(?:out|err)\\.print/u],\n\t\t[\"printStackTrace(\", /\\.printStackTrace\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tkotlin: [[\"println(\", /\\bprintln\\s*\\(/u]],\n\tgo: [\n\t\t[\"fmt.Print*\", /\\bfmt\\.Print[a-z]*\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"println(\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])println\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\truby: [[\"puts/p/pp\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])(?:puts|pp?)\\s+[\"''\\d:@]/u]],\n\trust: [[\"println!/dbg!/eprintln!\", /\\b(?:println|eprintln|dbg|print|eprint)\\s*!/u]],\n};\n\n// A TODO/FIXME/XXX/HACK marker added in the diff — a residue signal across all languages.\nconst TODO_MARKER = /\\b(?:TODO|FIXME|XXX|HACK)\\b/u;\n\n// A comment that narrates the editing session rather than the code, on any language''s comment line.\nconst PROCESS_NOTE =\n\t/^\\s*(?:\\/\\/|#|\\*)\\s*(?:FIX(?:ED)?\\s*\\d*\\s*:|CHANGED?\\s*:|ADDED\\s*:|MOVED\\s*:|UPDATED?\\s*:|REMOVED?\\s*:|NEW\\s*:)/iu;\n// A template placeholder left in a manifest the change edited.\nconst TEMPLATE_PLACEHOLDER = /#\\s*TODO:\\s*Adjust|<your[- ]|REPLACE[_ ]ME|CHANGE[_ ]ME/iu;\n\nexport default function leavesTheCodeCleanWithIntentRevealingComments(\n\t_repo: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst byLang: Record<string, number> = {};\n\tlet debugCandidates = 0;\n\tlet todoCandidates = 0;\n\tlet processNotes = 0;\n\tlet placeholders = 0;\n\tlet scratchFiles = 0;\n\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tconst patterns = lang === null ? [] : (LANG_PATTERNS[lang] ?? []);\n\t\t// A file added with a line or two of content and no language is a scratch file until read.\n\t\tconst added = [...df.addedLines.values()].filter((l) => l.trim().length > 0);\n\t\tif (\n\t\t\tlang === null &&\n\t\t\tdf.removedLines.size === 0 &&\n\t\t\tadded.length > 0 &&\n\t\t\tadded.length <= 2 &&\n\t\t\t/\\.(?:txt|md)$/iu.test(path) &&\n\t\t\t!/README|CHANGELOG|LICENSE/iu.test(path)\n\t\t) {\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\tline: [...df.addedLines.keys()][0] ?? 1,\n\t\t\t\tpattern: \"near-empty new file\",\n\t\t\t\tcontext: added[0]?.trim().slice(0, 160) ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\tflags: { kind: \"scratch\" },\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t\tscratchFiles += 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor (const [lineNum, text] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\t// Skip lines that are themselves comments — a debug call inside a comment is not live residue.\n\t\t\tconst trimmed = text.trim();\n\t\t\tconst isComment = /^(?:\\/\\/|#|\\*|\\/\\*)/u.test(trimmed);\n\t\t\t// One debug-output hint per added line, as the sibling validates-inputs script does.\n\t\t\tconst debugOutput = isComment ? undefined : patterns.find(([, re]) => re.test(text));\n\t\t\tif (debugOutput !== undefined) {\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline: lineNum,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: debugOutput[0],\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: trimmed.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: { kind: \"debug-output\" },\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\tdebugCandidates += 1;\n\t\t\t\tif (lang !== null) {\n\t\t\t\t\tbyLang[lang] = (byLang[lang] ?? 0) + 1;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (TODO_MARKER.test(text)) {\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline: lineNum,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: \"TODO/FIXME marker\",\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: trimmed.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: { kind: \"marker\" },\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\ttodoCandidates += 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (PROCESS_NOTE.test(text)) {\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline: lineNum,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: \"editing-process note\",\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: trimmed.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: { kind: \"process-note\" },\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\tprocessNotes += 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (TEMPLATE_PLACEHOLDER.test(text)) {\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline: lineNum,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: \"template placeholder\",\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: trimmed.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: { kind: \"placeholder\" },\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\tplaceholders += 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (debugCandidates > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Found ${debugCandidates} debug-output call(s) on added lines (print/console/log-style) — investigate whether each is leftover debug residue or a deliberate, intent-revealing log.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (todoCandidates > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Found ${todoCandidates} TODO/FIXME/XXX/HACK marker(s) added — investigate whether each is actionable residue or an intentional, tracked note.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (processNotes + placeholders + scratchFiles > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Found ${processNotes} comment(s) narrating the editing session, ${placeholders} template placeholder(s) and ${scratchFiles} near-empty new file(s) — residue of the writing process unless the surrounding text says otherwise.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tdebugCandidates,\n\t\t\ttodoCandidates,\n\t\t\tprocessNotes,\n\t\t\tplaceholders,\n\t\t\tscratchFiles,\n\t\t\t...byLang,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Stray debug output, blocks of commented-out code, and comments that just echo the line all add noise the next reader has to wade through. Clean code with a comment that explains the why reads like someone meant it.","whatGoodLooksLike":"No leftover print statements or commented-out branches, and where a line looks surprising, a short comment gives the reason, such as ''retry once: the upstream API drops the first call after an idle period'', rather than restating what the line does.","groupSlug":"code-craftsmanship","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 132\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Comment says what</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"70.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">// sleep for 250 ms</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"81\" font-size=\"15\">sleep(250)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"15\">Adds noise</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Comment says why</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"70.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">// stay under the rate limit</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"81\" font-size=\"15\">sleep(250)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"15\">Reason is clear</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A comment that says what, sleep for 250 ms, only adds noise above sleep(250). A comment that says why, stay under the rate limit, makes the reason clear."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, delete the code that you commented out while you worked. Version control keeps the old version for you.\n- Remove what you used only while you worked. Examples are a breakpoint, a “TEMP” stub, or an empty comment where code was meant to go.\n- If your project allows a TODO, link it to a tracked issue.\n- Delete a comment that only repeats its line. You must keep every comment up to date when the code changes, so each one must earn its place.\n- Where a line looks surprising, add a comment that gives the reason. Name the intent, a constraint, a unit, a caveat or a reference. For example, instead of `// sleep for 250 ms` above `sleep(250)`, write `// stay under the rate limit`.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The change is empty.\n- The change holds only generated or vendored files, such as lockfiles, build output, specs or migrations.\n- The change adds no code. It changes only documentation, diagrams, images or configuration.\n\nIt still applies when the change adds markup or templates that make the app work.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Deleting comments that help. A comment on a function can tell its callers what it takes, what it returns, and how it fails. A code example in that comment shows how to call it. Keep both when they say more than the declaration does.\n- Keeping old code in a comment in case you need it again. Nobody updates it, so it soon stops matching the code around it. Instead, delete it and find it in version control when you need it.\n- Writing about your edits instead of the code, such as “FIX 2: added the date field” or “moved from Foo”. The next reader sees only the code as it is now. Instead, delete the note, or say why the code is this way.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [What to look for in a code review](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/looking-for.html), Google Engineering Practices. Google recommends comments for reasons that the code cannot show, and function documentation for callers.\n- [Google Python Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html), Google. Google recommends comments for tricky code, and TODO links that give context, preferably through a tracked bug.\n- [The secret life of commented-out source code](https://doi.org/10.1145/3387904.3389259), Pham and Yang, 2020. Commented-out code is often not maintained, and the authors point to version control for keeping old code.\n- [A large-scale empirical study on code-comment inconsistencies](https://www.inf.usi.ch/faculty/lanza/PUBS/P/Wen2019a.pdf), Wen, Nagy, Bavota and Lanza, 2019. Keeping comments up to date as the code changes takes a lot of time and care.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Leave the code clean and comment the why","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added hand-written code has no established writing residue, and comments explain intent or constraints rather than restating code.\n\n## The standard\nJudge the added implementation lines, every one, for code that ships clean and comments that earn their place. Implementation is hand-written source, and markup or templates that implement application behavior. Decide each added surface by its purpose, never by its file extension or by what else the change contains: drawing data, documentation prose and configuration text are not implementation, even as editable text with comments and even beside added code, so a comment that labels the parts of a vector drawing is not residue. A senior reviewer scanning the implementation lines should find no residue: no commented-out code, no leftover debug or trace output, no throwaway scaffolding shipped as if final. Any comment that IS present should explain the WHY — intent, a non-obvious constraint, a unit, a caveat, a reference — rather than restate the WHAT the next line already says. An interface comment may instead tell callers the contract they rely on — behavior, inputs, results, failures — even though the body below implements it; whether it adds that context, rather than repeating the declaration, decides it. Blemishes, open and illustrative: an added line that is itself plausible source behind a comment marker (a commented-out assignment, call or control statement); a leftover breakpoint; a \"TEMP\" or \"remove before merge\" stub; \"// increment i\" above `i++`; a comment that narrates the editing session rather than the code (\"// FIX 2: added the date field\", \"// changed this to use X\", \"// moved from Foo\"); a comment with no code under it, left where code was meant to follow. Print-style diagnostics belong to `logs-through-the-platform-logger`, even when they are temporary traces. Do not report the same print here as residue. Clean, open and illustrative: a comment that gives a reason (\"retry once: upstream drops the first call after idle\"); output that is the file''s deliberate purpose (a CLI''s own stdout, a test''s expected output); a logger call through a real logging facility; a sanctioned TODO referencing a tracked issue. Code-shaped text inside a comment is not sufficient evidence of debris: documentation examples, API usage, commands and explanations may legitimately quote executable syntax. Assess the surrounding purpose, and flag an inactive implementation only when the captured context establishes it is leftover rather than an intentional example.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when there were no hand-written added lines to read: an empty diff, or a diff that is all generated or vendored surface (lockfiles, `*.gen.*`, specs, build output, migrations). Exclude those files first; if no hand-written added surface remains, the work had no subject here. Implementation is the subject: a change that adds no implementation line — a documentation, diagram, image or configuration-only diff — has no subject here and is NOT_APPLICABLE. When the diff is truncated where the added lines live, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Sources\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): only the lines marked ADDED (`[L<n>] +` in the annotated diff). Residue is visible in the line itself. Removed and unchanged lines are not graded; code the author commented out and DELETED is a cleanup, never a blemish.\n2. The file list (`work/change/files.json`): which files are generated or vendored and so excluded.\n3. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the surrounding purpose of a file — whether its output is deliberate, whether a quoted snippet is an example. It never turns an unquotable line into a blemish.\nAn image or an external link: nothing.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- NOT_MET: a quotable blemish on an added hand-written implementation line. Do not count a print-style diagnostic as a blemish here; `logs-through-the-platform-logger` owns it. One blemish is enough: a single restating comment, marker or leftover you can quote is this cell at its lightest severity, never rounded down to MET because it is alone.\n- MET: you read all the added hand-written implementation lines through and none carries a blemish. This is a covered search, not a lucky miss: the residue that could have shipped did not. Record it.\n- UNDETERMINED only for a single candidate line you read and genuinely cannot call — a prose comment that may be restatement or explanation, or an inactive implementation that may be leftover or an example. Quote it and do not resolve the doubt in either direction.\nOne observation per review, over all added lines; a scatter of leftovers is one observation.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a proportionate, non-blocking nudge about clarity and maintenance burden. MINOR, the usual band: a lone stray comment or marker is the lightest touch; a scatter of leftovers is a modest cleanup. MAJOR only for a demonstrated, consequential source of confusion about the maintained implementation. Line count never raises severity: a long commented-out block is still one cleanliness nudge. Do not borrow error-handling or security consequences from other practices. Identical added lines yield identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the exact `[L<n>]` span and file path for every blemish; a blemish you cannot quote is not counted. For an inactive implementation, also quote the context that shows it is leftover. Never infer that a line is dead or debug-only unless its quoted text shows it. For MET, cite the search over the diff and state the boundary you did not cover: unchanged lines, files this diff does not touch, generated and vendored surface, added surface that is not implementation. Never claim a build or test ran. Describe what was left in the code; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nNaming, function and file size belong to `keeps-functions-small-and-single-purpose`; scope to `scope-one-reviewable-change`; duplication to `removes-duplication-instead-of-copy-pasting`; swallowed errors to `handles-errors-instead-of-swallowing-them`. Dead branches in live code and runtime defects belong to other practices. Here only: residue on added lines, and WHAT-versus-WHY comments.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for leaves-the-code-clean-with-intent-revealing-comments. Surfaces facts only — the LLM\n// judges whether each is real residue. On a large diff the model under-scans length and misses obvious\n// debug traces / leftover markers sitting right there; this points it at the exact added lines. General by\n// design: a per-language debug-output token table keyed off file extension. Adding a language = a row.\n// CANDIDATES, never an observation.\nimport { languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// language key -> [human label, regex] of debug-output / residue constructs that, when ADDED, are worth a look.\nconst LANG_PATTERNS: Record<string, [string, RegExp][]> = {\n\tswift: [\n\t\t[\"print(\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])print\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"debugPrint(\", /\\bdebugPrint\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"NSLog(\", /\\bNSLog\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"dump(\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])dump\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\ttypescript: [\n\t\t[\"console.*\", /\\bconsole\\.(?:log|debug|info|warn|error|trace)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"debugger\", /\\bdebugger\\b/u],\n\t],\n\tjavascript: [\n\t\t[\"console.*\", /\\bconsole\\.(?:log|debug|info|warn|error|trace)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"debugger\", /\\bdebugger\\b/u],\n\t],\n\tpython: [\n\t\t[\"print(\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])print\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"breakpoint(\", /\\bbreakpoint\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"pprint(\", /\\bpprint\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tjava: [\n\t\t[\"System.out/err.print\", /\\bSystem\\.(?:out|err)\\.print/u],\n\t\t[\"printStackTrace(\", /\\.printStackTrace\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tkotlin: [[\"println(\", /\\bprintln\\s*\\(/u]],\n\tgo: [\n\t\t[\"fmt.Print*\", /\\bfmt\\.Print[a-z]*\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"println(\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])println\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\truby: [[\"puts/p/pp\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])(?:puts|pp?)\\s+[\"''\\d:@]/u]],\n\trust: [[\"println!/dbg!/eprintln!\", /\\b(?:println|eprintln|dbg|print|eprint)\\s*!/u]],\n};\n\n// A TODO/FIXME/XXX/HACK marker added in the diff — a residue signal across all languages.\nconst TODO_MARKER = /\\b(?:TODO|FIXME|XXX|HACK)\\b/u;\n\n// A comment that narrates the editing session rather than the code, on any language''s comment line.\nconst PROCESS_NOTE =\n\t/^\\s*(?:\\/\\/|#|\\*)\\s*(?:FIX(?:ED)?\\s*\\d*\\s*:|CHANGED?\\s*:|ADDED\\s*:|MOVED\\s*:|UPDATED?\\s*:|REMOVED?\\s*:|NEW\\s*:)/iu;\n// A template placeholder left in a manifest the change edited.\nconst TEMPLATE_PLACEHOLDER = /#\\s*TODO:\\s*Adjust|<your[- ]|REPLACE[_ ]ME|CHANGE[_ ]ME/iu;\n\nexport default function leavesTheCodeCleanWithIntentRevealingComments(\n\t_repo: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst byLang: Record<string, number> = {};\n\tlet debugCandidates = 0;\n\tlet todoCandidates = 0;\n\tlet processNotes = 0;\n\tlet placeholders = 0;\n\tlet scratchFiles = 0;\n\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tconst patterns = lang === null ? [] : (LANG_PATTERNS[lang] ?? []);\n\t\t// A file added with a line or two of content and no language is a scratch file until read.\n\t\tconst added = [...df.addedLines.values()].filter((l) => l.trim().length > 0);\n\t\tif (\n\t\t\tlang === null &&\n\t\t\tdf.removedLines.size === 0 &&\n\t\t\tadded.length > 0 &&\n\t\t\tadded.length <= 2 &&\n\t\t\t/\\.(?:txt|md)$/iu.test(path) &&\n\t\t\t!/README|CHANGELOG|LICENSE/iu.test(path)\n\t\t) {\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\tline: [...df.addedLines.keys()][0] ?? 1,\n\t\t\t\tpattern: \"near-empty new file\",\n\t\t\t\tcontext: added[0]?.trim().slice(0, 160) ?? \"\",\n\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\tflags: { kind: \"scratch\" },\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t\tscratchFiles += 1;\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor (const [lineNum, text] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\t// Skip lines that are themselves comments — a debug call inside a comment is not live residue.\n\t\t\tconst trimmed = text.trim();\n\t\t\tconst isComment = /^(?:\\/\\/|#|\\*|\\/\\*)/u.test(trimmed);\n\t\t\t// One debug-output hint per added line, as the sibling validates-inputs script does.\n\t\t\tconst debugOutput = isComment ? undefined : patterns.find(([, re]) => re.test(text));\n\t\t\tif (debugOutput !== undefined) {\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline: lineNum,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: debugOutput[0],\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: trimmed.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: { kind: \"debug-output\" },\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\tdebugCandidates += 1;\n\t\t\t\tif (lang !== null) {\n\t\t\t\t\tbyLang[lang] = (byLang[lang] ?? 0) + 1;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (TODO_MARKER.test(text)) {\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline: lineNum,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: \"TODO/FIXME marker\",\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: trimmed.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: { kind: \"marker\" },\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\ttodoCandidates += 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (PROCESS_NOTE.test(text)) {\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline: lineNum,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: \"editing-process note\",\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: trimmed.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: { kind: \"process-note\" },\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\tprocessNotes += 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (TEMPLATE_PLACEHOLDER.test(text)) {\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline: lineNum,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: \"template placeholder\",\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: trimmed.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: { kind: \"placeholder\" },\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\tplaceholders += 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (debugCandidates > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Found ${debugCandidates} debug-output call(s) on added lines (print/console/log-style) — investigate whether each is leftover debug residue or a deliberate, intent-revealing log.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (todoCandidates > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Found ${todoCandidates} TODO/FIXME/XXX/HACK marker(s) added — investigate whether each is actionable residue or an intentional, tracked note.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (processNotes + placeholders + scratchFiles > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Found ${processNotes} comment(s) narrating the editing session, ${placeholders} template placeholder(s) and ${scratchFiles} near-empty new file(s) — residue of the writing process unless the surrounding text says otherwise.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tdebugCandidates,\n\t\t\ttodoCandidates,\n\t\t\tprocessNotes,\n\t\t\tplaceholders,\n\t\t\tscratchFiles,\n\t\t\t...byLang,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Stray debug output, blocks of commented-out code, and comments that just echo the line all add noise the next reader has to wade through. Clean code with a comment that explains the why reads like someone meant it.","whatGoodLooksLike":"No leftover print statements or commented-out branches, and where a line looks surprising, a short comment gives the reason, such as ''retry once: the upstream API drops the first call after an idle period'', rather than restating what the line does.","groupSlug":"code-craftsmanship","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 132\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Comment says what</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"70.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">// sleep for 250 ms</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"81\" font-size=\"15\">sleep(250)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"15\">Adds noise</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Comment says why</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"70.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">// stay under the rate limit</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"81\" font-size=\"15\">sleep(250)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"15\">Reason is clear</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A comment that says what, sleep for 250 ms, only adds noise above sleep(250). A comment that says why, stay under the rate limit, makes the reason clear."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, delete the code that you commented out while you worked. Version control keeps the old version for you.\n- Remove what you used only while you worked. Examples are a breakpoint, a “TEMP” stub, or an empty comment where code was meant to go.\n- If your project allows a TODO, link it to a tracked issue.\n- Delete a comment that only repeats its line. You must keep every comment up to date when the code changes, so each one must earn its place.\n- Where a line looks surprising, add a comment that gives the reason. Name the intent, a constraint, a unit, a caveat or a reference. For example, instead of `// sleep for 250 ms` above `sleep(250)`, write `// stay under the rate limit`.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The change is empty.\n- The change holds only generated or vendored files, such as lockfiles, build output, specs or migrations.\n- The change adds no code. It changes only documentation, diagrams, images or configuration.\n\nIt still applies when the change adds markup or templates that make the app work.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Deleting comments that help. A comment on a function can tell its callers what it takes, what it returns, and how it fails. A code example in that comment shows how to call it. Keep both when they say more than the declaration does.\n- Keeping old code in a comment in case you need it again. Nobody updates it, so it soon stops matching the code around it. Instead, delete it and find it in version control when you need it.\n- Writing about your edits instead of the code, such as “FIX 2: added the date field” or “moved from Foo”. The next reader sees only the code as it is now. Instead, delete the note, or say why the code is this way.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [What to look for in a code review](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/looking-for.html), Google Engineering Practices. Google recommends comments for reasons that the code cannot show, and function documentation for callers.\n- [Google Python Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html), Google. Google recommends comments for tricky code, and TODO links that give context, preferably through a tracked bug.\n- [The secret life of commented-out source code](https://doi.org/10.1145/3387904.3389259), Pham and Yang, 2020. Commented-out code is often not maintained, and the authors point to version control for keeping old code.\n- [A large-scale empirical study on code-comment inconsistencies](https://www.inf.usi.ch/faculty/lanza/PUBS/P/Wen2019a.pdf), Wen, Nagy, Bavota and Lanza, 2019. Keeping comments up to date as the code changes takes a lot of time and care.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Code lands clean, with comments that explain why"'::jsonb),'v5:c37e4f50c4608a60316177e1d077ea880dc08494042f2dcc125ea921888340ff','practice:v3:735ea063029a566e31ddc2c59b27156a759a7e071f42bc7c6a1faa34154c304f');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','logs-through-the-platform-logger','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Log through the platform logger, not print","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: diagnostic messages the change adds go through the platform''s logger, not through print-style output, in application code.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one thing about the application code this change adds: when it reports a diagnostic — a state, an error, a step reached — does it use the platform''s logger or print-style output? Print-style output is the closed list `print(`, `debugPrint(`, `dump(` (Swift), `printf(` (Objective-C), `println(`/`print(` (Kotlin), `System.out.println`/`System.err.println`/`printStackTrace()` (JVM), `console.log/debug/info/warn/error` (TypeScript and JavaScript in application code), `print(` (Python). A logger is `Logger(subsystem:category:)`, the `os_log` functions or `NSLog`/`NSLogv`, which write to the Apple system log (Swift and Objective-C), Android''s `android.util.Log` (`Log.v/d/i/w/e/wtf`, whatever its tag) or Timber, SLF4J/Log4j/`java.util.logging`, a `winston`/`pino`/framework logger, Python `logging`. Print-style output is not a lapse where it is the program''s output: a command-line tool, a script, a build step, a test, a playground, a `#Preview`, or a `DEBUG`-only block (`#if DEBUG`) that removes itself from release. Exclude credential-bearing diagnostics that `avoids-insecure-defaults-and-over-broad-permissions` owns. Judge added lines only; an existing print the change did not touch is not this change''s.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the change adds no diagnostic line at all: no added print-style call and no added logger call in application code after the credential-exposure exclusion above. A change that adds only logger calls is an occasion with a positive answer; a change whose only prints are the program''s output (a CLI, a script) has no occasion. Added diagnostic lines are the occasion.\n\n## Sources\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`) with `[L<n>]` markers and the file list (`work/change/files.json`): each added print-style or logger call, the file it lands in, and whether a `#if DEBUG` or a test target encloses it. It establishes what was added where. It never establishes what an unchanged file does.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): whether the project already has a logger (`Logger(` definitions, a logging dependency in the manifest) and whether the file is an application target or a tool. Read it to classify the file and to say whether the print was written beside an existing logger.\n3. The precomputed leads below these criteria: the print-style and logger calls it found per file, and whether the checkout defines a logger, as leads, never conclusions.\nIf the diff is truncated where the calls are added, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nEnumerate the added diagnostic lines first, then assess the standard.\n- MET: every added diagnostic in application code goes through the platform logger, and any print-style call is the program''s output or `DEBUG`-only. Quote a representative logger call.\n- NOT_MET: an added print-style call reports a diagnostic in application code that ships — outside a CLI, script, test, preview or `DEBUG` block. Quote the line and the file.\n- UNDETERMINED: whether a file is an application target or a tool cannot be told from the diff or the checkout; name the file.\nOne observation per review, on the single most consequential lapse when there are several; count the rest in the summary.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this alone — non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: one or two prints in application code. MAJOR: an error path reported only by print, or a print that emits personal data. Credential exposure belongs to the security practice named below. Identical evidence yields identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the `[L<n>]` call and its file; for NOT_MET say why the file is application code (its target, its `import SwiftUI`/`UIKit`, its path). Never claim a print is debugging output without the line; never claim a file is a tool without the fact that makes it one.\n\n## Defer\nAn error swallowed rather than reported belongs to `handles-errors-instead-of-swallowing-them`; a secret in a message to `avoids-insecure-defaults-and-over-broad-permissions`; a leftover debugging line of another kind to `leaves-the-code-clean-with-intent-revealing-comments`. Do not report the same credential-bearing diagnostic here when `avoids-insecure-defaults-and-over-broad-permissions` owns its exposure. Here only: does the diagnostic go through the logger?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for logs-through-the-platform-logger: the print-style and logger calls ADDED in\n// application code, per language, and whether a bounded search of the checkout matches a logger. The\n// closed list of print shapes and logger shapes per language mirrors the criteria; whether a print is the\n// program''s output (a CLI, a script) or a shipped debugging line is the review''s to decide from the file.\nimport { grep } from \"../lib/grep.ts\";\nimport { countLabel, scanAddedLines, type SourcePattern } from \"../lib/source-scan.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\ninterface Shapes {\n\tprint: SourcePattern[];\n\tlogger: SourcePattern[];\n}\n\nconst SCRIPTING: Shapes = {\n\tprint: [[\"console.*\", /\\bconsole\\.(?:log|debug|info|warn|error)\\s*\\(/u]],\n\tlogger: [[\"logger\", /\\b(?:log|logger)\\.(?:trace|debug|info|warn|error|fatal)\\s*\\(/u]],\n};\n\n// The Apple system log: NSLog and NSLogv write to it, as the os_log family does.\nconst APPLE_SYSTEM_LOG: SourcePattern[] = [\n\t[\"os_log\", /\\bos_log(?:_(?:info|debug|error|fault))?\\s*\\(/u],\n\t[\"NSLog\", /\\bNSLogv?\\s*\\(/u],\n];\n\n// android.util.Log at any level and whatever its tag, and Timber over it. A bare `Log.` is read by name:\n// the import that makes it android.util.Log is the review''s to confirm.\nconst ANDROID_LOG: SourcePattern[] = [\n\t[\"android.util.Log\", /\\b(?:android\\.util\\.)?Log\\.(?:v|d|i|w|e|wtf)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t[\"Timber\", /\\bTimber\\.(?:v|d|i|w|e|wtf)\\s*\\(/u],\n];\n\n// language -> the print-style shapes and the logger shapes the criteria list for it.\nconst DIAGNOSTICS: Record<string, Shapes> = {\n\tswift: {\n\t\tprint: [\n\t\t\t[\"print(\", /(?:^|[^\\w.])print\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t\t[\"debugPrint(\", /\\bdebugPrint\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t\t[\"dump(\", /(?:^|[^\\w.])dump\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tlogger: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"Logger\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\bLogger\\s*\\(|\\b(?:logger|Logger\\.shared)\\.(?:trace|debug|info|notice|warning|error|critical|fault|log)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t...APPLE_SYSTEM_LOG,\n\t\t],\n\t},\n\t\"objective-c\": {\n\t\tprint: [[\"printf(\", /\\bprintf\\s*\\(/u]],\n\t\tlogger: APPLE_SYSTEM_LOG,\n\t},\n\tkotlin: {\n\t\tprint: [[\"println(\", /(?:^|[^\\w.])println?\\s*\\(/u]],\n\t\tlogger: [...ANDROID_LOG, [\"logger\", /\\blog(?:ger)?\\.(?:trace|debug|info|warn|error)\\s*\\(/u]],\n\t},\n\tjava: {\n\t\tprint: [\n\t\t\t[\"System.out/err\", /\\bSystem\\.(?:out|err)\\.print/u],\n\t\t\t[\"printStackTrace()\", /\\.printStackTrace\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tlogger: [\n\t\t\t...ANDROID_LOG,\n\t\t\t[\"logger\", /\\b(?:log|logger|LOG|LOGGER)\\.(?:trace|debug|info|warn|error|severe|fine)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t},\n\ttypescript: SCRIPTING,\n\tjavascript: SCRIPTING,\n\tpython: {\n\t\tprint: [[\"print(\", /(?:^|[^\\w.])print\\s*\\(/u]],\n\t\tlogger: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"logging\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\b(?:logging|logger|log)\\.(?:debug|info|warning|error|exception|critical)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t],\n\t},\n};\n\n/** Where a print is the program''s output rather than a diagnostic: the criteria''s exemptions, by path. */\nconst TOOL_PATH =\n\t/(?:^|\\/)(?:scripts?|tools?|bin|cli|Scripts|fastlane|ci)\\/|\\.(?:sh|mjs|cjs)$|(?:^|\\/)main\\.(?:py|ts|js)$|Playground|\\.playground\\//u;\n\nexport default async function logsThroughThePlatformLogger(\n\trepoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_metadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tlet prints = 0;\n\tlet loggers = 0;\n\tlet filesScanned = 0;\n\tlet linesAdded = 0;\n\tfor (const [language, shapes] of Object.entries(DIAGNOSTICS)) {\n\t\tconst scan = await scanAddedLines(repoPath, diffFiles, {\n\t\t\tlanguages: [language],\n\t\t\tpatterns: [...shapes.print, ...shapes.logger],\n\t\t\tmaxHints: 40,\n\t\t});\n\t\tfilesScanned += scan.filesScanned;\n\t\tlinesAdded += scan.linesAdded;\n\t\tprints += shapes.print.reduce((sum, [label]) => sum + countLabel(scan, label), 0);\n\t\tloggers += shapes.logger.reduce((sum, [label]) => sum + countLabel(scan, label), 0);\n\t\tfor (const hint of scan.hints) {\n\t\t\tconst isPrint = shapes.print.some(([label]) => label === hint.pattern);\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t...hint,\n\t\t\t\tpattern: `${language}:${hint.pattern}`,\n\t\t\t\tflags: {\n\t\t\t\t\t...hint.flags,\n\t\t\t\t\tkind: isPrint ? \"print\" : \"logger\",\n\t\t\t\t\ttoolPath: TOOL_PATH.test(hint.file),\n\t\t\t\t},\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tconst listed = hints.slice(0, 40);\n\tconst printsInToolPaths = listed.filter(\n\t\t(h) => h.flags.kind === \"print\" && h.flags.toolPath === true,\n\t).length;\n\t// Whether a bounded lexical search of the checkout matches a logger definition, import or native call.\n\tconst existing = await grep(\n\t\tString.raw`\\bLogger\\s*\\(|\\bos_log|\\bNSLogv?\\s*\\(|import android\\.util\\.Log\\b|\\bTimber\\b|LoggerFactory|import logging|from ''pino''|from \"pino\"|winston`,\n\t\trepoPath,\n\t\t{ maxResults: 5 },\n\t);\n\tconst directions: string[] =\n\t\tprints + loggers > listed.length\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`${String(prints + loggers)} diagnostic line(s) added; ${String(listed.length)} are listed. The counts by kind cover every line; the tool-path count covers only the listed rows.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\tif (prints > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`${prints} print-style call(s) added (${printsInToolPaths} listed under a tool or script path) against ${loggers} logger call(s); ${existing.length > 0 ? `the checkout matches a logger (${existing[0]?.file ?? \"\"})` : \"a bounded search of the checkout matched no logger definition, which does not show there is none\"}. Read each print''s file to decide whether it is the program''s output, a DEBUG-only block or a shipped diagnostic.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (loggers > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(`${loggers} logger call(s) added and no print-style call.`);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: listed,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tprintsAdded: prints,\n\t\t\tloggerCallsAdded: loggers,\n\t\t\tprintsInToolPaths,\n\t\t\tcheckoutHasLogger: existing.length > 0 ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tfilesScanned,\n\t\t\tlinesAdded,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Print-style diagnostics bypass the platform''s logging facilities. The platform logger — `Logger` on Apple platforms, `Log`/Timber on Android, SLF4J on the JVM, `logging` in Python, a logger module in Node — lets application diagnostics be collected and filtered where the app runs.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Diagnostics in application code go through the platform''s logger. `print`, `console.log`, `println` and `System.out` are left to output a program means to print — a command-line tool, a script, a build step, a test, a playground or a `#Preview` — or to a `DEBUG`-only block.","groupSlug":"code-craftsmanship","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 170\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Diagnostic printed</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">print(\"Save failed\")</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"96.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">Console</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"124\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">Save failed</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"15\">No level to filter by</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Diagnostic logged</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">log.error(\"Save failed\")</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"96.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">Platform log</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"460\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">ERROR Save failed</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"15\">Filterable by level</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"print(\"Save failed\") writes plain text Save failed to the console, with no level to filter by. log.error(\"Save failed\") writes ERROR Save failed to the platform log, so it is filterable by level."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Use the logger that your project already uses for the code around your change.\n- If the project has no logger yet, add the standard logger for your platform or framework.\n- Send each state, error or step through the logger, at the level that fits it. For example, instead of `print(\"Sync failed\")`, write `logger.error(\"Sync failed\")`.\n- If you print only while you debug, log the message at the debug level instead. On Apple platforms, you can also put the print in an `#if DEBUG` block.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change adds no diagnostic line to application code: no print call, such as `print` or `console.log`, and no logger call.\n- The only prints you add are the program’s own output, as in a command-line tool or a script.\n- The only diagnostic lines you add show a secret, such as a password or a token. A security practice covers those.\n\nIt still applies when your change adds only logger calls.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Replacing every print with a logger call. A print that is the program’s own output is correct, as in a command-line tool or a test. Instead, keep those prints and move only the diagnostics to the logger.\n- Reporting an error path only with a print. Instead, log the error at the error level. Then it reaches the platform logs at the error level, where you can filter for it.\n- Printing personal data, such as an email address. Instead, leave it out of the message, or log it through a logger that can hide private values.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Generating log messages from your code](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/os/generating-log-messages-from-your-code), Apple. Log messages carry a subsystem, a category and a level, so you can filter them. Errors are kept on disk, and private values can be hidden.\n- [Logging HOWTO](https://docs.python.org/3/howto/logging.html), Python Software Foundation. Use `print()` for the console output of a command-line program. Use a logger to report events, warnings and handled errors while a program runs.\n- [Logging Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP. Keep logging consistent across an application, and build on the logging that your framework gives you. Keep sensitive personal data out of logs, and take care with other personal data, such as email addresses.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Log through the platform logger, not print","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: diagnostic messages the change adds go through the platform''s logger, not through print-style output, in application code.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one thing about the application code this change adds: when it reports a diagnostic — a state, an error, a step reached — does it use the platform''s logger or print-style output? Print-style output is the closed list `print(`, `debugPrint(`, `dump(` (Swift), `printf(` (Objective-C), `println(`/`print(` (Kotlin), `System.out.println`/`System.err.println`/`printStackTrace()` (JVM), `console.log/debug/info/warn/error` (TypeScript and JavaScript in application code), `print(` (Python). A logger is `Logger(subsystem:category:)`, the `os_log` functions or `NSLog`/`NSLogv`, which write to the Apple system log (Swift and Objective-C), Android''s `android.util.Log` (`Log.v/d/i/w/e/wtf`, whatever its tag) or Timber, SLF4J/Log4j/`java.util.logging`, a `winston`/`pino`/framework logger, Python `logging`. Print-style output is not a lapse where it is the program''s output: a command-line tool, a script, a build step, a test, a playground, a `#Preview`, or a `DEBUG`-only block (`#if DEBUG`) that removes itself from release. Exclude credential-bearing diagnostics that `avoids-insecure-defaults-and-over-broad-permissions` owns. Judge added lines only; an existing print the change did not touch is not this change''s.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the change adds no diagnostic line at all: no added print-style call and no added logger call in application code after the credential-exposure exclusion above. A change that adds only logger calls is an occasion with a positive answer; a change whose only prints are the program''s output (a CLI, a script) has no occasion. Added diagnostic lines are the occasion.\n\n## Sources\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`) with `[L<n>]` markers and the file list (`work/change/files.json`): each added print-style or logger call, the file it lands in, and whether a `#if DEBUG` or a test target encloses it. It establishes what was added where. It never establishes what an unchanged file does.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): whether the project already has a logger (`Logger(` definitions, a logging dependency in the manifest) and whether the file is an application target or a tool. Read it to classify the file and to say whether the print was written beside an existing logger.\n3. The precomputed leads below these criteria: the print-style and logger calls it found per file, and whether the checkout defines a logger, as leads, never conclusions.\nIf the diff is truncated where the calls are added, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nEnumerate the added diagnostic lines first, then assess the standard.\n- MET: every added diagnostic in application code goes through the platform logger, and any print-style call is the program''s output or `DEBUG`-only. Quote a representative logger call.\n- NOT_MET: an added print-style call reports a diagnostic in application code that ships — outside a CLI, script, test, preview or `DEBUG` block. Quote the line and the file.\n- UNDETERMINED: whether a file is an application target or a tool cannot be told from the diff or the checkout; name the file.\nOne observation per review, on the single most consequential lapse when there are several; count the rest in the summary.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this alone — non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: one or two prints in application code. MAJOR: an error path reported only by print, or a print that emits personal data. Credential exposure belongs to the security practice named below. Identical evidence yields identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the `[L<n>]` call and its file; for NOT_MET say why the file is application code (its target, its `import SwiftUI`/`UIKit`, its path). Never claim a print is debugging output without the line; never claim a file is a tool without the fact that makes it one.\n\n## Defer\nAn error swallowed rather than reported belongs to `handles-errors-instead-of-swallowing-them`; a secret in a message to `avoids-insecure-defaults-and-over-broad-permissions`; a leftover debugging line of another kind to `leaves-the-code-clean-with-intent-revealing-comments`. Do not report the same credential-bearing diagnostic here when `avoids-insecure-defaults-and-over-broad-permissions` owns its exposure. Here only: does the diagnostic go through the logger?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for logs-through-the-platform-logger: the print-style and logger calls ADDED in\n// application code, per language, and whether a bounded search of the checkout matches a logger. The\n// closed list of print shapes and logger shapes per language mirrors the criteria; whether a print is the\n// program''s output (a CLI, a script) or a shipped debugging line is the review''s to decide from the file.\nimport { grep } from \"../lib/grep.ts\";\nimport { countLabel, scanAddedLines, type SourcePattern } from \"../lib/source-scan.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\ninterface Shapes {\n\tprint: SourcePattern[];\n\tlogger: SourcePattern[];\n}\n\nconst SCRIPTING: Shapes = {\n\tprint: [[\"console.*\", /\\bconsole\\.(?:log|debug|info|warn|error)\\s*\\(/u]],\n\tlogger: [[\"logger\", /\\b(?:log|logger)\\.(?:trace|debug|info|warn|error|fatal)\\s*\\(/u]],\n};\n\n// The Apple system log: NSLog and NSLogv write to it, as the os_log family does.\nconst APPLE_SYSTEM_LOG: SourcePattern[] = [\n\t[\"os_log\", /\\bos_log(?:_(?:info|debug|error|fault))?\\s*\\(/u],\n\t[\"NSLog\", /\\bNSLogv?\\s*\\(/u],\n];\n\n// android.util.Log at any level and whatever its tag, and Timber over it. A bare `Log.` is read by name:\n// the import that makes it android.util.Log is the review''s to confirm.\nconst ANDROID_LOG: SourcePattern[] = [\n\t[\"android.util.Log\", /\\b(?:android\\.util\\.)?Log\\.(?:v|d|i|w|e|wtf)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t[\"Timber\", /\\bTimber\\.(?:v|d|i|w|e|wtf)\\s*\\(/u],\n];\n\n// language -> the print-style shapes and the logger shapes the criteria list for it.\nconst DIAGNOSTICS: Record<string, Shapes> = {\n\tswift: {\n\t\tprint: [\n\t\t\t[\"print(\", /(?:^|[^\\w.])print\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t\t[\"debugPrint(\", /\\bdebugPrint\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t\t[\"dump(\", /(?:^|[^\\w.])dump\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tlogger: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"Logger\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\bLogger\\s*\\(|\\b(?:logger|Logger\\.shared)\\.(?:trace|debug|info|notice|warning|error|critical|fault|log)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t...APPLE_SYSTEM_LOG,\n\t\t],\n\t},\n\t\"objective-c\": {\n\t\tprint: [[\"printf(\", /\\bprintf\\s*\\(/u]],\n\t\tlogger: APPLE_SYSTEM_LOG,\n\t},\n\tkotlin: {\n\t\tprint: [[\"println(\", /(?:^|[^\\w.])println?\\s*\\(/u]],\n\t\tlogger: [...ANDROID_LOG, [\"logger\", /\\blog(?:ger)?\\.(?:trace|debug|info|warn|error)\\s*\\(/u]],\n\t},\n\tjava: {\n\t\tprint: [\n\t\t\t[\"System.out/err\", /\\bSystem\\.(?:out|err)\\.print/u],\n\t\t\t[\"printStackTrace()\", /\\.printStackTrace\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tlogger: [\n\t\t\t...ANDROID_LOG,\n\t\t\t[\"logger\", /\\b(?:log|logger|LOG|LOGGER)\\.(?:trace|debug|info|warn|error|severe|fine)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t},\n\ttypescript: SCRIPTING,\n\tjavascript: SCRIPTING,\n\tpython: {\n\t\tprint: [[\"print(\", /(?:^|[^\\w.])print\\s*\\(/u]],\n\t\tlogger: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"logging\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\b(?:logging|logger|log)\\.(?:debug|info|warning|error|exception|critical)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t],\n\t},\n};\n\n/** Where a print is the program''s output rather than a diagnostic: the criteria''s exemptions, by path. */\nconst TOOL_PATH =\n\t/(?:^|\\/)(?:scripts?|tools?|bin|cli|Scripts|fastlane|ci)\\/|\\.(?:sh|mjs|cjs)$|(?:^|\\/)main\\.(?:py|ts|js)$|Playground|\\.playground\\//u;\n\nexport default async function logsThroughThePlatformLogger(\n\trepoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_metadata: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tlet prints = 0;\n\tlet loggers = 0;\n\tlet filesScanned = 0;\n\tlet linesAdded = 0;\n\tfor (const [language, shapes] of Object.entries(DIAGNOSTICS)) {\n\t\tconst scan = await scanAddedLines(repoPath, diffFiles, {\n\t\t\tlanguages: [language],\n\t\t\tpatterns: [...shapes.print, ...shapes.logger],\n\t\t\tmaxHints: 40,\n\t\t});\n\t\tfilesScanned += scan.filesScanned;\n\t\tlinesAdded += scan.linesAdded;\n\t\tprints += shapes.print.reduce((sum, [label]) => sum + countLabel(scan, label), 0);\n\t\tloggers += shapes.logger.reduce((sum, [label]) => sum + countLabel(scan, label), 0);\n\t\tfor (const hint of scan.hints) {\n\t\t\tconst isPrint = shapes.print.some(([label]) => label === hint.pattern);\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t...hint,\n\t\t\t\tpattern: `${language}:${hint.pattern}`,\n\t\t\t\tflags: {\n\t\t\t\t\t...hint.flags,\n\t\t\t\t\tkind: isPrint ? \"print\" : \"logger\",\n\t\t\t\t\ttoolPath: TOOL_PATH.test(hint.file),\n\t\t\t\t},\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tconst listed = hints.slice(0, 40);\n\tconst printsInToolPaths = listed.filter(\n\t\t(h) => h.flags.kind === \"print\" && h.flags.toolPath === true,\n\t).length;\n\t// Whether a bounded lexical search of the checkout matches a logger definition, import or native call.\n\tconst existing = await grep(\n\t\tString.raw`\\bLogger\\s*\\(|\\bos_log|\\bNSLogv?\\s*\\(|import android\\.util\\.Log\\b|\\bTimber\\b|LoggerFactory|import logging|from ''pino''|from \"pino\"|winston`,\n\t\trepoPath,\n\t\t{ maxResults: 5 },\n\t);\n\tconst directions: string[] =\n\t\tprints + loggers > listed.length\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`${String(prints + loggers)} diagnostic line(s) added; ${String(listed.length)} are listed. The counts by kind cover every line; the tool-path count covers only the listed rows.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\tif (prints > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`${prints} print-style call(s) added (${printsInToolPaths} listed under a tool or script path) against ${loggers} logger call(s); ${existing.length > 0 ? `the checkout matches a logger (${existing[0]?.file ?? \"\"})` : \"a bounded search of the checkout matched no logger definition, which does not show there is none\"}. Read each print''s file to decide whether it is the program''s output, a DEBUG-only block or a shipped diagnostic.`,\n\t\t);\n\t} else if (loggers > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(`${loggers} logger call(s) added and no print-style call.`);\n\t}\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: listed,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tprintsAdded: prints,\n\t\t\tloggerCallsAdded: loggers,\n\t\t\tprintsInToolPaths,\n\t\t\tcheckoutHasLogger: existing.length > 0 ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tfilesScanned,\n\t\t\tlinesAdded,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Print-style diagnostics bypass the platform''s logging facilities. The platform logger — `Logger` on Apple platforms, `Log`/Timber on Android, SLF4J on the JVM, `logging` in Python, a logger module in Node — lets application diagnostics be collected and filtered where the app runs.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Diagnostics in application code go through the platform''s logger. `print`, `console.log`, `println` and `System.out` are left to output a program means to print — a command-line tool, a script, a build step, a test, a playground or a `#Preview` — or to a `DEBUG`-only block.","groupSlug":"code-craftsmanship","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 170\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Diagnostic printed</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">print(\"Save failed\")</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"96.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">Console</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"124\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">Save failed</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"15\">No level to filter by</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Diagnostic logged</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">log.error(\"Save failed\")</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"96.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"46.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">Platform log</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"460\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">ERROR Save failed</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"15\">Filterable by level</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"print(\"Save failed\") writes plain text Save failed to the console, with no level to filter by. log.error(\"Save failed\") writes ERROR Save failed to the platform log, so it is filterable by level."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Use the logger that your project already uses for the code around your change.\n- If the project has no logger yet, add the standard logger for your platform or framework.\n- Send each state, error or step through the logger, at the level that fits it. For example, instead of `print(\"Sync failed\")`, write `logger.error(\"Sync failed\")`.\n- If you print only while you debug, log the message at the debug level instead. On Apple platforms, you can also put the print in an `#if DEBUG` block.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change adds no diagnostic line to application code: no print call, such as `print` or `console.log`, and no logger call.\n- The only prints you add are the program’s own output, as in a command-line tool or a script.\n- The only diagnostic lines you add show a secret, such as a password or a token. A security practice covers those.\n\nIt still applies when your change adds only logger calls.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Replacing every print with a logger call. A print that is the program’s own output is correct, as in a command-line tool or a test. Instead, keep those prints and move only the diagnostics to the logger.\n- Reporting an error path only with a print. Instead, log the error at the error level. Then it reaches the platform logs at the error level, where you can filter for it.\n- Printing personal data, such as an email address. Instead, leave it out of the message, or log it through a logger that can hide private values.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Generating log messages from your code](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/os/generating-log-messages-from-your-code), Apple. Log messages carry a subsystem, a category and a level, so you can filter them. Errors are kept on disk, and private values can be hidden.\n- [Logging HOWTO](https://docs.python.org/3/howto/logging.html), Python Software Foundation. Use `print()` for the console output of a command-line program. Use a logger to report events, warnings and handled errors while a program runs.\n- [Logging Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP. Keep logging consistent across an application, and build on the logging that your framework gives you. Keep sensitive personal data out of logs, and take care with other personal data, such as email addresses.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Application code logs through the logger, never print"'::jsonb),'v5:8cb97bc15e28512a8248b11887b7f268bf44a38e7ef531721c1da2f2313f400d','practice:v3:d6c32695d543c638fc81d6dc6218916a94d9644deff5658935c20d15d68751b8');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('GROUP','robust-error-handling',NULL,'{"name":"Handling failure well","description":"Fail safely when things go wrong. Handle errors instead of swallowing them, check inputs and edge cases where data enters, and do not crash on a problem the code could recover from.","icon":"ShieldAlert","color":"rose"}'::jsonb,'{"name":"Handling failure well","description":"Fail safely when things go wrong. Handle errors instead of swallowing them, check inputs and edge cases where data enters, and do not crash on a problem the code could recover from.","icon":"ShieldAlert","color":"rose"}'::jsonb,'7b24cb0fa5353b9ff2a8393504862552544afc54530be75e7b01a3c1d43e31cb','group:v1:7b24cb0fa5353b9ff2a8393504862552544afc54530be75e7b01a3c1d43e31cb');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','handles-errors-instead-of-swallowing-them','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Handle errors instead of swallowing them","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added failure paths keep their meaningful signal visible or are safely absorbed under the Standard.\n\n## The standard\nWhen the change adds an error, rejection, invalid-input or required-result path, establish the operation and what its failure means from its captured contract, caller or owned state. Optional syntax, a defensive branch or an ordinary no-selection result alone does not establish failure.\n\nThe failure should stay visible: surfaced to a caller who can react, or recorded with enough context to diagnose it. It is handled when rethrown, returned, forwarded, wrapped with context, meaningfully recorded, or safely absorbed under the conditions below. A record may be a noticeable log, failure metric or error state the UI reads. Returning the operation''s own failure result through a declared result interface propagates it: a failable parse''s nil returned unchanged has not discarded an Error the operation never supplied. A captured caller is not needed to establish that return; downstream reaction is not proved. Reducing errors to one recognizable failure or unavailable result preserves their signal when no required work remains unfinished; distinct details are needed only when the captured contract or caller requires a different response to them. That coded failure indicator needs no intent comment. A success-looking default needs failure surfaced or recorded unless the captured source establishes why absorbing it is safe. The try? syntax alone settles neither safety nor failure.\n\nExpected cancellation is not a failure: work the program itself cancels, because a newer request superseded it or its owner went away, ends early by design, and ending it quietly is control flow that needs no comment, log or error state. The source must establish that cause — what cancels the work, and that its successor or owner then governs the state — and an error''s type or name alone does not. If the captured source genuinely leaves the cause or state ownership unknown, record UNDETERMINED and name what would settle it. A cancellation that the source shows leaves a caller waiting or a state showing unfinished work that nothing replaces is judged as a failure.\n\nA failure is swallowed when its meaningful signal is discarded and neither surfaced, recorded nor safely absorbed. Examples are an empty catch, unused error, ignored failure result, dropped rejection or bare return leaving required work unfinished. An empty-value check does not swallow a failure already recorded by its handler. A valid fixed URL adds no runtime-dependent parse failure. A runtime parse or network request may fail, but how that failure is handled is a separate judgment. Judge the ADDED code''s handling as a craft practice, not a syntax sweep.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop only when the hand-written change adds no failure path at all; neither expected cancellation nor a guarded case the source establishes cannot occur is one. That value says this work had no subject for the practice; saying it about a change with sound error handling tells the developer there was nothing here to see. It is valid only after the enumeration below finds no added failure path. Source code is the subject: a change that adds no source-code line — a documentation, diagram, image or configuration-only diff — has no subject here and is NOT_APPLICABLE.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added and modified lines with their `[L<n>]` new-side markers and the visible in-diff context. It establishes every failure path the change adds — a closed set you can enumerate from the added lines — and where each error goes. It never establishes that the handling is CORRECT, that the recovery works, or that paths outside the diff behave.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): pinned source around a hunk. Read it when where a caught error goes, or what downstream does with the missing value, sits just outside the hunk. It never establishes that anything was run, compiled or tested.\nPer-commit patches are not in your context; reason from what the diff shows. If the change adds a failure path but the relevant diff is truncated or unavailable, report a collection gap and record no observation: unread required evidence establishes neither absence nor uncertainty in the work.\n\n## Judge\nEnumerate the added operations and their error, invalid-input and required-result paths. Catches, try shortcuts, guards, optional bindings, early returns, failure results and fallbacks are leads to inspect, not proof of failure. Establish what fails and where it goes from the value origin, captured contract, caller or state, then apply the Standard to all actual paths. Do not assume a clean surface from a glance.\n- NOT_MET: quote the added hunk that loses a failure signal or leaves required work unfinished. Without a quotable discard or unfinished state, you do not have an observation.\n- MET: failure paths are added and each is handled under the Standard. Record evidence.search over the diff and the boundary of what is visibly returned, recorded or safely absorbed, not that downstream handling or recovery works.\n- UNDETERMINED: an actual failure path whose relevant handling the captured source genuinely does not show; name that path and what would settle it. Optional syntax or missing external knowledge alone is not this result.\nOne observation per review. Name the single most consequential swallowed failure, not an exhaustive list.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a formative practice to grow, never a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a swallow on a best-effort or clearly secondary path — cleanup, teardown, optional telemetry, or a failure merely logged a touch too quietly. MAJOR: a swallow that hides a real fault from the caller or user, on a path that persists, communicates, authenticates, parses or otherwise carries consequence. Several distinct swallows, together hiding more of the change''s failures, lean MAJOR. Apply these bands to the path''s source-established role and consequence, not its syntax. The examples of consequential operations do not by themselves make an optional or best-effort path MAJOR.\n\n## Grounding\nJudge only added or modified constructs (new-side [L<n>] markers); never grade pre-existing context or assume unseen code. Cite the added failure and discard or unfinished state for NOT_MET; for MET, cite its returned result, recorded error or evidenced safe absorption, with the source scan and its boundary. Do not claim a caller reacted, a UI displayed it or recovery worked without evidence.\n\nKnown language and API semantics can explain the captured operation, type, value origin or state; cite that captured anchor. A specific guarantee or possible failure of an exact API is a factual premise, not a conclusion from a guard or name. Use only semantics you can establish for that operation; do not invent a documented contract or claim to have read documentation you did not read. If those semantics are uncertain, distinguish that uncertainty from what the source shows and apply the Standard. Missing knowledge is not a fault in the work. Types and fixed values visible in source can establish a case without execution. Never claim to run, compile or test, invent an exoneration or give an unverified clean bill. Describe observable behavior, not intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nWhether inputs and edge cases are validated belongs to `validates-inputs-and-edge-cases-at-the-boundary`; a deliberate fail-fast crash on an unrecoverable state belongs to `avoids-unsafe-panics-and-chosen-crashes`. A printed failure with diagnostic context is recorded, not swallowed. Logger choice belongs to `logs-through-the-platform-logger`. Here only: does an added failure stay visible, or is it dropped?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for handles-errors-instead-of-swallowing-them: locate error-discarding / swallowing\n// constructs in ADDED code, across languages. CANDIDATES only — the LLM decides whether the error is\n// genuinely swallowed (no surfacing, logging, or recovery). Empty/no-op catch bodies routinely span lines\n// (`catch {\\n}`), so patterns run over a window of CONSECUTIVE added lines per file, NOT a single line.\n// General by design: a per-language pattern table keyed off the file extension. Adding a language = a row.\nimport { isCommentLine } from \"../lib/declarations.ts\";\nimport { languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// language key -> [human label, regex] of error-discarding / swallowing constructs in ADDED code. The `\\s`\n// in each regex spans the newlines joined into a window, so multi-line empty bodies match.\nconst LANG_PATTERNS: Record<string, [string, RegExp][]> = {\n\tswift: [\n\t\t[\"try? (discards error)\", /\\btry\\?\\s/u],\n\t\t[\"empty catch\", /\\bcatch\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\"catch with only print\", /\\bcatch\\s*\\{\\s*print\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\"error ignored: if let error {}\", /\\bif\\s+let\\s+error\\b[^{]*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t// Constructs the criteria ask to classify, not swallows in themselves: a fallback on a failable\n\t\t// conversion and a bare early return are where a failure is most often dropped quietly.\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"?? fallback on a failable conversion\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:Int|Double|Float|Decimal|Bool|URL|UUID)\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*\\?\\?/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"guard … else { return }\", /\\bguard\\b[^{]*\\belse\\s*\\{\\s*return\\s*\\}/u],\n\t],\n\ttypescript: [\n\t\t[\"empty catch\", /\\bcatch\\s*(?:\\([^)]*\\))?\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\".catch(noop)\", /\\.catch\\s*\\(\\s*\\(\\s*[^)]*\\)\\s*=>\\s*\\{\\s*\\}\\s*\\)/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"catch with only console\",\n\t\t\t/\\bcatch\\s*(?:\\([^)]*\\))?\\s*\\{\\s*console\\.[a-z]+\\([^)]*\\)\\s*;?\\s*\\}/u,\n\t\t],\n\t],\n\tjavascript: [\n\t\t[\"empty catch\", /\\bcatch\\s*(?:\\([^)]*\\))?\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\".catch(noop)\", /\\.catch\\s*\\(\\s*\\(\\s*[^)]*\\)\\s*=>\\s*\\{\\s*\\}\\s*\\)/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"catch with only console\",\n\t\t\t/\\bcatch\\s*(?:\\([^)]*\\))?\\s*\\{\\s*console\\.[a-z]+\\([^)]*\\)\\s*;?\\s*\\}/u,\n\t\t],\n\t],\n\tpython: [\n\t\t[\"except: pass\", /\\bexcept[^:]*:\\s*pass\\b/u],\n\t\t[\"bare except\", /^\\s*except\\s*:/mu],\n\t\t[\"except ...: continue\", /\\bexcept[^:]*:\\s*continue\\b/u],\n\t],\n\tgo: [\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"blank-identifier assignment (possible ignored error)\",\n\t\t\t/\\b_\\s*(?:,\\s*[a-zA-Z0-9_]+)?\\s*:?=\\s*[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_.]*\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"empty if err != nil\", /\\bif\\s+err\\s*!=\\s*nil\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t],\n\tjava: [\n\t\t[\"empty catch\", /\\bcatch\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"catch printStackTrace only\",\n\t\t\t/\\bcatch\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*[a-zA-Z0-9_.]*\\.printStackTrace\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*\\}/u,\n\t\t],\n\t],\n\tkotlin: [\n\t\t[\"empty catch\", /\\bcatch\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\"runCatching{}.getOrNull\", /runCatching\\s*\\{[\\s\\S]*?\\}\\s*\\.getOrNull\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t],\n\trust: [\n\t\t[\".ok(); (discards Err)\", /\\.ok\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;/u],\n\t\t[\"let _ = call()\", /\\blet\\s+_\\s*=\\s*[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_.:]*\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"if let Err(_) {} empty\", /\\bif\\s+let\\s+Err\\s*\\(\\s*_\\s*\\)[^{]*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t],\n\truby: [\n\t\t[\"rescue => e (unused)\", /\\brescue\\s*=>\\s*[a-z_]+\\s*$/mu],\n\t\t[\"rescue; end\", /\\brescue\\b[^;\\n]*;\\s*end\\b/u],\n\t],\n};\n\n// Group a file''s added lines into windows of CONSECUTIVE line numbers so a multi-line construct\n// (e.g. `catch {` then `}`) is one searchable text block without falsely joining distant additions.\nfunction consecutiveWindows(added: Map<number, string>): { start: number; lines: string[] }[] {\n\tconst windows: { start: number; lines: string[] }[] = [];\n\t// The open window carries its own first and last line number, so \"is there a window\" and \"what does\n\t// it end at\" are one nullable value rather than length checks against a scratch array.\n\tlet open: { start: number; end: number; lines: string[] } | null = null;\n\tfor (const n of [...added.keys()].toSorted((a, b) => a - b)) {\n\t\tif (open !== null && n !== open.end + 1) {\n\t\t\twindows.push({ start: open.start, lines: open.lines });\n\t\t\topen = null;\n\t\t}\n\t\topen ??= { start: n, end: n, lines: [] };\n\t\topen.end = n;\n\t\topen.lines.push(added.get(n) ?? \"\");\n\t}\n\tif (open !== null) {\n\t\twindows.push({ start: open.start, lines: open.lines });\n\t}\n\treturn windows;\n}\n\nexport default function handlesErrorsInsteadOfSwallowingThem(\n\t_repo: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst byLang: Record<string, number> = {};\n\tlet filesScanned = 0;\n\tlet linesAdded = 0;\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tif (lang === null) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst patterns = LANG_PATTERNS[lang];\n\t\tif (!patterns) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tfilesScanned += 1;\n\t\tlinesAdded += df.addedLines.size;\n\t\tfor (const w of consecutiveWindows(df.addedLines)) {\n\t\t\tconst text = w.lines.join(\"\\n\");\n\t\t\tfor (const [name, re] of patterns) {\n\t\t\t\tconst match = text.match(re);\n\t\t\t\tif (!match || match.index === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tconst offset = text.slice(0, match.index).split(\"\\n\").length - 1;\n\t\t\t\tconst lineNum = w.start + offset;\n\t\t\t\tconst lineContent = w.lines[offset] ?? \"\";\n\t\t\t\tif (isCommentLine(lineContent, lang)) {\n\t\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline: lineNum,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: `${lang}:${name}`,\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: lineContent.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: {},\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\tbyLang[lang] = (byLang[lang] ?? 0) + 1;\n\t\t\t\t// one hint per window — the LLM reads the full diff for the rest\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tconst directions =\n\t\thints.length > 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`Found ${hints.length} possible error-discard constructs in ${Object.keys(byLang).length} languages. First establish whether each call can fail. For each failure, inspect whether it stays visible. A valid fixed URL has no runtime-dependent parse failure. A later empty-value check does not swallow an error already recorded by its handler.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: { errorSwallowCandidates: hints.length, filesScanned, linesAdded, ...byLang },\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"An empty catch block turns a failure into a silent one: the code carries on as if nothing went wrong, and you find out only when something downstream breaks with no clue why. Surfacing the error saves the 2am debugging session.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A failed call reaches someone who can act. The code passes the error to a caller who can react, records it where a person will notice, or ignores it only for a reason that the code shows.","groupSlug":"robust-error-handling","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 104\">\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">Call fails</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M136 52h20v-32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M192 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"0.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">catch (e) { }</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M424 20h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M448 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">Error ignored</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M156 52v32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M192 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-accent-soft pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"64.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">throw SaveError(e)</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M424 84h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M448 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\">Caller shows error</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"When a call fails and an empty catch ignores the error, nobody learns of it. When the code throws SaveError(e), the caller shows the error."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- When a call fails and this code cannot fix it, pass the error to the caller with context. For example, instead of an empty `catch` on `saveOrder`, throw an error that names the order and wraps the original.\n- When no caller can react, record the failure where a person will see it. Use a log that someone reads, a failure metric, or an error state on the screen.\n- When the code does not show why a failure is safe to ignore, explain the reason in the handler.\n- Handle each error that a call returns and each promise that rejects.\n- When a lookup or a parse can fail, handle its failure branch too. A bare early return tells nobody what went wrong.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your hand-written code adds no call that can throw, reject or return an error. Generated or vendored files do not count.\n- Your change adds no source code, such as docs, diagrams, images, or configuration only.\n- Your change only ends work that a newer request replaces, or whose owner went away.\n- The source proves that the guarded bad case cannot occur, such as parsing a fixed valid URL.\n\nIt still applies when your new code already handles each failure well.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that every handler must rethrow. A log that a person will see keeps the failure visible. So does an error state on the screen. A normal empty state counts only when the failure is also logged with enough context to diagnose it.\n- Catching an error and never using it, or dropping it with a shortcut such as Swift’s `try?` or Go’s `_`. Instead, pass it on, log it, or write why it is safe to ignore.\n- Writing “ignore” over an empty handler. Say why silence is safe, such as “the file can already be gone”.\n- Leaving a TODO in place of a handler. Handle the failure before review.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Simple testing can prevent most critical failures](https://www.eecg.utoronto.ca/~stumm/Yuan-OSDI-14.html), Yuan and others, 2014. The study looked at 198 failures of five distributed systems. Wrong handling of signaled errors caused 92% of the catastrophic failures. About a third came from simple mistakes, such as an empty handler or a TODO.\n- [Caught exceptions: not ignored](https://google.github.io/styleguide/javaguide.html#s6.2-caught-exceptions), Google Java Style Guide. It is rarely correct to do nothing with a caught exception. When it is, a comment explains why.\n- [Handle errors](https://go.dev/wiki/CodeReviewComments#handle-errors), Go Code Review Comments, the Go project. Do not throw away an error with `_`. Handle it or return it.\n- [CWE-390: Detection of error condition without action](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/390.html), MITRE. An attacker can use an ignored error to put the system in an unexpected state.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Handle errors instead of swallowing them","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added failure paths keep their meaningful signal visible or are safely absorbed under the Standard.\n\n## The standard\nWhen the change adds an error, rejection, invalid-input or required-result path, establish the operation and what its failure means from its captured contract, caller or owned state. Optional syntax, a defensive branch or an ordinary no-selection result alone does not establish failure.\n\nThe failure should stay visible: surfaced to a caller who can react, or recorded with enough context to diagnose it. It is handled when rethrown, returned, forwarded, wrapped with context, meaningfully recorded, or safely absorbed under the conditions below. A record may be a noticeable log, failure metric or error state the UI reads. Returning the operation''s own failure result through a declared result interface propagates it: a failable parse''s nil returned unchanged has not discarded an Error the operation never supplied. A captured caller is not needed to establish that return; downstream reaction is not proved. Reducing errors to one recognizable failure or unavailable result preserves their signal when no required work remains unfinished; distinct details are needed only when the captured contract or caller requires a different response to them. That coded failure indicator needs no intent comment. A success-looking default needs failure surfaced or recorded unless the captured source establishes why absorbing it is safe. The try? syntax alone settles neither safety nor failure.\n\nExpected cancellation is not a failure: work the program itself cancels, because a newer request superseded it or its owner went away, ends early by design, and ending it quietly is control flow that needs no comment, log or error state. The source must establish that cause — what cancels the work, and that its successor or owner then governs the state — and an error''s type or name alone does not. If the captured source genuinely leaves the cause or state ownership unknown, record UNDETERMINED and name what would settle it. A cancellation that the source shows leaves a caller waiting or a state showing unfinished work that nothing replaces is judged as a failure.\n\nA failure is swallowed when its meaningful signal is discarded and neither surfaced, recorded nor safely absorbed. Examples are an empty catch, unused error, ignored failure result, dropped rejection or bare return leaving required work unfinished. An empty-value check does not swallow a failure already recorded by its handler. A valid fixed URL adds no runtime-dependent parse failure. A runtime parse or network request may fail, but how that failure is handled is a separate judgment. Judge the ADDED code''s handling as a craft practice, not a syntax sweep.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop only when the hand-written change adds no failure path at all; neither expected cancellation nor a guarded case the source establishes cannot occur is one. That value says this work had no subject for the practice; saying it about a change with sound error handling tells the developer there was nothing here to see. It is valid only after the enumeration below finds no added failure path. Source code is the subject: a change that adds no source-code line — a documentation, diagram, image or configuration-only diff — has no subject here and is NOT_APPLICABLE.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added and modified lines with their `[L<n>]` new-side markers and the visible in-diff context. It establishes every failure path the change adds — a closed set you can enumerate from the added lines — and where each error goes. It never establishes that the handling is CORRECT, that the recovery works, or that paths outside the diff behave.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): pinned source around a hunk. Read it when where a caught error goes, or what downstream does with the missing value, sits just outside the hunk. It never establishes that anything was run, compiled or tested.\nPer-commit patches are not in your context; reason from what the diff shows. If the change adds a failure path but the relevant diff is truncated or unavailable, report a collection gap and record no observation: unread required evidence establishes neither absence nor uncertainty in the work.\n\n## Judge\nEnumerate the added operations and their error, invalid-input and required-result paths. Catches, try shortcuts, guards, optional bindings, early returns, failure results and fallbacks are leads to inspect, not proof of failure. Establish what fails and where it goes from the value origin, captured contract, caller or state, then apply the Standard to all actual paths. Do not assume a clean surface from a glance.\n- NOT_MET: quote the added hunk that loses a failure signal or leaves required work unfinished. Without a quotable discard or unfinished state, you do not have an observation.\n- MET: failure paths are added and each is handled under the Standard. Record evidence.search over the diff and the boundary of what is visibly returned, recorded or safely absorbed, not that downstream handling or recovery works.\n- UNDETERMINED: an actual failure path whose relevant handling the captured source genuinely does not show; name that path and what would settle it. Optional syntax or missing external knowledge alone is not this result.\nOne observation per review. Name the single most consequential swallowed failure, not an exhaustive list.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a formative practice to grow, never a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a swallow on a best-effort or clearly secondary path — cleanup, teardown, optional telemetry, or a failure merely logged a touch too quietly. MAJOR: a swallow that hides a real fault from the caller or user, on a path that persists, communicates, authenticates, parses or otherwise carries consequence. Several distinct swallows, together hiding more of the change''s failures, lean MAJOR. Apply these bands to the path''s source-established role and consequence, not its syntax. The examples of consequential operations do not by themselves make an optional or best-effort path MAJOR.\n\n## Grounding\nJudge only added or modified constructs (new-side [L<n>] markers); never grade pre-existing context or assume unseen code. Cite the added failure and discard or unfinished state for NOT_MET; for MET, cite its returned result, recorded error or evidenced safe absorption, with the source scan and its boundary. Do not claim a caller reacted, a UI displayed it or recovery worked without evidence.\n\nKnown language and API semantics can explain the captured operation, type, value origin or state; cite that captured anchor. A specific guarantee or possible failure of an exact API is a factual premise, not a conclusion from a guard or name. Use only semantics you can establish for that operation; do not invent a documented contract or claim to have read documentation you did not read. If those semantics are uncertain, distinguish that uncertainty from what the source shows and apply the Standard. Missing knowledge is not a fault in the work. Types and fixed values visible in source can establish a case without execution. Never claim to run, compile or test, invent an exoneration or give an unverified clean bill. Describe observable behavior, not intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nWhether inputs and edge cases are validated belongs to `validates-inputs-and-edge-cases-at-the-boundary`; a deliberate fail-fast crash on an unrecoverable state belongs to `avoids-unsafe-panics-and-chosen-crashes`. A printed failure with diagnostic context is recorded, not swallowed. Logger choice belongs to `logs-through-the-platform-logger`. Here only: does an added failure stay visible, or is it dropped?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for handles-errors-instead-of-swallowing-them: locate error-discarding / swallowing\n// constructs in ADDED code, across languages. CANDIDATES only — the LLM decides whether the error is\n// genuinely swallowed (no surfacing, logging, or recovery). Empty/no-op catch bodies routinely span lines\n// (`catch {\\n}`), so patterns run over a window of CONSECUTIVE added lines per file, NOT a single line.\n// General by design: a per-language pattern table keyed off the file extension. Adding a language = a row.\nimport { isCommentLine } from \"../lib/declarations.ts\";\nimport { languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// language key -> [human label, regex] of error-discarding / swallowing constructs in ADDED code. The `\\s`\n// in each regex spans the newlines joined into a window, so multi-line empty bodies match.\nconst LANG_PATTERNS: Record<string, [string, RegExp][]> = {\n\tswift: [\n\t\t[\"try? (discards error)\", /\\btry\\?\\s/u],\n\t\t[\"empty catch\", /\\bcatch\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\"catch with only print\", /\\bcatch\\s*\\{\\s*print\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\"error ignored: if let error {}\", /\\bif\\s+let\\s+error\\b[^{]*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t// Constructs the criteria ask to classify, not swallows in themselves: a fallback on a failable\n\t\t// conversion and a bare early return are where a failure is most often dropped quietly.\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"?? fallback on a failable conversion\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:Int|Double|Float|Decimal|Bool|URL|UUID)\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*\\?\\?/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"guard … else { return }\", /\\bguard\\b[^{]*\\belse\\s*\\{\\s*return\\s*\\}/u],\n\t],\n\ttypescript: [\n\t\t[\"empty catch\", /\\bcatch\\s*(?:\\([^)]*\\))?\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\".catch(noop)\", /\\.catch\\s*\\(\\s*\\(\\s*[^)]*\\)\\s*=>\\s*\\{\\s*\\}\\s*\\)/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"catch with only console\",\n\t\t\t/\\bcatch\\s*(?:\\([^)]*\\))?\\s*\\{\\s*console\\.[a-z]+\\([^)]*\\)\\s*;?\\s*\\}/u,\n\t\t],\n\t],\n\tjavascript: [\n\t\t[\"empty catch\", /\\bcatch\\s*(?:\\([^)]*\\))?\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\".catch(noop)\", /\\.catch\\s*\\(\\s*\\(\\s*[^)]*\\)\\s*=>\\s*\\{\\s*\\}\\s*\\)/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"catch with only console\",\n\t\t\t/\\bcatch\\s*(?:\\([^)]*\\))?\\s*\\{\\s*console\\.[a-z]+\\([^)]*\\)\\s*;?\\s*\\}/u,\n\t\t],\n\t],\n\tpython: [\n\t\t[\"except: pass\", /\\bexcept[^:]*:\\s*pass\\b/u],\n\t\t[\"bare except\", /^\\s*except\\s*:/mu],\n\t\t[\"except ...: continue\", /\\bexcept[^:]*:\\s*continue\\b/u],\n\t],\n\tgo: [\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"blank-identifier assignment (possible ignored error)\",\n\t\t\t/\\b_\\s*(?:,\\s*[a-zA-Z0-9_]+)?\\s*:?=\\s*[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_.]*\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"empty if err != nil\", /\\bif\\s+err\\s*!=\\s*nil\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t],\n\tjava: [\n\t\t[\"empty catch\", /\\bcatch\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"catch printStackTrace only\",\n\t\t\t/\\bcatch\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*[a-zA-Z0-9_.]*\\.printStackTrace\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*\\}/u,\n\t\t],\n\t],\n\tkotlin: [\n\t\t[\"empty catch\", /\\bcatch\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t\t[\"runCatching{}.getOrNull\", /runCatching\\s*\\{[\\s\\S]*?\\}\\s*\\.getOrNull\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t],\n\trust: [\n\t\t[\".ok(); (discards Err)\", /\\.ok\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;/u],\n\t\t[\"let _ = call()\", /\\blet\\s+_\\s*=\\s*[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_.:]*\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"if let Err(_) {} empty\", /\\bif\\s+let\\s+Err\\s*\\(\\s*_\\s*\\)[^{]*\\{\\s*\\}/u],\n\t],\n\truby: [\n\t\t[\"rescue => e (unused)\", /\\brescue\\s*=>\\s*[a-z_]+\\s*$/mu],\n\t\t[\"rescue; end\", /\\brescue\\b[^;\\n]*;\\s*end\\b/u],\n\t],\n};\n\n// Group a file''s added lines into windows of CONSECUTIVE line numbers so a multi-line construct\n// (e.g. `catch {` then `}`) is one searchable text block without falsely joining distant additions.\nfunction consecutiveWindows(added: Map<number, string>): { start: number; lines: string[] }[] {\n\tconst windows: { start: number; lines: string[] }[] = [];\n\t// The open window carries its own first and last line number, so \"is there a window\" and \"what does\n\t// it end at\" are one nullable value rather than length checks against a scratch array.\n\tlet open: { start: number; end: number; lines: string[] } | null = null;\n\tfor (const n of [...added.keys()].toSorted((a, b) => a - b)) {\n\t\tif (open !== null && n !== open.end + 1) {\n\t\t\twindows.push({ start: open.start, lines: open.lines });\n\t\t\topen = null;\n\t\t}\n\t\topen ??= { start: n, end: n, lines: [] };\n\t\topen.end = n;\n\t\topen.lines.push(added.get(n) ?? \"\");\n\t}\n\tif (open !== null) {\n\t\twindows.push({ start: open.start, lines: open.lines });\n\t}\n\treturn windows;\n}\n\nexport default function handlesErrorsInsteadOfSwallowingThem(\n\t_repo: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst byLang: Record<string, number> = {};\n\tlet filesScanned = 0;\n\tlet linesAdded = 0;\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tif (lang === null) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst patterns = LANG_PATTERNS[lang];\n\t\tif (!patterns) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tfilesScanned += 1;\n\t\tlinesAdded += df.addedLines.size;\n\t\tfor (const w of consecutiveWindows(df.addedLines)) {\n\t\t\tconst text = w.lines.join(\"\\n\");\n\t\t\tfor (const [name, re] of patterns) {\n\t\t\t\tconst match = text.match(re);\n\t\t\t\tif (!match || match.index === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tconst offset = text.slice(0, match.index).split(\"\\n\").length - 1;\n\t\t\t\tconst lineNum = w.start + offset;\n\t\t\t\tconst lineContent = w.lines[offset] ?? \"\";\n\t\t\t\tif (isCommentLine(lineContent, lang)) {\n\t\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline: lineNum,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: `${lang}:${name}`,\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: lineContent.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: {},\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\tbyLang[lang] = (byLang[lang] ?? 0) + 1;\n\t\t\t\t// one hint per window — the LLM reads the full diff for the rest\n\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tconst directions =\n\t\thints.length > 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`Found ${hints.length} possible error-discard constructs in ${Object.keys(byLang).length} languages. First establish whether each call can fail. For each failure, inspect whether it stays visible. A valid fixed URL has no runtime-dependent parse failure. A later empty-value check does not swallow an error already recorded by its handler.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: { errorSwallowCandidates: hints.length, filesScanned, linesAdded, ...byLang },\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"An empty catch block turns a failure into a silent one: the code carries on as if nothing went wrong, and you find out only when something downstream breaks with no clue why. Surfacing the error saves the 2am debugging session.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A failed call reaches someone who can act. The code passes the error to a caller who can react, records it where a person will notice, or ignores it only for a reason that the code shows.","groupSlug":"robust-error-handling","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 104\">\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">Call fails</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M136 52h20v-32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M192 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"0.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">catch (e) { }</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M424 20h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M448 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">Error ignored</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M156 52v32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M192 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-accent-soft pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"64.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">throw SaveError(e)</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M424 84h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M448 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\">Caller shows error</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"When a call fails and an empty catch ignores the error, nobody learns of it. When the code throws SaveError(e), the caller shows the error."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- When a call fails and this code cannot fix it, pass the error to the caller with context. For example, instead of an empty `catch` on `saveOrder`, throw an error that names the order and wraps the original.\n- When no caller can react, record the failure where a person will see it. Use a log that someone reads, a failure metric, or an error state on the screen.\n- When the code does not show why a failure is safe to ignore, explain the reason in the handler.\n- Handle each error that a call returns and each promise that rejects.\n- When a lookup or a parse can fail, handle its failure branch too. A bare early return tells nobody what went wrong.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your hand-written code adds no call that can throw, reject or return an error. Generated or vendored files do not count.\n- Your change adds no source code, such as docs, diagrams, images, or configuration only.\n- Your change only ends work that a newer request replaces, or whose owner went away.\n- The source proves that the guarded bad case cannot occur, such as parsing a fixed valid URL.\n\nIt still applies when your new code already handles each failure well.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that every handler must rethrow. A log that a person will see keeps the failure visible. So does an error state on the screen. A normal empty state counts only when the failure is also logged with enough context to diagnose it.\n- Catching an error and never using it, or dropping it with a shortcut such as Swift’s `try?` or Go’s `_`. Instead, pass it on, log it, or write why it is safe to ignore.\n- Writing “ignore” over an empty handler. Say why silence is safe, such as “the file can already be gone”.\n- Leaving a TODO in place of a handler. Handle the failure before review.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Simple testing can prevent most critical failures](https://www.eecg.utoronto.ca/~stumm/Yuan-OSDI-14.html), Yuan and others, 2014. The study looked at 198 failures of five distributed systems. Wrong handling of signaled errors caused 92% of the catastrophic failures. About a third came from simple mistakes, such as an empty handler or a TODO.\n- [Caught exceptions: not ignored](https://google.github.io/styleguide/javaguide.html#s6.2-caught-exceptions), Google Java Style Guide. It is rarely correct to do nothing with a caught exception. When it is, a comment explains why.\n- [Handle errors](https://go.dev/wiki/CodeReviewComments#handle-errors), Go Code Review Comments, the Go project. Do not throw away an error with `_`. Handle it or return it.\n- [CWE-390: Detection of error condition without action](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/390.html), MITRE. An attacker can use an ignored error to put the system in an unexpected state.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Failures surface or recover, never get swallowed"'::jsonb),'v5:ca01bde4132935f6b9e38c6fec89a4aa0f35a1f4abb6006cb3bc337fd80cecc4','practice:v3:86c22639a2ef431e1ef3fef97630533da8c5b43fe373e404ea40dff325d9b39f');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','validates-inputs-and-edge-cases-at-the-boundary','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Validate inputs and edge cases at the boundary","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added consuming sites handle the obvious bad case of values they do not control.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether added or modified code that consumes a value it does not control handles the obvious failure case on purpose, instead of assuming a happy path. A boundary value enters from outside the local logic: a request param, body or header; text a user typed, once it is parsed or used as a number; a decoded network or file payload, including a bundled data file; a collection filled at runtime, such as a response array or a stored list the user populates; an env, config or defaults read; the result of a third-party or RPC call. Not a boundary value: a parameter of the app''s own function or view, a framework callback''s arguments, view state the code owns, an author literal or constant, and a value a control already bounds (a Slider or Stepper range, a Picker over an enum). A site is in scope only when the same change also USES that value — indexes or slices it, divides by it, converts it to an integer, aggregates it, or passes it into logic that assumes a shape. A value merely forwarded, stored or displayed as the type it already has is not a consuming site: an empty string saved or shown breaks nothing.\nA guard is anything on the value''s path that rules out the case the site itself needs, in the hunk or in code you can trace to: a count, bounds or empty check (`isEmpty` guards `[0]`, not `[1]` or `[0..<2]`); a zero check before integer division or before a ratio that is shown or converted to an integer; a clamp; do/catch, `try?`, or a `try` that propagates out of a throwing function; a default for an absent value; an early return; a button disabled on the bad case. An operation already total on the bad case needs no guard: a Swift optional the type forces the author to unwrap (`Int(text)`, `.first`, `dict[key]`, `URL(string:)`, `as?`) handled with `if let`, `guard let`, `?.` or `??`; `reduce`, `map`, `filter`, `prefix` or `ForEach` over an empty collection; `items[i]` inside `ForEach(items.indices)`. A default counts unless it is an extreme sentinel (`Int.max`, `.infinity`) that reaches output unchanged. Judge the PRACTICE — is the obvious failure handled at all — not an exhaustive lint: a finite check on a decoded Double, or a zero check on a field the source never sends as zero, is not the obvious case.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the change consumes no boundary value at all: an empty diff, a diff with no consuming site, or only generated or vendored files remaining. Source code is the subject: a change that adds no source-code line — a documentation, diagram, image or configuration-only diff — has no subject here and is NOT_APPLICABLE. So is a change whose values are all typed in-app data, view state and bounded controls, such as a view drawing a typed array it was handed. Never invent an input surface.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added and modified lines with their `[L<n>]` new-side markers and the visible in-diff context. It establishes every consuming site the change introduces — a closed set you can enumerate from the added lines — and whether each meets a guard you can see. It never establishes that the guards are sufficient for every input or that callers beyond the diff are safe.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): pinned source around a hunk. Read it when the guard that would decide a site could sit outside the hunk — above a truncated hunk, or in the caller or view that builds the value. It establishes whether such a guard exists. It never establishes that anything was run, compiled or tested.\nPer-commit patches are not in your context. If the context a deciding site needs remains unavailable after reading the pinned source, report a collection gap and record no observation, rather than asserting absence or inapplicability.\n\n## Judge\nScan the changed lines for a boundary value that gets consumed and trace each to its guard; then assess the standard.\n- NOT_MET: a boundary value is consumed with nothing on its path ruling out its obvious bad case, so a realistic input — an empty or short response array, an empty bundled list, a library the user has not filled yet — crashes the new path or shows a wrong value. For example `Array(items[0..<2])` on a decoded array behind only an `isEmpty` check, or a minimum seeded with `Int.max` and printed while the list is still empty. Quote the exact `[L<n>]` span of each unguarded site, name the realistic input that breaks it and the missing guard (\"count >= 2 before [0..<2]\").\n- MET: the ordinary result when the change consumes boundary values: you walked every consuming site and each handles its obvious bad case. Looks like a lapse but is not: `a[3]` inside `if a.count >= 4`; `Int(text)` bound with `guard let`; `try decoder.decode` in a throwing function whose caller catches; `pages[index]` when the only way in is a button disabled while `pages` is empty; `offset / 250` in a gesture. The claim is about the coverage of your reading, nothing about correctness.\n- UNDETERMINED for a consuming site you can see whose guard the diff and the pinned source do not settle; name the site and what would settle it. Having walked every site and found each guarded is MET, not UNDETERMINED. Missing required context is a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review, covering every unguarded site found.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a practice to grow, not a merge gate. Calibrate to the demonstrated consequence at the boundary and explain the observable risk. MINOR, the usual band: an omission on a low-impact operation. MAJOR: a missing check on a consequential operation, which one site can warrant; several harmless omissions do not escalate on their own. Identical hunks yield identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nJudge only the added and modified lines and their visible in-diff context, plus pinned source you actually read; a site in unchanged code is never charged, even when a changed line feeds it. For NOT_MET, cite the `[L<n>]` span of each unguarded site. For MET, record `evidence.search` over the diff and state in the boundary what you did NOT establish: that the guards are sufficient, that they cover every case, that callers beyond the diff are safe. Never assert a missing guard you cannot point to; never claim to run, compile or test anything; never invent a clean bill of health. Describe the observable code behaviour, never intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nA value interpolated raw into a URL, query, command or markup belongs to `validates-and-escapes-untrusted-input`. A crash operator the author wrote — `x!`, `try!`, `as!`, `fatalError`, `unwrap()` — belongs to `avoids-unsafe-panics-and-chosen-crashes`: it is neither a guard nor a consuming site here. An implicit trap stays here: an unchecked subscript or slice, an integer division, a conversion to an integer of a value that can be NaN or infinite. The quality of error handling once input is guarded belongs to `handles-errors-instead-of-swallowing-them`; a catch that merely prevents the crash satisfies the guard here regardless of its body. Do not repeat another practice''s complaint, and do not let another kind of defect stand in for this one.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for validates-inputs-and-edge-cases-at-the-boundary: the added lines of source code\n// that hold a construct which can fail or mislead on an edge value — a subscript or slice, a division by\n// a name, a parse the language does not force the author to handle, an extreme sentinel, a payload\n// decode, a request''s own input — across languages. A row marks where a consuming site may be, never an\n// observation: whether its value comes from outside the local logic, and what already handles its bad case,\n// is read from the code, and the criteria decide. String-literal text and comments are not scanned; a\n// lookup or parse whose result the language makes optional is not listed, and a crash operator the author\n// wrote (`!`, `try!`, `unwrap()`) is the crash practice''s. Adding a language = adding a row.\nimport { codeLines, isCommentLine } from \"../lib/declarations.ts\";\nimport { isTestPath, languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nconst COMMON: [string, RegExp][] = [\n\t// `items[i]`, `cast[0..<2]`; not a lookup already made safe (`dict[k] ?? d`, `[k, default: 0]`,\n\t// `ns[k] as? T`) nor an assignment target.\n\t[\n\t\t\"subscript or slice\",\n\t\t/[A-Za-z0-9_)\\]]\\[(?![^\\]]*\\bdefault:)[^\\]]+\\](?!\\s*(?:\\?\\?|as\\?|=(?!=)))/u,\n\t],\n\t// A divisor that is a name or a call; a literal divisor cannot be zero.\n\t[\"division or remainder by a name\", /[A-Za-z0-9_)\\]]\\s*[/%](?![/*=])\\s*[A-Za-z_(]/u],\n\t// The seed of a minimum, a maximum or a fallback that can reach output unchanged.\n\t[\n\t\t\"extreme sentinel\",\n\t\t/\\b(?:U?Int\\d*|Double|Float|CGFloat)\\.(?:max|min|infinity|greatestFiniteMagnitude)\\b|\\b(?:Integer|Long|Double|Float)\\.(?:MAX|MIN)_VALUE\\b|\\bNumber\\.(?:MAX|MIN)_(?:SAFE_INTEGER|VALUE)\\b|\\bsys\\.maxsize\\b|\\bmath\\.inf\\b/u,\n\t],\n];\n\n// language key -> [label, regex] of edge constructs in added code, beside COMMON.\nconst LANG_PATTERNS: Record<string, [string, RegExp][]> = {\n\t// `Int(text)`, `Double(s)`, `.first`, `dict[key]` return Optionals the compiler makes the author handle.\n\tswift: [[\"payload decode\", /(?<![Cc]ontainer)\\.decode\\s*\\(/u]],\n\ttypescript: [\n\t\t[\"parseInt/parseFloat\", /\\bparse(?:Int|Float)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"Number(...) parse\", /\\bNumber\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"JSON.parse\", /\\bJSON\\.parse\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tjavascript: [\n\t\t[\"parseInt/parseFloat\", /\\bparse(?:Int|Float)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"Number(...) parse\", /\\bNumber\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"JSON.parse\", /\\bJSON\\.parse\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tpython: [\n\t\t[\"int()/float() parse\", /\\b(?:int|float)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"json.loads\", /\\bjson\\.loads\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\".pop()\", /\\.pop\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"next(...)\", /\\bnext\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tgo: [\n\t\t[\"strconv parse\", /\\bstrconv\\.(?:Atoi|Parse[A-Za-z]+)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"json.Unmarshal\", /\\bjson\\.Unmarshal\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tjava: [\n\t\t[\".get(i) access\", /\\.get\\s*\\(\\s*[A-Za-z_]/u],\n\t\t[\"Integer/Long/Double.parse\", /\\b(?:Integer|Long|Double|Float)\\.parse[A-Za-z]+\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"valueOf parse\", /\\b(?:Integer|Long|Double|Float)\\.valueOf\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\".charAt/.substring\", /\\.(?:charAt|substring)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tkotlin: [\n\t\t[\"toInt/toDouble parse\", /\\.to(?:Int|Long|Double|Float)\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t\t[\".first()/.last()\", /\\.(?:first|last)\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t],\n\trust: [],\n\truby: [\n\t\t[\"JSON.parse\", /\\bJSON\\.parse\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\".fetch\", /\\.fetch\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tc: [\n\t\t[\"atoi/strtol parse\", /\\b(?:atoi|atol|atof|strtol|strtod)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"pointer deref *\", /(?:^|[^\\w)])\\*[A-Za-z_]/u],\n\t],\n\tcsharp: [\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"int.Parse/Convert\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:int|long|double|float|decimal|Int32|Int64|Convert)\\.(?:Parse|To[A-Za-z]+)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"JsonSerializer.Deserialize\", /\\.Deserialize\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\".First()/.Last()/.Single()\", /\\.(?:First|Last|Single)\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t],\n};\n\n// Where a request''s own input enters a handler: the criteria''s \"a request param, body or header\".\nconst REQUEST_INPUT: Record<string, RegExp> = {\n\tjava: /@(?:RequestParam|PathVariable|RequestBody|RequestHeader)\\b/u,\n\tkotlin: /@(?:RequestParam|PathVariable|RequestBody|RequestHeader)\\b/u,\n\ttypescript: /\\breq(?:uest)?\\.(?:params|query|body|headers)\\b/u,\n\tjavascript: /\\breq(?:uest)?\\.(?:params|query|body|headers)\\b/u,\n\tpython: /\\brequest\\.(?:args|form|json|GET|POST|query_params|data)\\b/u,\n\tgo: /\\br\\.(?:URL\\.Query|FormValue|PostFormValue|PathValue)\\b/u,\n\tswift: /\\breq\\.(?:parameters|query|content)\\b/u,\n};\n\nexport default function validatesInputsAndEdgeCasesAtTheBoundary(\n\t_repo: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst byKind: Record<string, number> = {};\n\tlet filesScanned = 0;\n\tlet linesScanned = 0;\n\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tconst own = lang === null ? undefined : LANG_PATTERNS[lang];\n\t\t// A test''s fixtures are not a boundary.\n\t\tif (lang === null || !own || isTestPath(path)) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst request = REQUEST_INPUT[lang];\n\t\tconst patterns: [string, RegExp][] =\n\t\t\trequest === undefined ? [...COMMON, ...own] : [[\"request input\", request], ...COMMON, ...own];\n\t\tconst code = codeLines(lang, df.addedLines);\n\t\tfilesScanned += 1;\n\t\tfor (const [line, content] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\tif (isCommentLine(content, lang)) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tlinesScanned += 1;\n\t\t\tconst labels = patterns\n\t\t\t\t.filter(([, re]) => re.test(code.get(line) ?? \"\"))\n\t\t\t\t.map(([name]) => name);\n\t\t\tif (labels.length === 0) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfor (const label of labels) {\n\t\t\t\tbyKind[label] = (byKind[label] ?? 0) + 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\tline,\n\t\t\t\tpattern: `${lang}:${labels.join(\" + \")}`,\n\t\t\t\tcontext: content.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\tflags: {},\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tconst kinds = Object.entries(byKind)\n\t\t.map(([kind, count]) => `${String(count)} ${kind}`)\n\t\t.join(\", \");\n\tconst directions =\n\t\thints.length > 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`${hints.length} added line(s) hold a construct that can fail or mislead on an edge value (${kinds}). String-literal text, comments and test files were not scanned; lookups and parses whose result the language makes optional, and crash operators, are not listed. A listed line marks where a consuming site may be, not an observation: whether its value comes from outside the local logic, and what already handles its bad case, is read from the code.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tedgeConstructLines: hints.length,\n\t\t\tfilesScanned,\n\t\t\tlinesScanned,\n\t\t\t...byKind,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Data from a user, a file, or another service will not always arrive in the shape you expected: it can be empty, null, negative, or huge. Checking it where it enters means a clear rejection instead of a confusing crash three layers deep.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Code that reads an external value first asks what happens if it is missing or out of range and handles that on purpose, so an empty list or a malformed payload meets a clean guard instead of a null-pointer surprise.","groupSlug":"robust-error-handling","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 104\">\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">Input count: 0</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M136 52h20v-32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M192 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"0.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">return total / count</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M424 20h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M448 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">Crash: divide by zero</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M156 52v32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M192 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-accent-soft pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"64.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">if count == 0 reject</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M424 84h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M448 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\">Clear error, no crash</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"When the input count is 0, return total / count crashes with a divide by zero. With if count == 0 reject, the zero count gets a clear error and no crash."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Find each value in your change that comes from outside your code. Examples are request data, typed text, a decoded file, a configuration value, and a service response.\n- Find the lines that index it, slice it, divide by it or add it up. Also find the lines that make it a whole number or pass it to code that expects a shape.\n- For each line, ask what its obvious bad case is, such as empty, missing, zero, or out of range.\n- Rule out that case before the line runs. Return early, reject the input with a clear error, use a safe default, or catch the failure. For example, instead of dividing by the item count from a response, return early when the count is zero.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change is empty.\n- Your change only passes outside values on, saves them, or shows them as they are.\n- Your change holds only generated or vendored files.\n- Your change adds no source code, such as a change to docs, images, or configuration only.\n- Your change uses only typed data from your own app, view state, or controls that already limit the value.\n\nIt still applies when the value comes from a data file that ships with your app, or from a list that the user fills.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Trying to check every possible value. Instead, find the obvious input that breaks the line, such as an empty list, and rule out that one.\n- Checking a smaller case than the line needs. A check for an empty list protects `items[0]`, but not `items[1]`. Instead, check for as many items as the line reads.\n- Trusting a parsed number when you divide by it or use it as an index. Parsing only proves its type. Instead, also check that it is not zero or out of range before that line.\n- Starting a minimum at the largest possible number and showing it while the list is empty. Instead, show an empty state or a sensible default.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Input Validation Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP. Check each value before use, including the range of a parsed number. Reject bad input with a clear error.\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html), MITRE. Lists sizes, positions and types to check, with an example of a zero size that the code did not reject.\n- [CWE-369: Divide By Zero](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/369.html), MITRE. A division by zero usually comes from a value that the code did not expect.\n- [Collection Types](https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/collectiontypes/), The Swift Programming Language, the Swift project. An index outside an array causes a runtime error, and an empty array has no valid index.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Validate inputs and edge cases at the boundary","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added consuming sites handle the obvious bad case of values they do not control.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether added or modified code that consumes a value it does not control handles the obvious failure case on purpose, instead of assuming a happy path. A boundary value enters from outside the local logic: a request param, body or header; text a user typed, once it is parsed or used as a number; a decoded network or file payload, including a bundled data file; a collection filled at runtime, such as a response array or a stored list the user populates; an env, config or defaults read; the result of a third-party or RPC call. Not a boundary value: a parameter of the app''s own function or view, a framework callback''s arguments, view state the code owns, an author literal or constant, and a value a control already bounds (a Slider or Stepper range, a Picker over an enum). A site is in scope only when the same change also USES that value — indexes or slices it, divides by it, converts it to an integer, aggregates it, or passes it into logic that assumes a shape. A value merely forwarded, stored or displayed as the type it already has is not a consuming site: an empty string saved or shown breaks nothing.\nA guard is anything on the value''s path that rules out the case the site itself needs, in the hunk or in code you can trace to: a count, bounds or empty check (`isEmpty` guards `[0]`, not `[1]` or `[0..<2]`); a zero check before integer division or before a ratio that is shown or converted to an integer; a clamp; do/catch, `try?`, or a `try` that propagates out of a throwing function; a default for an absent value; an early return; a button disabled on the bad case. An operation already total on the bad case needs no guard: a Swift optional the type forces the author to unwrap (`Int(text)`, `.first`, `dict[key]`, `URL(string:)`, `as?`) handled with `if let`, `guard let`, `?.` or `??`; `reduce`, `map`, `filter`, `prefix` or `ForEach` over an empty collection; `items[i]` inside `ForEach(items.indices)`. A default counts unless it is an extreme sentinel (`Int.max`, `.infinity`) that reaches output unchanged. Judge the PRACTICE — is the obvious failure handled at all — not an exhaustive lint: a finite check on a decoded Double, or a zero check on a field the source never sends as zero, is not the obvious case.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the change consumes no boundary value at all: an empty diff, a diff with no consuming site, or only generated or vendored files remaining. Source code is the subject: a change that adds no source-code line — a documentation, diagram, image or configuration-only diff — has no subject here and is NOT_APPLICABLE. So is a change whose values are all typed in-app data, view state and bounded controls, such as a view drawing a typed array it was handed. Never invent an input surface.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added and modified lines with their `[L<n>]` new-side markers and the visible in-diff context. It establishes every consuming site the change introduces — a closed set you can enumerate from the added lines — and whether each meets a guard you can see. It never establishes that the guards are sufficient for every input or that callers beyond the diff are safe.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): pinned source around a hunk. Read it when the guard that would decide a site could sit outside the hunk — above a truncated hunk, or in the caller or view that builds the value. It establishes whether such a guard exists. It never establishes that anything was run, compiled or tested.\nPer-commit patches are not in your context. If the context a deciding site needs remains unavailable after reading the pinned source, report a collection gap and record no observation, rather than asserting absence or inapplicability.\n\n## Judge\nScan the changed lines for a boundary value that gets consumed and trace each to its guard; then assess the standard.\n- NOT_MET: a boundary value is consumed with nothing on its path ruling out its obvious bad case, so a realistic input — an empty or short response array, an empty bundled list, a library the user has not filled yet — crashes the new path or shows a wrong value. For example `Array(items[0..<2])` on a decoded array behind only an `isEmpty` check, or a minimum seeded with `Int.max` and printed while the list is still empty. Quote the exact `[L<n>]` span of each unguarded site, name the realistic input that breaks it and the missing guard (\"count >= 2 before [0..<2]\").\n- MET: the ordinary result when the change consumes boundary values: you walked every consuming site and each handles its obvious bad case. Looks like a lapse but is not: `a[3]` inside `if a.count >= 4`; `Int(text)` bound with `guard let`; `try decoder.decode` in a throwing function whose caller catches; `pages[index]` when the only way in is a button disabled while `pages` is empty; `offset / 250` in a gesture. The claim is about the coverage of your reading, nothing about correctness.\n- UNDETERMINED for a consuming site you can see whose guard the diff and the pinned source do not settle; name the site and what would settle it. Having walked every site and found each guarded is MET, not UNDETERMINED. Missing required context is a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review, covering every unguarded site found.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a practice to grow, not a merge gate. Calibrate to the demonstrated consequence at the boundary and explain the observable risk. MINOR, the usual band: an omission on a low-impact operation. MAJOR: a missing check on a consequential operation, which one site can warrant; several harmless omissions do not escalate on their own. Identical hunks yield identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nJudge only the added and modified lines and their visible in-diff context, plus pinned source you actually read; a site in unchanged code is never charged, even when a changed line feeds it. For NOT_MET, cite the `[L<n>]` span of each unguarded site. For MET, record `evidence.search` over the diff and state in the boundary what you did NOT establish: that the guards are sufficient, that they cover every case, that callers beyond the diff are safe. Never assert a missing guard you cannot point to; never claim to run, compile or test anything; never invent a clean bill of health. Describe the observable code behaviour, never intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nA value interpolated raw into a URL, query, command or markup belongs to `validates-and-escapes-untrusted-input`. A crash operator the author wrote — `x!`, `try!`, `as!`, `fatalError`, `unwrap()` — belongs to `avoids-unsafe-panics-and-chosen-crashes`: it is neither a guard nor a consuming site here. An implicit trap stays here: an unchecked subscript or slice, an integer division, a conversion to an integer of a value that can be NaN or infinite. The quality of error handling once input is guarded belongs to `handles-errors-instead-of-swallowing-them`; a catch that merely prevents the crash satisfies the guard here regardless of its body. Do not repeat another practice''s complaint, and do not let another kind of defect stand in for this one.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for validates-inputs-and-edge-cases-at-the-boundary: the added lines of source code\n// that hold a construct which can fail or mislead on an edge value — a subscript or slice, a division by\n// a name, a parse the language does not force the author to handle, an extreme sentinel, a payload\n// decode, a request''s own input — across languages. A row marks where a consuming site may be, never an\n// observation: whether its value comes from outside the local logic, and what already handles its bad case,\n// is read from the code, and the criteria decide. String-literal text and comments are not scanned; a\n// lookup or parse whose result the language makes optional is not listed, and a crash operator the author\n// wrote (`!`, `try!`, `unwrap()`) is the crash practice''s. Adding a language = adding a row.\nimport { codeLines, isCommentLine } from \"../lib/declarations.ts\";\nimport { isTestPath, languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\nconst COMMON: [string, RegExp][] = [\n\t// `items[i]`, `cast[0..<2]`; not a lookup already made safe (`dict[k] ?? d`, `[k, default: 0]`,\n\t// `ns[k] as? T`) nor an assignment target.\n\t[\n\t\t\"subscript or slice\",\n\t\t/[A-Za-z0-9_)\\]]\\[(?![^\\]]*\\bdefault:)[^\\]]+\\](?!\\s*(?:\\?\\?|as\\?|=(?!=)))/u,\n\t],\n\t// A divisor that is a name or a call; a literal divisor cannot be zero.\n\t[\"division or remainder by a name\", /[A-Za-z0-9_)\\]]\\s*[/%](?![/*=])\\s*[A-Za-z_(]/u],\n\t// The seed of a minimum, a maximum or a fallback that can reach output unchanged.\n\t[\n\t\t\"extreme sentinel\",\n\t\t/\\b(?:U?Int\\d*|Double|Float|CGFloat)\\.(?:max|min|infinity|greatestFiniteMagnitude)\\b|\\b(?:Integer|Long|Double|Float)\\.(?:MAX|MIN)_VALUE\\b|\\bNumber\\.(?:MAX|MIN)_(?:SAFE_INTEGER|VALUE)\\b|\\bsys\\.maxsize\\b|\\bmath\\.inf\\b/u,\n\t],\n];\n\n// language key -> [label, regex] of edge constructs in added code, beside COMMON.\nconst LANG_PATTERNS: Record<string, [string, RegExp][]> = {\n\t// `Int(text)`, `Double(s)`, `.first`, `dict[key]` return Optionals the compiler makes the author handle.\n\tswift: [[\"payload decode\", /(?<![Cc]ontainer)\\.decode\\s*\\(/u]],\n\ttypescript: [\n\t\t[\"parseInt/parseFloat\", /\\bparse(?:Int|Float)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"Number(...) parse\", /\\bNumber\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"JSON.parse\", /\\bJSON\\.parse\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tjavascript: [\n\t\t[\"parseInt/parseFloat\", /\\bparse(?:Int|Float)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"Number(...) parse\", /\\bNumber\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"JSON.parse\", /\\bJSON\\.parse\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tpython: [\n\t\t[\"int()/float() parse\", /\\b(?:int|float)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"json.loads\", /\\bjson\\.loads\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\".pop()\", /\\.pop\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"next(...)\", /\\bnext\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tgo: [\n\t\t[\"strconv parse\", /\\bstrconv\\.(?:Atoi|Parse[A-Za-z]+)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"json.Unmarshal\", /\\bjson\\.Unmarshal\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tjava: [\n\t\t[\".get(i) access\", /\\.get\\s*\\(\\s*[A-Za-z_]/u],\n\t\t[\"Integer/Long/Double.parse\", /\\b(?:Integer|Long|Double|Float)\\.parse[A-Za-z]+\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"valueOf parse\", /\\b(?:Integer|Long|Double|Float)\\.valueOf\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\".charAt/.substring\", /\\.(?:charAt|substring)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tkotlin: [\n\t\t[\"toInt/toDouble parse\", /\\.to(?:Int|Long|Double|Float)\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t\t[\".first()/.last()\", /\\.(?:first|last)\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t],\n\trust: [],\n\truby: [\n\t\t[\"JSON.parse\", /\\bJSON\\.parse\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\".fetch\", /\\.fetch\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tc: [\n\t\t[\"atoi/strtol parse\", /\\b(?:atoi|atol|atof|strtol|strtod)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"pointer deref *\", /(?:^|[^\\w)])\\*[A-Za-z_]/u],\n\t],\n\tcsharp: [\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"int.Parse/Convert\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:int|long|double|float|decimal|Int32|Int64|Convert)\\.(?:Parse|To[A-Za-z]+)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"JsonSerializer.Deserialize\", /\\.Deserialize\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\".First()/.Last()/.Single()\", /\\.(?:First|Last|Single)\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t],\n};\n\n// Where a request''s own input enters a handler: the criteria''s \"a request param, body or header\".\nconst REQUEST_INPUT: Record<string, RegExp> = {\n\tjava: /@(?:RequestParam|PathVariable|RequestBody|RequestHeader)\\b/u,\n\tkotlin: /@(?:RequestParam|PathVariable|RequestBody|RequestHeader)\\b/u,\n\ttypescript: /\\breq(?:uest)?\\.(?:params|query|body|headers)\\b/u,\n\tjavascript: /\\breq(?:uest)?\\.(?:params|query|body|headers)\\b/u,\n\tpython: /\\brequest\\.(?:args|form|json|GET|POST|query_params|data)\\b/u,\n\tgo: /\\br\\.(?:URL\\.Query|FormValue|PostFormValue|PathValue)\\b/u,\n\tswift: /\\breq\\.(?:parameters|query|content)\\b/u,\n};\n\nexport default function validatesInputsAndEdgeCasesAtTheBoundary(\n\t_repo: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst byKind: Record<string, number> = {};\n\tlet filesScanned = 0;\n\tlet linesScanned = 0;\n\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tconst own = lang === null ? undefined : LANG_PATTERNS[lang];\n\t\t// A test''s fixtures are not a boundary.\n\t\tif (lang === null || !own || isTestPath(path)) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst request = REQUEST_INPUT[lang];\n\t\tconst patterns: [string, RegExp][] =\n\t\t\trequest === undefined ? [...COMMON, ...own] : [[\"request input\", request], ...COMMON, ...own];\n\t\tconst code = codeLines(lang, df.addedLines);\n\t\tfilesScanned += 1;\n\t\tfor (const [line, content] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\tif (isCommentLine(content, lang)) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tlinesScanned += 1;\n\t\t\tconst labels = patterns\n\t\t\t\t.filter(([, re]) => re.test(code.get(line) ?? \"\"))\n\t\t\t\t.map(([name]) => name);\n\t\t\tif (labels.length === 0) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfor (const label of labels) {\n\t\t\t\tbyKind[label] = (byKind[label] ?? 0) + 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\tline,\n\t\t\t\tpattern: `${lang}:${labels.join(\" + \")}`,\n\t\t\t\tcontext: content.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\tflags: {},\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tconst kinds = Object.entries(byKind)\n\t\t.map(([kind, count]) => `${String(count)} ${kind}`)\n\t\t.join(\", \");\n\tconst directions =\n\t\thints.length > 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`${hints.length} added line(s) hold a construct that can fail or mislead on an edge value (${kinds}). String-literal text, comments and test files were not scanned; lookups and parses whose result the language makes optional, and crash operators, are not listed. A listed line marks where a consuming site may be, not an observation: whether its value comes from outside the local logic, and what already handles its bad case, is read from the code.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tedgeConstructLines: hints.length,\n\t\t\tfilesScanned,\n\t\t\tlinesScanned,\n\t\t\t...byKind,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Data from a user, a file, or another service will not always arrive in the shape you expected: it can be empty, null, negative, or huge. Checking it where it enters means a clear rejection instead of a confusing crash three layers deep.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Code that reads an external value first asks what happens if it is missing or out of range and handles that on purpose, so an empty list or a malformed payload meets a clean guard instead of a null-pointer surprise.","groupSlug":"robust-error-handling","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 104\">\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">Input count: 0</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M136 52h20v-32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M192 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"0.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">return total / count</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M424 20h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M448 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">Crash: divide by zero</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M156 52v32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M192 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-accent-soft pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"64.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">if count == 0 reject</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M424 84h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M448 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\">Clear error, no crash</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"When the input count is 0, return total / count crashes with a divide by zero. With if count == 0 reject, the zero count gets a clear error and no crash."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Find each value in your change that comes from outside your code. Examples are request data, typed text, a decoded file, a configuration value, and a service response.\n- Find the lines that index it, slice it, divide by it or add it up. Also find the lines that make it a whole number or pass it to code that expects a shape.\n- For each line, ask what its obvious bad case is, such as empty, missing, zero, or out of range.\n- Rule out that case before the line runs. Return early, reject the input with a clear error, use a safe default, or catch the failure. For example, instead of dividing by the item count from a response, return early when the count is zero.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change is empty.\n- Your change only passes outside values on, saves them, or shows them as they are.\n- Your change holds only generated or vendored files.\n- Your change adds no source code, such as a change to docs, images, or configuration only.\n- Your change uses only typed data from your own app, view state, or controls that already limit the value.\n\nIt still applies when the value comes from a data file that ships with your app, or from a list that the user fills.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Trying to check every possible value. Instead, find the obvious input that breaks the line, such as an empty list, and rule out that one.\n- Checking a smaller case than the line needs. A check for an empty list protects `items[0]`, but not `items[1]`. Instead, check for as many items as the line reads.\n- Trusting a parsed number when you divide by it or use it as an index. Parsing only proves its type. Instead, also check that it is not zero or out of range before that line.\n- Starting a minimum at the largest possible number and showing it while the list is empty. Instead, show an empty state or a sensible default.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Input Validation Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP. Check each value before use, including the range of a parsed number. Reject bad input with a clear error.\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html), MITRE. Lists sizes, positions and types to check, with an example of a zero size that the code did not reject.\n- [CWE-369: Divide By Zero](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/369.html), MITRE. A division by zero usually comes from a value that the code did not expect.\n- [Collection Types](https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/collectiontypes/), The Swift Programming Language, the Swift project. An index outside an array causes a runtime error, and an empty array has no valid index.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"External values meet a guard before they are used"'::jsonb),'v5:594f233ea1b04edfe760326716043e6a75706832ad638fe1deff536de1adf2e8','practice:v3:95616ee145f9a61152a9a922c020db8090d5d334525147c8641c60848ecc2b18');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','avoids-unsafe-panics-and-chosen-crashes','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Avoid crashing on recoverable problems","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added crash-shaped operators are safe under their captured provenance or contract, rather than aborting on recoverable runtime failure.\n\n## The standard\nCode degrades gracefully on ordinary, recoverable runtime failure rather than deliberately aborting. A chosen crash is an ADDED crash operator the author wrote — force-unwrapping or force-casting a value that can legitimately be absent, `try!` on a call that can throw, or a deliberate abort, panic, exit or fatal error — when its value rides an ordinary runtime path: user input, network, database, disk, a decoded payload, a fallible API''s result. Judge two things: can a real input reach the operator and make it fail, and did the change crash on it anyway. Bad, for example: `try! decoder.decode(T.self, from: data)` on a server response; `Int(ageText)!` on text a user typed; `results.first!` on a fetched array; `fatalError` in the catch of a network call. Not a chosen crash, though each looks like one: a genuine programmer invariant that cannot fail unless surrounding code is already broken (an unreachable enum default, a force unwrap of a value just proven non-nil); a fail-fast at a CLI or startup entry point where aborting IS the contract; data the author ships — a literal, a constant, an Info.plist key the change commits, a resource bundled with the app, `URL(string: \"https://api.example.com\")!`; an operation total by language or platform contract (`String.data(using: .utf8)!`). Code that runs only in an Xcode `#Preview`, a preview-only sample or in tests is not an ordinary runtime path: `try! ModelContainer(…)` or `fatalError` in a preview is scaffolding. A `!` alone is not the defect; whether the operation can ACTUALLY return nil or throw on some real input is. Reason about provenance across languages as an experienced reviewer would, not from the spelling of a construct or a precompute label.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate, decided by a closed list of crash operators the author writes: a force unwrap (`x!`, `.first!`, `dict[key]!`), `try!`, a force cast (`as!`), `fatalError`, `precondition`/`preconditionFailure`, `assert`/`assertionFailure`, `exit`/`abort` — or their equivalents in another language (`panic`, `unwrap()`, `expect()`, `!!`, `process.exit`, a non-null assertion, `Optional.get()`). An implicit trap — a subscript, a slice, an integer division, a trapping numeric conversion — is not on the list: it is an unguarded edge case, judged by `validates-inputs-and-edge-cases-at-the-boundary`. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop only when the added source lines contain no crash operator: a docs-, config- or data-only diff, a generated- or vendored-only diff, or code with none. An operator the change guards on the same path — behind `guard let`, `if let`, a nil check — is still observed and handled: the occasion holds and the cell is judged below, never NOT_APPLICABLE. The precompute lists lines whose spelling matches, with whether each sits in a preview block or a test file; a listed line is a place to read, not a crash, and the scan below is over the added lines themselves.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added and modified lines with their `[L<n>]` new-side markers. It establishes the closed set of crash operators the change adds and, where the hunk shows it, where each crashed-on value came from. It never establishes anything about removed lines, unchanged code or code outside the diff, and it never establishes that anything compiled or ran.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): pinned source around a hunk. Read it only to trace the origin of a value the hunk crashes on but does not source, or where the operator runs. It never adds an operator to judge — only operators the diff adds are judged — and it never establishes that unchanged code cannot crash.\nIf the diff is truncated or unavailable for a change that adds a crash operator, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nScan first, then classify. Enumerate every crash operator in the added lines and trace where each crashed-on value came from and where the code runs; look before you leap. Then assess the standard against all relevant evidence.\n- NOT_MET: a quotable added operator on an ordinary runtime path that crashes on a value with a named runtime source, and a real input you can name — a missing field, a failed request, text a user typed — makes it return nil or throw. Quote the deciding added line, name the source and the input.\n- MET: the change adds crash operators from the closed list, you read every one, and each is guarded on its path, sits on a genuine programmer invariant, a total operation, author-shipped data, preview or test code, or an entry-point contract rather than an ordinary runtime path. Looks like a lapse but is not: `try! ModelContainer(for: Note.self, configurations: inMemory)` inside `#Preview`; `URL(string: \"https://api.example.com\")!` on a literal. Record it with `evidence.search` over the diff and say in the boundary that you judged the operators the diff adds, not whether unchanged code can still crash.\n- UNDETERMINED: an operator on an ordinary runtime path whose value provenance the diff and pinned source do not settle — you see the diff, not the whole program. Name the operator and the fact that would settle it. It is earned by the trace running out of evidence, never by skipping the trace, and it is never filed under NOT_APPLICABLE, which claims no operator was here. A crash you cannot tie to a runtime-sourced value and a real input is not groundable as a negative.\nOne observation per review. When NOT_MET, name the single most consequential chosen crash and show the shape of the graceful alternative for that line — a guard or `if let`, try-with-fallback, return-an-error, `Result`; do not rewrite the whole change.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a defensive-programming practice to grow, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band, and the ceiling when the input that makes the operation fail is possible but unusual. MAJOR: a clearly recoverable failure on a clearly ordinary runtime source — decoded or fetched input crashed on outright. Genuine uncertainty about recoverability lowers the band. Identical hunks yield identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nCite the exact added-line span and the value''s origin from observable diff evidence. Judge only added or modified lines with new-side `[L<n>]` markers; never reason about removed lines or code outside the diff, and never claim to compile or run it. Never invent a file, a line, a value source or a clean bill you did not verify. For MET, cite the operators you enumerated and state the boundary you did not cross. Describe the observable behaviour of the code; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nEmpty or swallowed catches and logged-and-continued errors belong to `handles-errors-instead-of-swallowing-them`; missing input validation and implicit traps — an unchecked subscript or slice, an integer division, a trapping conversion — belong to `validates-inputs-and-edge-cases-at-the-boundary`. Here only: does an added crash operator deliberately crash on a recoverable runtime condition?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for avoids-unsafe-panics-and-chosen-crashes: the added lines that spell a crash\n// operator the author wrote — a force unwrap, `try!`, a force cast, an abort — across languages, each\n// with where it runs: inside a preview block or in a test file. A row matches spelling on one line; it\n// does not know the operand''s type, where the value came from, or what bounds it, and the criteria\n// decide which rows are lapses. Implicit traps (a subscript, an integer division) are the input\n// practice''s, so they are not listed here. Adding a language = adding a row, no engine change.\nimport { readFile } from \"node:fs/promises\";\nimport nodePath from \"node:path\";\n\nimport { blockExtents, codeLines, isCommentLine } from \"../lib/declarations.ts\";\nimport { isTestPath, languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// language key -> [label, regex] of crash operators the author writes, matched against the line''s code.\nconst LANG_PATTERNS: Record<string, [string, RegExp][]> = {\n\tswift: [\n\t\t[\"try!\", /\\btry!/u],\n\t\t[\"fatalError\", /\\bfatalError\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"force cast as!\", /\\bas!\\s/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"precondition or assert\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:precondition|preconditionFailure|assert|assertionFailure)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"exit or abort\", /\\b(?:exit|abort)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t// `try!` and `as!` have their own rows.\n\t\t[\"force unwrap\", /[A-Za-z0-9_)\\]](?<!\\btry|\\bas)!(?:[.\\s),\\]]|$)/u],\n\t],\n\ttypescript: [\n\t\t[\"process.exit\", /\\bprocess\\.exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"non-null assertion\", /[A-Za-z0-9_)\\]]!(?:[.;)\\s,\\]]|$)/u],\n\t],\n\tjavascript: [[\"process.exit\", /\\bprocess\\.exit\\s*\\(/u]],\n\tpython: [\n\t\t[\"sys.exit\", /\\bsys\\.exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"os._exit\", /\\bos\\._exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"raise SystemExit\", /\\braise\\s+SystemExit\\b/u],\n\t\t[\"bare assert in code\", /^\\s*assert\\s+/u],\n\t],\n\tgo: [\n\t\t[\"panic(\", /\\bpanic\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"log.Fatal\", /\\blog\\.Fatal[a-z]*\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"os.Exit\", /\\bos\\.Exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tjava: [\n\t\t[\"System.exit\", /\\bSystem\\.exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"throw AssertionError\", /\\bthrow\\s+new\\s+AssertionError\\b/u],\n\t\t[\"Optional.get()\", /\\bOptional[^;]*\\.get\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t],\n\tkotlin: [\n\t\t[\"!! force non-null\", /!!(?:[.\\s)]|$)/u],\n\t\t[\"error(\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])error\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"TODO(\", /\\bTODO\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\trust: [\n\t\t[\".unwrap()\", /\\.unwrap\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t\t[\".expect(\", /\\.expect\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"panic!\", /\\bpanic!\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"unreachable!\", /\\bunreachable!\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"unimplemented!/todo!\", /\\b(?:unimplemented|todo)!\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\truby: [\n\t\t[\"exit!\", /\\bexit!/u],\n\t\t[\"abort\", /\\babort\\b/u],\n\t],\n\tc: [\n\t\t[\"abort(\", /\\babort\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"exit(\", /\\bexit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"assert(\", /\\bassert\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\t// Objective-C is C with Cocoa''s assertion macros, not Swift: no bang operators to find.\n\t\"objective-c\": [\n\t\t[\"abort(\", /\\babort\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"exit(\", /\\bexit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"NSAssert\", /\\bNS(?:C)?Assert\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tcsharp: [\n\t\t[\"Environment.Exit\", /\\bEnvironment\\.Exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"Environment.FailFast\", /\\bEnvironment\\.FailFast\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"Debug.Assert\", /\\bDebug\\.Assert\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"null-forgiving !\", /[A-Za-z0-9_)\\]]!\\.(?=[A-Za-z_])/u],\n\t],\n};\n\n// Code that runs only when Xcode renders a preview, never on a user''s device.\nconst PREVIEW_OPENER = /^\\s*(?:#Preview\\b|(?:\\w+\\s+)*struct\\s+\\w+\\s*:\\s*PreviewProvider\\b)/u;\n\n/** The new side as far as the hunks show it: context and added lines at their numbers, gaps empty. */\nfunction newSideOfHunks(file: DiffFile): string {\n\tconst lines: string[] = [];\n\tfor (const hunk of file.hunks) {\n\t\tlet number = hunk.newStart;\n\t\tfor (const line of hunk.lines) {\n\t\t\tif (line.startsWith(\"-\")) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\twhile (lines.length < number - 1) {\n\t\t\t\tlines.push(\"\");\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tlines[number - 1] = line.slice(1);\n\t\t\tnumber += 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn lines.join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\n/** The lines inside a preview block: read from the pinned file, else from what the hunks show. */\nasync function previewLines(\n\trepoPath: string,\n\tfile: DiffFile,\n\tlanguage: string,\n): Promise<Set<number>> {\n\tlet source: string;\n\ttry {\n\t\tsource = await readFile(nodePath.join(repoPath, file.path), \"utf8\");\n\t} catch {\n\t\tsource = newSideOfHunks(file);\n\t}\n\tconst lines = new Set<number>();\n\tfor (const { start, end } of blockExtents(language, source, PREVIEW_OPENER) ?? []) {\n\t\tfor (let line = start; line <= end; line += 1) {\n\t\t\tlines.add(line);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn lines;\n}\n\nexport default async function avoidsUnsafePanicsAndChosenCrashes(\n\trepoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst byKind: Record<string, number> = {};\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tconst patterns = lang === null ? undefined : LANG_PATTERNS[lang];\n\t\tif (lang === null || !patterns) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst code = codeLines(lang, df.addedLines);\n\t\tconst preview = lang === \"swift\" ? await previewLines(repoPath, df, lang) : new Set<number>();\n\t\tconst testFile = isTestPath(path);\n\t\tfor (const [line, content] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\tif (isCommentLine(content, lang)) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst labels = patterns\n\t\t\t\t.filter(([, re]) => re.test(code.get(line) ?? \"\"))\n\t\t\t\t.map(([name]) => name);\n\t\t\tif (labels.length === 0) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfor (const label of labels) {\n\t\t\t\tbyKind[label] = (byKind[label] ?? 0) + 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\tline,\n\t\t\t\tpattern: `${lang}:${labels.join(\" + \")}`,\n\t\t\t\tcontext: content.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\tflags: { inPreview: preview.has(line), testFile },\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// Rows on an ordinary path first, so preview and test rows cannot push them out of a shown sample.\n\thints.sort(\n\t\t(a, b) =>\n\t\t\tNumber(a.flags.inPreview === true || a.flags.testFile === true) -\n\t\t\tNumber(b.flags.inPreview === true || b.flags.testFile === true),\n\t);\n\tconst inPreview = hints.filter((h) => h.flags.inPreview === true).length;\n\tconst inTests = hints.filter((h) => h.flags.testFile === true).length;\n\tconst kinds = Object.entries(byKind)\n\t\t.map(([kind, count]) => `${String(count)} ${kind}`)\n\t\t.join(\", \");\n\tconst directions =\n\t\thints.length > 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`${hints.length} added line(s) spell a crash operator (${kinds}); ${inPreview} sit inside a preview block and ${inTests} in test files. String-literal text and comments were not scanned. A row matches spelling on one line: it does not know the operand''s type, where the value came from, or what bounds it.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: { crashOperatorLines: hints.length, inPreview, inTests, ...byKind },\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Reaching for a force-unwrap or a panic on a condition that can actually happen turns an ordinary hiccup into a hard crash for your users. A problem the code can recover from deserves a graceful path, not a deliberate stop.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Where a value might legitimately be absent, the code unwraps it safely and falls back to a default or a clear error, instead of force-unwrapping and bringing the whole thing down.","groupSlug":"robust-error-handling","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 104\">\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">Age: abc</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M136 52h20v-32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M192 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"0.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">panic(err)</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M424 20h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M448 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">App crashes for users</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M156 52v32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M192 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-accent-soft pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"64.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">showError(err)</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M424 84h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M448 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\">App keeps running</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"When a user types “abc” as their age, code that calls panic(err) crashes the app for users. Code that calls showError(err) handles the failure, and the app keeps running."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, find each crash operator that you added. Examples are `x!`, `try!`, `as!`, `fatalError`, `unwrap()`, `expect()`, `panic`, `!!` and `process.exit`.\n- For each one, ask where its value comes from and where the code runs. A value from a user, the network, a database, or a file can be missing.\n- On such a path, give the code a way out. Use `if let`, a default value, or an error that the caller can handle. For example, instead of `Int(ageText)!` on text a user typed, use `if let` and show an error when it fails.\n- Keep a crash operator where a failure means the code is already broken. Examples are a value that you just checked or a fixed URL that you wrote. Keep one in a test, in a preview, or at a command-line or startup entry point.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The lines that you added have no crash operator. Examples are a change to only docs, configuration or data, or to only generated or copied-in files.\n- The only lines that can crash use no crash operator, such as an index into a list or a division. Another practice covers those.\n\nIt still applies when you guard the operator on the same path, such as behind `if let` or a nil check.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Treating every `!` as a bug. What matters is whether a real input can make it fail. Instead, trace where the value comes from.\n- Force-unwrapping the first item of a fetched list, such as `results.first!`. An empty result is normal. Instead, handle the empty case.\n- Calling `fatalError` or `panic` when a network call fails. Requests fail in normal use. Instead, return the error so that the caller can react.\n- Copying `try!` from a preview or a test into app code. Instead, catch the error and show the person a way to recover.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [To panic! or not to panic!](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.html), Klabnik, Nichols and Krycho, The Rust Programming Language. The book says to return an error for expected failure, such as malformed data. It allows a panic in tests or where your code makes sure a value is valid.\n- [The basics](https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/thebasics/), The Swift Programming Language, Swift project. Force unwrap only when a missing value is a programmer error, and otherwise use optional binding or a fallback value.\n- [Error handling](https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/errorhandling/), The Swift Programming Language, Swift project. Use `try!` only when the call cannot throw, such as loading an image that ships with the app.\n- [Go code review comments](https://go.dev/wiki/CodeReviewComments#dont-panic), the Go project. Do not use panic for normal error handling, and return an error instead.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Avoid crashing on recoverable problems","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added crash-shaped operators are safe under their captured provenance or contract, rather than aborting on recoverable runtime failure.\n\n## The standard\nCode degrades gracefully on ordinary, recoverable runtime failure rather than deliberately aborting. A chosen crash is an ADDED crash operator the author wrote — force-unwrapping or force-casting a value that can legitimately be absent, `try!` on a call that can throw, or a deliberate abort, panic, exit or fatal error — when its value rides an ordinary runtime path: user input, network, database, disk, a decoded payload, a fallible API''s result. Judge two things: can a real input reach the operator and make it fail, and did the change crash on it anyway. Bad, for example: `try! decoder.decode(T.self, from: data)` on a server response; `Int(ageText)!` on text a user typed; `results.first!` on a fetched array; `fatalError` in the catch of a network call. Not a chosen crash, though each looks like one: a genuine programmer invariant that cannot fail unless surrounding code is already broken (an unreachable enum default, a force unwrap of a value just proven non-nil); a fail-fast at a CLI or startup entry point where aborting IS the contract; data the author ships — a literal, a constant, an Info.plist key the change commits, a resource bundled with the app, `URL(string: \"https://api.example.com\")!`; an operation total by language or platform contract (`String.data(using: .utf8)!`). Code that runs only in an Xcode `#Preview`, a preview-only sample or in tests is not an ordinary runtime path: `try! ModelContainer(…)` or `fatalError` in a preview is scaffolding. A `!` alone is not the defect; whether the operation can ACTUALLY return nil or throw on some real input is. Reason about provenance across languages as an experienced reviewer would, not from the spelling of a construct or a precompute label.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate, decided by a closed list of crash operators the author writes: a force unwrap (`x!`, `.first!`, `dict[key]!`), `try!`, a force cast (`as!`), `fatalError`, `precondition`/`preconditionFailure`, `assert`/`assertionFailure`, `exit`/`abort` — or their equivalents in another language (`panic`, `unwrap()`, `expect()`, `!!`, `process.exit`, a non-null assertion, `Optional.get()`). An implicit trap — a subscript, a slice, an integer division, a trapping numeric conversion — is not on the list: it is an unguarded edge case, judged by `validates-inputs-and-edge-cases-at-the-boundary`. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop only when the added source lines contain no crash operator: a docs-, config- or data-only diff, a generated- or vendored-only diff, or code with none. An operator the change guards on the same path — behind `guard let`, `if let`, a nil check — is still observed and handled: the occasion holds and the cell is judged below, never NOT_APPLICABLE. The precompute lists lines whose spelling matches, with whether each sits in a preview block or a test file; a listed line is a place to read, not a crash, and the scan below is over the added lines themselves.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added and modified lines with their `[L<n>]` new-side markers. It establishes the closed set of crash operators the change adds and, where the hunk shows it, where each crashed-on value came from. It never establishes anything about removed lines, unchanged code or code outside the diff, and it never establishes that anything compiled or ran.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): pinned source around a hunk. Read it only to trace the origin of a value the hunk crashes on but does not source, or where the operator runs. It never adds an operator to judge — only operators the diff adds are judged — and it never establishes that unchanged code cannot crash.\nIf the diff is truncated or unavailable for a change that adds a crash operator, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nScan first, then classify. Enumerate every crash operator in the added lines and trace where each crashed-on value came from and where the code runs; look before you leap. Then assess the standard against all relevant evidence.\n- NOT_MET: a quotable added operator on an ordinary runtime path that crashes on a value with a named runtime source, and a real input you can name — a missing field, a failed request, text a user typed — makes it return nil or throw. Quote the deciding added line, name the source and the input.\n- MET: the change adds crash operators from the closed list, you read every one, and each is guarded on its path, sits on a genuine programmer invariant, a total operation, author-shipped data, preview or test code, or an entry-point contract rather than an ordinary runtime path. Looks like a lapse but is not: `try! ModelContainer(for: Note.self, configurations: inMemory)` inside `#Preview`; `URL(string: \"https://api.example.com\")!` on a literal. Record it with `evidence.search` over the diff and say in the boundary that you judged the operators the diff adds, not whether unchanged code can still crash.\n- UNDETERMINED: an operator on an ordinary runtime path whose value provenance the diff and pinned source do not settle — you see the diff, not the whole program. Name the operator and the fact that would settle it. It is earned by the trace running out of evidence, never by skipping the trace, and it is never filed under NOT_APPLICABLE, which claims no operator was here. A crash you cannot tie to a runtime-sourced value and a real input is not groundable as a negative.\nOne observation per review. When NOT_MET, name the single most consequential chosen crash and show the shape of the graceful alternative for that line — a guard or `if let`, try-with-fallback, return-an-error, `Result`; do not rewrite the whole change.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a defensive-programming practice to grow, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band, and the ceiling when the input that makes the operation fail is possible but unusual. MAJOR: a clearly recoverable failure on a clearly ordinary runtime source — decoded or fetched input crashed on outright. Genuine uncertainty about recoverability lowers the band. Identical hunks yield identical severity.\n\n## Grounding\nCite the exact added-line span and the value''s origin from observable diff evidence. Judge only added or modified lines with new-side `[L<n>]` markers; never reason about removed lines or code outside the diff, and never claim to compile or run it. Never invent a file, a line, a value source or a clean bill you did not verify. For MET, cite the operators you enumerated and state the boundary you did not cross. Describe the observable behaviour of the code; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nEmpty or swallowed catches and logged-and-continued errors belong to `handles-errors-instead-of-swallowing-them`; missing input validation and implicit traps — an unchecked subscript or slice, an integer division, a trapping conversion — belong to `validates-inputs-and-edge-cases-at-the-boundary`. Here only: does an added crash operator deliberately crash on a recoverable runtime condition?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute FACTS for avoids-unsafe-panics-and-chosen-crashes: the added lines that spell a crash\n// operator the author wrote — a force unwrap, `try!`, a force cast, an abort — across languages, each\n// with where it runs: inside a preview block or in a test file. A row matches spelling on one line; it\n// does not know the operand''s type, where the value came from, or what bounds it, and the criteria\n// decide which rows are lapses. Implicit traps (a subscript, an integer division) are the input\n// practice''s, so they are not listed here. Adding a language = adding a row, no engine change.\nimport { readFile } from \"node:fs/promises\";\nimport nodePath from \"node:path\";\n\nimport { blockExtents, codeLines, isCommentLine } from \"../lib/declarations.ts\";\nimport { isTestPath, languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// language key -> [label, regex] of crash operators the author writes, matched against the line''s code.\nconst LANG_PATTERNS: Record<string, [string, RegExp][]> = {\n\tswift: [\n\t\t[\"try!\", /\\btry!/u],\n\t\t[\"fatalError\", /\\bfatalError\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"force cast as!\", /\\bas!\\s/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"precondition or assert\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:precondition|preconditionFailure|assert|assertionFailure)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"exit or abort\", /\\b(?:exit|abort)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t// `try!` and `as!` have their own rows.\n\t\t[\"force unwrap\", /[A-Za-z0-9_)\\]](?<!\\btry|\\bas)!(?:[.\\s),\\]]|$)/u],\n\t],\n\ttypescript: [\n\t\t[\"process.exit\", /\\bprocess\\.exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"non-null assertion\", /[A-Za-z0-9_)\\]]!(?:[.;)\\s,\\]]|$)/u],\n\t],\n\tjavascript: [[\"process.exit\", /\\bprocess\\.exit\\s*\\(/u]],\n\tpython: [\n\t\t[\"sys.exit\", /\\bsys\\.exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"os._exit\", /\\bos\\._exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"raise SystemExit\", /\\braise\\s+SystemExit\\b/u],\n\t\t[\"bare assert in code\", /^\\s*assert\\s+/u],\n\t],\n\tgo: [\n\t\t[\"panic(\", /\\bpanic\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"log.Fatal\", /\\blog\\.Fatal[a-z]*\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"os.Exit\", /\\bos\\.Exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tjava: [\n\t\t[\"System.exit\", /\\bSystem\\.exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"throw AssertionError\", /\\bthrow\\s+new\\s+AssertionError\\b/u],\n\t\t[\"Optional.get()\", /\\bOptional[^;]*\\.get\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t],\n\tkotlin: [\n\t\t[\"!! force non-null\", /!!(?:[.\\s)]|$)/u],\n\t\t[\"error(\", /(?:^|[^.\\w])error\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"TODO(\", /\\bTODO\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\trust: [\n\t\t[\".unwrap()\", /\\.unwrap\\s*\\(\\s*\\)/u],\n\t\t[\".expect(\", /\\.expect\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"panic!\", /\\bpanic!\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"unreachable!\", /\\bunreachable!\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"unimplemented!/todo!\", /\\b(?:unimplemented|todo)!\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\truby: [\n\t\t[\"exit!\", /\\bexit!/u],\n\t\t[\"abort\", /\\babort\\b/u],\n\t],\n\tc: [\n\t\t[\"abort(\", /\\babort\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"exit(\", /\\bexit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"assert(\", /\\bassert\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\t// Objective-C is C with Cocoa''s assertion macros, not Swift: no bang operators to find.\n\t\"objective-c\": [\n\t\t[\"abort(\", /\\babort\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"exit(\", /\\bexit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"NSAssert\", /\\bNS(?:C)?Assert\\s*\\(/u],\n\t],\n\tcsharp: [\n\t\t[\"Environment.Exit\", /\\bEnvironment\\.Exit\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"Environment.FailFast\", /\\bEnvironment\\.FailFast\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"Debug.Assert\", /\\bDebug\\.Assert\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\"null-forgiving !\", /[A-Za-z0-9_)\\]]!\\.(?=[A-Za-z_])/u],\n\t],\n};\n\n// Code that runs only when Xcode renders a preview, never on a user''s device.\nconst PREVIEW_OPENER = /^\\s*(?:#Preview\\b|(?:\\w+\\s+)*struct\\s+\\w+\\s*:\\s*PreviewProvider\\b)/u;\n\n/** The new side as far as the hunks show it: context and added lines at their numbers, gaps empty. */\nfunction newSideOfHunks(file: DiffFile): string {\n\tconst lines: string[] = [];\n\tfor (const hunk of file.hunks) {\n\t\tlet number = hunk.newStart;\n\t\tfor (const line of hunk.lines) {\n\t\t\tif (line.startsWith(\"-\")) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\twhile (lines.length < number - 1) {\n\t\t\t\tlines.push(\"\");\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tlines[number - 1] = line.slice(1);\n\t\t\tnumber += 1;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn lines.join(\"\\n\");\n}\n\n/** The lines inside a preview block: read from the pinned file, else from what the hunks show. */\nasync function previewLines(\n\trepoPath: string,\n\tfile: DiffFile,\n\tlanguage: string,\n): Promise<Set<number>> {\n\tlet source: string;\n\ttry {\n\t\tsource = await readFile(nodePath.join(repoPath, file.path), \"utf8\");\n\t} catch {\n\t\tsource = newSideOfHunks(file);\n\t}\n\tconst lines = new Set<number>();\n\tfor (const { start, end } of blockExtents(language, source, PREVIEW_OPENER) ?? []) {\n\t\tfor (let line = start; line <= end; line += 1) {\n\t\t\tlines.add(line);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn lines;\n}\n\nexport default async function avoidsUnsafePanicsAndChosenCrashes(\n\trepoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst byKind: Record<string, number> = {};\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tconst patterns = lang === null ? undefined : LANG_PATTERNS[lang];\n\t\tif (lang === null || !patterns) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst code = codeLines(lang, df.addedLines);\n\t\tconst preview = lang === \"swift\" ? await previewLines(repoPath, df, lang) : new Set<number>();\n\t\tconst testFile = isTestPath(path);\n\t\tfor (const [line, content] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\tif (isCommentLine(content, lang)) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst labels = patterns\n\t\t\t\t.filter(([, re]) => re.test(code.get(line) ?? \"\"))\n\t\t\t\t.map(([name]) => name);\n\t\t\tif (labels.length === 0) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfor (const label of labels) {\n\t\t\t\tbyKind[label] = (byKind[label] ?? 0) + 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\tline,\n\t\t\t\tpattern: `${lang}:${labels.join(\" + \")}`,\n\t\t\t\tcontext: content.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\tflags: { inPreview: preview.has(line), testFile },\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// Rows on an ordinary path first, so preview and test rows cannot push them out of a shown sample.\n\thints.sort(\n\t\t(a, b) =>\n\t\t\tNumber(a.flags.inPreview === true || a.flags.testFile === true) -\n\t\t\tNumber(b.flags.inPreview === true || b.flags.testFile === true),\n\t);\n\tconst inPreview = hints.filter((h) => h.flags.inPreview === true).length;\n\tconst inTests = hints.filter((h) => h.flags.testFile === true).length;\n\tconst kinds = Object.entries(byKind)\n\t\t.map(([kind, count]) => `${String(count)} ${kind}`)\n\t\t.join(\", \");\n\tconst directions =\n\t\thints.length > 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`${hints.length} added line(s) spell a crash operator (${kinds}); ${inPreview} sit inside a preview block and ${inTests} in test files. String-literal text and comments were not scanned. A row matches spelling on one line: it does not know the operand''s type, where the value came from, or what bounds it.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: { crashOperatorLines: hints.length, inPreview, inTests, ...byKind },\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Reaching for a force-unwrap or a panic on a condition that can actually happen turns an ordinary hiccup into a hard crash for your users. A problem the code can recover from deserves a graceful path, not a deliberate stop.","whatGoodLooksLike":"Where a value might legitimately be absent, the code unwraps it safely and falls back to a default or a clear error, instead of force-unwrapping and bringing the whole thing down.","groupSlug":"robust-error-handling","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 104\">\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-ink\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"134.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">Age: abc</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M136 52h20v-32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M192 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"0.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">panic(err)</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M424 20h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M448 20l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"460\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"15\">App crashes for users</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M156 52v32h28\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M192 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-accent-soft pv-stroke-accent\" x=\"200.75\" y=\"64.75\" width=\"214.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"216\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\">showError(err)</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-accent\" d=\"M424 84h16\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-accent\" d=\"M448 84l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"460\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"15\">App keeps running</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"When a user types “abc” as their age, code that calls panic(err) crashes the app for users. Code that calls showError(err) handles the failure, and the app keeps running."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, find each crash operator that you added. Examples are `x!`, `try!`, `as!`, `fatalError`, `unwrap()`, `expect()`, `panic`, `!!` and `process.exit`.\n- For each one, ask where its value comes from and where the code runs. A value from a user, the network, a database, or a file can be missing.\n- On such a path, give the code a way out. Use `if let`, a default value, or an error that the caller can handle. For example, instead of `Int(ageText)!` on text a user typed, use `if let` and show an error when it fails.\n- Keep a crash operator where a failure means the code is already broken. Examples are a value that you just checked or a fixed URL that you wrote. Keep one in a test, in a preview, or at a command-line or startup entry point.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The lines that you added have no crash operator. Examples are a change to only docs, configuration or data, or to only generated or copied-in files.\n- The only lines that can crash use no crash operator, such as an index into a list or a division. Another practice covers those.\n\nIt still applies when you guard the operator on the same path, such as behind `if let` or a nil check.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Treating every `!` as a bug. What matters is whether a real input can make it fail. Instead, trace where the value comes from.\n- Force-unwrapping the first item of a fetched list, such as `results.first!`. An empty result is normal. Instead, handle the empty case.\n- Calling `fatalError` or `panic` when a network call fails. Requests fail in normal use. Instead, return the error so that the caller can react.\n- Copying `try!` from a preview or a test into app code. Instead, catch the error and show the person a way to recover.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [To panic! or not to panic!](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch09-03-to-panic-or-not-to-panic.html), Klabnik, Nichols and Krycho, The Rust Programming Language. The book says to return an error for expected failure, such as malformed data. It allows a panic in tests or where your code makes sure a value is valid.\n- [The basics](https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/thebasics/), The Swift Programming Language, Swift project. Force unwrap only when a missing value is a programmer error, and otherwise use optional binding or a fallback value.\n- [Error handling](https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/errorhandling/), The Swift Programming Language, Swift project. Use `try!` only when the call cannot throw, such as loading an image that ships with the app.\n- [Go code review comments](https://go.dev/wiki/CodeReviewComments#dont-panic), the Go project. Do not use panic for normal error handling, and return an error instead.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Missing values fall back instead of crashing"'::jsonb),'v5:6c7cffbce403da4edd750705b696c4a5dc788f8c20ea2d2d034bf2237397dcff','practice:v3:d394c0978be81539f1f6c69ad4dbc4a2c5a7bf80c74fde91df08de672e6f2041');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('GROUP','secure-by-default-changes',NULL,'{"name":"Making changes secure by default","description":"Make each change secure by default. Validate and escape untrusted input before it reaches a dangerous sink, avoid insecure defaults and over-broad permissions, and change dependencies deliberately.","icon":"ShieldCheck","color":"red"}'::jsonb,'{"name":"Making changes secure by default","description":"Make each change secure by default. Validate and escape untrusted input before it reaches a dangerous sink, avoid insecure defaults and over-broad permissions, and change dependencies deliberately.","icon":"ShieldCheck","color":"red"}'::jsonb,'824a91fc62aa857f64b8b4c01f60ec170d5fd10b115656f4eb9129d80ca0ef09','group:v1:824a91fc62aa857f64b8b4c01f60ec170d5fd10b115656f4eb9129d80ca0ef09');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','validates-and-escapes-untrusted-input','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Neutralize untrusted input before a dangerous sink","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: untrusted input is neutralized for the exact dangerous sink it reaches in added code.\n\n## The standard\nWhen code added in this diff carries a value from outside the trust boundary into an operation where that value can change the operation''s meaning, the value must be neutralized first — parameterized, escaped for the exact sink context, or validated against an allow-list or strict type — rather than concatenated or interpolated raw. Reason as an experienced security reviewer. A source is any externally-controlled value: a request param, header or body, an uploaded filename, a CLI arg, a remote service''s response. A dangerous sink is any place where raw text changes execution: a built query string, a shell command, an HTML or template render, a filesystem path, a redirect target, a deserializer or eval, or a constructed URL whose path or query interpolates the value — `\"/auth/\\(token)\"` then `URL(string: base + path)`, where the fix is percent-encoding via the platform URL builder, not raw interpolation. These lists are illustrative; recognize the shape in any language or stack, including sinks not named here. Neutralized looks like: bound as a parameter, run through a context-correct encoder, passed as an args array to exec, confined under a canonicalized base path, rendered through auto-escaping left on. Not neutralized looks like: string-concatenated or interpolated straight into the sink with nothing in the quoted hunk neutralizing it. For the constructed URL, the most common sink in an app, the rules are these: a remote or typed String interpolated into a path or query segment is a flow, and it is neutralized only by an encoder correct for the segment — `.urlQueryAllowed` is not a safe encoder for an individual query value or path segment: reserved delimiters can remain and change structure. Use a component builder or an encoder appropriate to the exact component; a value whose type renders as digits (an integer, a coordinate) cannot change the URL''s meaning and is no flow; a whole remote URL string handed to the platform parser (`URL(string: remoteUrl)`) and then to a loader is parsed, not interpolated, and is no sink here; an author constant is no source.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop only when no untrusted value reaches a dangerous sink in the added lines at all. A dangerous sink is a query, command, path, URL or markup built from the value as text. A source-to-sink flow remains an occasion when a typed or parameterized API neutralizes it — a prepared statement, a component-built URL or an auto-escaped render can establish MET. A value only displayed by a text control that does not interpret markup reaches no dangerous sink and is NOT_APPLICABLE. Scan the added lines for a source-to-sink flow before abstaining; do not skip the scan because the diff looks routine. When the diff contains a sink-shaped line — raw input concatenated into a query, command, path or markup — and you still abstain, name the single most suspicious line by file:line and state why it is safe.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added and modified `[L<n>]` lines and their immediate in-diff context. It establishes the closed set of source-to-sink flows this change introduces and whether each is neutralized in those same lines. It never establishes that code outside the diff sanitizes — or fails to sanitize — the value; never assume either.\n2. The file spread (`work/change/files.json`) and the checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): which paths are generated or vendored. They establish the hand-written remainder you assess. They never establish neutralization for a flow the added lines do not show.\nTrace only what you can see. If the diff was not captured completely, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- NOT_MET: a quotable added hunk where a source reaches a sink unneutralized. Name the source and the sink end-to-end.\n- MET: the added lines do carry untrusted input toward a sink, and you traced every introduced flow and each is neutralized before its sink. The injection that could have arrived with this change did not. This is a covered reading of the diff, never a security clearance.\n- UNDETERMINED for a flow you can see whose neutralization the added lines do not settle; name the flow and what would settle it. UNDETERMINED comes after the scan, never instead of it. No flow visible at all is NOT_APPLICABLE, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, covering every unneutralized flow found; show the shape of the fix for the single most consequential one.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a security practice to grow, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a render, redirect or header-style flow; the lightest nudge when the value is partly constrained but the context-correct escape is still missing. MAJOR: raw input that changes code or data execution — injection, traversal, RCE shapes. Identical hunks land at the same level.\n\n## Grounding\nFor NOT_MET quote the exact added `[L<n>]` hunk where the source reaches the sink and name both ends; if you cannot point to both on quoted added lines, it is not assertable. For MET record `evidence.search` over the diff and state in the boundary what you did NOT establish: that the escaping is correct for every input, that sinks reached from unchanged code are safe, that the application has no other exposure. Never claim the code runs, compiles, is exploitable, or that a payload triggers; assert only the visible practice. Never invent a file, a line or a clean bill. Describe the observable behaviour — raw input flowing into a sink — never intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nA generic missing bounds, null or format check with no dangerous sink belongs to `validates-inputs-and-edge-cases-at-the-boundary`; a committed secret belongs to `avoids-insecure-defaults-and-over-broad-permissions`. Defer to the sibling rather than double-flag. Here only: a taint-to-sink injection flow.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for validates-and-escapes-untrusted-input: surface ADDED lines where an untrusted-INPUT\n// token (a SOURCE) and a dangerous OPERATION token (a SINK) co-occur in the same file within a small line\n// window — i.e. the taint flow a reviewer is most likely to miss by eye. CANDIDATES only: the LLM traces\n// whether the source actually reaches the sink unvalidated/unescaped and decides. General by design: a\n// per-language SOURCE table + a SINK table keyed off the file extension. Adding a language = adding rows,\n// no engine change. The taint flow spans lines, so we pair each source to nearby sinks and hand the LLM\n// the exact span to trace.\nimport { isCommentLine } from \"../lib/declarations.ts\";\nimport { languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n/** A label -> pattern table row. The label is what a hint reports, so it is rendered verbatim. */\ntype PatternRows = [string, RegExp][];\n\n/**\n * `all` is checked for every file whatever its language; `byLanguage` refines it for the languages\n * that have their own surfaces. Splitting the two apart makes \"there are always cross-language rows\"\n * a fact the type carries, instead of one key of a string-indexed record that reads as optional.\n */\ninterface PatternTable {\n\tall: PatternRows;\n\tbyLanguage: Record<string, PatternRows>;\n}\n\n// Sources of untrusted input (request/CLI/env/file/stdin). Cross-language patterns live under \"all\" and are\n// checked for every file; language-specific rows refine the common web/runtime surfaces.\nconst SOURCES: PatternTable = {\n\tall: [\n\t\t[\"request/req param\", /\\b(?:request|req)\\b\\s*[.[]/iu],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"params/query/body/headers/cookies\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:params|query|queryString|body|headers|cookies)\\b\\s*[.[]/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"env var\", /\\b(?:process\\.env|os\\.environ|System\\.getenv|getenv|std::env::var|ENV)\\b/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"argv / CLI args\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:argv|sys\\.args|os\\.Args|process\\.argv|CommandLine\\.arguments|args\\[)\\b/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"stdin / scanner read\", /\\b(?:stdin|readLine|Scanner|BufferedReader|input\\s*\\(|gets\\b)\\b/u],\n\t],\n\tbyLanguage: {\n\t\tjava: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"servlet getParameter/getHeader\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\.get(?:Parameter|Header|QueryString|Cookies|InputStream|Reader)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"@RequestParam/@PathVariable/@RequestBody\",\n\t\t\t\t/@(?:RequestParam|PathVariable|RequestBody|RequestHeader|CookieValue)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"file read\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\bnew\\s+(?:FileReader|FileInputStream)\\s*\\(|Files\\.(?:read|newInputStream)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t],\n\t\ttypescript: [\n\t\t\t[\"express/koa req\", /\\breq\\.(?:params|query|body|headers|cookies|get)\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\"fs read\", /\\bfs\\.(?:readFile|readFileSync|createReadStream)\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\"URL/searchParams\", /\\b(?:searchParams|URLSearchParams|location\\.(?:search|hash|href))\\b/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tpython: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"flask/django request\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\brequest\\.(?:args|form|values|json|GET|POST|data|files|headers|cookies)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\"open() read\", /\\bopen\\s*\\([^)]*[''\"]r/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tgo: [\n\t\t\t[\"http.Request fields\", /\\br\\.(?:URL|Form|PostForm|Body|Header|Cookie)\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\"FormValue/Query\", /\\.(?:FormValue|Query|PathValue)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\truby: [[\"rails params\", /\\bparams\\[/u]],\n\t\tphp: [[\"superglobals\", /\\$_(?:GET|POST|REQUEST|COOKIE|SERVER|FILES)\\b/u]],\n\t\tcsharp: [[\"Request fields\", /\\bRequest\\.(?:Query|Form|Headers|Cookies|Body|QueryString)\\b/u]],\n\t\tswift: [\n\t\t\t[\"URLSession / response data\", /\\bURLSession\\b|\\.dataTask\\b|\\bdata\\s*\\(\\s*for\\s*:/u],\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"request header / URL component\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\.value\\s*\\(\\s*forHTTPHeaderField|\\bURLComponents\\b|\\.queryItems\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"UserDefaults / FileManager / env\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\b(?:UserDefaults\\.standard|FileManager\\.default|ProcessInfo\\.processInfo\\.environment)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t],\n\t},\n};\n\n// Sinks: dangerous operations that must receive validated/escaped input (SQL, command/eval, markup, path,\n// templating, deserialization). Cross-language rows under \"all\"; language rows refine.\nconst SINKS: PatternTable = {\n\tall: [\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"raw SQL string-concat\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:SELECT|INSERT|UPDATE|DELETE|WHERE|FROM)\\b[^;]*(?:\\+|\\$\\{|%s|f[\"'']|`|\\|\\||\\.\\.)/iu,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"eval\", /\\beval\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"exec / shell\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:exec|execSync|execve|spawn|popen|os\\.system|subprocess\\.(?:call|run|Popen)|shell_exec)\\s*\\(|(?<![.\\w])system\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"deserialize\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:pickle\\.loads|yaml\\.load\\b|Marshal\\.load|unserialize|JSON\\.parse|deserialize)\\s*\\(/iu,\n\t\t],\n\t\t// A render call, not the word: SwiftUI''s `.renderingMode(.template)` is a native image mode, and\n\t\t// `Handlebars.compile(` or `jinja2.Template(` only builds a template that a later call renders.\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"template render\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:render(?:_template(?:_string)?)?|Mustache\\.render|ejs\\.render)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"path join with input\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:path\\.join|os\\.path\\.join|filepath\\.Join|Paths\\.get|File\\s*\\()/u,\n\t\t],\n\t],\n\tbyLanguage: {\n\t\tjava: [\n\t\t\t[\"Runtime.exec / ProcessBuilder\", /\\b(?:Runtime\\.getRuntime\\(\\)\\.exec|ProcessBuilder)\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"Statement.execute (no prepare)\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\b(?:createStatement|Statement)\\b[^;]*\\.(?:execute|executeQuery|executeUpdate)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\"ObjectInputStream\", /\\bObjectInputStream\\b/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\ttypescript: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"innerHTML / dangerouslySetInnerHTML\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\b(?:innerHTML|outerHTML|dangerouslySetInnerHTML|insertAdjacentHTML|document\\.write)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\"new Function\", /\\bnew\\s+Function\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tpython: [\n\t\t\t[\"cursor.execute concat\", /\\bcursor\\.execute\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\"os.system / subprocess shell=True\", /shell\\s*=\\s*True/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tphp: [[\"echo/print to HTML\", /\\b(?:echo|print)\\b/u]],\n\t\tcsharp: [\n\t\t\t[\"SqlCommand concat\", /\\bnew\\s+SqlCommand\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\"Html.Raw\", /\\bHtml\\.Raw\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tswift: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"WKWebView loadHTMLString / evaluateJavaScript\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\b(?:loadHTMLString|evaluateJavaScript)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\"sqlite3 exec/prepare\", /\\bsqlite3_(?:exec|prepare(?:_v2)?)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t\t[\"Process / shell launch\", /\\bProcess\\s*\\(\\)|\\.launchPath\\b|\\blaunch\\s*\\(\\)/u],\n\t\t\t[\"NSExpression / String(format:)\", /\\bNSExpression\\b|\\bString\\s*\\(\\s*format\\s*:/u],\n\t\t],\n\t},\n};\n\n// Sources and sinks co-occurring within this many added lines are surfaced as a candidate flow.\nconst WINDOW = 25;\n\n/** JavaScript shares TypeScript''s surfaces; the lib names the two apart. */\nfunction rowsLanguage(lang: string | null): string | null {\n\treturn lang === \"javascript\" ? \"typescript\" : lang;\n}\n\n/** Cross-language rows first, then the rows for this file''s language (none when it is unrecognised). */\nfunction rowsFor(table: PatternTable, lang: string | null): PatternRows {\n\treturn [...table.all, ...(lang === null ? [] : (table.byLanguage[lang] ?? []))];\n}\n\nfunction firstMatch(rows: PatternRows, content: string): string | null {\n\tfor (const [label, re] of rows) {\n\t\tif (re.test(content)) {\n\t\t\treturn label;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn null;\n}\n\nexport default function validatesAndEscapesUntrustedInput(\n\t_repo: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tlet flowCount = 0;\n\tlet linesAdded = 0;\n\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tconst sourceRows = rowsFor(SOURCES, rowsLanguage(lang));\n\t\tconst sinkRows = rowsFor(SINKS, rowsLanguage(lang));\n\n\t\t// Collect source / sink positions on ADDED lines only.\n\t\tconst srcLines: { line: number; label: string; content: string }[] = [];\n\t\tconst sinkLines: { line: number; label: string; content: string }[] = [];\n\t\tfor (const [line, content] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\tif (isCommentLine(content, lang ?? \"\")) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tlinesAdded += 1;\n\t\t\tconst s = firstMatch(sourceRows, content);\n\t\t\tif (s !== null) {\n\t\t\t\tsrcLines.push({ line, label: s, content });\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst k = firstMatch(sinkRows, content);\n\t\t\tif (k !== null) {\n\t\t\t\tsinkLines.push({ line, label: k, content });\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (srcLines.length === 0 || sinkLines.length === 0) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t// Surface each sink that has a source within WINDOW lines — that pairing is the flow to trace.\n\t\tfor (const sink of sinkLines) {\n\t\t\tconst near = srcLines.find((s) => Math.abs(s.line - sink.line) <= WINDOW);\n\t\t\tif (!near) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tflowCount += 1;\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\tline: sink.line,\n\t\t\t\tpattern: `source→sink: ${near.label} → ${sink.label}`,\n\t\t\t\tcontext: sink.content.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\tflags: {\n\t\t\t\t\tsourceLine: near.line,\n\t\t\t\t\tsourceToken: near.label,\n\t\t\t\t\tsinkToken: sink.label,\n\t\t\t\t\tlineDistance: Math.abs(near.line - sink.line),\n\t\t\t\t},\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tconst directions =\n\t\tflowCount > 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`Found ${flowCount} added line(s) where an untrusted-input source (request/params/env/file/stdin/argv) and a dangerous sink (raw SQL concat, exec/eval/system, innerHTML, path join, template render, deserialize) co-occur within ${WINDOW} lines — trace whether the source reaches the sink without validation/escaping/parameterization, and confirm before deciding.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: { sourceSinkFlows: flowCount, filesScanned: diffFiles.size, linesAdded },\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Gluing raw user input straight into a query, a shell command, or a page is how injection bugs are born, the kind that leak a database or run an attacker''s code. Parameterizing or escaping it first is what keeps that door shut.","whatGoodLooksLike":"User-supplied values reach the database through bound parameters and the page through proper escaping, never concatenated into a SQL string or dropped into HTML as-is.","groupSlug":"secure-by-default-changes","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 124\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Glued in raw</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"62.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">query(\"WHERE name=''\"</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"34\" y=\"77\" font-size=\"15\">+ name + \"''\")</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"15\">Input changes the query</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Bound as parameter</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"62.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">query(\"WHERE name = ?\",</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"370\" y=\"77\" font-size=\"15\">[name])</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"15\">Input stays data</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A query that glues the name in raw, with + name +, lets the input change the query. A query that binds the name as a parameter with ? keeps the input as data."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Treat each value from outside your code as untrusted. This means request data, uploaded file names, command-line arguments, and replies from other services.\n- Put a value into a database query only as a bound parameter. For example, instead of `\"… WHERE name = ''\" + name + \"''\"`, write `WHERE name = ?` and bind `name`.\n- In a page, keep the template’s auto-escaping on. If you build markup yourself, encode the value for its exact place: text, an attribute, or a script.\n- In a command, a file path, or a URL, keep the value in its own slot. Examples are one list item per argument, one part of a built URL, and a path under a base folder.\n- Some values cannot be bound, such as a column name. Check each of them against a list of allowed values.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- In the lines you added, no outside value reaches a query, a command, a file path, a URL, or markup.\n- The value is only shown in a text control that does not read markup.\n\nIt still applies when you use a safe API, such as a prepared statement, a URL builder, or an auto-escaped template. The safe API is how you meet the practice.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Encoding a whole URL with one general encoder. Characters such as `&`, `=` and `/` can stay, and change the URL’s structure. Instead, encode each path segment and query value on its own.\n- Escaping for the wrong place. HTML escaping does not protect a value inside a script or an event handler. Instead, use the encoder for the place where the value lands.\n- Turning off auto-escaping for one value, such as with React’s `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. Instead, keep escaping on, or first clean the HTML with a trusted sanitizer, a library that removes dangerous tags.\n- Writing your own escaping for a query. It breaks easily. Instead, bind the value, and check the parts that cannot be bound against a list.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP. Bound parameters keep code and data apart, and hand-made escaping is strongly discouraged.\n- [Cross Site Scripting Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP. Encoding must match the exact place in the page, and each URL query value needs its own encoding.\n- [OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP. Prefer a library function to a shell command, and pass the program and each argument separately.\n- [2025 CWE Top 25 Most Dangerous Software Weaknesses](https://cwe.mitre.org/top25/archive/2025/2025_cwe_top25.html), MITRE, 2025. Cross-site scripting and SQL injection rank first and second. Path traversal and OS command injection are also in the top 10.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Neutralize untrusted input before a dangerous sink","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: untrusted input is neutralized for the exact dangerous sink it reaches in added code.\n\n## The standard\nWhen code added in this diff carries a value from outside the trust boundary into an operation where that value can change the operation''s meaning, the value must be neutralized first — parameterized, escaped for the exact sink context, or validated against an allow-list or strict type — rather than concatenated or interpolated raw. Reason as an experienced security reviewer. A source is any externally-controlled value: a request param, header or body, an uploaded filename, a CLI arg, a remote service''s response. A dangerous sink is any place where raw text changes execution: a built query string, a shell command, an HTML or template render, a filesystem path, a redirect target, a deserializer or eval, or a constructed URL whose path or query interpolates the value — `\"/auth/\\(token)\"` then `URL(string: base + path)`, where the fix is percent-encoding via the platform URL builder, not raw interpolation. These lists are illustrative; recognize the shape in any language or stack, including sinks not named here. Neutralized looks like: bound as a parameter, run through a context-correct encoder, passed as an args array to exec, confined under a canonicalized base path, rendered through auto-escaping left on. Not neutralized looks like: string-concatenated or interpolated straight into the sink with nothing in the quoted hunk neutralizing it. For the constructed URL, the most common sink in an app, the rules are these: a remote or typed String interpolated into a path or query segment is a flow, and it is neutralized only by an encoder correct for the segment — `.urlQueryAllowed` is not a safe encoder for an individual query value or path segment: reserved delimiters can remain and change structure. Use a component builder or an encoder appropriate to the exact component; a value whose type renders as digits (an integer, a coordinate) cannot change the URL''s meaning and is no flow; a whole remote URL string handed to the platform parser (`URL(string: remoteUrl)`) and then to a loader is parsed, not interpolated, and is no sink here; an author constant is no source.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop only when no untrusted value reaches a dangerous sink in the added lines at all. A dangerous sink is a query, command, path, URL or markup built from the value as text. A source-to-sink flow remains an occasion when a typed or parameterized API neutralizes it — a prepared statement, a component-built URL or an auto-escaped render can establish MET. A value only displayed by a text control that does not interpret markup reaches no dangerous sink and is NOT_APPLICABLE. Scan the added lines for a source-to-sink flow before abstaining; do not skip the scan because the diff looks routine. When the diff contains a sink-shaped line — raw input concatenated into a query, command, path or markup — and you still abstain, name the single most suspicious line by file:line and state why it is safe.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added and modified `[L<n>]` lines and their immediate in-diff context. It establishes the closed set of source-to-sink flows this change introduces and whether each is neutralized in those same lines. It never establishes that code outside the diff sanitizes — or fails to sanitize — the value; never assume either.\n2. The file spread (`work/change/files.json`) and the checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): which paths are generated or vendored. They establish the hand-written remainder you assess. They never establish neutralization for a flow the added lines do not show.\nTrace only what you can see. If the diff was not captured completely, report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- NOT_MET: a quotable added hunk where a source reaches a sink unneutralized. Name the source and the sink end-to-end.\n- MET: the added lines do carry untrusted input toward a sink, and you traced every introduced flow and each is neutralized before its sink. The injection that could have arrived with this change did not. This is a covered reading of the diff, never a security clearance.\n- UNDETERMINED for a flow you can see whose neutralization the added lines do not settle; name the flow and what would settle it. UNDETERMINED comes after the scan, never instead of it. No flow visible at all is NOT_APPLICABLE, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, covering every unneutralized flow found; show the shape of the fix for the single most consequential one.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a security practice to grow, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a render, redirect or header-style flow; the lightest nudge when the value is partly constrained but the context-correct escape is still missing. MAJOR: raw input that changes code or data execution — injection, traversal, RCE shapes. Identical hunks land at the same level.\n\n## Grounding\nFor NOT_MET quote the exact added `[L<n>]` hunk where the source reaches the sink and name both ends; if you cannot point to both on quoted added lines, it is not assertable. For MET record `evidence.search` over the diff and state in the boundary what you did NOT establish: that the escaping is correct for every input, that sinks reached from unchanged code are safe, that the application has no other exposure. Never claim the code runs, compiles, is exploitable, or that a payload triggers; assert only the visible practice. Never invent a file, a line or a clean bill. Describe the observable behaviour — raw input flowing into a sink — never intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nA generic missing bounds, null or format check with no dangerous sink belongs to `validates-inputs-and-edge-cases-at-the-boundary`; a committed secret belongs to `avoids-insecure-defaults-and-over-broad-permissions`. Defer to the sibling rather than double-flag. Here only: a taint-to-sink injection flow.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for validates-and-escapes-untrusted-input: surface ADDED lines where an untrusted-INPUT\n// token (a SOURCE) and a dangerous OPERATION token (a SINK) co-occur in the same file within a small line\n// window — i.e. the taint flow a reviewer is most likely to miss by eye. CANDIDATES only: the LLM traces\n// whether the source actually reaches the sink unvalidated/unescaped and decides. General by design: a\n// per-language SOURCE table + a SINK table keyed off the file extension. Adding a language = adding rows,\n// no engine change. The taint flow spans lines, so we pair each source to nearby sinks and hand the LLM\n// the exact span to trace.\nimport { isCommentLine } from \"../lib/declarations.ts\";\nimport { languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n/** A label -> pattern table row. The label is what a hint reports, so it is rendered verbatim. */\ntype PatternRows = [string, RegExp][];\n\n/**\n * `all` is checked for every file whatever its language; `byLanguage` refines it for the languages\n * that have their own surfaces. Splitting the two apart makes \"there are always cross-language rows\"\n * a fact the type carries, instead of one key of a string-indexed record that reads as optional.\n */\ninterface PatternTable {\n\tall: PatternRows;\n\tbyLanguage: Record<string, PatternRows>;\n}\n\n// Sources of untrusted input (request/CLI/env/file/stdin). Cross-language patterns live under \"all\" and are\n// checked for every file; language-specific rows refine the common web/runtime surfaces.\nconst SOURCES: PatternTable = {\n\tall: [\n\t\t[\"request/req param\", /\\b(?:request|req)\\b\\s*[.[]/iu],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"params/query/body/headers/cookies\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:params|query|queryString|body|headers|cookies)\\b\\s*[.[]/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"env var\", /\\b(?:process\\.env|os\\.environ|System\\.getenv|getenv|std::env::var|ENV)\\b/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"argv / CLI args\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:argv|sys\\.args|os\\.Args|process\\.argv|CommandLine\\.arguments|args\\[)\\b/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"stdin / scanner read\", /\\b(?:stdin|readLine|Scanner|BufferedReader|input\\s*\\(|gets\\b)\\b/u],\n\t],\n\tbyLanguage: {\n\t\tjava: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"servlet getParameter/getHeader\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\.get(?:Parameter|Header|QueryString|Cookies|InputStream|Reader)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"@RequestParam/@PathVariable/@RequestBody\",\n\t\t\t\t/@(?:RequestParam|PathVariable|RequestBody|RequestHeader|CookieValue)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"file read\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\bnew\\s+(?:FileReader|FileInputStream)\\s*\\(|Files\\.(?:read|newInputStream)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t],\n\t\ttypescript: [\n\t\t\t[\"express/koa req\", /\\breq\\.(?:params|query|body|headers|cookies|get)\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\"fs read\", /\\bfs\\.(?:readFile|readFileSync|createReadStream)\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\"URL/searchParams\", /\\b(?:searchParams|URLSearchParams|location\\.(?:search|hash|href))\\b/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tpython: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"flask/django request\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\brequest\\.(?:args|form|values|json|GET|POST|data|files|headers|cookies)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\"open() read\", /\\bopen\\s*\\([^)]*[''\"]r/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tgo: [\n\t\t\t[\"http.Request fields\", /\\br\\.(?:URL|Form|PostForm|Body|Header|Cookie)\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\"FormValue/Query\", /\\.(?:FormValue|Query|PathValue)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\truby: [[\"rails params\", /\\bparams\\[/u]],\n\t\tphp: [[\"superglobals\", /\\$_(?:GET|POST|REQUEST|COOKIE|SERVER|FILES)\\b/u]],\n\t\tcsharp: [[\"Request fields\", /\\bRequest\\.(?:Query|Form|Headers|Cookies|Body|QueryString)\\b/u]],\n\t\tswift: [\n\t\t\t[\"URLSession / response data\", /\\bURLSession\\b|\\.dataTask\\b|\\bdata\\s*\\(\\s*for\\s*:/u],\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"request header / URL component\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\.value\\s*\\(\\s*forHTTPHeaderField|\\bURLComponents\\b|\\.queryItems\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"UserDefaults / FileManager / env\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\b(?:UserDefaults\\.standard|FileManager\\.default|ProcessInfo\\.processInfo\\.environment)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t],\n\t},\n};\n\n// Sinks: dangerous operations that must receive validated/escaped input (SQL, command/eval, markup, path,\n// templating, deserialization). Cross-language rows under \"all\"; language rows refine.\nconst SINKS: PatternTable = {\n\tall: [\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"raw SQL string-concat\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:SELECT|INSERT|UPDATE|DELETE|WHERE|FROM)\\b[^;]*(?:\\+|\\$\\{|%s|f[\"'']|`|\\|\\||\\.\\.)/iu,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\"eval\", /\\beval\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"exec / shell\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:exec|execSync|execve|spawn|popen|os\\.system|subprocess\\.(?:call|run|Popen)|shell_exec)\\s*\\(|(?<![.\\w])system\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"deserialize\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:pickle\\.loads|yaml\\.load\\b|Marshal\\.load|unserialize|JSON\\.parse|deserialize)\\s*\\(/iu,\n\t\t],\n\t\t// A render call, not the word: SwiftUI''s `.renderingMode(.template)` is a native image mode, and\n\t\t// `Handlebars.compile(` or `jinja2.Template(` only builds a template that a later call renders.\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"template render\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:render(?:_template(?:_string)?)?|Mustache\\.render|ejs\\.render)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t],\n\t\t[\n\t\t\t\"path join with input\",\n\t\t\t/\\b(?:path\\.join|os\\.path\\.join|filepath\\.Join|Paths\\.get|File\\s*\\()/u,\n\t\t],\n\t],\n\tbyLanguage: {\n\t\tjava: [\n\t\t\t[\"Runtime.exec / ProcessBuilder\", /\\b(?:Runtime\\.getRuntime\\(\\)\\.exec|ProcessBuilder)\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"Statement.execute (no prepare)\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\b(?:createStatement|Statement)\\b[^;]*\\.(?:execute|executeQuery|executeUpdate)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\"ObjectInputStream\", /\\bObjectInputStream\\b/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\ttypescript: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"innerHTML / dangerouslySetInnerHTML\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\b(?:innerHTML|outerHTML|dangerouslySetInnerHTML|insertAdjacentHTML|document\\.write)\\b/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\"new Function\", /\\bnew\\s+Function\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tpython: [\n\t\t\t[\"cursor.execute concat\", /\\bcursor\\.execute\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\"os.system / subprocess shell=True\", /shell\\s*=\\s*True/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tphp: [[\"echo/print to HTML\", /\\b(?:echo|print)\\b/u]],\n\t\tcsharp: [\n\t\t\t[\"SqlCommand concat\", /\\bnew\\s+SqlCommand\\b/u],\n\t\t\t[\"Html.Raw\", /\\bHtml\\.Raw\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t],\n\t\tswift: [\n\t\t\t[\n\t\t\t\t\"WKWebView loadHTMLString / evaluateJavaScript\",\n\t\t\t\t/\\b(?:loadHTMLString|evaluateJavaScript)\\s*\\(/u,\n\t\t\t],\n\t\t\t[\"sqlite3 exec/prepare\", /\\bsqlite3_(?:exec|prepare(?:_v2)?)\\s*\\(/u],\n\t\t\t[\"Process / shell launch\", /\\bProcess\\s*\\(\\)|\\.launchPath\\b|\\blaunch\\s*\\(\\)/u],\n\t\t\t[\"NSExpression / String(format:)\", /\\bNSExpression\\b|\\bString\\s*\\(\\s*format\\s*:/u],\n\t\t],\n\t},\n};\n\n// Sources and sinks co-occurring within this many added lines are surfaced as a candidate flow.\nconst WINDOW = 25;\n\n/** JavaScript shares TypeScript''s surfaces; the lib names the two apart. */\nfunction rowsLanguage(lang: string | null): string | null {\n\treturn lang === \"javascript\" ? \"typescript\" : lang;\n}\n\n/** Cross-language rows first, then the rows for this file''s language (none when it is unrecognised). */\nfunction rowsFor(table: PatternTable, lang: string | null): PatternRows {\n\treturn [...table.all, ...(lang === null ? [] : (table.byLanguage[lang] ?? []))];\n}\n\nfunction firstMatch(rows: PatternRows, content: string): string | null {\n\tfor (const [label, re] of rows) {\n\t\tif (re.test(content)) {\n\t\t\treturn label;\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn null;\n}\n\nexport default function validatesAndEscapesUntrustedInput(\n\t_repo: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tlet flowCount = 0;\n\tlet linesAdded = 0;\n\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tconst sourceRows = rowsFor(SOURCES, rowsLanguage(lang));\n\t\tconst sinkRows = rowsFor(SINKS, rowsLanguage(lang));\n\n\t\t// Collect source / sink positions on ADDED lines only.\n\t\tconst srcLines: { line: number; label: string; content: string }[] = [];\n\t\tconst sinkLines: { line: number; label: string; content: string }[] = [];\n\t\tfor (const [line, content] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\tif (isCommentLine(content, lang ?? \"\")) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tlinesAdded += 1;\n\t\t\tconst s = firstMatch(sourceRows, content);\n\t\t\tif (s !== null) {\n\t\t\t\tsrcLines.push({ line, label: s, content });\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst k = firstMatch(sinkRows, content);\n\t\t\tif (k !== null) {\n\t\t\t\tsinkLines.push({ line, label: k, content });\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (srcLines.length === 0 || sinkLines.length === 0) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t// Surface each sink that has a source within WINDOW lines — that pairing is the flow to trace.\n\t\tfor (const sink of sinkLines) {\n\t\t\tconst near = srcLines.find((s) => Math.abs(s.line - sink.line) <= WINDOW);\n\t\t\tif (!near) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tflowCount += 1;\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\tline: sink.line,\n\t\t\t\tpattern: `source→sink: ${near.label} → ${sink.label}`,\n\t\t\t\tcontext: sink.content.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\tflags: {\n\t\t\t\t\tsourceLine: near.line,\n\t\t\t\t\tsourceToken: near.label,\n\t\t\t\t\tsinkToken: sink.label,\n\t\t\t\t\tlineDistance: Math.abs(near.line - sink.line),\n\t\t\t\t},\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tconst directions =\n\t\tflowCount > 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`Found ${flowCount} added line(s) where an untrusted-input source (request/params/env/file/stdin/argv) and a dangerous sink (raw SQL concat, exec/eval/system, innerHTML, path join, template render, deserialize) co-occur within ${WINDOW} lines — trace whether the source reaches the sink without validation/escaping/parameterization, and confirm before deciding.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: { sourceSinkFlows: flowCount, filesScanned: diffFiles.size, linesAdded },\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Gluing raw user input straight into a query, a shell command, or a page is how injection bugs are born, the kind that leak a database or run an attacker''s code. Parameterizing or escaping it first is what keeps that door shut.","whatGoodLooksLike":"User-supplied values reach the database through bound parameters and the page through proper escaping, never concatenated into a SQL string or dropped into HTML as-is.","groupSlug":"secure-by-default-changes","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 124\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Glued in raw</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"62.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">query(\"WHERE name=''\"</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"34\" y=\"77\" font-size=\"15\">+ name + \"''\")</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"15\">Input changes the query</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Bound as parameter</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"62.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">query(\"WHERE name = ?\",</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"370\" y=\"77\" font-size=\"15\">[name])</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"15\">Input stays data</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A query that glues the name in raw, with + name +, lets the input change the query. A query that binds the name as a parameter with ? keeps the input as data."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Treat each value from outside your code as untrusted. This means request data, uploaded file names, command-line arguments, and replies from other services.\n- Put a value into a database query only as a bound parameter. For example, instead of `\"… WHERE name = ''\" + name + \"''\"`, write `WHERE name = ?` and bind `name`.\n- In a page, keep the template’s auto-escaping on. If you build markup yourself, encode the value for its exact place: text, an attribute, or a script.\n- In a command, a file path, or a URL, keep the value in its own slot. Examples are one list item per argument, one part of a built URL, and a path under a base folder.\n- Some values cannot be bound, such as a column name. Check each of them against a list of allowed values.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- In the lines you added, no outside value reaches a query, a command, a file path, a URL, or markup.\n- The value is only shown in a text control that does not read markup.\n\nIt still applies when you use a safe API, such as a prepared statement, a URL builder, or an auto-escaped template. The safe API is how you meet the practice.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Encoding a whole URL with one general encoder. Characters such as `&`, `=` and `/` can stay, and change the URL’s structure. Instead, encode each path segment and query value on its own.\n- Escaping for the wrong place. HTML escaping does not protect a value inside a script or an event handler. Instead, use the encoder for the place where the value lands.\n- Turning off auto-escaping for one value, such as with React’s `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. Instead, keep escaping on, or first clean the HTML with a trusted sanitizer, a library that removes dangerous tags.\n- Writing your own escaping for a query. It breaks easily. Instead, bind the value, and check the parts that cannot be bound against a list.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [SQL Injection Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP. Bound parameters keep code and data apart, and hand-made escaping is strongly discouraged.\n- [Cross Site Scripting Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP. Encoding must match the exact place in the page, and each URL query value needs its own encoding.\n- [OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP. Prefer a library function to a shell command, and pass the program and each argument separately.\n- [2025 CWE Top 25 Most Dangerous Software Weaknesses](https://cwe.mitre.org/top25/archive/2025/2025_cwe_top25.html), MITRE, 2025. Cross-site scripting and SQL injection rank first and second. Path traversal and OS command injection are also in the top 10.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Untrusted input is bound or escaped before a sink"'::jsonb),'v5:bff0e7af0cc00f71ec238f5db5cbf145b814743a9a5d5f25265f9466227a4a08','practice:v3:8f9dfb34268756d3f9cde9de9af62c8bce4f42a024ce9524620f1c3596ba00ce');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','avoids-insecure-defaults-and-over-broad-permissions','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Avoid insecure defaults and over-broad permissions","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added access and exposure settings stay appropriately constrained under the captured boundaries and safeguards.\n\n## The standard\nCode should start locked down and open only what it can justify. Hunt the added lines for a change that ships an insecure default or widens an access or exposure boundary without an adequate, evidenced justification. Judge the SETTING the change adopts, not the data flowing through it, and never as a blanket vulnerability scan. A sink-hunt, not a posture rating: do not read the top-level intent, conclude \"looks secure\" and stop. Walk these sink classes over the added lines; they are classes to think in, not a closed value list, so recognise the same shape on any stack:\n1. transport, certificate or host verification toggled off;\n2. cross-origin, CORS or permission scope widened — a credentialed response that reflects the request Origin back without an allowlist, or a bare wildcard on an authenticated route;\n3. a file, resource or object mode made world-readable or world-writable, or otherwise over-broad;\n4. an existing auth or authz guard removed or loosened;\n5. secret or credential exposure — trace dataflow here, do not pattern-match names: inspect EVERY logging, string-interpolation, persistence and URL-building call in the added lines and ask what the interpolated value can hold at runtime. A print or log of a value that can carry credential material is a leak even when the variable is not named \"token\": the body of an auth, login, token or credential exchange (a login response that carries the issued token, for example), an Authorization or session cookie header, or a private API credential. Credential material protects access or data through confidentiality. Establish its authority and audience from use and captured contract, not its name, literal form or URL position. Provider-published public identifiers, shared free/test keys and nonfunctional placeholders are not secret merely because committed or logged. Account-specific sandbox/test secrets and flows that can carry private runtime credentials remain in scope. An ordinary API response or error body, a status code or the name of a missing key carries none;\n6. a debug, verbose-error or permissive default shipped to a non-test surface.\nSpotting one tightened setting (an ATS flag removed, say) does not discharge the hunt; finish every class. A candidate sink class establishes what to inspect, not an automatic defect. Clean: a setting correctly scoped, least-privilege or tightened; a widening whose quoted purpose and captured constraints establish an appropriate exception. A reason, or a test, sample or local filename, does not by itself make exposure safe: verify the actual boundary, the permitted audience and the applicable safeguards from the captured evidence. The absence of an insecure setting is a clean baseline, not a hardening act.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the added lines touch no setting of any sink class at all: a pure UI, layout, documentation, model or test change with no transport, origin, permission, file-mode, auth, credential, logging or debug setting in it. That value claims the work had no subject here; MET is for a change that touches such a setting and does so securely. An added logging, print or persistence call that interpolates any runtime value touches class 5 (credential exposure) and is a setting of that class: a change with one is never NOT_APPLICABLE; walk the class and record what the value can hold. Never abstain on a shallow pass. When the diff contains a config-shaped line (a keychain, permission, CORS or allow-all setting, a token in a URL or path), NOT_APPLICABLE must name the single most suspicious line by file and `[L<n>]` and state why it is safe. Assess only the hand-written remainder of a generated- or vendored-dominated diff.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added lines with their `[L<n>]` markers, and any removed or weakened guard line. It establishes every candidate sink the change introduces — a closed corpus you can walk — and any in-diff comment or profile guard that justifies a widening. It never establishes that the existing configuration is sound, that unchanged settings are safe, or that the system has no other exposure.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): pinned source around a hunk. Read it when the boundary, audience or safeguard that would decide a candidate line sits outside the hunk (a dev profile, a network policy, the route''s auth wrapper). It establishes the constraints an exception rests on. It never establishes that anything was run, compiled or tested.\n3. The precomputed leads below these criteria, when present: leads to inspect, never evidence. An absent or empty candidate list is never grounds for NOT_APPLICABLE while a candidate line is visible.\nNever infer a setting from a filename or a count you cannot quote.\n\n## Judge\nWalk every sink class first; then assess the standard.\n- NOT_MET: a concrete added setting whose exposure is inappropriate under the evidenced constraints. Quote the added `[L<n>]` line, or the removed or weakened guard line, and name the file.\n- MET: the added lines touch at least one setting of a sink class, you walked every class over them, and none was touched insecurely. A COVERED search over a closed corpus, not an impression: an unwalked class makes it a false claim rather than an incomplete one. Record it with `evidence.search` over the diff, name the sink classes you walked in `lookedFor`, and state in the boundary what you did NOT establish. A report on a search, never a security clearance.\n- UNDETERMINED: a candidate sink line is visible but the fact that would settle it is not; name the line and what would settle it. Never file an unreadable sink under NOT_APPLICABLE.\nBefore either abstention, state in the rationale what you found for the credential-exposure class (5). One observation per review; with several issues, name the single most consequential and mention the rest in a clause.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a security-hygiene practice to grow, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a lower-blast-radius misconfiguration. MAJOR: a meaningfully widened blast radius — disabled verification, credentialed origin-reflection or wildcard exposure, a removed or loosened auth guard, or exposed credential material — when the captured boundaries establish consequential exposure. A merely asserted internal or dev rationale does not reduce that consequence; an evidenced appropriate exception is not a negative to grade at all. Do not sum severities.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery observation quotes the exact added `[L<n>]` line, or the removed or weakened guard line, and names the file. A claim that a setting was added, removed, hardened or mitigated must quote the changed line that does it. An appropriate exception must quote both the justification and the constraints that support it, and it does not remove an otherwise present review occasion. Never claim to run, compile or test; never invent a file, a line or a clean bill of health. Point forward to the safer default: verification on; the exact origin named instead of `*`; the toggle behind a dev profile; the least-privilege mode; the guard restored or its reason recorded. Talk about the configuration, never the contributor.\n\n## Defer\nWhether untrusted input is escaped or validated belongs to `validates-and-escapes-untrusted-input`. A missing or placeholder usage description in `Info.plist` belongs to `declares-permissions-truthfully-at-point-of-use`, not to this practice. An access scope wider than the feature needs remains in scope here. Credential exposure stays here, graded by Severity above. If captured evidence cannot settle confidentiality or authority, record UNDETERMINED and name the deciding missing fact.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for avoids-insecure-defaults-and-over-broad-permissions: locate insecure-default /\n// over-broad-permission / secret-exposure constructs ADDED in the diff, across languages. CANDIDATES only —\n// the LLM confirms whether each is a real, exploitable insecure default on a reachable path. General by\n// design: a pattern table keyed off the construct, applied to every added code line regardless of language.\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// [label, regex] — each fires on an ADDED line. Tuned to the auth/transport/secret surface a login or API\n// change touches, where a \"looks fine\" review most often misses the real insecure default.\nconst PATTERNS: [string, RegExp][] = [\n\t// A credential-named value interpolated into a URL path or query. Matches\n\t// ${...}/#{...} (JS/Ruby/Kotlin) and Swift''s \\(...) interpolation of a credential-named variable into a path.\n\t[\n\t\t\"credential-named value in URL path/query\",\n\t\t/[\"''`](?:https?:\\/\\/|\\/)[^\"''`\\n]*(?:\\\\\\(|[$#]\\{)[^\"''`\\n)}]*\\b(?:token|secret|password|api[_-]?key|auth|session|refresh)\\w*/iu,\n\t],\n\t// Keychain write — an omitted kSecAttrAccessible defaults to WhenUnlocked; whether the effective class,\n\t// synchronization and backup migration fit the stored secret is the review''s.\n\t[\"keychain write\", /\\bSecItemAdd\\b|kSecValueData\\b/u],\n\t// Logging a credential-named value or response body to console/log.\n\t[\n\t\t\"logs credential-named value / response body\",\n\t\t/\\b(?:print|NSLog|console\\.(?:log|debug|info)|System\\.out\\.print\\w*|log\\.(?:debug|info|warn))\\s*\\([^)]*\\b(?:token|password|secret|credential|response|body|account)\\b/iu,\n\t],\n\t// Insecure transport / disabled TLS verification.\n\t[\n\t\t\"insecure transport / TLS off\",\n\t\t/NSAllowsArbitraryLoads|allowsArbitraryLoads|rejectUnauthorized\\s*:\\s*false|InsecureSkipVerify\\s*:\\s*true|verify\\s*=\\s*False|\\.insecure\\(\\)|http:\\/\\/(?!localhost|127\\.0\\.0\\.1|0\\.0\\.0\\.0)/u,\n\t],\n\t// Over-broad permissions / wildcard access.\n\t[\n\t\t\"over-broad permission / wildcard\",\n\t\t/chmod\\s+0?777|\\b0o?777\\b|permitAll\\(\\)|\\.anyRequest\\(\\)\\.permitAll|Access-Control-Allow-Origin[\"''\\s:]*\\*|allowedOrigins?[\"''\\s:]*\\*/u,\n\t],\n\t// A literal assigned to a credential-named field.\n\t[\n\t\t\"credential-named literal\",\n\t\t/\\b(?:password|secret|api[_-]?key|token)\\s*[:=]\\s*[\"''`][A-Za-z0-9_\\-/+]{8,}[\"''`]/iu,\n\t],\n];\n\nfunction isComment(t: string): boolean {\n\treturn t.startsWith(\"//\") || t.startsWith(\"#\") || t.startsWith(\"*\") || t.startsWith(\"/*\");\n}\n\nexport default function avoidsInsecureDefaultsAndOverBroadPermissions(\n\t_repo: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst byKind: Record<string, number> = {};\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tfor (const [line, content] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\tconst trimmed = content.trimStart();\n\t\t\tif (isComment(trimmed)) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfor (const [label, re] of PATTERNS) {\n\t\t\t\tif (re.test(content)) {\n\t\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tline,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tpattern: label,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tcontext: content.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tflags: {},\n\t\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\t\tbyKind[label] = (byKind[label] ?? 0) + 1;\n\t\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tconst directions =\n\t\thints.length > 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`Found ${hints.length} insecure-default / secret-exposure candidate(s) on added lines — investigate each on the reachable auth/transport surface: for credential-named URL, literal and logging leads, establish the value’s role under class 5 of the standard before deciding whether it exposes credential material; a keychain write takes its accessibility from kSecAttrAccessible, WhenUnlocked when omitted, which is not a defect by itself; disabled TLS / wildcard CORS / world-writable perms are over-broad. Confirm whether each is real and exploitable before deciding.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: { insecureDefaultCandidates: hints.length, ...byKind },\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A default or permission that ships wider than it needs sits quietly until someone exploits it, and this kind of misconfiguration is among the most common security gaps there is. Starting locked down means you only open what you can justify.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A new endpoint or credential grants exactly the access it needs and no more, with scoped permissions and a specific allowed origin from an allowlist, not an origin reflected back from the request or a blanket wildcard, instead of a permissive default dropped in to make things work.","groupSlug":"secure-by-default-changes","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 141\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Any origin</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">origin: \"*\"</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M24 72v60h24M24 100h24\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M56 100l-8-5v10zM56 132l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"68\" y=\"105\" font-size=\"15\">app.test reads</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"68\" y=\"137\" font-size=\"15\">evil.test reads</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Named origin</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">origin: \"https://app.test\"</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M360 72v60h24M360 100h24\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M392 100l-8-5v10zM392 132l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"404\" y=\"105\" font-size=\"15\">app.test reads</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"404\" y=\"137\" font-size=\"15\">evil.test cannot read</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"With any origin allowed, both app.test and evil.test read the API. With the named origin https://app.test, app.test reads and evil.test cannot read."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Start each new setting closed, with no access, and add access for one named need at a time.\n- Name the exact websites that may read your endpoint’s responses in a browser. For example, instead of copying the request’s `Origin` header into the response, check it against a fixed list first.\n- Turn on each debug setting only in local development. Examples are detailed error pages and a skipped certificate check.\n- Before you log, print or save a value, check what it can hold at runtime. Leave out or mask any token, password, key, cookie or `Authorization` header.\n- If you must widen access or loosen an access check, say why in a code comment next to the setting. Keep the real limit in the code too, such as an access check or a development-only setting.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change only changes the user interface, layout, docs, data models or tests. It adds no transport, origin, permission, file mode, access check, credential, logging or debug setting.\n- Your change is mostly generated or vendored files. None of the lines you wrote by hand touches such a setting.\n\nIt still applies when you add a log, print or save call that includes any runtime value, because that value can hold a credential.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Trusting a reason to make a wide setting safe. A comment such as “internal only”, or a test or sample file name, does not limit who can reach it. Instead, check the real boundary and who is allowed through it.\n- Fixing one setting and stopping. Instead, check every setting that your change adds.\n- Trusting the variable name. A log of a login response or a request header can leak a token, even when nothing is named “token”. Instead, follow what the value can hold.\n- Using a wildcard origin, or a file that anyone on the machine can change, to get past an error. Instead, name the origin, user or group that needs access, and grant only that.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [The protection of information in computer systems](https://web.mit.edu/Saltzer/www/publications/protection/Basic.html), Saltzer and Schroeder, 1975. Base access on permission, so the default is no access. Give each program only the privileges it needs.\n- [A02:2025 Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/Top10/2025/A02_2025-Security_Misconfiguration/), OWASP Top 10, 2025. Every application tested had some misconfiguration, such as unneeded features or error messages that say too much.\n- [Testing cross origin resource sharing](https://wstg.owasp.org/v4.2/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/11-Client-side_Testing/07-Testing_Cross_Origin_Resource_Sharing/), OWASP Web Security Testing Guide. A `*` origin, or an origin sent back unchecked, is insecure unless the API is public.\n- [Logging cheat sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP Cheat Sheet Series. Remove or mask access tokens, session IDs, passwords and keys before they reach a log.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Avoid insecure defaults and over-broad permissions","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: added access and exposure settings stay appropriately constrained under the captured boundaries and safeguards.\n\n## The standard\nCode should start locked down and open only what it can justify. Hunt the added lines for a change that ships an insecure default or widens an access or exposure boundary without an adequate, evidenced justification. Judge the SETTING the change adopts, not the data flowing through it, and never as a blanket vulnerability scan. A sink-hunt, not a posture rating: do not read the top-level intent, conclude \"looks secure\" and stop. Walk these sink classes over the added lines; they are classes to think in, not a closed value list, so recognise the same shape on any stack:\n1. transport, certificate or host verification toggled off;\n2. cross-origin, CORS or permission scope widened — a credentialed response that reflects the request Origin back without an allowlist, or a bare wildcard on an authenticated route;\n3. a file, resource or object mode made world-readable or world-writable, or otherwise over-broad;\n4. an existing auth or authz guard removed or loosened;\n5. secret or credential exposure — trace dataflow here, do not pattern-match names: inspect EVERY logging, string-interpolation, persistence and URL-building call in the added lines and ask what the interpolated value can hold at runtime. A print or log of a value that can carry credential material is a leak even when the variable is not named \"token\": the body of an auth, login, token or credential exchange (a login response that carries the issued token, for example), an Authorization or session cookie header, or a private API credential. Credential material protects access or data through confidentiality. Establish its authority and audience from use and captured contract, not its name, literal form or URL position. Provider-published public identifiers, shared free/test keys and nonfunctional placeholders are not secret merely because committed or logged. Account-specific sandbox/test secrets and flows that can carry private runtime credentials remain in scope. An ordinary API response or error body, a status code or the name of a missing key carries none;\n6. a debug, verbose-error or permissive default shipped to a non-test surface.\nSpotting one tightened setting (an ATS flag removed, say) does not discharge the hunt; finish every class. A candidate sink class establishes what to inspect, not an automatic defect. Clean: a setting correctly scoped, least-privilege or tightened; a widening whose quoted purpose and captured constraints establish an appropriate exception. A reason, or a test, sample or local filename, does not by itself make exposure safe: verify the actual boundary, the permitted audience and the applicable safeguards from the captured evidence. The absence of an insecure setting is a clean baseline, not a hardening act.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the added lines touch no setting of any sink class at all: a pure UI, layout, documentation, model or test change with no transport, origin, permission, file-mode, auth, credential, logging or debug setting in it. That value claims the work had no subject here; MET is for a change that touches such a setting and does so securely. An added logging, print or persistence call that interpolates any runtime value touches class 5 (credential exposure) and is a setting of that class: a change with one is never NOT_APPLICABLE; walk the class and record what the value can hold. Never abstain on a shallow pass. When the diff contains a config-shaped line (a keychain, permission, CORS or allow-all setting, a token in a URL or path), NOT_APPLICABLE must name the single most suspicious line by file and `[L<n>]` and state why it is safe. Assess only the hand-written remainder of a generated- or vendored-dominated diff.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`): the added lines with their `[L<n>]` markers, and any removed or weakened guard line. It establishes every candidate sink the change introduces — a closed corpus you can walk — and any in-diff comment or profile guard that justifies a widening. It never establishes that the existing configuration is sound, that unchanged settings are safe, or that the system has no other exposure.\n2. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): pinned source around a hunk. Read it when the boundary, audience or safeguard that would decide a candidate line sits outside the hunk (a dev profile, a network policy, the route''s auth wrapper). It establishes the constraints an exception rests on. It never establishes that anything was run, compiled or tested.\n3. The precomputed leads below these criteria, when present: leads to inspect, never evidence. An absent or empty candidate list is never grounds for NOT_APPLICABLE while a candidate line is visible.\nNever infer a setting from a filename or a count you cannot quote.\n\n## Judge\nWalk every sink class first; then assess the standard.\n- NOT_MET: a concrete added setting whose exposure is inappropriate under the evidenced constraints. Quote the added `[L<n>]` line, or the removed or weakened guard line, and name the file.\n- MET: the added lines touch at least one setting of a sink class, you walked every class over them, and none was touched insecurely. A COVERED search over a closed corpus, not an impression: an unwalked class makes it a false claim rather than an incomplete one. Record it with `evidence.search` over the diff, name the sink classes you walked in `lookedFor`, and state in the boundary what you did NOT establish. A report on a search, never a security clearance.\n- UNDETERMINED: a candidate sink line is visible but the fact that would settle it is not; name the line and what would settle it. Never file an unreadable sink under NOT_APPLICABLE.\nBefore either abstention, state in the rationale what you found for the credential-exposure class (5). One observation per review; with several issues, name the single most consequential and mention the rest in a clause.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on this practice alone — a security-hygiene practice to grow, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a lower-blast-radius misconfiguration. MAJOR: a meaningfully widened blast radius — disabled verification, credentialed origin-reflection or wildcard exposure, a removed or loosened auth guard, or exposed credential material — when the captured boundaries establish consequential exposure. A merely asserted internal or dev rationale does not reduce that consequence; an evidenced appropriate exception is not a negative to grade at all. Do not sum severities.\n\n## Grounding\nEvery observation quotes the exact added `[L<n>]` line, or the removed or weakened guard line, and names the file. A claim that a setting was added, removed, hardened or mitigated must quote the changed line that does it. An appropriate exception must quote both the justification and the constraints that support it, and it does not remove an otherwise present review occasion. Never claim to run, compile or test; never invent a file, a line or a clean bill of health. Point forward to the safer default: verification on; the exact origin named instead of `*`; the toggle behind a dev profile; the least-privilege mode; the guard restored or its reason recorded. Talk about the configuration, never the contributor.\n\n## Defer\nWhether untrusted input is escaped or validated belongs to `validates-and-escapes-untrusted-input`. A missing or placeholder usage description in `Info.plist` belongs to `declares-permissions-truthfully-at-point-of-use`, not to this practice. An access scope wider than the feature needs remains in scope here. Credential exposure stays here, graded by Severity above. If captured evidence cannot settle confidentiality or authority, record UNDETERMINED and name the deciding missing fact.","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for avoids-insecure-defaults-and-over-broad-permissions: locate insecure-default /\n// over-broad-permission / secret-exposure constructs ADDED in the diff, across languages. CANDIDATES only —\n// the LLM confirms whether each is a real, exploitable insecure default on a reachable path. General by\n// design: a pattern table keyed off the construct, applied to every added code line regardless of language.\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// [label, regex] — each fires on an ADDED line. Tuned to the auth/transport/secret surface a login or API\n// change touches, where a \"looks fine\" review most often misses the real insecure default.\nconst PATTERNS: [string, RegExp][] = [\n\t// A credential-named value interpolated into a URL path or query. Matches\n\t// ${...}/#{...} (JS/Ruby/Kotlin) and Swift''s \\(...) interpolation of a credential-named variable into a path.\n\t[\n\t\t\"credential-named value in URL path/query\",\n\t\t/[\"''`](?:https?:\\/\\/|\\/)[^\"''`\\n]*(?:\\\\\\(|[$#]\\{)[^\"''`\\n)}]*\\b(?:token|secret|password|api[_-]?key|auth|session|refresh)\\w*/iu,\n\t],\n\t// Keychain write — an omitted kSecAttrAccessible defaults to WhenUnlocked; whether the effective class,\n\t// synchronization and backup migration fit the stored secret is the review''s.\n\t[\"keychain write\", /\\bSecItemAdd\\b|kSecValueData\\b/u],\n\t// Logging a credential-named value or response body to console/log.\n\t[\n\t\t\"logs credential-named value / response body\",\n\t\t/\\b(?:print|NSLog|console\\.(?:log|debug|info)|System\\.out\\.print\\w*|log\\.(?:debug|info|warn))\\s*\\([^)]*\\b(?:token|password|secret|credential|response|body|account)\\b/iu,\n\t],\n\t// Insecure transport / disabled TLS verification.\n\t[\n\t\t\"insecure transport / TLS off\",\n\t\t/NSAllowsArbitraryLoads|allowsArbitraryLoads|rejectUnauthorized\\s*:\\s*false|InsecureSkipVerify\\s*:\\s*true|verify\\s*=\\s*False|\\.insecure\\(\\)|http:\\/\\/(?!localhost|127\\.0\\.0\\.1|0\\.0\\.0\\.0)/u,\n\t],\n\t// Over-broad permissions / wildcard access.\n\t[\n\t\t\"over-broad permission / wildcard\",\n\t\t/chmod\\s+0?777|\\b0o?777\\b|permitAll\\(\\)|\\.anyRequest\\(\\)\\.permitAll|Access-Control-Allow-Origin[\"''\\s:]*\\*|allowedOrigins?[\"''\\s:]*\\*/u,\n\t],\n\t// A literal assigned to a credential-named field.\n\t[\n\t\t\"credential-named literal\",\n\t\t/\\b(?:password|secret|api[_-]?key|token)\\s*[:=]\\s*[\"''`][A-Za-z0-9_\\-/+]{8,}[\"''`]/iu,\n\t],\n];\n\nfunction isComment(t: string): boolean {\n\treturn t.startsWith(\"//\") || t.startsWith(\"#\") || t.startsWith(\"*\") || t.startsWith(\"/*\");\n}\n\nexport default function avoidsInsecureDefaultsAndOverBroadPermissions(\n\t_repo: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst byKind: Record<string, number> = {};\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tfor (const [line, content] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\tconst trimmed = content.trimStart();\n\t\t\tif (isComment(trimmed)) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfor (const [label, re] of PATTERNS) {\n\t\t\t\tif (re.test(content)) {\n\t\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tline,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tpattern: label,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tcontext: content.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tflags: {},\n\t\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\t\tbyKind[label] = (byKind[label] ?? 0) + 1;\n\t\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tconst directions =\n\t\thints.length > 0\n\t\t\t? [\n\t\t\t\t\t`Found ${hints.length} insecure-default / secret-exposure candidate(s) on added lines — investigate each on the reachable auth/transport surface: for credential-named URL, literal and logging leads, establish the value’s role under class 5 of the standard before deciding whether it exposes credential material; a keychain write takes its accessibility from kSecAttrAccessible, WhenUnlocked when omitted, which is not a defect by itself; disabled TLS / wildcard CORS / world-writable perms are over-broad. Confirm whether each is real and exploitable before deciding.`,\n\t\t\t\t]\n\t\t\t: [];\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: hints.slice(0, 40),\n\t\tmetrics: { insecureDefaultCandidates: hints.length, ...byKind },\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A default or permission that ships wider than it needs sits quietly until someone exploits it, and this kind of misconfiguration is among the most common security gaps there is. Starting locked down means you only open what you can justify.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A new endpoint or credential grants exactly the access it needs and no more, with scoped permissions and a specific allowed origin from an allowlist, not an origin reflected back from the request or a blanket wildcard, instead of a permissive default dropped in to make things work.","groupSlug":"secure-by-default-changes","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 141\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Any origin</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">origin: \"*\"</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M24 72v60h24M24 100h24\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M56 100l-8-5v10zM56 132l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"68\" y=\"105\" font-size=\"15\">app.test reads</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"68\" y=\"137\" font-size=\"15\">evil.test reads</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Named origin</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"38.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"15\">origin: \"https://app.test\"</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M360 72v60h24M360 100h24\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path class=\"pv-fill-muted\" d=\"M392 100l-8-5v10zM392 132l-8-5v10z\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"404\" y=\"105\" font-size=\"15\">app.test reads</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"404\" y=\"137\" font-size=\"15\">evil.test cannot read</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"With any origin allowed, both app.test and evil.test read the API. With the named origin https://app.test, app.test reads and evil.test cannot read."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Start each new setting closed, with no access, and add access for one named need at a time.\n- Name the exact websites that may read your endpoint’s responses in a browser. For example, instead of copying the request’s `Origin` header into the response, check it against a fixed list first.\n- Turn on each debug setting only in local development. Examples are detailed error pages and a skipped certificate check.\n- Before you log, print or save a value, check what it can hold at runtime. Leave out or mask any token, password, key, cookie or `Authorization` header.\n- If you must widen access or loosen an access check, say why in a code comment next to the setting. Keep the real limit in the code too, such as an access check or a development-only setting.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change only changes the user interface, layout, docs, data models or tests. It adds no transport, origin, permission, file mode, access check, credential, logging or debug setting.\n- Your change is mostly generated or vendored files. None of the lines you wrote by hand touches such a setting.\n\nIt still applies when you add a log, print or save call that includes any runtime value, because that value can hold a credential.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Trusting a reason to make a wide setting safe. A comment such as “internal only”, or a test or sample file name, does not limit who can reach it. Instead, check the real boundary and who is allowed through it.\n- Fixing one setting and stopping. Instead, check every setting that your change adds.\n- Trusting the variable name. A log of a login response or a request header can leak a token, even when nothing is named “token”. Instead, follow what the value can hold.\n- Using a wildcard origin, or a file that anyone on the machine can change, to get past an error. Instead, name the origin, user or group that needs access, and grant only that.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [The protection of information in computer systems](https://web.mit.edu/Saltzer/www/publications/protection/Basic.html), Saltzer and Schroeder, 1975. Base access on permission, so the default is no access. Give each program only the privileges it needs.\n- [A02:2025 Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/Top10/2025/A02_2025-Security_Misconfiguration/), OWASP Top 10, 2025. Every application tested had some misconfiguration, such as unneeded features or error messages that say too much.\n- [Testing cross origin resource sharing](https://wstg.owasp.org/v4.2/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/11-Client-side_Testing/07-Testing_Cross_Origin_Resource_Sharing/), OWASP Web Security Testing Guide. A `*` origin, or an origin sent back unchecked, is insecure unless the API is public.\n- [Logging cheat sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html), OWASP Cheat Sheet Series. Remove or mask access tokens, session IDs, passwords and keys before they reach a log.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"New access grants exactly what it needs and no more"'::jsonb),'v5:eeb5f2866a45f9d1fb4d0308796f0aeaaca8889d1f0fce610f0e5103363da45a','practice:v3:321e47fb2bc43c03c18c801b47be0ef2acee54700b404457391dbaf50469e6d9');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','changes-dependencies-deliberately','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Change dependencies deliberately","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: an external dependency edit, as the Occasion defines it, whose manifest and lockfile stay coherent under the ecosystem''s resolution rules, with a recorded reason for a notable new declaration or version bump.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether this pull request makes the external dependency edit defined by the Occasion deliberately. Two things hold together: the manifest and any lockfile remain consistent with the ecosystem''s resolution rules and the repository''s documented policy, and a notable new external declaration or version bump carries a one-line reason a reviewer can read — in the manifest context, the title or body, or a commit subject. The reason states the package''s purpose in this change or why its version changes. A package name or an edit label is not a reason, even when the name describes the package''s function. This is a supply-chain workflow practice about how a dependency edit is justified and kept coherent. It is never a claim about the dependency''s own correctness, its CVEs, or whether the chosen version is the \"right\" one. The principle — coherence plus a traceable reason for notable changes — decides, not a fixed file list: reason as an experienced reviewer about manifests, lockfiles and ecosystems not named here. A patch or minor bump and a transitive-only delta need no prose. A large generated lockfile delta is normal evidence of coherence, not a problem. A supported version range is not itself a defect: libraries may deliberately declare compatible ranges, and a manifest edit need not change an already compatible locked resolution.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the change edits no external dependency: no added, removed or changed declaration, version constraint, source, resolution or pin of a third-party package, library, framework, plugin, container image or workflow action, in a manifest or lockfile of any ecosystem. The changed lines decide, not the file''s name. Internal wiring alone — an internal target or module, a test target that depends on the app, a scheme or test-plan entry, build settings, sources or resources — declares no external dependency and is NOT_APPLICABLE, even inside a dependency manifest. A resolution change on its own, such as a lockfile refresh, is an occasion. A deciding hunk that failed acquisition is a collection gap, not NOT_APPLICABLE.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`) and the file spread (`work/change/files.json`): the manifest hunks and the matching or missing lockfile spans. They establish what changed on the dependency surface and whether manifest and lockfile move together. They never establish the dependency''s quality or the correctness of the chosen version.\n2. The title (`context/metadata.json`) and the whole description (`context/description.md`): where an explaining sentence for a notable new declaration or bump may live. An image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n3. The commit subjects and bodies (`context/commits.json`): a subject can carry the reason. Per-commit patches are not in your context; judge from subjects, the file spread and the quoted hunks.\n4. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the repository''s documented policy — a stated reproducibility requirement, a lockfile rule, a pinning convention. It establishes the policy a constraint removal is measured against. It never establishes a defect on its own.\nBefore calling a notable change unexplained you must have read the diff, the title, the body and the commit subjects. If one of those surfaces was not materialized for you, your search is incomplete: report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: the dependency edit is coherent and, where a notable new declaration or bump needs one, explained. A new direct external declaration arrives with a sentence on what it is for and any required resolution update is present; a routine transitive resolution refresh is internally consistent.\n- NOT_MET: the captured manifest and lockfile contradict one another under the ecosystem''s resolution rules, with the deciding entries quoted; or a resolution constraint is removed in conflict with an evidenced repository reproducibility requirement. Establish the ecosystem''s semantics and the captured policy before reporting inconsistency.\n- NOT_MET: a notable new declaration or version bump is coherent but lands as an unexplained pin — no quotable why in the manifest context, the title, the body or the commit subjects. Name the pin and the surfaces you read.\n- UNDETERMINED when the deciding policy or resolution context remains ambiguous after reading the sources; name the question. An unchanged or absent lockfile alone proves neither coherence nor a defect. A required source that was not captured is a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review. Identical hunks yield identical assessments.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on dependency deliberateness alone — a non-blocking practice to grow, not a merge gate. Scale to how much reproducibility is actually at risk. MINOR, the usual band: an unexplained but coherent notable pin — a formative nudge. MAJOR: manifest and lockfile out of step on something that ships — the most serious case here.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the manifest line, the matching or missing lockfile span, and the explaining sentence or commit subject verbatim. For NOT_MET the citations show the pin and the boundary searched: the diff, the title, the body, the commit subjects. Never invent a file, a reason, a CVE, or a clean bill you did not verify. Never claim a build resolved or ran. Describe the observable edit; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nArtifact hygiene about generated churn belongs to `excludes-generated-and-build-artifacts`; review size belongs to `scope-one-reviewable-change`. Rationale for a separate architectural decision belongs to `records-significant-decisions-with-rationale`. Here only: is the dependency edit coherent, and is a notable change explained?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for changes-dependencies-deliberately. Surfaces candidates only — the LLM judges whether a\n// dependency edit was deliberate (intentional add/remove/bump with an appropriate constraint) or careless\n// (loosened pin, dropped version, no lockfile). General by design: a per-manifest pattern table keyed off\n// the file basename, NOT a single ecosystem. Adding an ecosystem = adding a row, no engine change.\n//\n// The table reads changed lines, not manifest sections, so a match is a CANDIDATE dependency line: a\n// package.json `engines` entry or a Cargo `[package]` `rust-version` has a dependency line''s shape too, and\n// only the section around it says which it is. We pair an added line against a removed line for the SAME\n// name and label the constraint delta between the two lines:\n//   ONLY_ADDED    — the name matches only on a + line (new, or moved or reformatted from outside the diff)\n//   ONLY_REMOVED  — the name matches only on a - line (dropped, or moved or reformatted)\n//   PIN_LOOSENED  — exact/narrower constraint became a range/caret/tilde (==1.2.3 -> >=1.2, 1.2.3 -> ^1.2.3)\n//   PIN_DROPPED   — a version constraint was present and is now entirely absent\n//   BUMPED        — same constraint shape, different version value\n// Manifests that spread one dependency over several lines (Maven, XcodeGen) get no such label; their changed\n// dependency lines are surfaced as raw leads.\nimport { globFilesSync } from \"../lib/files.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// ecosystem key -> how to recognise its manifest + lockfile, and how to read a \"name => constraint\" line.\ninterface Ecosystem {\n\t// manifest basename test (lowercased)\n\tisManifest: (base: string) => boolean;\n\t// lockfile basenames this ecosystem writes\n\tlockfiles: string[];\n\t// extract { name, constraint } from a single manifest line, or null if it lacks a dependency line''s shape.\n\tparse: (line: string) => { name: string; constraint: string } | null;\n\t// For a manifest that spreads one dependency over several lines: the changed lines to surface raw,\n\t// in place of parse().\n\tdependencyLine?: RegExp;\n}\n\n// A constraint is \"loose\" if it admits more than one version: range operators, caret, tilde, wildcard, or empty.\nconst LOOSE = /(?:^$|[\\^~*]|>=|<=|>|<|\\.x\\b|\\bx\\b|\\|\\||\\s-\\s|,)/u;\n// A constraint is \"exact\" if it pins a single version: leading ==, =, or a bare semver-ish token.\nconst EXACT = /^(?:==?|v)?\\d/u;\n\nfunction isLoose(c: string): boolean {\n\tconst t = c.trim();\n\tif (t === \"\" || t === \"*\") {\n\t\treturn true;\n\t}\n\tif (EXACT.test(t) && !LOOSE.test(t.replace(/^==?/u, \"\"))) {\n\t\treturn false;\n\t}\n\treturn LOOSE.test(t);\n}\n\n// A package.json string value LOOKS like a dependency constraint (semver/range/protocol) rather than a\n// script body, an `engines`/`exports`/`resolutions`/`config` value, etc. The per-line JSON parser cannot\n// see which object block a line sits in, so this shape guard keeps `\"build\": \"tsc\"` or `\"./dist\": \"...\"`\n// out of the dependency tally. A bare \"*\"/\"x\"/\"latest\" and the npm pseudo-protocols (workspace:/npm:/\n// file:/link:/git/http) are real dependency specifiers and are admitted.\nconst VERSIONISH =\n\t/^(?:[\\^~>=<* v]|\\d|x\\b|latest$|workspace:|npm:|file:|link:|git[+:]|https?:|github:|gitlab:|bitbucket:)/iu;\nfunction isVersionish(c: string): boolean {\n\treturn VERSIONISH.test(c.trim());\n}\n\n// --- per-ecosystem line parsers (kept deliberately small + tolerant; the LLM reads full context) ---\n\n// JSON \"name\": \"constraint\"  (package.json dependency blocks)\nconst reJsonDep = /^\\s*\"(?<name>[^\"]+)\"\\s*:\\s*\"(?<constraint>[^\"]*)\"\\s*,?\\s*$/u;\n// Well-known package.json scalar keys that are NOT dependencies — skip so we don''t emit a \"dep\" hint for the\n// package''s own name/version/etc. Generic to npm manifests, not repo-specific.\nconst NPM_NON_DEP_KEYS = new Set([\n\t\"name\",\n\t\"version\",\n\t\"description\",\n\t\"license\",\n\t\"main\",\n\t\"module\",\n\t\"type\",\n\t\"types\",\n\t\"typings\",\n\t\"homepage\",\n\t\"author\",\n\t\"private\",\n\t\"packagemanager\",\n\t\"bin\",\n]);\n// TOML name = \"constraint\"  or  name = { version = \"constraint\" }\nconst reTomlDep =\n\t/^\\s*(?<name>[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)\\s*=\\s*(?:\"(?<quoted>[^\"]*)\"|\\{[^}]*version\\s*=\\s*\"(?<table>[^\"]*)\"[^}]*\\})/u;\n// requirements.txt  name==1.2.3 / name>=1,<2 / name\nconst reReqDep = /^\\s*(?<name>[A-Za-z0-9_.\\-[\\]]+)\\s*(?<constraint>(?:[<>=!~]=?|@)\\S.*)?$/u;\n// Gemfile  gem \"name\", \"~> 1.2\"\nconst reGemDep = /^\\s*gem\\s+[\"''](?<name>[^\"'']+)[\"'']\\s*(?:,\\s*[\"''](?<constraint>[^\"'']*)[\"''])?/u;\n// Gradle  implementation(\"group:name:1.2.3\")  or  implementation ''group:name:1.2.3''\nconst reGradleDep = /[\"''](?<coordinate>[\\w.-]+:[\\w.-]+):(?<constraint>[^\"'']*)[\"'']/u;\n// Swift PM  .package(url: \"...\", from: \"1.2.3\") / exact: \"1.2.3\" / \"1.0.0\"...\"2.0.0\"\nconst reSwiftPkg =\n\t/\\.package\\(\\s*url:\\s*[\"''](?<url>[^\"'']+)[\"''][^)]*?(?:from:\\s*[\"''](?<from>[^\"'']+)[\"'']|exact:\\s*[\"''](?<exact>[^\"'']+)[\"'']|[\"''](?<low>[^\"'']+)[\"'']\\s*\\.\\.[.<]\\s*[\"''](?<high>[^\"'']+)[\"''])/u;\n// go.mod  require module v1.2.3  (single-line or block-body line)\nconst reGoMod =\n\t/^\\s*(?:require\\s+)?(?<name>[\\w./-]+\\.[\\w./-]+\\/\\S+|[\\w.-]+\\/\\S+)\\s+(?<version>v\\d\\S*)/u;\n\n/** A Swift PM constraint as one word: `from:`, `exact:`, a range, or nothing when the package pins none. */\nfunction swiftConstraint(\n\tfromVersion: string | undefined,\n\texactVersion: string | undefined,\n\trangeLow: string | undefined,\n\trangeHigh: string | undefined,\n): string {\n\tif (fromVersion !== undefined && fromVersion !== \"\") {\n\t\treturn `from:${fromVersion}`;\n\t}\n\tif (exactVersion !== undefined && exactVersion !== \"\") {\n\t\treturn `exact:${exactVersion}`;\n\t}\n\tif (rangeLow !== undefined && rangeLow !== \"\" && rangeHigh !== undefined && rangeHigh !== \"\") {\n\t\treturn `${rangeLow}..${rangeHigh}`;\n\t}\n\treturn \"\";\n}\n\n// Cargo.toml and pyproject.toml read dependency lines identically. The two version groups are the two\n// arms of an alternation — `name = \"1.2\"` fills the first, `name = { version = \"1.2\" }` the second, and\n// an inline table with no version key (`name = { features = [...] }`) fills neither.\nfunction parseTomlDependency(line: string): { name: string; constraint: string } | null {\n\tconst [, name, quotedVersion, tableVersion] = reTomlDep.exec(line) ?? [];\n\tif (name === undefined) {\n\t\treturn null;\n\t}\n\treturn { name, constraint: quotedVersion ?? tableVersion ?? \"\" };\n}\n\nconst ECOSYSTEMS: Ecosystem[] = [\n\t{\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"package.json\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"package-lock.json\", \"yarn.lock\", \"pnpm-lock.yaml\", \"npm-shrinkwrap.json\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\t// The value group matches empty (`\"dep\": \"\"`); the key group cannot, so its absence means the\n\t\t\t// line is not a `\"key\": \"value\"` pair at all.\n\t\t\tconst [, name, constraint = \"\"] = reJsonDep.exec(line) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (name === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// Skip the package''s own scalar fields (name/version/etc.) so we only surface dependency-block edits.\n\t\t\tif (NPM_NON_DEP_KEYS.has(name.toLowerCase())) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// Per-line parsing can''t tell a dependency block from scripts/engines/exports/resolutions/config —\n\t\t\t// they all share the `\"key\": \"value\"` shape. Require the value to look like a version specifier so a\n\t\t\t// `\"build\": \"tsc\"` line is not offered as a candidate at all.\n\t\t\tif (!isVersionish(constraint)) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn { name, constraint };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Rust Cargo\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"cargo.toml\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"Cargo.lock\"],\n\t\tparse: parseTomlDependency,\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Python requirements\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"requirements.txt\" || b.endsWith(\".requirements.txt\"),\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"requirements.lock\", \"Pipfile.lock\", \"poetry.lock\", \"uv.lock\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\tconst t = line.trim();\n\t\t\tif (t === \"\" || t.startsWith(\"#\") || t.startsWith(\"-\")) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// A bare `numpy` line has no constraint group at all — that absence is the PIN_DROPPED signal\n\t\t\t// this script exists to surface, so it becomes \"\" rather than dropping the dependency.\n\t\t\tconst [, name, constraint = \"\"] = reReqDep.exec(t) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (name === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn { name, constraint };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Python pyproject (PEP 621 / poetry tables)\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"pyproject.toml\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"poetry.lock\", \"uv.lock\", \"pdm.lock\"],\n\t\tparse: parseTomlDependency,\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Ruby Bundler\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"gemfile\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"Gemfile.lock\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\t// `gem \"puma\"` carries no constraint group — an absent pin, not an absent dependency.\n\t\t\tconst [, name, constraint = \"\"] = reGemDep.exec(line) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (name === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn { name, constraint };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Maven and XcodeGen spread one dependency over several lines — an artifactId and its version, a\n\t\t// url and its bound — and a diff carries only the lines that changed, so the unchanged half may sit\n\t\t// outside it. No added, removed or pin label is read from such a subset.\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"pom.xml\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [],\n\t\tparse: () => null,\n\t\tdependencyLine: /<(?:artifactId|version)>/u,\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Gradle (Groovy or Kotlin DSL)\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"build.gradle\" || b === \"build.gradle.kts\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"gradle.lockfile\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\t// The version group matches empty for a trailing-colon coordinate (`\"g:a:\"`); the coordinate\n\t\t\t// group cannot be empty.\n\t\t\tconst [, coordinate, constraint = \"\"] = reGradleDep.exec(line) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (coordinate === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn { name: coordinate, constraint };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Swift Package Manager\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"package.swift\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"Package.resolved\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\t// The url group is required; the three constraint forms are alternatives, so at most one of\n\t\t\t// them is present on any given line and the range form always yields BOTH of its bounds.\n\t\t\tconst [, url, fromVersion, exactVersion, rangeLow, rangeHigh] = reSwiftPkg.exec(line) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (url === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst name =\n\t\t\t\turl\n\t\t\t\t\t.split(\"/\")\n\t\t\t\t\t.pop()\n\t\t\t\t\t?.replace(/\\.git$/u, \"\") ?? url;\n\t\t\t// from: => caret-like (loose), exact: => exact, range => loose\n\t\t\tconst constraint = swiftConstraint(fromVersion, exactVersion, rangeLow, rangeHigh);\n\t\t\treturn { name, constraint };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// XcodeGen project.yml: a package is declared under `packages:` as a url line followed by a\n\t\t// constraint line, so it is read as Maven is.\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"project.yml\" || b === \"project.yaml\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"Package.resolved\"],\n\t\tparse: () => null,\n\t\tdependencyLine:\n\t\t\t/^\\s*(?:url|from|exactVersion|majorVersion|minorVersion|branch|revision):\\s*\\S/u,\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Go modules\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"go.mod\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"go.sum\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\t// Both groups are required: a go.mod require line without a `v…` version is not matched at all.\n\t\t\tconst [, name, version] = reGoMod.exec(line) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (name === undefined || version === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn { name, constraint: version };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n];\n\nfunction basenameLower(path: string): string {\n\treturn (path.split(\"/\").pop() ?? path).toLowerCase();\n}\n\n// Escape a dependency name for safe embedding in a RegExp (names can contain ., -, /, @ and similar).\nfunction escapeRegExp(s: string): string {\n\treturn s.replaceAll(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/gu, String.raw`\\$&`);\n}\n\nfunction ecosystemFor(path: string): Ecosystem | null {\n\tconst base = basenameLower(path);\n\treturn ECOSYSTEMS.find((e) => e.isManifest(base)) ?? null;\n}\n\n// Classify the constraint delta for a dependency present on both sides (added + removed for same name).\nfunction classifyDelta(oldC: string, newC: string): string {\n\tconst o = oldC.trim();\n\tconst n = newC.trim();\n\t// Identical constraint text on both sides means the -X/+X pair differs only by whitespace/newline (e.g. a\n\t// trailing-newline reflow): there is no version change, so surface a neutral no-change fact rather than a\n\t// phantom version bump. UNCHANGED is informational only and is never counted toward the bump tally.\n\tif (o === n) {\n\t\treturn \"UNCHANGED\";\n\t}\n\tconst oExact = !isLoose(o);\n\tconst nLoose = isLoose(n);\n\tif (n === \"\" && o !== \"\") {\n\t\treturn \"PIN_DROPPED\";\n\t}\n\tif (oExact && nLoose) {\n\t\treturn \"PIN_LOOSENED\";\n\t}\n\treturn \"BUMPED\";\n}\n\n// Collect { name -> constraint } from added/removed manifest lines for a single ecosystem file.\nfunction collectDeps(df: DiffFile, eco: Ecosystem, side: \"added\" | \"removed\"): Map<string, string> {\n\tconst out = new Map<string, string>();\n\tconst lines = side === \"added\" ? df.addedLines : df.removedLines;\n\tfor (const [, content] of lines) {\n\t\tconst parsed = eco.parse(content);\n\t\tif (parsed) {\n\t\t\tout.set(parsed.name, parsed.constraint);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn out;\n}\n\n/** How many captured paths a direction names before it only counts the rest. */\nconst PATHS_NAMED = 5;\n\nfunction namedPaths(paths: readonly string[]): string {\n\tconst named = paths.slice(0, PATHS_NAMED).join(\", \");\n\treturn paths.length > PATHS_NAMED ? `${named} and ${paths.length - PATHS_NAMED} more` : named;\n}\n\n/** The hint''s context line: the matched name and constraint on its side, or the delta between the two. */\nfunction dependencyContext(fact: string, name: string, oldC: string, newC: string): string {\n\tif (fact === \"ONLY_ADDED\") {\n\t\treturn `+ ${name} ${newC}`.trim();\n\t}\n\treturn fact === \"ONLY_REMOVED\" ? `- ${name} ${oldC}`.trim() : `${name}: ${oldC} -> ${newC}`;\n}\n\nexport default function changesDependenciesDeliberately(\n\trepoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst changedManifests = new Set<string>();\n\tconst touchedLockfiles = new Set<string>();\n\tlet onlyAdded = 0;\n\tlet onlyRemoved = 0;\n\tlet pinsLoosened = 0;\n\tlet pinsDropped = 0;\n\tlet bumped = 0;\n\tlet unpairedManifestLines = 0;\n\n\t// Where the checkout holds a file with a lockfile''s name. A path anywhere in the repository is not\n\t// evidence that it belongs to a changed manifest; the paths are reported for the review to relate.\n\tconst allLockfileNames = new Set(\n\t\tECOSYSTEMS.flatMap((e) => e.lockfiles.map((l) => l.toLowerCase())),\n\t);\n\tconst checkoutLockfiles: string[] = [];\n\tfor (const ext of [\"json\", \"lock\", \"yaml\", \"resolved\", \"lockfile\", \"sum\"]) {\n\t\tfor (const f of globFilesSync(`**/*.${ext}`, repoPath)) {\n\t\t\tif (allLockfileNames.has(basenameLower(f))) {\n\t\t\t\tcheckoutLockfiles.push(f);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst base = basenameLower(path);\n\t\tif (allLockfileNames.has(base)) {\n\t\t\ttouchedLockfiles.add(path);\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst eco = ecosystemFor(path);\n\t\tif (!eco) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tchangedManifests.add(path);\n\n\t\tconst { dependencyLine } = eco;\n\t\tif (dependencyLine !== undefined) {\n\t\t\tfor (const side of [\"removed\", \"added\"] as const) {\n\t\t\t\tconst lines = [...(side === \"added\" ? df.addedLines : df.removedLines)];\n\t\t\t\tfor (const [ln, content] of lines.filter(([, c]) => dependencyLine.test(c))) {\n\t\t\t\t\tunpairedManifestLines += 1;\n\t\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tline: ln,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tpattern: \"candidate:raw manifest line\",\n\t\t\t\t\t\tcontext: `${side === \"added\" ? \"+\" : \"-\"} ${content.trim()}`.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tflags: { ecosystem: base, side },\n\t\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst added = collectDeps(df, eco, \"added\");\n\t\tconst removed = collectDeps(df, eco, \"removed\");\n\n\t\t// helper to find the diff line for a dependency name on a given side (for hint placement). Match on a\n\t\t// quote/word/coordinate boundary, not a bare substring, so a prefix-sharing sibling (react vs\n\t\t// react-dom, or a scoped name appearing inside another package''s URL) doesn''t grab the wrong line.\n\t\tconst lineFor = (name: string, side: \"added\" | \"removed\"): number => {\n\t\t\tconst lines = side === \"added\" ? df.addedLines : df.removedLines;\n\t\t\tconst bounded = new RegExp(`(?:^|[^\\\\w.\\\\-/])${escapeRegExp(name)}(?:[^\\\\w.\\\\-/]|$)`, \"u\");\n\t\t\tfor (const [ln, content] of lines) {\n\t\t\t\tif (bounded.test(content)) {\n\t\t\t\t\treturn ln;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// Fallback: a constructed key (e.g. a Gradle group:name coordinate) may not survive the boundary\n\t\t\t// test against the raw line — keep the substring scan so the hint still lands on a real line.\n\t\t\tfor (const [ln, content] of lines) {\n\t\t\t\tif (content.includes(name)) {\n\t\t\t\t\treturn ln;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn 0;\n\t\t};\n\n\t\tconst allNames = new Set<string>([...added.keys(), ...removed.keys()]);\n\t\tfor (const name of allNames) {\n\t\t\tconst inAdded = added.has(name);\n\t\t\tconst inRemoved = removed.has(name);\n\t\t\tlet fact: string;\n\t\t\tlet side: \"added\" | \"removed\" = \"added\";\n\t\t\tif (inAdded && !inRemoved) {\n\t\t\t\tfact = \"ONLY_ADDED\";\n\t\t\t\tonlyAdded += 1;\n\t\t\t} else if (!inAdded && inRemoved) {\n\t\t\t\tfact = \"ONLY_REMOVED\";\n\t\t\t\tside = \"removed\";\n\t\t\t\tonlyRemoved += 1;\n\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\tfact = classifyDelta(removed.get(name) ?? \"\", added.get(name) ?? \"\");\n\t\t\t\tif (fact === \"PIN_LOOSENED\") {\n\t\t\t\t\tpinsLoosened += 1;\n\t\t\t\t} else if (fact === \"PIN_DROPPED\") {\n\t\t\t\t\tpinsDropped += 1;\n\t\t\t\t} else if (fact === \"UNCHANGED\") {\n\t\t\t\t\t// whitespace/newline-only line pair — not a version change, count nothing\n\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\tbumped += 1;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst oldC = removed.get(name) ?? \"\";\n\t\t\tconst newC = added.get(name) ?? \"\";\n\t\t\tconst ctx = dependencyContext(fact, name, oldC, newC);\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\tline: lineFor(name, side),\n\t\t\t\tpattern: `candidate:${fact}`,\n\t\t\t\tcontext: ctx.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\tflags: { ecosystem: base, name },\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tconst lockfilePresent = checkoutLockfiles.length > 0 || touchedLockfiles.size > 0;\n\tconst listed = hints.slice(0, 40);\n\tconst rawLinesListed = listed.filter((h) => h.pattern === \"candidate:raw manifest line\").length;\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (changedManifests.size > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Manifest-named file(s) changed (${changedManifests.size}). The name alone is no occasion: a changed external dependency declaration, resolution, source or pin inside one is, and internal target, test-target, scheme or build-setting wiring alone is not. Candidate lines, matched by shape and not yet placed in a dependency section: ${onlyAdded} only on an added line, ${onlyRemoved} only on a removed line, ${pinsLoosened} pin(s) loosened, ${pinsDropped} pin(s) dropped, ${bumped} version bump(s). A metadata field such as a package.json engines entry or a Cargo rust-version has the same shape, and a name on one side only may be a moved or reformatted line rather than a new or dropped dependency — read the section around each before treating it as a dependency change${unpairedManifestLines > 0 ? `; the ${unpairedManifestLines} raw multi-line manifest line(s) are not in these counts, so a zero here does not show that nothing changed` : \"\"}. Then investigate whether each dependency constraint change is deliberate and appropriately bounded.`,\n\t\t);\n\t\tif (hints.length === 0) {\n\t\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\t\"No changed line in these manifests has the dependency shape this script reads. Read the changed sections before deciding: a declaration spread over lines can escape a per-line reading, while internal wiring such as a target dependency, a test target or a scheme entry declares no external dependency.\",\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (unpairedManifestLines > 0) {\n\t\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\t`${unpairedManifestLines} changed dependency line(s) in a pom.xml or XcodeGen project.yml carry no added, removed or pin label${rawLinesListed < unpairedManifestLines ? `, and only ${rawLinesListed} of them are listed as raw lines here — read the manifest diffs for the other ${unpairedManifestLines - rawLinesListed}` : \" and are listed as raw lines\"}: these manifests spread one dependency over several lines and the diff shows only the changed ones. Read the manifest around each to tell an addition from a version change or a reordering; a pom.xml coordinate may also be a build plugin or a parent/BOM rather than a dependency.`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (lockfilePresent) {\n\t\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\t`Lockfile-named file(s) in the checkout: ${checkoutLockfiles.length === 0 ? \"none\" : namedPaths(checkoutLockfiles)}; touched in this diff: ${touchedLockfiles.size === 0 ? \"none\" : namedPaths([...touchedLockfiles])}. A lockfile answers for a changed manifest when the ecosystem resolves that manifest through it — beside it, or at a parent workspace root whose configuration includes it — so read the paths and that configuration before expecting a matching update.`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\t\"No file with a lockfile name these ecosystems write was found in the checkout or touched in the diff — investigate whether the ecosystem expects a committed lockfile to make the resolved versions reproducible.\",\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: listed,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tmanifestsChanged: changedManifests.size,\n\t\t\tonlyAdded,\n\t\t\tonlyRemoved,\n\t\t\tpinsLoosened,\n\t\t\tpinsDropped,\n\t\t\tbumped,\n\t\t\tunpairedManifestLines,\n\t\t\trawLinesListed,\n\t\t\tlockfilesTouched: touchedLockfiles.size,\n\t\t\tlockfilePresent: lockfilePresent ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Every dependency you add is code you now trust and have to keep patched, and many malicious packages slip in disguised as a real name a letter or two off. A one-line note on why a package is there, plus a lockfile that moves with the manifest, lets a reviewer sanity-check the addition and keeps the build reproducible.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A new package arrives with a sentence on what it is for. The manifest and resolution stay consistent with the ecosystem and the repository policy, updating a lockfile when needed rather than requiring one for every edit.","groupSlug":"secure-by-default-changes","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 140\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Unexplained package</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">PR: Add yaml-lite</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Why: Add yaml-lite</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer guesses why</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Explained package</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">PR: Add yaml-lite</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Why: Preserve config comments</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer can check</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A pull request that adds yaml-lite gives only Add yaml-lite as its reason, so the reviewer guesses why. The same pull request says Preserve config comments, so the reviewer can check."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you add a package, check whether you need it.\n- Before you add a package, check that its name is exactly the one you meant.\n- When you add a direct dependency or make a notable version change, write one sentence on why. Say what the package does in this change, or why its version changes. For example, instead of “Add yaml-lite”, write “Add yaml-lite to keep comments in plugin config files”.\n- Put that sentence where a reviewer reads it. Use the description, a commit subject, or a comment in the manifest, the file that lists packages.\n- Keep the manifest and any lockfile (the file of chosen versions) consistent with your package manager and repository policy. A compatible lockfile can stay unchanged.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change does not add, remove or change a third-party dependency, its version, its source or its fixed version. Dependencies include packages, libraries, frameworks, plugins, container images and workflow actions.\n- Your change only wires up your own code. Examples are an internal module, a test target, build settings, sources or resources. This is true even when the edit is inside a dependency manifest.\n\nIt still applies when your change only refreshes a lockfile.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Writing a reason for every version change. A patch or minor version change needs no sentence, and neither does a change to the dependencies of your dependencies. A large lockfile diff from your tool is normal. Instead, explain each new direct dependency and each notable version change.\n- Giving the package name as the reason. “Add yaml-lite” or “Update yaml-lite to 2.0” only says what changed. This is true even when the name hints at what the package does. Instead, say what the package does in this change, or why its version changes.\n- Removing a fixed version or a lockfile entry to get past a conflict when your repository asks for reproducible builds. Instead, let your package manager resolve the conflict, and keep the lockfile consistent with the manifest.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Concise guide for evaluating open source software](https://best.openssf.org/Concise-Guide-for-Evaluating-Open-Source-Software), OpenSSF Best Practices Working Group, 2025. OpenSSF recommends checking whether you need a dependency and whether its name is authentic.\n- [Dependency management](https://abseil.io/resources/swe-book/html/ch21.html), Titus Winters, in Software Engineering at Google, 2020. A new dependency is not free, so weigh its costs before you import it.\n- [package-lock.json](https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v10/configuring-npm/package-lock-json), npm Docs. npm records exact dependency versions in a lockfile intended for version control.\n- [Why have Cargo.lock in version control?](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/faq.html#why-have-cargolock-in-version-control), The Cargo Book. A lockfile gives the same dependency versions at different times and on different systems.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Change dependencies deliberately","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: an external dependency edit, as the Occasion defines it, whose manifest and lockfile stay coherent under the ecosystem''s resolution rules, with a recorded reason for a notable new declaration or version bump.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether this pull request makes the external dependency edit defined by the Occasion deliberately. Two things hold together: the manifest and any lockfile remain consistent with the ecosystem''s resolution rules and the repository''s documented policy, and a notable new external declaration or version bump carries a one-line reason a reviewer can read — in the manifest context, the title or body, or a commit subject. The reason states the package''s purpose in this change or why its version changes. A package name or an edit label is not a reason, even when the name describes the package''s function. This is a supply-chain workflow practice about how a dependency edit is justified and kept coherent. It is never a claim about the dependency''s own correctness, its CVEs, or whether the chosen version is the \"right\" one. The principle — coherence plus a traceable reason for notable changes — decides, not a fixed file list: reason as an experienced reviewer about manifests, lockfiles and ecosystems not named here. A patch or minor bump and a transitive-only delta need no prose. A large generated lockfile delta is normal evidence of coherence, not a problem. A supported version range is not itself a defect: libraries may deliberately declare compatible ranges, and a manifest edit need not change an already compatible locked resolution.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the change edits no external dependency: no added, removed or changed declaration, version constraint, source, resolution or pin of a third-party package, library, framework, plugin, container image or workflow action, in a manifest or lockfile of any ecosystem. The changed lines decide, not the file''s name. Internal wiring alone — an internal target or module, a test target that depends on the app, a scheme or test-plan entry, build settings, sources or resources — declares no external dependency and is NOT_APPLICABLE, even inside a dependency manifest. A resolution change on its own, such as a lockfile refresh, is an occasion. A deciding hunk that failed acquisition is a collection gap, not NOT_APPLICABLE.\n\n## Sources\n1. The annotated diff (`work/change/diff.patch`) and the file spread (`work/change/files.json`): the manifest hunks and the matching or missing lockfile spans. They establish what changed on the dependency surface and whether manifest and lockfile move together. They never establish the dependency''s quality or the correctness of the chosen version.\n2. The title (`context/metadata.json`) and the whole description (`context/description.md`): where an explaining sentence for a notable new declaration or bump may live. An image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n3. The commit subjects and bodies (`context/commits.json`): a subject can carry the reason. Per-commit patches are not in your context; judge from subjects, the file spread and the quoted hunks.\n4. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the repository''s documented policy — a stated reproducibility requirement, a lockfile rule, a pinning convention. It establishes the policy a constraint removal is measured against. It never establishes a defect on its own.\nBefore calling a notable change unexplained you must have read the diff, the title, the body and the commit subjects. If one of those surfaces was not materialized for you, your search is incomplete: report a collection gap and record no observation.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: the dependency edit is coherent and, where a notable new declaration or bump needs one, explained. A new direct external declaration arrives with a sentence on what it is for and any required resolution update is present; a routine transitive resolution refresh is internally consistent.\n- NOT_MET: the captured manifest and lockfile contradict one another under the ecosystem''s resolution rules, with the deciding entries quoted; or a resolution constraint is removed in conflict with an evidenced repository reproducibility requirement. Establish the ecosystem''s semantics and the captured policy before reporting inconsistency.\n- NOT_MET: a notable new declaration or version bump is coherent but lands as an unexplained pin — no quotable why in the manifest context, the title, the body or the commit subjects. Name the pin and the surfaces you read.\n- UNDETERMINED when the deciding policy or resolution context remains ambiguous after reading the sources; name the question. An unchanged or absent lockfile alone proves neither coherence nor a defect. A required source that was not captured is a collection gap, not an observation.\nOne observation per review. Identical hunks yield identical assessments.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on dependency deliberateness alone — a non-blocking practice to grow, not a merge gate. Scale to how much reproducibility is actually at risk. MINOR, the usual band: an unexplained but coherent notable pin — a formative nudge. MAJOR: manifest and lockfile out of step on something that ships — the most serious case here.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the manifest line, the matching or missing lockfile span, and the explaining sentence or commit subject verbatim. For NOT_MET the citations show the pin and the boundary searched: the diff, the title, the body, the commit subjects. Never invent a file, a reason, a CVE, or a clean bill you did not verify. Never claim a build resolved or ran. Describe the observable edit; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nArtifact hygiene about generated churn belongs to `excludes-generated-and-build-artifacts`; review size belongs to `scope-one-reviewable-change`. Rationale for a separate architectural decision belongs to `records-significant-decisions-with-rationale`. Here only: is the dependency edit coherent, and is a notable change explained?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for changes-dependencies-deliberately. Surfaces candidates only — the LLM judges whether a\n// dependency edit was deliberate (intentional add/remove/bump with an appropriate constraint) or careless\n// (loosened pin, dropped version, no lockfile). General by design: a per-manifest pattern table keyed off\n// the file basename, NOT a single ecosystem. Adding an ecosystem = adding a row, no engine change.\n//\n// The table reads changed lines, not manifest sections, so a match is a CANDIDATE dependency line: a\n// package.json `engines` entry or a Cargo `[package]` `rust-version` has a dependency line''s shape too, and\n// only the section around it says which it is. We pair an added line against a removed line for the SAME\n// name and label the constraint delta between the two lines:\n//   ONLY_ADDED    — the name matches only on a + line (new, or moved or reformatted from outside the diff)\n//   ONLY_REMOVED  — the name matches only on a - line (dropped, or moved or reformatted)\n//   PIN_LOOSENED  — exact/narrower constraint became a range/caret/tilde (==1.2.3 -> >=1.2, 1.2.3 -> ^1.2.3)\n//   PIN_DROPPED   — a version constraint was present and is now entirely absent\n//   BUMPED        — same constraint shape, different version value\n// Manifests that spread one dependency over several lines (Maven, XcodeGen) get no such label; their changed\n// dependency lines are surfaced as raw leads.\nimport { globFilesSync } from \"../lib/files.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// ecosystem key -> how to recognise its manifest + lockfile, and how to read a \"name => constraint\" line.\ninterface Ecosystem {\n\t// manifest basename test (lowercased)\n\tisManifest: (base: string) => boolean;\n\t// lockfile basenames this ecosystem writes\n\tlockfiles: string[];\n\t// extract { name, constraint } from a single manifest line, or null if it lacks a dependency line''s shape.\n\tparse: (line: string) => { name: string; constraint: string } | null;\n\t// For a manifest that spreads one dependency over several lines: the changed lines to surface raw,\n\t// in place of parse().\n\tdependencyLine?: RegExp;\n}\n\n// A constraint is \"loose\" if it admits more than one version: range operators, caret, tilde, wildcard, or empty.\nconst LOOSE = /(?:^$|[\\^~*]|>=|<=|>|<|\\.x\\b|\\bx\\b|\\|\\||\\s-\\s|,)/u;\n// A constraint is \"exact\" if it pins a single version: leading ==, =, or a bare semver-ish token.\nconst EXACT = /^(?:==?|v)?\\d/u;\n\nfunction isLoose(c: string): boolean {\n\tconst t = c.trim();\n\tif (t === \"\" || t === \"*\") {\n\t\treturn true;\n\t}\n\tif (EXACT.test(t) && !LOOSE.test(t.replace(/^==?/u, \"\"))) {\n\t\treturn false;\n\t}\n\treturn LOOSE.test(t);\n}\n\n// A package.json string value LOOKS like a dependency constraint (semver/range/protocol) rather than a\n// script body, an `engines`/`exports`/`resolutions`/`config` value, etc. The per-line JSON parser cannot\n// see which object block a line sits in, so this shape guard keeps `\"build\": \"tsc\"` or `\"./dist\": \"...\"`\n// out of the dependency tally. A bare \"*\"/\"x\"/\"latest\" and the npm pseudo-protocols (workspace:/npm:/\n// file:/link:/git/http) are real dependency specifiers and are admitted.\nconst VERSIONISH =\n\t/^(?:[\\^~>=<* v]|\\d|x\\b|latest$|workspace:|npm:|file:|link:|git[+:]|https?:|github:|gitlab:|bitbucket:)/iu;\nfunction isVersionish(c: string): boolean {\n\treturn VERSIONISH.test(c.trim());\n}\n\n// --- per-ecosystem line parsers (kept deliberately small + tolerant; the LLM reads full context) ---\n\n// JSON \"name\": \"constraint\"  (package.json dependency blocks)\nconst reJsonDep = /^\\s*\"(?<name>[^\"]+)\"\\s*:\\s*\"(?<constraint>[^\"]*)\"\\s*,?\\s*$/u;\n// Well-known package.json scalar keys that are NOT dependencies — skip so we don''t emit a \"dep\" hint for the\n// package''s own name/version/etc. Generic to npm manifests, not repo-specific.\nconst NPM_NON_DEP_KEYS = new Set([\n\t\"name\",\n\t\"version\",\n\t\"description\",\n\t\"license\",\n\t\"main\",\n\t\"module\",\n\t\"type\",\n\t\"types\",\n\t\"typings\",\n\t\"homepage\",\n\t\"author\",\n\t\"private\",\n\t\"packagemanager\",\n\t\"bin\",\n]);\n// TOML name = \"constraint\"  or  name = { version = \"constraint\" }\nconst reTomlDep =\n\t/^\\s*(?<name>[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)\\s*=\\s*(?:\"(?<quoted>[^\"]*)\"|\\{[^}]*version\\s*=\\s*\"(?<table>[^\"]*)\"[^}]*\\})/u;\n// requirements.txt  name==1.2.3 / name>=1,<2 / name\nconst reReqDep = /^\\s*(?<name>[A-Za-z0-9_.\\-[\\]]+)\\s*(?<constraint>(?:[<>=!~]=?|@)\\S.*)?$/u;\n// Gemfile  gem \"name\", \"~> 1.2\"\nconst reGemDep = /^\\s*gem\\s+[\"''](?<name>[^\"'']+)[\"'']\\s*(?:,\\s*[\"''](?<constraint>[^\"'']*)[\"''])?/u;\n// Gradle  implementation(\"group:name:1.2.3\")  or  implementation ''group:name:1.2.3''\nconst reGradleDep = /[\"''](?<coordinate>[\\w.-]+:[\\w.-]+):(?<constraint>[^\"'']*)[\"'']/u;\n// Swift PM  .package(url: \"...\", from: \"1.2.3\") / exact: \"1.2.3\" / \"1.0.0\"...\"2.0.0\"\nconst reSwiftPkg =\n\t/\\.package\\(\\s*url:\\s*[\"''](?<url>[^\"'']+)[\"''][^)]*?(?:from:\\s*[\"''](?<from>[^\"'']+)[\"'']|exact:\\s*[\"''](?<exact>[^\"'']+)[\"'']|[\"''](?<low>[^\"'']+)[\"'']\\s*\\.\\.[.<]\\s*[\"''](?<high>[^\"'']+)[\"''])/u;\n// go.mod  require module v1.2.3  (single-line or block-body line)\nconst reGoMod =\n\t/^\\s*(?:require\\s+)?(?<name>[\\w./-]+\\.[\\w./-]+\\/\\S+|[\\w.-]+\\/\\S+)\\s+(?<version>v\\d\\S*)/u;\n\n/** A Swift PM constraint as one word: `from:`, `exact:`, a range, or nothing when the package pins none. */\nfunction swiftConstraint(\n\tfromVersion: string | undefined,\n\texactVersion: string | undefined,\n\trangeLow: string | undefined,\n\trangeHigh: string | undefined,\n): string {\n\tif (fromVersion !== undefined && fromVersion !== \"\") {\n\t\treturn `from:${fromVersion}`;\n\t}\n\tif (exactVersion !== undefined && exactVersion !== \"\") {\n\t\treturn `exact:${exactVersion}`;\n\t}\n\tif (rangeLow !== undefined && rangeLow !== \"\" && rangeHigh !== undefined && rangeHigh !== \"\") {\n\t\treturn `${rangeLow}..${rangeHigh}`;\n\t}\n\treturn \"\";\n}\n\n// Cargo.toml and pyproject.toml read dependency lines identically. The two version groups are the two\n// arms of an alternation — `name = \"1.2\"` fills the first, `name = { version = \"1.2\" }` the second, and\n// an inline table with no version key (`name = { features = [...] }`) fills neither.\nfunction parseTomlDependency(line: string): { name: string; constraint: string } | null {\n\tconst [, name, quotedVersion, tableVersion] = reTomlDep.exec(line) ?? [];\n\tif (name === undefined) {\n\t\treturn null;\n\t}\n\treturn { name, constraint: quotedVersion ?? tableVersion ?? \"\" };\n}\n\nconst ECOSYSTEMS: Ecosystem[] = [\n\t{\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"package.json\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"package-lock.json\", \"yarn.lock\", \"pnpm-lock.yaml\", \"npm-shrinkwrap.json\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\t// The value group matches empty (`\"dep\": \"\"`); the key group cannot, so its absence means the\n\t\t\t// line is not a `\"key\": \"value\"` pair at all.\n\t\t\tconst [, name, constraint = \"\"] = reJsonDep.exec(line) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (name === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// Skip the package''s own scalar fields (name/version/etc.) so we only surface dependency-block edits.\n\t\t\tif (NPM_NON_DEP_KEYS.has(name.toLowerCase())) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// Per-line parsing can''t tell a dependency block from scripts/engines/exports/resolutions/config —\n\t\t\t// they all share the `\"key\": \"value\"` shape. Require the value to look like a version specifier so a\n\t\t\t// `\"build\": \"tsc\"` line is not offered as a candidate at all.\n\t\t\tif (!isVersionish(constraint)) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn { name, constraint };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Rust Cargo\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"cargo.toml\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"Cargo.lock\"],\n\t\tparse: parseTomlDependency,\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Python requirements\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"requirements.txt\" || b.endsWith(\".requirements.txt\"),\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"requirements.lock\", \"Pipfile.lock\", \"poetry.lock\", \"uv.lock\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\tconst t = line.trim();\n\t\t\tif (t === \"\" || t.startsWith(\"#\") || t.startsWith(\"-\")) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// A bare `numpy` line has no constraint group at all — that absence is the PIN_DROPPED signal\n\t\t\t// this script exists to surface, so it becomes \"\" rather than dropping the dependency.\n\t\t\tconst [, name, constraint = \"\"] = reReqDep.exec(t) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (name === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn { name, constraint };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Python pyproject (PEP 621 / poetry tables)\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"pyproject.toml\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"poetry.lock\", \"uv.lock\", \"pdm.lock\"],\n\t\tparse: parseTomlDependency,\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Ruby Bundler\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"gemfile\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"Gemfile.lock\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\t// `gem \"puma\"` carries no constraint group — an absent pin, not an absent dependency.\n\t\t\tconst [, name, constraint = \"\"] = reGemDep.exec(line) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (name === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn { name, constraint };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Maven and XcodeGen spread one dependency over several lines — an artifactId and its version, a\n\t\t// url and its bound — and a diff carries only the lines that changed, so the unchanged half may sit\n\t\t// outside it. No added, removed or pin label is read from such a subset.\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"pom.xml\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [],\n\t\tparse: () => null,\n\t\tdependencyLine: /<(?:artifactId|version)>/u,\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Gradle (Groovy or Kotlin DSL)\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"build.gradle\" || b === \"build.gradle.kts\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"gradle.lockfile\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\t// The version group matches empty for a trailing-colon coordinate (`\"g:a:\"`); the coordinate\n\t\t\t// group cannot be empty.\n\t\t\tconst [, coordinate, constraint = \"\"] = reGradleDep.exec(line) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (coordinate === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn { name: coordinate, constraint };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Swift Package Manager\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"package.swift\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"Package.resolved\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\t// The url group is required; the three constraint forms are alternatives, so at most one of\n\t\t\t// them is present on any given line and the range form always yields BOTH of its bounds.\n\t\t\tconst [, url, fromVersion, exactVersion, rangeLow, rangeHigh] = reSwiftPkg.exec(line) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (url === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst name =\n\t\t\t\turl\n\t\t\t\t\t.split(\"/\")\n\t\t\t\t\t.pop()\n\t\t\t\t\t?.replace(/\\.git$/u, \"\") ?? url;\n\t\t\t// from: => caret-like (loose), exact: => exact, range => loose\n\t\t\tconst constraint = swiftConstraint(fromVersion, exactVersion, rangeLow, rangeHigh);\n\t\t\treturn { name, constraint };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// XcodeGen project.yml: a package is declared under `packages:` as a url line followed by a\n\t\t// constraint line, so it is read as Maven is.\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"project.yml\" || b === \"project.yaml\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"Package.resolved\"],\n\t\tparse: () => null,\n\t\tdependencyLine:\n\t\t\t/^\\s*(?:url|from|exactVersion|majorVersion|minorVersion|branch|revision):\\s*\\S/u,\n\t},\n\t{\n\t\t// Go modules\n\t\tisManifest: (b) => b === \"go.mod\",\n\t\tlockfiles: [\"go.sum\"],\n\t\tparse: (line) => {\n\t\t\t// Both groups are required: a go.mod require line without a `v…` version is not matched at all.\n\t\t\tconst [, name, version] = reGoMod.exec(line) ?? [];\n\t\t\tif (name === undefined || version === undefined) {\n\t\t\t\treturn null;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn { name, constraint: version };\n\t\t},\n\t},\n];\n\nfunction basenameLower(path: string): string {\n\treturn (path.split(\"/\").pop() ?? path).toLowerCase();\n}\n\n// Escape a dependency name for safe embedding in a RegExp (names can contain ., -, /, @ and similar).\nfunction escapeRegExp(s: string): string {\n\treturn s.replaceAll(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/gu, String.raw`\\$&`);\n}\n\nfunction ecosystemFor(path: string): Ecosystem | null {\n\tconst base = basenameLower(path);\n\treturn ECOSYSTEMS.find((e) => e.isManifest(base)) ?? null;\n}\n\n// Classify the constraint delta for a dependency present on both sides (added + removed for same name).\nfunction classifyDelta(oldC: string, newC: string): string {\n\tconst o = oldC.trim();\n\tconst n = newC.trim();\n\t// Identical constraint text on both sides means the -X/+X pair differs only by whitespace/newline (e.g. a\n\t// trailing-newline reflow): there is no version change, so surface a neutral no-change fact rather than a\n\t// phantom version bump. UNCHANGED is informational only and is never counted toward the bump tally.\n\tif (o === n) {\n\t\treturn \"UNCHANGED\";\n\t}\n\tconst oExact = !isLoose(o);\n\tconst nLoose = isLoose(n);\n\tif (n === \"\" && o !== \"\") {\n\t\treturn \"PIN_DROPPED\";\n\t}\n\tif (oExact && nLoose) {\n\t\treturn \"PIN_LOOSENED\";\n\t}\n\treturn \"BUMPED\";\n}\n\n// Collect { name -> constraint } from added/removed manifest lines for a single ecosystem file.\nfunction collectDeps(df: DiffFile, eco: Ecosystem, side: \"added\" | \"removed\"): Map<string, string> {\n\tconst out = new Map<string, string>();\n\tconst lines = side === \"added\" ? df.addedLines : df.removedLines;\n\tfor (const [, content] of lines) {\n\t\tconst parsed = eco.parse(content);\n\t\tif (parsed) {\n\t\t\tout.set(parsed.name, parsed.constraint);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn out;\n}\n\n/** How many captured paths a direction names before it only counts the rest. */\nconst PATHS_NAMED = 5;\n\nfunction namedPaths(paths: readonly string[]): string {\n\tconst named = paths.slice(0, PATHS_NAMED).join(\", \");\n\treturn paths.length > PATHS_NAMED ? `${named} and ${paths.length - PATHS_NAMED} more` : named;\n}\n\n/** The hint''s context line: the matched name and constraint on its side, or the delta between the two. */\nfunction dependencyContext(fact: string, name: string, oldC: string, newC: string): string {\n\tif (fact === \"ONLY_ADDED\") {\n\t\treturn `+ ${name} ${newC}`.trim();\n\t}\n\treturn fact === \"ONLY_REMOVED\" ? `- ${name} ${oldC}`.trim() : `${name}: ${oldC} -> ${newC}`;\n}\n\nexport default function changesDependenciesDeliberately(\n\trepoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tconst changedManifests = new Set<string>();\n\tconst touchedLockfiles = new Set<string>();\n\tlet onlyAdded = 0;\n\tlet onlyRemoved = 0;\n\tlet pinsLoosened = 0;\n\tlet pinsDropped = 0;\n\tlet bumped = 0;\n\tlet unpairedManifestLines = 0;\n\n\t// Where the checkout holds a file with a lockfile''s name. A path anywhere in the repository is not\n\t// evidence that it belongs to a changed manifest; the paths are reported for the review to relate.\n\tconst allLockfileNames = new Set(\n\t\tECOSYSTEMS.flatMap((e) => e.lockfiles.map((l) => l.toLowerCase())),\n\t);\n\tconst checkoutLockfiles: string[] = [];\n\tfor (const ext of [\"json\", \"lock\", \"yaml\", \"resolved\", \"lockfile\", \"sum\"]) {\n\t\tfor (const f of globFilesSync(`**/*.${ext}`, repoPath)) {\n\t\t\tif (allLockfileNames.has(basenameLower(f))) {\n\t\t\t\tcheckoutLockfiles.push(f);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst base = basenameLower(path);\n\t\tif (allLockfileNames.has(base)) {\n\t\t\ttouchedLockfiles.add(path);\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst eco = ecosystemFor(path);\n\t\tif (!eco) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tchangedManifests.add(path);\n\n\t\tconst { dependencyLine } = eco;\n\t\tif (dependencyLine !== undefined) {\n\t\t\tfor (const side of [\"removed\", \"added\"] as const) {\n\t\t\t\tconst lines = [...(side === \"added\" ? df.addedLines : df.removedLines)];\n\t\t\t\tfor (const [ln, content] of lines.filter(([, c]) => dependencyLine.test(c))) {\n\t\t\t\t\tunpairedManifestLines += 1;\n\t\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tline: ln,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tpattern: \"candidate:raw manifest line\",\n\t\t\t\t\t\tcontext: `${side === \"added\" ? \"+\" : \"-\"} ${content.trim()}`.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\t\tflags: { ecosystem: base, side },\n\t\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst added = collectDeps(df, eco, \"added\");\n\t\tconst removed = collectDeps(df, eco, \"removed\");\n\n\t\t// helper to find the diff line for a dependency name on a given side (for hint placement). Match on a\n\t\t// quote/word/coordinate boundary, not a bare substring, so a prefix-sharing sibling (react vs\n\t\t// react-dom, or a scoped name appearing inside another package''s URL) doesn''t grab the wrong line.\n\t\tconst lineFor = (name: string, side: \"added\" | \"removed\"): number => {\n\t\t\tconst lines = side === \"added\" ? df.addedLines : df.removedLines;\n\t\t\tconst bounded = new RegExp(`(?:^|[^\\\\w.\\\\-/])${escapeRegExp(name)}(?:[^\\\\w.\\\\-/]|$)`, \"u\");\n\t\t\tfor (const [ln, content] of lines) {\n\t\t\t\tif (bounded.test(content)) {\n\t\t\t\t\treturn ln;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// Fallback: a constructed key (e.g. a Gradle group:name coordinate) may not survive the boundary\n\t\t\t// test against the raw line — keep the substring scan so the hint still lands on a real line.\n\t\t\tfor (const [ln, content] of lines) {\n\t\t\t\tif (content.includes(name)) {\n\t\t\t\t\treturn ln;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn 0;\n\t\t};\n\n\t\tconst allNames = new Set<string>([...added.keys(), ...removed.keys()]);\n\t\tfor (const name of allNames) {\n\t\t\tconst inAdded = added.has(name);\n\t\t\tconst inRemoved = removed.has(name);\n\t\t\tlet fact: string;\n\t\t\tlet side: \"added\" | \"removed\" = \"added\";\n\t\t\tif (inAdded && !inRemoved) {\n\t\t\t\tfact = \"ONLY_ADDED\";\n\t\t\t\tonlyAdded += 1;\n\t\t\t} else if (!inAdded && inRemoved) {\n\t\t\t\tfact = \"ONLY_REMOVED\";\n\t\t\t\tside = \"removed\";\n\t\t\t\tonlyRemoved += 1;\n\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\tfact = classifyDelta(removed.get(name) ?? \"\", added.get(name) ?? \"\");\n\t\t\t\tif (fact === \"PIN_LOOSENED\") {\n\t\t\t\t\tpinsLoosened += 1;\n\t\t\t\t} else if (fact === \"PIN_DROPPED\") {\n\t\t\t\t\tpinsDropped += 1;\n\t\t\t\t} else if (fact === \"UNCHANGED\") {\n\t\t\t\t\t// whitespace/newline-only line pair — not a version change, count nothing\n\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\tbumped += 1;\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst oldC = removed.get(name) ?? \"\";\n\t\t\tconst newC = added.get(name) ?? \"\";\n\t\t\tconst ctx = dependencyContext(fact, name, oldC, newC);\n\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\tline: lineFor(name, side),\n\t\t\t\tpattern: `candidate:${fact}`,\n\t\t\t\tcontext: ctx.slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\tflags: { ecosystem: base, name },\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tconst lockfilePresent = checkoutLockfiles.length > 0 || touchedLockfiles.size > 0;\n\tconst listed = hints.slice(0, 40);\n\tconst rawLinesListed = listed.filter((h) => h.pattern === \"candidate:raw manifest line\").length;\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (changedManifests.size > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`Manifest-named file(s) changed (${changedManifests.size}). The name alone is no occasion: a changed external dependency declaration, resolution, source or pin inside one is, and internal target, test-target, scheme or build-setting wiring alone is not. Candidate lines, matched by shape and not yet placed in a dependency section: ${onlyAdded} only on an added line, ${onlyRemoved} only on a removed line, ${pinsLoosened} pin(s) loosened, ${pinsDropped} pin(s) dropped, ${bumped} version bump(s). A metadata field such as a package.json engines entry or a Cargo rust-version has the same shape, and a name on one side only may be a moved or reformatted line rather than a new or dropped dependency — read the section around each before treating it as a dependency change${unpairedManifestLines > 0 ? `; the ${unpairedManifestLines} raw multi-line manifest line(s) are not in these counts, so a zero here does not show that nothing changed` : \"\"}. Then investigate whether each dependency constraint change is deliberate and appropriately bounded.`,\n\t\t);\n\t\tif (hints.length === 0) {\n\t\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\t\"No changed line in these manifests has the dependency shape this script reads. Read the changed sections before deciding: a declaration spread over lines can escape a per-line reading, while internal wiring such as a target dependency, a test target or a scheme entry declares no external dependency.\",\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (unpairedManifestLines > 0) {\n\t\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\t`${unpairedManifestLines} changed dependency line(s) in a pom.xml or XcodeGen project.yml carry no added, removed or pin label${rawLinesListed < unpairedManifestLines ? `, and only ${rawLinesListed} of them are listed as raw lines here — read the manifest diffs for the other ${unpairedManifestLines - rawLinesListed}` : \" and are listed as raw lines\"}: these manifests spread one dependency over several lines and the diff shows only the changed ones. Read the manifest around each to tell an addition from a version change or a reordering; a pom.xml coordinate may also be a build plugin or a parent/BOM rather than a dependency.`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (lockfilePresent) {\n\t\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\t`Lockfile-named file(s) in the checkout: ${checkoutLockfiles.length === 0 ? \"none\" : namedPaths(checkoutLockfiles)}; touched in this diff: ${touchedLockfiles.size === 0 ? \"none\" : namedPaths([...touchedLockfiles])}. A lockfile answers for a changed manifest when the ecosystem resolves that manifest through it — beside it, or at a parent workspace root whose configuration includes it — so read the paths and that configuration before expecting a matching update.`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\t\"No file with a lockfile name these ecosystems write was found in the checkout or touched in the diff — investigate whether the ecosystem expects a committed lockfile to make the resolved versions reproducible.\",\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints: listed,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\tmanifestsChanged: changedManifests.size,\n\t\t\tonlyAdded,\n\t\t\tonlyRemoved,\n\t\t\tpinsLoosened,\n\t\t\tpinsDropped,\n\t\t\tbumped,\n\t\t\tunpairedManifestLines,\n\t\t\trawLinesListed,\n\t\t\tlockfilesTouched: touchedLockfiles.size,\n\t\t\tlockfilePresent: lockfilePresent ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"Every dependency you add is code you now trust and have to keep patched, and many malicious packages slip in disguised as a real name a letter or two off. A one-line note on why a package is there, plus a lockfile that moves with the manifest, lets a reviewer sanity-check the addition and keeps the build reproducible.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A new package arrives with a sentence on what it is for. The manifest and resolution stay consistent with the ecosystem and the repository policy, updating a lockfile when needed rather than requiring one for every edit.","groupSlug":"secure-by-default-changes","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 140\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Unexplained package</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">PR: Add yaml-lite</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Why: Add yaml-lite</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer guesses why</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Explained package</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">PR: Add yaml-lite</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Why: Preserve config comments</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"15\">Reviewer can check</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A pull request that adds yaml-lite gives only Add yaml-lite as its reason, so the reviewer guesses why. The same pull request says Preserve config comments, so the reviewer can check."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you add a package, check whether you need it.\n- Before you add a package, check that its name is exactly the one you meant.\n- When you add a direct dependency or make a notable version change, write one sentence on why. Say what the package does in this change, or why its version changes. For example, instead of “Add yaml-lite”, write “Add yaml-lite to keep comments in plugin config files”.\n- Put that sentence where a reviewer reads it. Use the description, a commit subject, or a comment in the manifest, the file that lists packages.\n- Keep the manifest and any lockfile (the file of chosen versions) consistent with your package manager and repository policy. A compatible lockfile can stay unchanged.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your change does not add, remove or change a third-party dependency, its version, its source or its fixed version. Dependencies include packages, libraries, frameworks, plugins, container images and workflow actions.\n- Your change only wires up your own code. Examples are an internal module, a test target, build settings, sources or resources. This is true even when the edit is inside a dependency manifest.\n\nIt still applies when your change only refreshes a lockfile.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Writing a reason for every version change. A patch or minor version change needs no sentence, and neither does a change to the dependencies of your dependencies. A large lockfile diff from your tool is normal. Instead, explain each new direct dependency and each notable version change.\n- Giving the package name as the reason. “Add yaml-lite” or “Update yaml-lite to 2.0” only says what changed. This is true even when the name hints at what the package does. Instead, say what the package does in this change, or why its version changes.\n- Removing a fixed version or a lockfile entry to get past a conflict when your repository asks for reproducible builds. Instead, let your package manager resolve the conflict, and keep the lockfile consistent with the manifest.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Concise guide for evaluating open source software](https://best.openssf.org/Concise-Guide-for-Evaluating-Open-Source-Software), OpenSSF Best Practices Working Group, 2025. OpenSSF recommends checking whether you need a dependency and whether its name is authentic.\n- [Dependency management](https://abseil.io/resources/swe-book/html/ch21.html), Titus Winters, in Software Engineering at Google, 2020. A new dependency is not free, so weigh its costs before you import it.\n- [package-lock.json](https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v10/configuring-npm/package-lock-json), npm Docs. npm records exact dependency versions in a lockfile intended for version control.\n- [Why have Cargo.lock in version control?](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/faq.html#why-have-cargolock-in-version-control), The Cargo Book. A lockfile gives the same dependency versions at different times and on different systems.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Dependency changes stay coherent and notable choices have a reason"'::jsonb),'v5:79ac2b89c17b444114b78a7df125aaf468b63fecf496b38edd08359b5cfc8f47','practice:v3:42b2cdff30a06ebc016b3131d85a75eb9d267f26d7c0e9d8c7b714fdb6296950');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('GROUP','decisions-and-documentation',NULL,'{"name":"Recording decisions and documenting changes","description":"Capture the why. Record the rationale behind significant design decisions, and document changes to public APIs and shared behaviour, so the people who maintain or build on the code later can understand it.","icon":"BookText","color":"slate"}'::jsonb,'{"name":"Recording decisions and documenting changes","description":"Capture the why. Record the rationale behind significant design decisions, and document changes to public APIs and shared behaviour, so the people who maintain or build on the code later can understand it.","icon":"BookText","color":"slate"}'::jsonb,'8ded77c597c0be43275e84349a39048358f87f2f1174ad025461bde046a940f0','group:v1:8ded77c597c0be43275e84349a39048358f87f2f1174ad025461bde046a940f0');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','records-significant-decisions-with-rationale','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Record significant decisions and the rationale","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: a written rationale for the architecturally significant decision this change makes, recorded where a future maintainer can find it.\n\n## The standard\nWork in two stages, in order. First establish an architecturally significant decision from the hand-written diff. Only then judge whether its need, constraint or trade-off is recorded rather than left to be reverse-engineered from the code. A recorded WHY may be one clause, but it must say something the code does not already show: the need the decision serves (\"so a customer can save a draft and return to it\", \"a stored type because attachments must survive a restart\") or the constraint that forced it (\"the provider only accepts signed requests\") records it; the lapse is silence about the decision, not brevity, and a clause quoted for the description''s WHY may be the same clause. A user benefit can record the need without a \"because\" formula: \"edits survive restart; returning users skip setup\" records why a profile is persisted. A sentence that only restates what the model or the mechanism itself does (\"added the AuditLog interceptor; every request is now logged\", \"the profile is now saved to disk\") says WHAT the diff shows and is not a rationale, and a commit subject that names the addition is a label, not a why. Never demand the alternatives considered or the reason this approach over another: the need served is the rationale a maintainer needs. A feature goal counts for a separate schema, security or public-contract decision only when it explains the need for that decision. Significance is a closed list, so two readers find the same subject: a change to a PERSISTED model or schema (a stored type, a migration, a field that is saved); a public-contract or wire-API change (an endpoint, a message format, an exported library API); a new cross-cutting mechanism the rest of the app plugs into (a shared aspect, interceptor or event bus, a base class many types extend); an auth or security mechanism; the removal of a feature with persisted state. Not on the list, and never significant on their own: a dependency addition or version change, which `changes-dependencies-deliberately` judges; a call to an external web service (an HTTP API consumed at runtime is a mechanism used, not a dependency added to a manifest); a view or view model and how screens navigate; an in-memory draft, cache or value type; using the stack the project already uses (its persistence framework, UI framework or map kit) without one of the listed changes — a new persisted field built with that stack is still a persisted-model change; a helper, extension or calculation; a rename. When the only candidate is one of these, stage 1 fails and the cell is NOT_APPLICABLE. For a significant decision, look for its rationale in the title, the description, a commit subject or body, or an ADR or design document the change links, adds or edits. A reason given in a review comment or the conversation is not recorded where a maintainer finds it and does not count; the discussion is where a rationale was asked for, not where it lives. A rationale clause counts in prose or inside a bullet or checklist item — \"chose X because Y\", \"an event queue so a slow consumer can''t block checkout\", \"to avoid Z\" — so scan the whole body. Restating WHAT changed, an empty or template body, or a bare \"added mechanism X\" does not record a why.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the change is routine and carries no architectural decision: tests, formatting, docs, a rename without a contract change, a version bump of an already-present dependency, a tuned constant or threshold on an internal symbol, or an empty diff. Significance comes from hand-written hunks only: a hand-authored migration changeset is a decision, while a dependency edit alone is not. When the diff is unreadable, the change still made whatever decisions it made and you could not read them: report a collection gap and record no observation, never a routine or a significant verdict.\n\n## Sources\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`, with `work/change/files.json`): the changed lines, added or removed, that establish significance, and any ADR or design document the same change adds or edits. Code establishes what was decided, not why. An ADR or design document can record the rationale in the same change. Read its prose.\n2. The title (`context/metadata.json`) and the WHOLE description (`context/description.md`): every paragraph, bullet and checklist item. They establish a recorded rationale you can quote. An image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n3. The commit subjects and bodies (`context/commits.json`): a rationale stated there counts. Per-commit patches are not in your context; never claim a per-commit file mapping.\n4. A linked ADR or design document materialised as a `.md` file under `context/docs/`: read it before concluding the why is absent. Cite its file path together with the quoted or closely paraphrased rationale sentence; a bare URL is not evidence the why was recorded. When the change names a specific document that was not materialised, the rationale may be there: report a collection gap and record no observation, never a claim against prose you were not shown.\n5. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): what a changed symbol or mechanism means, and an in-repository ADR the change links, adds or edits. It never supplies a rationale the change did not write.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: a significant decision is present and its rationale is written down in one of the sources above, so the intent survives without re-reading the code.\n- NOT_MET: a significant decision ships with no recorded why anywhere in the description, the commit subjects or bodies, or an ADR or design document the change links, adds or edits. Name the decision.\n- UNDETERMINED: no ordinary case. Having searched every source and found no why is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED; a document you were not shown is a collection gap.\nOne observation per review. When several significant decisions co-occur, judge them together in one observation.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on rationale alone — non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: a single contained unexplained significant decision — one isolated persisted schema field. MAJOR: the genuinely consequential — an unexplained auth or security mechanism, a persistence, public-API or wire-contract change, a real cross-cutting mechanism others build on, or several significant decisions co-occurring unexplained. When unsure, prefer the lower severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the exact changed `[L<n>]` line(s), added or removed, that establish significance in every cell. For MET, also quote the verbatim rationale sentence, commit subject or document reference and where it lives. For NOT_MET, the citations show the search: the description, the commit subjects, and the referenced rationale sources or their absence. Never invent a rationale, never infer the why from the code itself, and never assert that the schema or endpoint is wrong — judge only whether the why is recorded. Never claim a build or test ran. Describe the observable record; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nThe overall adequacy of the description — a clear title and a why for the change as a whole — belongs to `describe-what-and-why`; here judge only whether THAT significant decision''s rationale is recorded, so the two never double-flag. Whether a public-surface change is documented for its consumers belongs to `documents-public-api-and-behaviour-changes`; whether a linked wiki document now contradicts the code belongs to `change-keeps-linked-docs-consistent`. Dependency edits belong only to `changes-dependencies-deliberately`. A separate architectural decision in the same change remains in scope here.","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"When you pick one design over another, the rationale is the first thing to fade. A maintainer who cannot find why you chose it will either undo a good decision or be too afraid to touch it, and both cost the team later.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A meaningful design choice arrives with its rationale written down, in the description or a short architecture decision record (ADR), such as ''chose an event queue over direct calls so a slow consumer cannot block checkout'', not just the code that does it.","groupSlug":"decisions-and-documentation","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 148\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Unexplained pull request</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"86.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\">Add <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">EventQueue</tspan></text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"15\">Orders use it</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"144\" font-size=\"15\">Maintainer guesses</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Explained pull request</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"86.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\">Add <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">EventQueue</tspan></text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"15\">Why: slow consumers</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"103\" font-size=\"15\">never block checkout</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"144\" font-size=\"15\">Maintainer knows why</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A pull request “Add EventQueue” says only “Orders use it”, so the maintainer guesses. When it adds “Why: slow consumers never block checkout”, the maintainer knows why."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, check whether the change makes a significant decision. Only these kinds count. A change to stored data or a schema counts. So do a change to a public API or a message format, and an auth or security mechanism. A new mechanism that other code plugs into counts too, and so does removing a feature with stored data.\n- If it does, say why in the title, the description, or a commit message. A decision record that the change adds or links works too.\n- Name the need the decision serves or the constraint that forced it. One clause is enough. For example, instead of “Added an attachments table”, write “Store attachments in a table, because they must survive a restart”.\n- If the change makes more than one significant decision, give a reason for each one.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The change makes no architectural decision. A new screen, a cache, or a helper is not one by itself.\n- The change only touches tests, formatting, or docs.\n- The change renames something without changing a contract, such as stored data, a public API or a message format.\n- The change only adds a dependency or changes its version.\n- The change tunes a constant or a threshold inside the code.\n- The change only touches generated code. Only lines that a person wrote count.\n- The change is empty.\n\nIt still applies when you write a migration by hand.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking you need a long document that lists every option you turned down. You do not. Instead, write one clause that names the need or the constraint.\n- Repeating what the code does as the reason. “Added an audit log step that runs on every request, so every request is now logged” says what changed. Instead, say what need the logging serves.\n- Giving only the goal of the feature. “Users can attach files” does not say why attachments are stored. Instead, connect the goal to the decision.\n- Explaining the decision only in a reply to a review comment. A future maintainer will not look there. Instead, put the reason in the change itself.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Documenting architecture decisions](https://www.cognitect.com/blog/2011/11/15/documenting-architecture-decisions), Michael Nygard, 2011. A short record of the reasons, kept with the code, stops a team from accepting or changing a decision blindly.\n- [Maintain an architecture decision record](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/architect-role/architecture-decision-record), Microsoft Azure Well-Architected Framework. A decision recorded without its reason loses value, because readers cannot judge if it still holds.\n- [A survey of architecture design rationale](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jss.2006.04.029), Tang, Babar, Gorton and Han, 2006. In 81 responses, practitioners said that design rationale matters, but they reported barriers to writing it down.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Record significant decisions and the rationale","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: a written rationale for the architecturally significant decision this change makes, recorded where a future maintainer can find it.\n\n## The standard\nWork in two stages, in order. First establish an architecturally significant decision from the hand-written diff. Only then judge whether its need, constraint or trade-off is recorded rather than left to be reverse-engineered from the code. A recorded WHY may be one clause, but it must say something the code does not already show: the need the decision serves (\"so a customer can save a draft and return to it\", \"a stored type because attachments must survive a restart\") or the constraint that forced it (\"the provider only accepts signed requests\") records it; the lapse is silence about the decision, not brevity, and a clause quoted for the description''s WHY may be the same clause. A user benefit can record the need without a \"because\" formula: \"edits survive restart; returning users skip setup\" records why a profile is persisted. A sentence that only restates what the model or the mechanism itself does (\"added the AuditLog interceptor; every request is now logged\", \"the profile is now saved to disk\") says WHAT the diff shows and is not a rationale, and a commit subject that names the addition is a label, not a why. Never demand the alternatives considered or the reason this approach over another: the need served is the rationale a maintainer needs. A feature goal counts for a separate schema, security or public-contract decision only when it explains the need for that decision. Significance is a closed list, so two readers find the same subject: a change to a PERSISTED model or schema (a stored type, a migration, a field that is saved); a public-contract or wire-API change (an endpoint, a message format, an exported library API); a new cross-cutting mechanism the rest of the app plugs into (a shared aspect, interceptor or event bus, a base class many types extend); an auth or security mechanism; the removal of a feature with persisted state. Not on the list, and never significant on their own: a dependency addition or version change, which `changes-dependencies-deliberately` judges; a call to an external web service (an HTTP API consumed at runtime is a mechanism used, not a dependency added to a manifest); a view or view model and how screens navigate; an in-memory draft, cache or value type; using the stack the project already uses (its persistence framework, UI framework or map kit) without one of the listed changes — a new persisted field built with that stack is still a persisted-model change; a helper, extension or calculation; a rename. When the only candidate is one of these, stage 1 fails and the cell is NOT_APPLICABLE. For a significant decision, look for its rationale in the title, the description, a commit subject or body, or an ADR or design document the change links, adds or edits. A reason given in a review comment or the conversation is not recorded where a maintainer finds it and does not count; the discussion is where a rationale was asked for, not where it lives. A rationale clause counts in prose or inside a bullet or checklist item — \"chose X because Y\", \"an event queue so a slow consumer can''t block checkout\", \"to avoid Z\" — so scan the whole body. Restating WHAT changed, an empty or template body, or a bare \"added mechanism X\" does not record a why.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the change is routine and carries no architectural decision: tests, formatting, docs, a rename without a contract change, a version bump of an already-present dependency, a tuned constant or threshold on an internal symbol, or an empty diff. Significance comes from hand-written hunks only: a hand-authored migration changeset is a decision, while a dependency edit alone is not. When the diff is unreadable, the change still made whatever decisions it made and you could not read them: report a collection gap and record no observation, never a routine or a significant verdict.\n\n## Sources\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`, with `work/change/files.json`): the changed lines, added or removed, that establish significance, and any ADR or design document the same change adds or edits. Code establishes what was decided, not why. An ADR or design document can record the rationale in the same change. Read its prose.\n2. The title (`context/metadata.json`) and the WHOLE description (`context/description.md`): every paragraph, bullet and checklist item. They establish a recorded rationale you can quote. An image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n3. The commit subjects and bodies (`context/commits.json`): a rationale stated there counts. Per-commit patches are not in your context; never claim a per-commit file mapping.\n4. A linked ADR or design document materialised as a `.md` file under `context/docs/`: read it before concluding the why is absent. Cite its file path together with the quoted or closely paraphrased rationale sentence; a bare URL is not evidence the why was recorded. When the change names a specific document that was not materialised, the rationale may be there: report a collection gap and record no observation, never a claim against prose you were not shown.\n5. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): what a changed symbol or mechanism means, and an in-repository ADR the change links, adds or edits. It never supplies a rationale the change did not write.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: a significant decision is present and its rationale is written down in one of the sources above, so the intent survives without re-reading the code.\n- NOT_MET: a significant decision ships with no recorded why anywhere in the description, the commit subjects or bodies, or an ADR or design document the change links, adds or edits. Name the decision.\n- UNDETERMINED: no ordinary case. Having searched every source and found no why is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED; a document you were not shown is a collection gap.\nOne observation per review. When several significant decisions co-occur, judge them together in one observation.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on rationale alone — non-blocking. MINOR, the usual band: a single contained unexplained significant decision — one isolated persisted schema field. MAJOR: the genuinely consequential — an unexplained auth or security mechanism, a persistence, public-API or wire-contract change, a real cross-cutting mechanism others build on, or several significant decisions co-occurring unexplained. When unsure, prefer the lower severity.\n\n## Grounding\nQuote the exact changed `[L<n>]` line(s), added or removed, that establish significance in every cell. For MET, also quote the verbatim rationale sentence, commit subject or document reference and where it lives. For NOT_MET, the citations show the search: the description, the commit subjects, and the referenced rationale sources or their absence. Never invent a rationale, never infer the why from the code itself, and never assert that the schema or endpoint is wrong — judge only whether the why is recorded. Never claim a build or test ran. Describe the observable record; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nThe overall adequacy of the description — a clear title and a why for the change as a whole — belongs to `describe-what-and-why`; here judge only whether THAT significant decision''s rationale is recorded, so the two never double-flag. Whether a public-surface change is documented for its consumers belongs to `documents-public-api-and-behaviour-changes`; whether a linked wiki document now contradicts the code belongs to `change-keeps-linked-docs-consistent`. Dependency edits belong only to `changes-dependencies-deliberately`. A separate architectural decision in the same change remains in scope here.","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"When you pick one design over another, the rationale is the first thing to fade. A maintainer who cannot find why you chose it will either undo a good decision or be too afraid to touch it, and both cost the team later.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A meaningful design choice arrives with its rationale written down, in the description or a short architecture decision record (ADR), such as ''chose an event queue over direct calls so a slow consumer cannot block checkout'', not just the code that does it.","groupSlug":"decisions-and-documentation","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 148\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Unexplained pull request</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"86.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\">Add <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">EventQueue</tspan></text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"15\">Orders use it</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"144\" font-size=\"15\">Maintainer guesses</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Explained pull request</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"86.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"15\">Add <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">EventQueue</tspan></text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"15\">Why: slow consumers</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"103\" font-size=\"15\">never block checkout</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"144\" font-size=\"15\">Maintainer knows why</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A pull request “Add EventQueue” says only “Orders use it”, so the maintainer guesses. When it adds “Why: slow consumers never block checkout”, the maintainer knows why."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, check whether the change makes a significant decision. Only these kinds count. A change to stored data or a schema counts. So do a change to a public API or a message format, and an auth or security mechanism. A new mechanism that other code plugs into counts too, and so does removing a feature with stored data.\n- If it does, say why in the title, the description, or a commit message. A decision record that the change adds or links works too.\n- Name the need the decision serves or the constraint that forced it. One clause is enough. For example, instead of “Added an attachments table”, write “Store attachments in a table, because they must survive a restart”.\n- If the change makes more than one significant decision, give a reason for each one.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The change makes no architectural decision. A new screen, a cache, or a helper is not one by itself.\n- The change only touches tests, formatting, or docs.\n- The change renames something without changing a contract, such as stored data, a public API or a message format.\n- The change only adds a dependency or changes its version.\n- The change tunes a constant or a threshold inside the code.\n- The change only touches generated code. Only lines that a person wrote count.\n- The change is empty.\n\nIt still applies when you write a migration by hand.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking you need a long document that lists every option you turned down. You do not. Instead, write one clause that names the need or the constraint.\n- Repeating what the code does as the reason. “Added an audit log step that runs on every request, so every request is now logged” says what changed. Instead, say what need the logging serves.\n- Giving only the goal of the feature. “Users can attach files” does not say why attachments are stored. Instead, connect the goal to the decision.\n- Explaining the decision only in a reply to a review comment. A future maintainer will not look there. Instead, put the reason in the change itself.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Documenting architecture decisions](https://www.cognitect.com/blog/2011/11/15/documenting-architecture-decisions), Michael Nygard, 2011. A short record of the reasons, kept with the code, stops a team from accepting or changing a decision blindly.\n- [Maintain an architecture decision record](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/architect-role/architecture-decision-record), Microsoft Azure Well-Architected Framework. A decision recorded without its reason loses value, because readers cannot judge if it still holds.\n- [A survey of architecture design rationale](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jss.2006.04.029), Tang, Babar, Gorton and Han, 2006. In 81 responses, practitioners said that design rationale matters, but they reported barriers to writing it down.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"Significant decisions land with their reasoning"'::jsonb),'v5:12d6e3b321a519f111cf7cff59b33f9f223ca150e3a78d3c2089f8af1e648f30','practice:v3:7b93e9fb38f1aa9ef35dd64f27e2a792a14c727e64252501df54bb38e17385ee');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','documents-public-api-and-behaviour-changes','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Document public API and behaviour changes","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: consumer-facing documentation of the changed public surface — a downstream caller hears what moved from the change itself.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one practice: when the change alters a PUBLIC or SHARED surface — something a SEPARATE consumer depends on and compiles or integrates against — does the change carry, in the same diff or in the description, documentation that tells consumers what moved? Public surfaces: a library''s or framework''s exported symbols, a published package''s API, a documented HTTP or RPC contract, a persisted or wire schema, an error or response shape callers branch on, a config default a consumer sets. Good: the renamed parameter, new endpoint, changed response field or breaking removal lands with its doc-comment, a changelog line, or a description sentence naming the consumer-facing effect. Bad: a real exported-surface change ships with nothing a consumer could read to adapt. A generated API specification or reference documents a contract when its authoritative source and meaning are captured: judge the contract and the information a consumer receives, not whether the text was handwritten. Irrelevant generated output is not documentation, and generated churn alone never establishes an authored contract decision.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Establish the separate consumer from the captured contract or usage, never from package type alone: an application can expose HTTP endpoints, configuration or wire formats to other consumers without publishing a library. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the changed symbols are internal types, private signatures, local refactors or app-only fields with no separate consumer, or when no public-surface change is observable in the diff and description at all. Decide whether a changed symbol is actually exported and consumer-facing as an experienced reviewer would, not by keyword; a plainly internal symbol is NOT_APPLICABLE.\n\n## Sources\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`, with `work/change/files.json`): the surface change itself, and any documentation shipped beside it — a doc-comment on or above the surface, a changelog or docs addition, an updated reference. It establishes what changed and what documentation arrived with it. It never establishes a consumer by itself.\n2. The description (`context/description.md`): a sentence naming the consumer-facing effect. It establishes documentation a consumer would read on the change; it never establishes a surface change the diff does not show.\n3. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the contract and its usage — who depends on the surface, and the existing reference docs the diff leaves untouched. It establishes whether a separate consumer exists and where the documentation would live. It never supplies the note the change failed to write.\n4. A linked wiki document the change references, when it was materialised as a `.md` file under `context/docs/`: its body may hold the documentation consumers need. Cite its file path together with the quoted or closely paraphrased sentence relied on; a bare URL is not evidence that the document says it. When the change points at a specific document that was not materialised for you, the documentation may live there: report a collection gap and record no observation, never an undocumented surface.\nAn image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: the public-surface change carries documentation naming what moved — quote the surface change and the documenting text.\n- NOT_MET: documentation exists for the change yet never names the specific changed parameter, field, route or default a consumer must adapt to. Quote both.\n- NOT_MET: a real public-surface change and, after looking in every place a consumer would — the doc-comment on or above the surface, a changelog or docs addition, the description, and a materialised wiki document — nothing a consumer could read to adapt.\n- UNDETERMINED only when the evidence genuinely cannot settle whether the one symbol this practice turns on crosses a module or service boundary; name the question. Do not resolve that doubt into an observation or into a claim that no public surface was here. Having looked everywhere and found no documentation for an established public surface is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review. When several surfaces change, grade by the least-documented one.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on documentation alone — a formative practice, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a new additive surface with no documentation, since nothing breaks but consumers cannot discover it; lighter still, documentation that exists yet never names the specific changed parameter or field. MAJOR: a breaking change — a removed or renamed symbol, route or field, a narrowed type, a changed default — shipping with no documentation anywhere, so callers break without a migration note. Equivalent situations land at equivalent severity.\n\n## Grounding\nCite the diff lines of the observable surface change in every cell. For MET, quote the documenting text and its file. For NOT_MET, quote the documentation and the changed element it omits. For NOT_MET, cite the surface change and name the boundary searched: the surface''s doc-comment, the changelog and docs in the diff, the description, and any materialised wiki document. Never invent a symbol, an export marker, a changelog or a clean bill you did not see. Never assert the compile or runtime correctness of the surface, and never impute intent or care; describe the documentation practice on the observable artifact only.\n\n## Defer\nThe pull request''s general what-and-why narrative belongs to `describe-what-and-why`; whether a design decision''s rationale is recorded belongs to `records-significant-decisions-with-rationale`. A thin description or an unjustified choice is not an observation here. Here only: is the public-surface change documented for its consumers?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for documents-public-api-and-behaviour-changes. Surfaces FACTS ONLY — the LLM judges.\n//\n// Two STRUCTURAL leads a diff hunk alone cannot reveal: whether a manifest declares a library/framework\n// product, and whether the declarations the diff adds are EXPORTED or internal/private. Neither settles the\n// public surface: an app without a library product can still expose an HTTP, configuration or wire contract\n// to separate consumers, which the review investigates. A modifier-less Swift declaration is INTERNAL — not\n// public — so the export-status table classifies it as internal. We surface {hasPublicProduct,\n// changedPublicSymbols, changedInternalSymbols} and let the model decide whether documentation was owed. The\n// table is keyed off file extension: adding a language = one row, no engine change.\nimport { isCommentLine } from \"../lib/declarations.ts\";\nimport { findFiles, readFileLines } from \"../lib/grep.ts\";\nimport { languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// ── (1) Public-product manifest scan ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// Each manifest filename maps to a predicate over its raw text that answers \"does this declare a consumable\n// library/framework product?\". One row per ecosystem; the predicate stays neutral (a structural test, no observation).\nconst PRODUCT_MANIFESTS: [RegExp, (text: string) => boolean][] = [\n\t// Swift Package Manager: a `.library(...)` product.\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)Package\\.swift$/u, (t) => /\\.library\\s*\\(/u.test(t)],\n\t// npm: a non-private package that declares an entry/types surface for consumers.\n\t[\n\t\t/(?:^|\\/)package\\.json$/u,\n\t\t(t) =>\n\t\t\t!/\"private\"\\s*:\\s*true/u.test(t) && /\"(?:main|module|exports|types|typings)\"\\s*:/u.test(t),\n\t],\n\t// Maven: packaged as a real artifact (jar/etc.), not an aggregator pom.\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)pom\\.xml$/u, (t) => !/<packaging>\\s*pom\\s*<\\/packaging>/u.test(t)],\n\t// Gradle: applies the java-library plugin or a publishing plugin.\n\t[\n\t\t/(?:^|\\/)build\\.gradle(?:\\.kts)?$/u,\n\t\t(t) =>\n\t\t\t/(?:java-library|maven-publish|`maven-publish`|com\\.vanniktech\\.maven\\.publish)/u.test(t),\n\t],\n\t// Python: declares packages to distribute.\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)setup\\.py$/u, (t) => /(?:packages\\s*=|find_packages\\s*\\()/u.test(t)],\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)setup\\.cfg$/u, (t) => /\\[options\\][^[]*packages\\s*=/u.test(t)],\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)pyproject\\.toml$/u, (t) => /(?:\\[project\\]|\\[tool\\.poetry\\]|packages\\s*=)/u.test(t)],\n\t// Go: a module declaration makes the package importable by others.\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)go\\.mod$/u, (t) => /^module\\s+\\S+/mu.test(t)],\n];\n\nconst MANIFEST_NAMES = [\"swift\", \"json\", \"xml\", \"gradle\", \"kts\", \"py\", \"cfg\", \"toml\", \"mod\"];\n\n// ── (2) Per-language export status of a CHANGED declaration ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// For each language: detect that a line declares a symbol, and whether that symbol is PUBLIC (exported) or not.\n// `isPublic` is only consulted when `decl` matches. Modifier-less Swift is INTERNAL, not public.\ninterface ExportRule {\n\t// line introduces a named declaration (func/class/type/var/const)\n\tdecl: RegExp;\n\t// is that declaration part of the public surface?\n\tisPublic: (line: string) => boolean;\n}\n\nconst EXPORT_RULES: Record<string, ExportRule> = {\n\t// Swift: public/open are exported; modifier-less / internal / private / fileprivate are NOT public.\n\tswift: {\n\t\tdecl: /\\b(?:func|class|struct|enum|protocol|extension|var|let|typealias|actor|init)\\b/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => /\\b(?:public|open)\\b/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\t// TypeScript and JavaScript: an `export` keyword (or `export default`) marks the public surface.\n\ttypescript: {\n\t\tdecl: /\\b(?:function|class|interface|type|enum|const|let|var|namespace)\\b/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => /\\bexport\\b/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\tjavascript: {\n\t\tdecl: /\\b(?:function|class|const|let|var)\\b/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => /\\bexport\\b/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\t// Java: only `public` declarations are part of the API; package-private/protected/private are not.\n\tjava: {\n\t\tdecl: /\\b(?:class|interface|enum|record|void|[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_<>[\\]]*)\\s+[A-Za-z_]\\w*\\s*[({]/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => /\\bpublic\\b/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\t// Kotlin: declarations are public by default; private/internal/protected demote them.\n\tkotlin: {\n\t\tdecl: /\\b(?:fun|class|interface|object|val|var)\\b/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => !/\\b(?:private|internal|protected)\\b/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\t// Python: a leading-underscore name is private by convention; anything else is public.\n\tpython: {\n\t\tdecl: /^\\s*(?:def|class)\\s+[A-Za-z_]/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => !/^\\s*(?:def|class)\\s+_/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\t// Go: an exported identifier starts with an upper-case letter.\n\tgo: {\n\t\tdecl: /^\\s*(?:func|type|var|const)\\s+[A-Za-z_]/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => /^\\s*(?:func|type|var|const)\\s+(?:\\([^)]*\\)\\s*)?[A-Z]/u.test(l),\n\t},\n};\n\n// (1) Does the repo declare a public library/framework product?\nasync function declaresPublicProduct(repoPath: string): Promise<boolean> {\n\tfor (const ext of MANIFEST_NAMES) {\n\t\tfor (const manifestPath of findFiles(repoPath, ext)) {\n\t\t\tconst matcher = PRODUCT_MANIFESTS.find(([re]) => re.test(manifestPath));\n\t\t\tif (!matcher) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst lines = await readFileLines(manifestPath);\n\t\t\tconst text = [...lines.values()].join(\"\\n\");\n\t\t\tif (matcher[1](text)) {\n\t\t\t\treturn true;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nexport default async function documentsPublicApiAndBehaviourChanges(\n\trepoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hasPublicProduct = await declaresPublicProduct(repoPath);\n\n\t// (2) Classify each declaration ADDED in the diff as public (exported) or internal.\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tlet changedPublicSymbols = 0;\n\tlet changedInternalSymbols = 0;\n\tlet filesScanned = 0;\n\tlet linesAdded = 0;\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tif (lang === null) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst rule = EXPORT_RULES[lang];\n\t\tif (!rule) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tfilesScanned += 1;\n\t\tfor (const [line, content] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\tif (isCommentLine(content, lang)) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tlinesAdded += 1;\n\t\t\tif (!rule.decl.test(content)) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst isPublic = rule.isPublic(content);\n\t\t\tif (isPublic) {\n\t\t\t\tchangedPublicSymbols += 1;\n\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\tchangedInternalSymbols += 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (hints.length < 40) {\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: `${lang}:${isPublic ? \"public-decl\" : \"internal-decl\"}`,\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: content.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: { exported: isPublic },\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (!hasPublicProduct) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\"No public library/framework product was detected in the repo manifests (no SwiftPM .library, non-private package.json entry, publishable Maven/Gradle artifact, distributable Python package, or Go module) — investigate whether this repo exposes any public API surface a consumer depends on before deciding documentation was owed.\",\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (changedPublicSymbols > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The diff adds ${changedPublicSymbols} exported/public declaration(s)${hasPublicProduct ? \" in a repo that ships a public product\" : \"\"} — investigate whether their documented behaviour or signature changed and whether that change is reflected in docs/comments.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (changedInternalSymbols > 0 && changedPublicSymbols === 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The diff adds ${changedInternalSymbols} declaration(s) but none are exported/public (e.g. modifier-less Swift is internal, non-exported TS, package-private Java, leading-underscore Python) — investigate whether any public-facing behaviour actually changed before expecting public-API documentation.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\thasPublicProduct: hasPublicProduct ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tchangedPublicSymbols,\n\t\t\tchangedInternalSymbols,\n\t\t\tfilesScanned,\n\t\t\tlinesAdded,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"People outside your code depend on what you export. Change a public function or endpoint without a note and they find out at runtime, in their own project, with no warning, and they trust your library a little less each time.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A renamed parameter or a changed response shape lands with its reference docs and a changelog line updated in the same change, so the people building on your API hear about it from you, not from a broken build.","groupSlug":"decisions-and-documentation","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 180\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Rename undocumented</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"118.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">-getUser(id)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"15\">+fetchUser(id)</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"16.75\" y=\"104.75\" width=\"126.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"28\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">No changelog</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"176\" font-size=\"15\">Callers break</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Rename documented</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"118.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">-getUser(id)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"15\">+fetchUser(id)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">Changelog: <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">getUser</tspan> → <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">fetchUser</tspan></text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"176\" font-size=\"15\">Callers adapt</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A change renames getUser(id) to fetchUser(id) with no changelog, so callers break. The same rename with the changelog line “getUser → fetchUser” lets callers adapt."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, list what your change alters that other software depends on. Look for exported functions, published packages and documented endpoints. Include the format of stored or sent data, the errors and responses that callers handle, and default settings.\n- For each item, write a note in the same pull request or merge request. Update the doc comment, add a changelog line, update the reference docs, or say it in the description.\n- Name the exact parameter, field, route or default that moved. For example, instead of “Updated the users endpoint”, write “The users endpoint returns `fullName` in place of `name`”.\n- If you remove or rename something, or change a default, tell consumers how to adapt. Say what to use instead, so their code keeps working after they update.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The changed code is internal and has no separate consumer. Examples are internal types, private signatures, local refactors and fields that only your app uses.\n- The diff and the description show no change to a public surface at all.\n\nIt still applies when your project is an app and not a library, and other software uses its endpoints, config or data formats.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Deciding by the kind of project or by a keyword such as `public` or `export`. Instead, ask whether a separate consumer depends on the code you changed. That can be another team’s service or a project that installs your package.\n- Writing a note that never names what changed, such as “API improvements”. Instead, name the parameter, field, route or default that a consumer must adapt to.\n- Leaving the note for a later change. Instead, ship it with the change, so consumers hear about it when the change lands.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [What to look for in a code review](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/looking-for.html), Google Engineering Practices. A change to how people use code needs matching docs, including generated references.\n- [Semantic Versioning 2.0.0](https://semver.org/), Tom Preston-Werner. Software that follows it declares a public API and updates its docs when it deprecates part of that API.\n- [Keep a Changelog 1.1.0](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/), Olivier Lacan. A changelog lists notable changes for people, with changed, deprecated and removed features in their own groups.\n- [How to break an API: cost negotiation and community values in three software ecosystems](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~ckaestne/pdf/fse16.pdf), Bogart and others, FSE 2016. In interviews with 28 developers, maintainers eased the cost of breaking changes by telling users and documenting how to upgrade.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Document public API and behaviour changes","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: consumer-facing documentation of the changed public surface — a downstream caller hears what moved from the change itself.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one practice: when the change alters a PUBLIC or SHARED surface — something a SEPARATE consumer depends on and compiles or integrates against — does the change carry, in the same diff or in the description, documentation that tells consumers what moved? Public surfaces: a library''s or framework''s exported symbols, a published package''s API, a documented HTTP or RPC contract, a persisted or wire schema, an error or response shape callers branch on, a config default a consumer sets. Good: the renamed parameter, new endpoint, changed response field or breaking removal lands with its doc-comment, a changelog line, or a description sentence naming the consumer-facing effect. Bad: a real exported-surface change ships with nothing a consumer could read to adapt. A generated API specification or reference documents a contract when its authoritative source and meaning are captured: judge the contract and the information a consumer receives, not whether the text was handwritten. Irrelevant generated output is not documentation, and generated churn alone never establishes an authored contract decision.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Establish the separate consumer from the captured contract or usage, never from package type alone: an application can expose HTTP endpoints, configuration or wire formats to other consumers without publishing a library. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when the changed symbols are internal types, private signatures, local refactors or app-only fields with no separate consumer, or when no public-surface change is observable in the diff and description at all. Decide whether a changed symbol is actually exported and consumer-facing as an experienced reviewer would, not by keyword; a plainly internal symbol is NOT_APPLICABLE.\n\n## Sources\n1. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`, with `work/change/files.json`): the surface change itself, and any documentation shipped beside it — a doc-comment on or above the surface, a changelog or docs addition, an updated reference. It establishes what changed and what documentation arrived with it. It never establishes a consumer by itself.\n2. The description (`context/description.md`): a sentence naming the consumer-facing effect. It establishes documentation a consumer would read on the change; it never establishes a surface change the diff does not show.\n3. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): the contract and its usage — who depends on the surface, and the existing reference docs the diff leaves untouched. It establishes whether a separate consumer exists and where the documentation would live. It never supplies the note the change failed to write.\n4. A linked wiki document the change references, when it was materialised as a `.md` file under `context/docs/`: its body may hold the documentation consumers need. Cite its file path together with the quoted or closely paraphrased sentence relied on; a bare URL is not evidence that the document says it. When the change points at a specific document that was not materialised for you, the documentation may live there: report a collection gap and record no observation, never an undocumented surface.\nAn image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- MET: the public-surface change carries documentation naming what moved — quote the surface change and the documenting text.\n- NOT_MET: documentation exists for the change yet never names the specific changed parameter, field, route or default a consumer must adapt to. Quote both.\n- NOT_MET: a real public-surface change and, after looking in every place a consumer would — the doc-comment on or above the surface, a changelog or docs addition, the description, and a materialised wiki document — nothing a consumer could read to adapt.\n- UNDETERMINED only when the evidence genuinely cannot settle whether the one symbol this practice turns on crosses a module or service boundary; name the question. Do not resolve that doubt into an observation or into a claim that no public surface was here. Having looked everywhere and found no documentation for an established public surface is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review. When several surfaces change, grade by the least-documented one.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR on documentation alone — a formative practice, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a new additive surface with no documentation, since nothing breaks but consumers cannot discover it; lighter still, documentation that exists yet never names the specific changed parameter or field. MAJOR: a breaking change — a removed or renamed symbol, route or field, a narrowed type, a changed default — shipping with no documentation anywhere, so callers break without a migration note. Equivalent situations land at equivalent severity.\n\n## Grounding\nCite the diff lines of the observable surface change in every cell. For MET, quote the documenting text and its file. For NOT_MET, quote the documentation and the changed element it omits. For NOT_MET, cite the surface change and name the boundary searched: the surface''s doc-comment, the changelog and docs in the diff, the description, and any materialised wiki document. Never invent a symbol, an export marker, a changelog or a clean bill you did not see. Never assert the compile or runtime correctness of the surface, and never impute intent or care; describe the documentation practice on the observable artifact only.\n\n## Defer\nThe pull request''s general what-and-why narrative belongs to `describe-what-and-why`; whether a design decision''s rationale is recorded belongs to `records-significant-decisions-with-rationale`. A thin description or an unjustified choice is not an observation here. Here only: is the public-surface change documented for its consumers?","precomputeScript":"// Precompute HINTS for documents-public-api-and-behaviour-changes. Surfaces FACTS ONLY — the LLM judges.\n//\n// Two STRUCTURAL leads a diff hunk alone cannot reveal: whether a manifest declares a library/framework\n// product, and whether the declarations the diff adds are EXPORTED or internal/private. Neither settles the\n// public surface: an app without a library product can still expose an HTTP, configuration or wire contract\n// to separate consumers, which the review investigates. A modifier-less Swift declaration is INTERNAL — not\n// public — so the export-status table classifies it as internal. We surface {hasPublicProduct,\n// changedPublicSymbols, changedInternalSymbols} and let the model decide whether documentation was owed. The\n// table is keyed off file extension: adding a language = one row, no engine change.\nimport { isCommentLine } from \"../lib/declarations.ts\";\nimport { findFiles, readFileLines } from \"../lib/grep.ts\";\nimport { languageOf } from \"../lib/languages.ts\";\nimport type { DiffFile, Hint, PullRequestMetadata } from \"../lib/types.ts\";\n\n// ── (1) Public-product manifest scan ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// Each manifest filename maps to a predicate over its raw text that answers \"does this declare a consumable\n// library/framework product?\". One row per ecosystem; the predicate stays neutral (a structural test, no observation).\nconst PRODUCT_MANIFESTS: [RegExp, (text: string) => boolean][] = [\n\t// Swift Package Manager: a `.library(...)` product.\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)Package\\.swift$/u, (t) => /\\.library\\s*\\(/u.test(t)],\n\t// npm: a non-private package that declares an entry/types surface for consumers.\n\t[\n\t\t/(?:^|\\/)package\\.json$/u,\n\t\t(t) =>\n\t\t\t!/\"private\"\\s*:\\s*true/u.test(t) && /\"(?:main|module|exports|types|typings)\"\\s*:/u.test(t),\n\t],\n\t// Maven: packaged as a real artifact (jar/etc.), not an aggregator pom.\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)pom\\.xml$/u, (t) => !/<packaging>\\s*pom\\s*<\\/packaging>/u.test(t)],\n\t// Gradle: applies the java-library plugin or a publishing plugin.\n\t[\n\t\t/(?:^|\\/)build\\.gradle(?:\\.kts)?$/u,\n\t\t(t) =>\n\t\t\t/(?:java-library|maven-publish|`maven-publish`|com\\.vanniktech\\.maven\\.publish)/u.test(t),\n\t],\n\t// Python: declares packages to distribute.\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)setup\\.py$/u, (t) => /(?:packages\\s*=|find_packages\\s*\\()/u.test(t)],\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)setup\\.cfg$/u, (t) => /\\[options\\][^[]*packages\\s*=/u.test(t)],\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)pyproject\\.toml$/u, (t) => /(?:\\[project\\]|\\[tool\\.poetry\\]|packages\\s*=)/u.test(t)],\n\t// Go: a module declaration makes the package importable by others.\n\t[/(?:^|\\/)go\\.mod$/u, (t) => /^module\\s+\\S+/mu.test(t)],\n];\n\nconst MANIFEST_NAMES = [\"swift\", \"json\", \"xml\", \"gradle\", \"kts\", \"py\", \"cfg\", \"toml\", \"mod\"];\n\n// ── (2) Per-language export status of a CHANGED declaration ────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// For each language: detect that a line declares a symbol, and whether that symbol is PUBLIC (exported) or not.\n// `isPublic` is only consulted when `decl` matches. Modifier-less Swift is INTERNAL, not public.\ninterface ExportRule {\n\t// line introduces a named declaration (func/class/type/var/const)\n\tdecl: RegExp;\n\t// is that declaration part of the public surface?\n\tisPublic: (line: string) => boolean;\n}\n\nconst EXPORT_RULES: Record<string, ExportRule> = {\n\t// Swift: public/open are exported; modifier-less / internal / private / fileprivate are NOT public.\n\tswift: {\n\t\tdecl: /\\b(?:func|class|struct|enum|protocol|extension|var|let|typealias|actor|init)\\b/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => /\\b(?:public|open)\\b/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\t// TypeScript and JavaScript: an `export` keyword (or `export default`) marks the public surface.\n\ttypescript: {\n\t\tdecl: /\\b(?:function|class|interface|type|enum|const|let|var|namespace)\\b/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => /\\bexport\\b/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\tjavascript: {\n\t\tdecl: /\\b(?:function|class|const|let|var)\\b/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => /\\bexport\\b/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\t// Java: only `public` declarations are part of the API; package-private/protected/private are not.\n\tjava: {\n\t\tdecl: /\\b(?:class|interface|enum|record|void|[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_<>[\\]]*)\\s+[A-Za-z_]\\w*\\s*[({]/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => /\\bpublic\\b/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\t// Kotlin: declarations are public by default; private/internal/protected demote them.\n\tkotlin: {\n\t\tdecl: /\\b(?:fun|class|interface|object|val|var)\\b/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => !/\\b(?:private|internal|protected)\\b/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\t// Python: a leading-underscore name is private by convention; anything else is public.\n\tpython: {\n\t\tdecl: /^\\s*(?:def|class)\\s+[A-Za-z_]/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => !/^\\s*(?:def|class)\\s+_/u.test(l),\n\t},\n\t// Go: an exported identifier starts with an upper-case letter.\n\tgo: {\n\t\tdecl: /^\\s*(?:func|type|var|const)\\s+[A-Za-z_]/u,\n\t\tisPublic: (l) => /^\\s*(?:func|type|var|const)\\s+(?:\\([^)]*\\)\\s*)?[A-Z]/u.test(l),\n\t},\n};\n\n// (1) Does the repo declare a public library/framework product?\nasync function declaresPublicProduct(repoPath: string): Promise<boolean> {\n\tfor (const ext of MANIFEST_NAMES) {\n\t\tfor (const manifestPath of findFiles(repoPath, ext)) {\n\t\t\tconst matcher = PRODUCT_MANIFESTS.find(([re]) => re.test(manifestPath));\n\t\t\tif (!matcher) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst lines = await readFileLines(manifestPath);\n\t\t\tconst text = [...lines.values()].join(\"\\n\");\n\t\t\tif (matcher[1](text)) {\n\t\t\t\treturn true;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false;\n}\n\nexport default async function documentsPublicApiAndBehaviourChanges(\n\trepoPath: string,\n\tdiffFiles: Map<string, DiffFile>,\n\t_m: PullRequestMetadata,\n) {\n\tconst hasPublicProduct = await declaresPublicProduct(repoPath);\n\n\t// (2) Classify each declaration ADDED in the diff as public (exported) or internal.\n\tconst hints: Hint[] = [];\n\tlet changedPublicSymbols = 0;\n\tlet changedInternalSymbols = 0;\n\tlet filesScanned = 0;\n\tlet linesAdded = 0;\n\tfor (const [path, df] of diffFiles) {\n\t\tconst lang = languageOf(path);\n\t\tif (lang === null) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst rule = EXPORT_RULES[lang];\n\t\tif (!rule) {\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tfilesScanned += 1;\n\t\tfor (const [line, content] of df.addedLines) {\n\t\t\tif (isCommentLine(content, lang)) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tlinesAdded += 1;\n\t\t\tif (!rule.decl.test(content)) {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tconst isPublic = rule.isPublic(content);\n\t\t\tif (isPublic) {\n\t\t\t\tchangedPublicSymbols += 1;\n\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\tchangedInternalSymbols += 1;\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif (hints.length < 40) {\n\t\t\t\thints.push({\n\t\t\t\t\tfile: path,\n\t\t\t\t\tline,\n\t\t\t\t\tpattern: `${lang}:${isPublic ? \"public-decl\" : \"internal-decl\"}`,\n\t\t\t\t\tcontext: content.trim().slice(0, 160),\n\t\t\t\t\tinDiff: true,\n\t\t\t\t\tflags: { exported: isPublic },\n\t\t\t\t});\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tconst directions: string[] = [];\n\tif (!hasPublicProduct) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t\"No public library/framework product was detected in the repo manifests (no SwiftPM .library, non-private package.json entry, publishable Maven/Gradle artifact, distributable Python package, or Go module) — investigate whether this repo exposes any public API surface a consumer depends on before deciding documentation was owed.\",\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (changedPublicSymbols > 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The diff adds ${changedPublicSymbols} exported/public declaration(s)${hasPublicProduct ? \" in a repo that ships a public product\" : \"\"} — investigate whether their documented behaviour or signature changed and whether that change is reflected in docs/comments.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tif (changedInternalSymbols > 0 && changedPublicSymbols === 0) {\n\t\tdirections.push(\n\t\t\t`The diff adds ${changedInternalSymbols} declaration(s) but none are exported/public (e.g. modifier-less Swift is internal, non-exported TS, package-private Java, leading-underscore Python) — investigate whether any public-facing behaviour actually changed before expecting public-API documentation.`,\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\n\treturn {\n\t\thints,\n\t\tmetrics: {\n\t\t\thasPublicProduct: hasPublicProduct ? 1 : 0,\n\t\t\tchangedPublicSymbols,\n\t\t\tchangedInternalSymbols,\n\t\t\tfilesScanned,\n\t\t\tlinesAdded,\n\t\t},\n\t\tdirections,\n\t};\n}\n","automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"People outside your code depend on what you export. Change a public function or endpoint without a note and they find out at runtime, in their own project, with no warning, and they trust your library a little less each time.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A renamed parameter or a changed response shape lands with its reference docs and a changelog line updated in the same change, so the people building on your API hear about it from you, not from a broken build.","groupSlug":"decisions-and-documentation","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 180\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Rename undocumented</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"118.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">-getUser(id)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"15\">+fetchUser(id)</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"16.75\" y=\"104.75\" width=\"126.5\" height=\"30.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"28\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">No changelog</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"176\" font-size=\"15\">Callers break</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Rename documented</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"118.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">-getUser(id)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"15\">+fetchUser(id)</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"15\">Changelog: <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">getUser</tspan> → <tspan class=\"pv-mono\">fetchUser</tspan></text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"176\" font-size=\"15\">Callers adapt</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A change renames getUser(id) to fetchUser(id) with no changelog, so callers break. The same rename with the changelog line “getUser → fetchUser” lets callers adapt."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, list what your change alters that other software depends on. Look for exported functions, published packages and documented endpoints. Include the format of stored or sent data, the errors and responses that callers handle, and default settings.\n- For each item, write a note in the same pull request or merge request. Update the doc comment, add a changelog line, update the reference docs, or say it in the description.\n- Name the exact parameter, field, route or default that moved. For example, instead of “Updated the users endpoint”, write “The users endpoint returns `fullName` in place of `name`”.\n- If you remove or rename something, or change a default, tell consumers how to adapt. Say what to use instead, so their code keeps working after they update.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The changed code is internal and has no separate consumer. Examples are internal types, private signatures, local refactors and fields that only your app uses.\n- The diff and the description show no change to a public surface at all.\n\nIt still applies when your project is an app and not a library, and other software uses its endpoints, config or data formats.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Deciding by the kind of project or by a keyword such as `public` or `export`. Instead, ask whether a separate consumer depends on the code you changed. That can be another team’s service or a project that installs your package.\n- Writing a note that never names what changed, such as “API improvements”. Instead, name the parameter, field, route or default that a consumer must adapt to.\n- Leaving the note for a later change. Instead, ship it with the change, so consumers hear about it when the change lands.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [What to look for in a code review](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/looking-for.html), Google Engineering Practices. A change to how people use code needs matching docs, including generated references.\n- [Semantic Versioning 2.0.0](https://semver.org/), Tom Preston-Werner. Software that follows it declares a public API and updates its docs when it deprecates part of that API.\n- [Keep a Changelog 1.1.0](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/), Olivier Lacan. A changelog lists notable changes for people, with changed, deprecated and removed features in their own groups.\n- [How to break an API: cost negotiation and community values in three software ecosystems](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~ckaestne/pdf/fse16.pdf), Bogart and others, FSE 2016. In interviews with 28 developers, maintainers eased the cost of breaking changes by telling users and documenting how to upgrade.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"API and behaviour changes ship with their docs"'::jsonb),'v5:0d211b090216c3e4168978b8e0ccbf1504d51f0706f1b0a4077e7b21f4e6f17d','practice:v3:0c4ede2f02fe512d1e333dc9fc1ffc41985e1681129b6b8e34bf98762fc2b6e4');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','change-keeps-linked-docs-consistent','scm.pull_request','{"name":"Keep linked wiki docs consistent with the change","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"outline.documents","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.linked-work-items","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: the captured linked document and changed behavior agree on the same contract, setting or operational step.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one thing: when this pull request changes a documented behaviour — a config default, a public API or wire contract, a CLI flag, an error or return code, an operational step — AND a linked wiki document describes that same behaviour, does the document still agree with the code, or has the diff left it contradicting reality? Work in two stages, in order. (1) From the hand-written diff, identify a concrete behaviour, contract or config the change alters. (2) Only if a materialised document describes that same thing, check whether the document now asserts something the code contradicts: an old default, a removed flag, a renamed field, a changed status code, a superseded step. The signal is a genuine contradiction between a specific document passage and a specific diff hunk — never staleness in the abstract, and never \"the code changed, so the document is probably old\". The presence or absence of a wiki link is never itself the observation; a change with no relevant document is not a weakness.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when: the complete pull request description and all captured linked issues reference no wiki document, with no unreadable or uncaptured adopted linked issue that could carry the reference; the diff is empty; or the diff does not touch anything the materialised document describes. Before concluding that no wiki document is referenced, inspect the complete description and every adopted linked issue. If an adopted linked issue was not captured or cannot be read, report a collection gap and record no observation unless the missing issue cannot affect the proposed result. A captured contradiction may support NOT_MET; MET or a no-relevant-document NOT_APPLICABLE result requires every adopted reference that could change that result. No linked issue and no document reference in the complete description needs no Outline capture. A document is available to you only when the pull request references it and its body was materialised as a `.md` file under `context/docs/`. When the pull request references a specific document that a reader would expect to describe the behaviour this diff changes, and that document was not materialised, you cannot tell whether it now contradicts the code: report a collection gap and record no observation. An unreadable diff is the same collection gap.\n\n## Sources\n1. The description (`context/description.md`) and each linked issue (`context/linked_work_items/<n>.md`): which wiki documents the change references, and any sentence saying the document was updated. They establish that a document is in scope; they never establish what the document says. A bare URL is not evidence of a document''s content. An image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n2. The materialised documents (`context/docs/<collection>/<document>.md`): the passages that describe the behaviour. They establish what the wiki asserts today. Read the file before concluding anything about it.\n3. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`, with `work/change/files.json`): the hand-written hunks that alter a behaviour, contract or config, and any documentation the same change updates. It establishes what the code now does; it never establishes what the wiki says.\n4. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): context for what a changed symbol, flag or step means. It never supplies a document passage.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- NOT_MET: the diff changes a documented behaviour and the materialised document still asserts the old behaviour, so a reader who trusts the wiki is misled. Quote both sides.\n- MET: the diff changes something the document describes AND the same pull request updates that document, or the document already reflects the new behaviour, so code and document do not contradict.\n- UNDETERMINED: no ordinary case. A referenced document you were not shown is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED. Having read the materialised document and found no passage the diff makes false is MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, over the whole change.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR — the aim is a trustworthy wiki, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a contradiction on a minor or internal-facing detail, a nudge to update the document. MAJOR: a contradiction a consumer would act on wrongly — a wrong documented default or limit, a removed API or flag the document still offers, a changed auth, security or data-handling step. When unsure, prefer the lower severity.\n\n## Grounding\nGround every NOT_MET in both sides: the exact added or removed `[L<n>]` diff line that changes the behaviour, and the verbatim sentence from the materialised `.md` document that still asserts the old behaviour, cited with its `context/docs/...` file path. If you cannot point to a specific document passage the diff makes false, do not raise the observation: never infer a contradiction you cannot cite, and never fabricate a document quote. For MET, cite the diff line of the changed behaviour and the document line (in the diff or in the materialised file) that now agrees with it. Never claim a build or test ran, and never impute intent or care; describe what the document says and what the code does.\n\n## Defer\nWhether a change was documented at all, in any surface, belongs to `documents-public-api-and-behaviour-changes`. Whether the WHY of a decision is recorded belongs to `records-significant-decisions-with-rationale`. Here only: does a specific linked wiki document now contradict the code?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A wiki that contradicts the code is worse than no wiki: teammates act on documented behaviour that no longer holds, and the drift compounds silently until someone is burned by it.","whatGoodLooksLike":"When a change alters something the team''s design or reference doc describes, the same PR brings the doc along, so the wiki keeps telling the truth about the system.","groupSlug":"decisions-and-documentation","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 140\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Wiki disagrees</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"110.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Change</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">retries=5</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M112 56h32\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"112\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"144\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"144.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"158.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(160 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M2.75 0.75h7l3.5 3.5v11h-10.5z\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M9.75 0.75v3.5h3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"188\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Linked wiki</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"160\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Retries: 3</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"15\">Teammates misled</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Wiki agrees</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"110.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Change</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">retries=5</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M448 56h32\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"448\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"480\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"480.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"158.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(496 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M2.75 0.75h7l3.5 3.5v11h-10.5z\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M9.75 0.75v3.5h3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"524\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Linked wiki</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"496\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Retries: 5</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"15\">Teammates can trust it</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A change sets retries=5, but its linked wiki says “Retries: 3”, so the wiki disagrees and teammates are misled. When the wiki says “Retries: 5”, it agrees and teammates can trust it."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, open each wiki page that your description or a linked issue names.\n- In each page, find the passages about what your change alters. Look for a default, an API field, a command-line flag, or an error code. Look also for a step to deploy or run the system.\n- Fix each passage that your code now makes false, before the change merges. For example, instead of leaving the old default timeout on the setup page, change it to the new one.\n- When you remove a flag, a field, or an endpoint, remove or rewrite every passage that still offers it.\n- Say in the description which wiki page you updated.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your description and your linked issues do not name a wiki page.\n- Your change is empty.\n- Your change does not touch anything that the linked page describes.\n\nIt still applies when only a linked issue names the page, and your description does not.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that every change needs a wiki link or a wiki edit. A change with no related page is fine. Instead, update only the passages that your change makes false.\n- Adding a link to the page, or writing “docs updated” in the description, and stopping there. Neither changes what the page says. Instead, read the passage and fix the old value in it.\n- Assuming that the page is fine because its title still fits. A page can describe the right feature with the wrong default. Instead, compare each passage about your change with the new code.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Documentation best practices](https://google.github.io/styleguide/docguide/best_practices.html), Google. Change the docs in the same change as the code, and delete docs that are wrong, because dead docs misinform.\n- [What to look for in a code review](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/looking-for.html), Google Engineering Practices. A reviewer checks that a change to how people build, test, use or release code also updates its documentation.\n- [Detecting outdated code element references in software repository documentation](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-023-10397-6), Tan, Wagner and Treude, 2024. Of over 3,000 GitHub projects, most had docs that still named deleted code at some point in their history.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Keep linked wiki docs consistent with the change","signals":["scm.pull_request.opened","scm.pull_request.ready","scm.pull_request.synchronized"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"outline.documents","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.linked-work-items","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.pull-request.diff","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"},{"sourceKind":"scm.repository.tree","stance":"CONTEXTUAL"}],"reviewWhen":{"draftStatus":["NOT_DRAFT"]},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured change: the added and removed lines and the surrounding code the practice names. Precompute hints are leads to inspect, never conclusions. Cite the old or new line coordinates of the diff; do not claim to have compiled or executed the change.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: the captured linked document and changed behavior agree on the same contract, setting or operational step.\n\n## The standard\nJudge one thing: when this pull request changes a documented behaviour — a config default, a public API or wire contract, a CLI flag, an error or return code, an operational step — AND a linked wiki document describes that same behaviour, does the document still agree with the code, or has the diff left it contradicting reality? Work in two stages, in order. (1) From the hand-written diff, identify a concrete behaviour, contract or config the change alters. (2) Only if a materialised document describes that same thing, check whether the document now asserts something the code contradicts: an old default, a removed flag, a renamed field, a changed status code, a superseded step. The signal is a genuine contradiction between a specific document passage and a specific diff hunk — never staleness in the abstract, and never \"the code changed, so the document is probably old\". The presence or absence of a wiki link is never itself the observation; a change with no relevant document is not a weakness.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the fact, and stop when: the complete pull request description and all captured linked issues reference no wiki document, with no unreadable or uncaptured adopted linked issue that could carry the reference; the diff is empty; or the diff does not touch anything the materialised document describes. Before concluding that no wiki document is referenced, inspect the complete description and every adopted linked issue. If an adopted linked issue was not captured or cannot be read, report a collection gap and record no observation unless the missing issue cannot affect the proposed result. A captured contradiction may support NOT_MET; MET or a no-relevant-document NOT_APPLICABLE result requires every adopted reference that could change that result. No linked issue and no document reference in the complete description needs no Outline capture. A document is available to you only when the pull request references it and its body was materialised as a `.md` file under `context/docs/`. When the pull request references a specific document that a reader would expect to describe the behaviour this diff changes, and that document was not materialised, you cannot tell whether it now contradicts the code: report a collection gap and record no observation. An unreadable diff is the same collection gap.\n\n## Sources\n1. The description (`context/description.md`) and each linked issue (`context/linked_work_items/<n>.md`): which wiki documents the change references, and any sentence saying the document was updated. They establish that a document is in scope; they never establish what the document says. A bare URL is not evidence of a document''s content. An image: its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing.\n2. The materialised documents (`context/docs/<collection>/<document>.md`): the passages that describe the behaviour. They establish what the wiki asserts today. Read the file before concluding anything about it.\n3. The diff (`work/change/diff.patch`, with `work/change/files.json`): the hand-written hunks that alter a behaviour, contract or config, and any documentation the same change updates. It establishes what the code now does; it never establishes what the wiki says.\n4. The checkout (`<repositoryRoot>`): context for what a changed symbol, flag or step means. It never supplies a document passage.\n\n## Judge\nAssess conformance to the standard in this context.\n- NOT_MET: the diff changes a documented behaviour and the materialised document still asserts the old behaviour, so a reader who trusts the wiki is misled. Quote both sides.\n- MET: the diff changes something the document describes AND the same pull request updates that document, or the document already reflects the new behaviour, so code and document do not contradict.\n- UNDETERMINED: no ordinary case. A referenced document you were not shown is a collection gap, not UNDETERMINED. Having read the materialised document and found no passage the diff makes false is MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, over the whole change.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; never above MAJOR — the aim is a trustworthy wiki, not a merge gate. MINOR, the usual band: a contradiction on a minor or internal-facing detail, a nudge to update the document. MAJOR: a contradiction a consumer would act on wrongly — a wrong documented default or limit, a removed API or flag the document still offers, a changed auth, security or data-handling step. When unsure, prefer the lower severity.\n\n## Grounding\nGround every NOT_MET in both sides: the exact added or removed `[L<n>]` diff line that changes the behaviour, and the verbatim sentence from the materialised `.md` document that still asserts the old behaviour, cited with its `context/docs/...` file path. If you cannot point to a specific document passage the diff makes false, do not raise the observation: never infer a contradiction you cannot cite, and never fabricate a document quote. For MET, cite the diff line of the changed behaviour and the document line (in the diff or in the materialised file) that now agrees with it. Never claim a build or test ran, and never impute intent or care; describe what the document says and what the code does.\n\n## Defer\nWhether a change was documented at all, in any surface, belongs to `documents-public-api-and-behaviour-changes`. Whether the WHY of a decision is recorded belongs to `records-significant-decisions-with-rationale`. Here only: does a specific linked wiki document now contradict the code?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"RUNTIME_BEHAVIOR_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Repository evidence does not establish behavior in a deployed runtime."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A wiki that contradicts the code is worse than no wiki: teammates act on documented behaviour that no longer holds, and the drift compounds silently until someone is burned by it.","whatGoodLooksLike":"When a change alters something the team''s design or reference doc describes, the same PR brings the doc along, so the wiki keeps telling the truth about the system.","groupSlug":"decisions-and-documentation","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 140\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Wiki disagrees</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"110.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Change</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"16\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">retries=5</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M112 56h32\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"112\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"144\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"144.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"158.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(160 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M2.75 0.75h7l3.5 3.5v11h-10.5z\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M9.75 0.75v3.5h3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"188\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Linked wiki</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"160\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Retries: 3</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"15\">Teammates misled</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Wiki agrees</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"110.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M4 5.5v5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"4\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M12 10.5V6a3 3 0 0 0-3-3H7.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Change</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink pv-mono\" x=\"352\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">retries=5</text>\n  <path class=\"pv-stroke-muted\" d=\"M448 56h32\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"448\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <circle class=\"pv-fill-muted\" cx=\"480\" cy=\"56\" r=\"4\"/>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"480.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"158.5\" height=\"78.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(496 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M2.75 0.75h7l3.5 3.5v11h-10.5z\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M9.75 0.75v3.5h3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"524\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Linked wiki</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"496\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Retries: 5</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"15\">Teammates can trust it</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A change sets retries=5, but its linked wiki says “Retries: 3”, so the wiki disagrees and teammates are misled. When the wiki says “Retries: 5”, it agrees and teammates can trust it."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Before you ask for a review, open each wiki page that your description or a linked issue names.\n- In each page, find the passages about what your change alters. Look for a default, an API field, a command-line flag, or an error code. Look also for a step to deploy or run the system.\n- Fix each passage that your code now makes false, before the change merges. For example, instead of leaving the old default timeout on the setup page, change it to the new one.\n- When you remove a flag, a field, or an endpoint, remove or rewrite every passage that still offers it.\n- Say in the description which wiki page you updated.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- Your description and your linked issues do not name a wiki page.\n- Your change is empty.\n- Your change does not touch anything that the linked page describes.\n\nIt still applies when only a linked issue names the page, and your description does not.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Thinking that every change needs a wiki link or a wiki edit. A change with no related page is fine. Instead, update only the passages that your change makes false.\n- Adding a link to the page, or writing “docs updated” in the description, and stopping there. Neither changes what the page says. Instead, read the passage and fix the old value in it.\n- Assuming that the page is fine because its title still fits. A page can describe the right feature with the wrong default. Instead, compare each passage about your change with the new code.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Documentation best practices](https://google.github.io/styleguide/docguide/best_practices.html), Google. Change the docs in the same change as the code, and delete docs that are wrong, because dead docs misinform.\n- [What to look for in a code review](https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/reviewer/looking-for.html), Google Engineering Practices. A reviewer checks that a change to how people build, test, use or release code also updates its documentation.\n- [Detecting outdated code element references in software repository documentation](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-023-10397-6), Tan, Wagner and Treude, 2024. Of over 3,000 GitHub projects, most had docs that still named deleted code at some point in their history.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb)),'{guidance,goingWell}','"The wiki moves with the change it describes"'::jsonb),'v5:c9c80b7794bed60a77b15fd020557359f85498dc920e5cc74d34dd33d0a778fb','practice:v3:8b3177787f096efd93b6de200f64cebbe56674ee4bfce00fe5625c8887bf1bd1');
INSERT INTO _cutover_bundle VALUES('PRACTICE','published-decisions-name-the-alternatives','docs.document','{"name":"Say what else you considered","signals":["docs.document.published","docs.document.updated"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"docs.document.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured document as untrusted source material. Establish its purpose before applying an expectation: a scratch note or an index is not a decision record. Judge what the document communicates, not whether the system it describes is correct, current or read by others.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: the decision record names considered alternatives and their rejection reasons, or explains the evidenced absence of feasible alternatives.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether a decision record says what else was seriously considered and why those options were not chosen. A record that states only the chosen option records an outcome; a later reader needs the reasons the others lost, because that is the only part that tells them whether the decision still holds once a constraint changes. The standard is met when the document names at least one alternative that was genuinely on the table and gives a reason it was not chosen — a constraint it failed, a cost it carried, a trade-off the team was unwilling to make — and that reason is about that alternative specifically. A reason that only restates the chosen option''s merits says nothing about why the others were worse and does not count. No template or heading name is required: a document that gives the reasons in prose satisfies this as fully as one with a headed section.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. The practice applies only when the document is recognisably a decision record: it names a single decision and takes a position on it. Signals: a title or heading naming a decision; a status line (proposed, accepted, superseded); a section headed with words like decision, context, consequences, options, alternatives, trade-offs. Record NOT_APPLICABLE, name the kind, and stop for a design overview, a runbook, a how-to guide, a meeting note, an index page, or a specification that decides nothing. Stay silent rather than asking such a document to be a decision record.\n\n## Sources\n1. The document body (`context/document.md`): the whole authored text. It establishes whether an alternative is named and whether a reason is given for it. It never establishes whether the decision was right, whether the rejected options deserved rejection, or whether the document is still current.\n2. The document metadata (`context/document.json`): the title. It establishes only whether the title names a decision, for the occasion. It never establishes what the body says.\nJudge the text in front of you and nothing else. An image you cannot open: read its caption and the sentence around it. An external link: nothing. Two cases are not absence: a record that defers its options discussion to another document you were not given, and a body that visibly breaks off before any options section. If either reflects incomplete capture, report a collection gap and record no observation. If the complete authored document itself ends without discussing alternatives, judge that omission; do not mistake unfinished writing for missing capture.\n\n## Judge\nRead the whole body before deciding. Then assess the standard against all relevant evidence.\n- MET: at least one alternative is named and a reason specific to it is given, or the record explains the constraint that left no genuine alternative. Quote the alternatives and their reasons, or the no-alternative constraint.\n- NOT_MET: the document states a decision and its rationale but names no alternative at all; or it lists alternatives with no reason any of them lost (a bare bullet list of technology names); or the only reason given restates the chosen option''s merits. A section headed \"Alternatives considered\" that is empty, or contains only \"none\", is absent unless the document explains why there was genuinely no other option.\n- UNDETERMINED: no ordinary case. A complete document either accounts for alternatives or does not; what Sources names as incomplete capture is a collection gap, not an open question, while a fully captured document that is itself unfinished is judged under the occasion and the standard. Having read the whole body and found no alternative with a reason is NOT_MET, not UNDETERMINED.\nOne observation per review, on the document as a whole.\n\n## Severity\nNOT_MET only; MINOR is the usual band: a bounded addition fixes it — one alternative and the sentence that ruled it out. Do not raise the band on this practice alone; the weight of the decision is not something the text lets you judge.\n\n## Grounding\nFor MET, cite the exact lines naming the alternative and its reason, or establishing why no genuine alternative existed. For NOT_MET, cite the decision statement and the boundary you searched: the whole body, including any options or alternatives section quoted or stated to be empty. Never claim the decision was correct or incorrect, never claim a rejected alternative deserved its fate, and never claim the document is or is not up to date. Describe what the record says; never impute intent or character.\n\n## Defer\nWhether a pull request records the decisions it makes belongs to `records-significant-decisions-with-rationale`. Whether the decision was right, whether the alternatives deserved rejection, and whether the document is current are not judged by any practice here. Here only: does the record name what else was considered and why it lost?","precomputeScript":null,"automatedReviewPolicy":{"sourceContractVersion":"1.3.0","automatedReview":{"mode":"LANGUAGE_MODEL","evidenceSufficiency":"SUFFICIENT_WHEN_REQUIREMENTS_MET"},"whenEvidenceIsInsufficient":"SKIP_AUTOMATED_REVIEW","knownLimitations":[{"code":"DESCRIBED_SYSTEM_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"Document evidence does not establish whether the system it describes behaves as written."},{"code":"READERSHIP_NOT_OBSERVED","description":"The mirrored document does not establish whether anyone read it or relied on it."}],"insufficiencyReason":null},"whyItMatters":"A decision record that names only the winner tells the next person what was done, not what it cost. When a constraint later changes — a library is deprecated, the traffic grows, the team shrinks — the first question is always \"what else did we look at, and does that reason still hold?\". If the alternatives were never written down, that question can only be answered by redoing the whole investigation, and usually it is not answered at all: the decision quietly becomes something nobody feels able to revisit.","whatGoodLooksLike":"A record that names the two or three options that were genuinely in play and gives each one a sentence saying what ruled it out — the constraint it failed, the cost it carried, the thing the team was not willing to trade. Written for someone who arrives a year later with a changed constraint, so they can tell in a minute whether the decision still stands.","groupSlug":"decisions-and-documentation","deliveryBehavior":{"summaryOnly":false,"overlapGroup":null,"redundantToSlug":null},"visual":{"svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 156\">\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"0\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Alternative named</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"0.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"94.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(16 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M2.75 0.75h7l3.5 3.5v11h-10.5z\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M9.75 0.75v3.5h3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"44\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Decision</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"16\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Use Postgres</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"16\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">Considered SQLite</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-muted\" x=\"0\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"15\">Hard to revisit</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"600\">Alternative weighed</text>\n  <rect class=\"pv-fill-surface pv-stroke-muted\" x=\"336.75\" y=\"32.75\" width=\"302.5\" height=\"94.5\" rx=\"8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <g transform=\"translate(352 48)\"><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M2.75 0.75h7l3.5 3.5v11h-10.5z\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path class=\"pv-stroke-ink\" d=\"M9.75 0.75v3.5h3.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></g>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"380\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"15\">Decision</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"352\" y=\"93\" font-size=\"15\">Use Postgres</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-accent\" x=\"352\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">SQLite rejected: single writer</text>\n  <text class=\"pv-fill-ink\" x=\"336\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"15\">Easy to revisit</text>\n</svg>\n","alt":"A decision to use Postgres says only that SQLite was considered, so it is hard to revisit. When it also says “SQLite rejected: single writer”, the alternative is weighed and the decision is easy to revisit."},"guide":{"markdown":"## How to do it\n\n- Name each option that was really on the table, not only the one you chose.\n- For each option you did not choose, explain why it lost. Say what limit it broke, what it would have cost, or what you would have had to give up. For example, instead of “We chose PostgreSQL”, write “We chose PostgreSQL. We ruled out SQLite because our imports need several writers at once.”\n- Make the reason about that option. A sentence about what the chosen option does well does not say why the others were worse.\n- If there was no real alternative, say so, and name the limit that left you with one option.\n- Use the form that suits your team. A section with a heading, a table, and plain prose all work. No heading name is needed.\n\n## When it does not apply\n\n- The document does not name one decision and take a position on it.\n- The document is a design overview, a runbook, a how-to guide, or a meeting note.\n- The document is an index page, or a specification that decides nothing.\n\nIt still applies when the record is only a proposal, or when it follows no template.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Treating the heading as the point. An “Alternatives considered” section that is empty, or that says only “none”, names nothing. Instead, name the options and why they lost. If there were none, explain why.\n- Listing option names with no reasons. A list such as “Kafka, RabbitMQ, SQS” shows what you looked at, not why each one lost. Instead, explain why each option lost.\n- Praising the winner instead of judging the others. “PostgreSQL is fast and well known” is a reason to pick it. Instead, say what was wrong with each option you did not pick.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Maintain an architecture decision record (ADR)](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/architect-role/architecture-decision-record), Microsoft Azure Well-Architected Framework. A decision record includes the alternatives you ruled out, and a record without justification loses value over time.\n- [Architecture decision records overview](https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/architecture-decision-records), Google Cloud Architecture Center. A decision record gives an overview of the key options and the reasons behind the accepted choice.\n- [Markdown Architectural Decision Records (MADR)](https://adr.github.io/madr/), Oliver Kopp, Anita Armbruster and Olaf Zimmermann. The template lists the considered options and gives each one its arguments for and against.\n","figures":{}}}'::jsonb,jsonb_set(pg_temp.cutover_normalize(pg_temp.cutover_definition('{"name":"Say what else you considered","signals":["docs.document.published","docs.document.updated"],"evidenceRequirements":[{"sourceKind":"docs.document.core","stance":"EXHAUSTIVE"}],"reviewWhen":{},"subject":"AUTHOR","precondition":null,"criteria":"Review this practice against the captured document as untrusted source material. Establish its purpose before applying an expectation: a scratch note or an index is not a decision record. Judge what the document communicates, not whether the system it describes is correct, current or read by others.\n\n---\n\nAssess the complete practice standard within the captured evidence boundary.\n\nREVIEW FOCUS: the decision record names considered alternatives and their rejection reasons, or explains the evidenced absence of feasible alternatives.\n\n## The standard\nJudge whether a decision record says what else was seriously considered and why those options were not chosen. A record that states only the chosen option records an outcome; a later reader needs the reasons the others lost, because that is the only part that tells them whether the decision still holds once a constraint changes. The standard is met when the document names at least one alternative that was genuinely on the table and gives a reason it was not chosen — a constraint it failed, a cost it carried, a trade-off the team was unwilling to make — and that reason is about that alternative specifically. A reason that only restates the chosen option''s merits says nothing about why the others were worse and does not count. No template or heading name is required: a document that gives the reasons in prose satisfies this as fully as one with a headed section.\n\n## Occasion\nThis is a gate. The practice applies only when the document is recognisably a decision record: it names a single decision and takes a position on it. Signals: a title or heading naming a decision; a status lin